E["npc:anduin-wrynn"] = {t="npc",n="Anduin Wrynn",z="multi",s="Anduin Llane Wrynn is the ten-year-old crowned King of Stormwind, reigning in the absence of his missing father, King Varian Wrynn. Due to his young age, the true administration of the kingdom rests in the hands of Highlord Bolvar Fordragon and the royal court.",h="Anduin Llane Wrynn is the ten-year-old crowned King of Stormwind, reigning in the absence of his missing...",sec={{t="A Boy on the Throne",b="Born in the peaceful interlude following the Second War, Prince Anduin was named in honor of both his grandfather King Llane and the legendary knight Sir Anduin Lothar. Following the death of his mother, Queen Tiffin, and the mysterious disappearance of his father en route to a diplomatic summit in Theramore, the child was abruptly crowned to maintain stability throughout the kingdom. Though intelligent and empathetic beyond his years, the ten-year-old monarch has little actual say in state affairs, leaving rule of the realm divided between Highlord Bolvar Fordragon and royal councilor Lady Katrana Prestor.",sp=0},{t="A Pawn in the Court",b="Within the gilded walls of Stormwind Keep, young Anduin is surrounded by escalating political tension. While Highlord Bolvar manages military defenses and strives to maintain the integrity of the kingdom, the noblewoman Katrana Prestor constantly whispers counsel into the court's ear, actively undermining troop deployments to outlying human settlements. Anduin senses the precarious nature of his court and misses his father dearly, yet for now, he remains a figurehead surrounded by scheming nobles and watchful guardians.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is a child ruling Stormwind?",a="King Varian Wrynn vanished under mysterious circumstances while sailing toward a diplomatic summit in Theramore. To avoid a succession crisis and retain order across the realm, ten-year-old Prince Anduin was formally crowned king, while Highlord Bolvar Fordragon was named regent to govern in his stead.",al={"why is anduin king","where is varian","who is the kid king"}},{q="Who actually runs the kingdom right now?",a="Highlord Bolvar Fordragon serves as Regent of Stormwind, commanding the realm's armies and holding ultimate executive power until Anduin comes of age. However, the noblewoman Lady Katrana Prestor serves as a senior advisor, wielding significant influence over the royal court and the House of Nobles.",al={"who rules stormwind","who is in charge","stormwind regent"}},{q="Who is Anduin named after?",a="He is named after two heroes of Stormwind's history: his grandfather, the fallen King Llane Wrynn, and Sir Anduin Lothar, the Lion of Azeroth who led the Alliance during the First and Second Wars.",al={"anduin name origin","anduin namesake","why named anduin"}},{q="Where is Anduin located in the city?",a="King Anduin can be found in the throne room of Stormwind Keep, seated on the royal throne flanked closely by Highlord Bolvar Fordragon and Lady Katrana Prestor.",al={"where is anduin","anduin location","anduin wrynn location"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="To the citizens and soldiers of the Alliance, King Anduin is an innocent symbol of the realm's resilience, even as many worry that his youth leaves the throne vulnerable to shadowy court intrigue.",["race:Human"]="For the people of Stormwind, the boy-king represents the survival of the Wrynn dynasty, carrying the memory of his fallen mother Tiffin and missing father Varian."},kw={"anduin","bolvar","boy-king","prestor","prince","stormwind","throne room","wrynn"},rel={"subzone:stormwind-keep","npc:bolvar-fordragon","topic:varian-wrynn","npc:onyxia","npc:tiffin-ellerian-wrynn","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:stormwind-kingdom","quest:1431"},src={"Anduin Wrynn"}}
E["npc:blanchy"] = {t="npc",n="Blanchy",z="multi",s="Old Blanchy is the loyal, hard-working mare belonging to Theodore and Verna Furlbrow, currently resting beside their wagon near the Jansen Stead in Westfall. Renowned for her gentle disposition and hefty appetite, she remains a steady companion to her owners amidst the turmoil gripping the countryside.",h="Old Blanchy is the loyal, hard-working mare belonging to Theodore and Verna Furlbrow, currently resting...",sec={{t="A Faithful Farmhand",b="Long before pulling the Furlbrows' wagon through the dust of Westfall, Blanchy was raised in the northern stables of Tarren Mill in Hillsbrad. Sold south across the sea to work the breadbasket of Stormwind, she earned a reputation as an exceptionally sweet-tempered, well-behaved mare. Decades of heavy labor have worn her down to 'Old Blanchy,' and the arduous journey fleeing troubled homesteads has left her exhausted. Though visibly weary, her appetite remains hearty, and travelers who bring her nourishing handfuls of oats scattered across the surrounding fields are rewarded by Verna with Blanchy's own treasured blanket and feed pouch.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who owns Blanchy?",a="Blanchy belongs to Theodore and Verna Furlbrow, homesteaders traveling through Westfall. She serves as their steadfast draft horse and beloved family companion.",al={"blanchy owner","who does blanchy belong to","furlbrow horse"}},{q="Where did Blanchy originally come from?",a="She was born and raised far to the north in Lordaeron, stabled at Tarren Mill in the Hillsbrad Foothills. As a filly, she was transported through Southshore and shipped south across the Great Sea to live on a Westfall farm.",al={"blanchy origins","where is blanchy from","young blanchy"}},{q="Why does Verna Furlbrow need oats for Blanchy?",a="Years of labor and the long haul along Westfall's dangerous roads have left Old Blanchy completely drained. Verna asks passing adventurers to scavenge remaining bags of oats from nearby farmlands to restore the faithful horse's strength.",al={"blanchy oats","why feed blanchy","furlbrows horse tired"}},{q="Where can Blanchy be found?",a="Old Blanchy stands hitched beside the Furlbrow wagon along the main road near the Jansen Stead in central Westfall.",al={"blanchy location","where is old blanchy","blanchy map"}}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="For Alliance travelers making the journey south from Elwynn Forest into Westfall, helping feed Old Blanchy is often one of the first quiet acts of charity they perform for the struggling local refugees."},kw={"blanchy","furlbrow","jansen stead","oats","old blanchy","verna furlbrow","westfall"},rel={"npc:verna-furlbrow","subzone:jansen-stead","zone:westfall","zone:hillsbrad","subzone:tarren-mill","subzone:southshore"},src={"Blanchy"}}
E["topic:agamaggan"] = {t="topic",n="Agamaggan",z="general",s="Agamaggan the Great Boar was one of the colossal Ancient Guardians who walked Azeroth in its earliest days. He fell ten thousand years ago fighting the Burning Legion in the War of the Ancients, sacrificing his life so mortals could reach the Well of Eternity. Where his blood and body crashed into the earth, vast networks of thorny vines arose, creating the Razorfen and granting life and purpose to his descendants, the quilboar.",h="Agamaggan the Great Boar was one of the colossal Ancient Guardians who walked Azeroth in its earliest days.",sec={{t="The War of the Ancients",b="Ten thousand years ago, during the first invasion of the Burning Legion, the demigod Cenarius rallied the Ancient Guardians to aid the night elves in defending Azeroth. Agamaggan answered the call with terrifying ferocity. Towering over the battlefield, the Great Boar trampled legions of doomguards under his massive hooves, exhaled streams of caustic breath, and launched salvos of razor-sharp thorns from his bristling back. His warpath carried him across the continent, where he clashed directly against demonic generals such as Mannoroth. Ultimately overwhelmed by sheer numbers, Agamaggan fought to his dying breath, buying Malfurion Stormrage and the kaldorei resistance precious time to reach the Well of Eternity.",sp=0},{t="The Razorfen and Blood Shards",b="Where Agamaggan collapsed in the southern Barrens, his colossal corpse reshaped the land. His spilled blood soaked into the soil, causing enormous, impenetrable brambles and thorny vines to burst upward from the dry earth. These tangled thickets became known as the Razorfen. The quilboar revere the site as their holiest ground, viewing the labyrinth of Razorfen Kraul as his resting body and Razorfen Downs as his gaping skull. Scattered throughout the Barrens are crystallized Blood Shards, believed to be the physical remnants of the demigod's spilled blood, pulsing with lingering primal energy that quilboar geomancers and shamans harness for power.",sp=0},{t="Father of the Quilboar",b="Agamaggan is revered as the divine progenitor of all quilboar. They view the surrounding brambles not merely as homes, but as the living armor left behind by their creator to shelter them from hostile neighbors. Quilboar tribes believe their god's spirit watches over them, granting martial vigor to their warriors and earth magic to their geomancers. In turn, they aggressively defend the thorns against encroaching tauren, orcs, and centaur, convinced that their sacred duty is to guard the fallen body of the boar god who birthed them.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Agamaggan?",a="Agamaggan is an Ancient Guardian, a colossal boar wild god who existed in the earliest eras of Azeroth. He fought alongside Cenarius and the night elves against the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients, sacrificing himself to save the world.",al={"agamaggan lore","who is the great boar","agamaggan background"},it="identity"},{q="How did the Razorfen form?",a="When Agamaggan died in battle against the Burning Legion, his colossal body fell in what is now the southern Barrens. Wherever his divine blood touched the ground, massive thorny briars surged upward, creating the dense thickets known as Razorfen Kraul and Razorfen Downs.",al={"how did razorfen get there","why are there huge thorns in the barrens","razorfen origin"},it="origin"},{q="What is the connection between Agamaggan and the quilboar?",a="The quilboar are the direct descendants of Agamaggan. They worship him fervently as their creator and god, building their settlements within his thorny remains and dedicating their shamanic rituals to his memory.",al={"are quilboar related to agamaggan","quilboar god","who made the quilboar"},it="ties"},{q="What are Blood Shards?",a="Blood Shards are red crystalline stones found in the Barrens, said to be formed from the dried, concentrated blood of Agamaggan. They hold residual primal magic, which quilboar geomancers and curious spellcasters use to empower their physical strength and rituals.",al={"what do blood shards do","blood shards lore","blood of agamaggan"},it="item"},{q="Did night elves worship Agamaggan?",a="Yes. Before the night elves arose, the dark trolls of the Shadowtooth clan revered Agamaggan as a loa. Night elves continue to revere him as one of the noble Ancients who gave his life defending Kalimdor from the Legion.",al={"night elves agamaggan","did dark trolls worship agamaggan"}},{q="Is Agamaggan's skull visible in the world?",a="Quilboar tradition holds that the great gaping entrance leading into Razorfen Downs is the hollowed skull and jaw of Agamaggan himself. Quilboar believe the maw will consume and destroy any trespassers who are not loyal to their god.",al={"razorfen downs skull","where is agamaggan head","agamaggan entrance"},it="place"},{q="What is the history of Agamaggan?",a="Agamaggan was one of the primeval Ancient Guardians, revered as the Great Boar. Ten thousand years ago during the War of the Ancients, Cenarius summoned him to join the night elves against the Burning Legion. He crushed thousands of doomguards beneath his hooves and hurled caustic breath and rains of thorns into the demonic ranks. Agamaggan fought all the way to the west, ultimately engaging the pit lord Mannoroth. Overwhelmed by endless demonic legions, the Great Boar sacrificed his life so Malfurion Stormrage and his companions could press on toward the Well of Eternity.",al={"agamaggan lore","story of agamaggan","how did agamaggan die","agamaggan war of the ancients"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["class:Druid"]="Druids hold Agamaggan in high esteem as one of the Ancient Guardians who fought alongside Cenarius, honoring his sacrifice during the War of the Ancients.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman who venture into the Barrens can sense the potent, lingering earth and spirit energies embedded within Agamaggan's blood and the Razorfen thorns.",["race:NightElf"]="Night elves remember Agamaggan as the fierce demigod who threw himself against Mannoroth's demons to clear a path to the Well of Eternity.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde contends daily with Agamaggan's quilboar children, whose fanatical devotion to their fallen god drives relentless attacks across the Barrens."},kw={"agamaggan","ancient guardian","barrens","blood shards","cenarius","great boar","quilboar","razorfen","war of the ancients"},rel={"topic:quilboar","topic:razorfen","zone:razorfenkraul","subzone:razorfen-downs-classic","topic:war-of-the-ancients","topic:cenarius","quest:5052","quest:5044"},src={"Agamaggan"}}
E["topic:alliance"] = {t="topic",n="Alliance",z="general",s="The Alliance is a grand coalition of mortal races bound by shared ideals of honor, duty, and the defense of order across Azeroth. Born out of the ashes of the northern kingdom of Lordaeron, its current heart beats in Stormwind City, united alongside the dwarves of Ironforge, the exiled gnomes, and the night elves of Kalimdor.",h="The Alliance is a grand coalition of mortal races bound by shared ideals of honor, duty, and the defense of...",sec={{t="From Lordaeron to Stormwind",b="The modern Alliance descends from the original Alliance of Lordaeron, founded when Sir Anduin Lothar led Stormwind refugees north during the Second War to unite the Seven Kingdoms, dwarves, and high elves against the Old Horde. While the Scourge and the Third War decimated the north—leaving Lordaeron in ruins and driving the survivors into scattered resistance—the restored Kingdom of Stormwind rose to fill the void. Today, Stormwind stands as the preeminent seat of human leadership, carrying the banners of honor and faith into a fractured world.",sp=0},{t="A Coalition of Necessity and Kinship",b="The faction's backbone in the Eastern Kingdoms relies on the steadfast bond between humans and the dwarves of Ironforge under King Magni Bronzebeard. Alongside the dwarves reside the gnomes, driven from Gnomeregan by a devastating trogg uprising. Guided by High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque, the exiled gnomes lend their brilliant engineering to the Alliance war effort, believing the coalition's survival is paramount to ever reclaiming their irradiated home.",sp=0},{t="The Kalimdor Front",b="Across the Great Sea, the reclusive night elves abandoned millennia of isolation after the Battle of Mount Hyjal. Reeling from the loss of their immortality and engaged in bitter territorial clashes against the Horde over logging in Ashenvale, the kaldorei allied with their newfound mortal companions. Though cultural friction remains—and some traditionalists decry the presence of humans and dwarves in ancient forests—the Alliance provides vital military and diplomatic cooperation to safeguard Kalimdor against demonic remnants and Horde expansion.",sp=0},{t="Uneasy Shadows",b="Despite its outward strength, the Alliance is beset by internal strain. King Varian Wrynn vanished under mysterious circumstances en route to a diplomatic summit, leaving his young son Anduin on the throne under Highlord Bolvar Fordragon's regency. Relations with former human allies are fractured: the maritime kingdom of Kul Tiras severed ties in outrage over the death of Grand Admiral Proudmoore, while diplomatic overtures from Sylvanas Windrunner's undead Forsaken in Lordaeron were rejected outright with silent hostility.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What races make up the Alliance?",a="The core Alliance comprises four peoples: the humans of Stormwind, the mountain dwarves of Ironforge, the exiled gnomes of Gnomeregan, and the night elves of Kalimdor.",al={"alliance races","who is in the alliance","playable alliance races"},it="people"},{q="Who leads the Alliance right now?",a="There is no single absolute monarch over the whole Alliance. In human lands, Stormwind is ruled by the boy-king Anduin Wrynn and his regent Highlord Bolvar Fordragon following the disappearance of King Varian Wrynn. The other member nations are led by King Magni Bronzebeard of Ironforge, High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque of the gnomes, and the night elf leadership on Kalimdor.",al={"alliance leader","who is high king","who runs stormwind"},it="leader"},{q="How did the Alliance of Lordaeron become the modern Alliance?",a="The original Alliance was established by King Terenas Menethil II and Anduin Lothar to repel the Old Horde during the Second War. After Lordaeron was destroyed by the Scourge in the Third War, leadership shifted south to the reconstructed Kingdom of Stormwind, which forged new covenants with the dwarves, gnomes, and night elves.",al={"alliance of lordaeron vs alliance","what happened to lordaeron","alliance origin"},it="history"},{q="Why did the night elves join the Alliance?",a="Following the Third War and the Battle of Mount Hyjal, the night elves lost their immortality and could no longer remain secluded. With the Orcish Horde actively deforesting Warsong Gulch in Ashenvale, the kaldorei forged a military pact with the humans and dwarves to defend their ancestral lands.",al={"why are night elves alliance","night elf alliance lore","kaldorei alliance"},it="ties"},{q="Why is Kul Tiras not part of the Alliance right now?",a="After Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore invaded Durotar and was slain by the Horde, the grief-stricken realm of Kul Tiras demanded vengeance. When the rest of the war-weary Alliance refused to pursue an unprovoked war, Kul Tiras isolated itself in bitter resentment.",al={"where is kul tiras","is kul tiras alliance","daelin proudmoore kul tiras"}},{q="Why didn't the gnomes help during the Third War?",a="During the Third War, a subterranean trogg invasion struck Gnomeregan. Believing the Scourge threat to the surface was greater, the gnomes hid their crisis from their allies and fought alone. The city fell to radiation and invasion, forcing survivors into Ironforge.",al={"where were gnomes in warcraft 3","gnomes third war","gnomeregan fall"},it="history"},{q="What is the Alliance's stance toward the Forsaken?",a="Many in the Alliance regard the undead as abominations and tools of the Scourge that destroyed Lordaeron. When Sylvanas Windrunner sent emissaries south seeking diplomacy, they vanished, and the Alliance maintains open hostility toward the undead reclaiming the northern kingdom.",al={"alliance view of forsaken","why hate undead","forsaken alliance relations"},it="hostile"},{q="What is the Alliance?",a="The Alliance is a grand coalition of mortal races dedicated to nobility, honor, justice, and preserving order across Azeroth. Evolved from the remnants of the fallen Alliance of Lordaeron, it unites humans, Ironforge dwarves, gnomes, and night elves under a shared purpose. Rather than being bound purely by desperation, its members are tied together by common traditions, faith, and an abiding loathing of demonic corruption. Together, they pool their military might, technical skill, and ancient wisdom to protect their people and bring light to a war-torn world.",al={"what is the alliance","who are the alliance","grand alliance"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why does the Alliance fight the Horde?",a="The conflict runs deep, born from generations of bloodshed. The Alliance remembers the brutality of the First and Second Wars, when the original Horde destroyed the kingdom of Stormwind and devastated the Eastern Kingdoms. Even after uniting temporarily at Mount Hyjal, peace remains fragile and bitter. Ongoing resource skirmishes, such as the Warsong Clan's continued logging in the ancestral forests of Ashenvale, keep animosity boiling. To many across the Alliance, the Horde represents an untamed and dangerous threat that must constantly be guarded against.",al={"why fight horde","why hate horde","alliance horde conflict","why war with horde"},it="conflict",add=true},{q="What does it mean to serve the Alliance?",a="To champion the Alliance is to stand as a bulwark of duty, justice, and order against the encroaching darkness. Whether you hail from the halls of Ironforge, the towers of Stormwind, the refugee workshops of Tinker Town, or the sacred boughs of Teldrassil, you are sworn to protect allied lands from monsters, invaders, and the savage reach of enemies. Serving means putting the greater good and noble ideals above individual gain, upholding sacred traditions, and ensuring the survival of free peoples across Azeroth.",al={"serving the alliance","alliance duty","what does alliance expect"},it="for_you",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="As the primary architects of the faction's recovery, humans view the Alliance as the rightful defender of civilization and the Church of the Holy Light against wild and demonic forces.",["race:Dwarf"]="The dwarves of Ironforge are the most steadfast and loyal allies of humanity, lending their formidable iron, mountain infantry, and deep friendship to the Alliance cause.",["race:Gnome"]="Refugees in Ironforge, gnomes channel their scientific ingenuity into Alliance armadas, trusting that mutual loyalty will eventually yield the forces required to liberate Gnomeregan.",["race:NightElf"]="Driven out of isolation by the Third War, night elves navigate a strange, necessary kinship with the younger races to check Horde territorial aggression in Ashenvale.",["faction:Alliance"]="Your sworn faction fights across two continents to maintain law, tradition, and mortal sovereignty against the Scourge, wild beasts, and the encroaching Horde.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde views the Alliance as an entrenched, expansionist power whose self-righteous ideals threaten the sovereignty and territorial survival of its members."},kw={"alliance","alliance of lordaeron","anduin wrynn","bolvar fordragon","darnassus","gnomeregan","grand alliance","ironforge","magni bronzebeard","mekkatorque","stormwind"},rel={"topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","topic:horde","zone:stormwind","zone:ironforge","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:varian-wrynn","topic:second-war"},src={"Alliance"}}
E["topic:alliance-of-lordaeron"] = {t="topic",n="Alliance of Lordaeron",z="general",s="The Alliance of Lordaeron was the historic grand coalition of the seven human kingdoms, Khaz Modan dwarves, Wildhammer gryphon riders, gnomes, and high elves formed to resist the Old Horde in the Second War. Led militarily by Supreme Commander Anduin Lothar and diplomatically by King Terenas Menethil II, it shattered the invading orcish forces and secured the Eastern Kingdoms. Though political fractures and the devastation of the Scourge tore it apart during the Third War, its legacy laid the foundation for the current Alliance.",h="The Alliance of Lordaeron was the historic grand coalition of the seven human kingdoms, Khaz Modan dwarves...",sec={{t="A Coalition Born in Ashes",b="Following the horrific sack of Stormwind in the First War, Regent Lord Anduin Lothar brought the battered southern refugees across the Great Sea to Capital City. Pleading their cause before King Terenas Menethil II, the leaders of the north assembled as the Council of Seven Nations. Old rivalries, border tensions, and isolationism—especially from Gilneas and Alterac—threatened to doom the summit before it began. It took urgent word of the Horde conquering Khaz Modan and an impassioned plea from the priest Turalyon to shake the rulers from their complacency. Choosing Lothar as their neutral Supreme Commander, the seven human nations bound themselves together in a desperate pact.",sp=0},{t="The Second War and the New Vanguard",b="Facing brutal orcish warriors, dark warlocks, and monstrous red dragons, the Alliance forged entirely new doctrines of war. Archbishop Alonsus Faol and Lothar established the Knights of the Silver Hand, elevating holy paladins like Uther the Lightbringer and Tirion Fordring to counter the Horde's dark magic. When the war raged across the Hinterlands and quelled the siege of Aerie Peak, Kurdran Wildhammer pledged his fierce gryphon riders to rule the skies. Shortly thereafter, the Bronzebeard dwarves and Gnomeregan gnomes were liberated, joining high elven rangers under Alleria Windrunner. Despite the treacherous betrayal of King Perenolde of Alterac, the grand coalition drove the Horde back to Blackrock Spire and crushed their dominion.",sp=0},{t="Fracture and Ruin",b="Victory brought bitterness rather than enduring peace. Bitter disputes erupted over the high taxes required to maintain the orc internment camps and rebuild the blasted kingdom of Stormwind. Seeing little personal gain, Gilneas built the Greymane Wall and severed ties, Stromgarde departed over territorial and prisoner quarrels, and Quel'Thalas pulled back behind its enchanted runestones. The coalition that remained under King Terenas attempted to weather the political storm, only to be utterly dismantled during the Third War when the Cult of the Damned and the Plague of Undeath swept across Lordaeron. Arthas Menethil's betrayal tore the heart out of the kingdom, ending the golden age of the northern realm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What was the Alliance of Lordaeron?",a="It was the military and political coalition formed between the Seven Kingdoms of humanity, the dwarves of Ironforge and Aerie Peak, the gnomes of Gnomeregan, and the high elves of Quel'Thalas to fight Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde during the Second War.",al={"what is lordaeron alliance","who was in the first alliance","grand alliance history"},it="identity"},{q="Why was the Alliance formed in Capital City instead of Stormwind?",a="Stormwind had been completely conquered and destroyed during the First War. Regent Lord Anduin Lothar led the surviving population north by ship to Lordaeron to warn King Terenas Menethil II of the alien threat.",al={"why lordaeron","stormwind in second war","why did lothar go north"},it="origin"},{q="Who led the Alliance of Lordaeron?",a="King Terenas Menethil II hosted the coalition and guided its political diplomacy, while Sir Anduin Lothar was named Supreme Commander of all allied armed forces. Daelin Proudmoore served as Grand Admiral of the fleets.",al={"leader of lordaeron alliance","supreme commander","who was in charge"},it="leader"},{q="Why did Alterac betray the Alliance?",a="Fearing that the Orcish Horde was unstoppable, King Aiden Perenolde secretly struck a deal with Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer. Alterac offered the Horde safe passage through its mountain passes in exchange for being spared from slaughter.",al={"perenolde betrayal","what did alterac do","alterac second war"},it="conflict"},{q="Why did the Alliance of Lordaeron fall apart?",a="After the Second War, disagreements over the cost of internment camps and rebuilding Stormwind caused Gilneas, Stromgarde, and Quel'Thalas to secede. The remnants were later destroyed during the Third War when Prince Arthas and the Scourge annihilated Lordaeron.",al={"why did alliance collapse","what happened to the old alliance","why did gilneas leave"},it="ruin"},{q="How is this different from the Alliance of today?",a="The Alliance of Lordaeron was centered entirely in the northern kingdoms around Capital City and Dalaran. Today's Alliance is centered in the rebuilt southern kingdom of Stormwind and includes the night elves of Kalimdor, though it draws inspiration from Lothar's original coalition.",al={"old alliance vs new alliance","difference between alliances","is this the current alliance"},it="today"},{q="Who was part of the Alliance of Lordaeron?",a="The coalition united all seven human kingdoms: Lordaeron, Stormwind, Stromgarde, Kul Tiras, Dalaran, Gilneas, and Alterac. As the Second War escalated, they were joined by the Bronzebeard dwarves of Ironforge, the Wildhammer dwarves of Aerie Peak, the gnomes of Gnomeregan, and the high elves of Quel'Thalas, alongside the paladins of the Silver Hand and the Church of the Holy Light.",al={"alliance of lordaeron members","who joined alliance of lordaeron","alliance of lordaeron nations"},it="allies",add=true},{q="How was the Alliance of Lordaeron formed?",a="After the Orcish Horde destroyed Stormwind in the First War, Regent Lord Anduin Lothar fled north with the survivors to seek aid from King Terenas Menethil II. Terenas convened the Council of Seven Nations in Capital City to confront the threat. Though monarchs like Genn Greymane and Aiden Perenolde initially balked at giving up regional authority, an impassioned plea by Turalyon convinced the council to put aside petty rivalries and unite under Lothar's supreme command.",al={"alliance of lordaeron history","how did alliance of lordaeron start","creation of alliance of lordaeron"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why was it called the Alliance of Lordaeron?",a="It earned the name because the northern kingdom of Lordaeron served as the heart and rallying ground of the union. King Terenas Menethil II of Lordaeron called the Seven Kingdoms together in Capital City to answer the fall of Stormwind, and Lordaeron stood as the political anchor and primary military supplier through the Second War.",al={"why alliance of lordaeron","alliance of lordaeron name meaning","why called alliance of lordaeron"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The memory of the Alliance of Lordaeron provides the philosophical foundation and heroic ideals for the modern Alliance centered in Stormwind.",["class:Paladin"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand were created specifically by Archbishop Alonsus Faol and Anduin Lothar to serve as the divine shock troops of the Alliance of Lordaeron.",["race:Human"]="The alliance brought all Seven Kingdoms together under one banner for the only time since the ancient Empire of Arathor, before regional pettiness and the Scourge scattered them.",["race:Dwarf"]="Both the Ironforge Bronzebeards and the Wildhammers of Aerie Peak forged their modern ties to human civilization during the battles of the Second War."},kw={"alliance of lordaeron","anduin lothar","capital city","grand alliance","second war","seven kingdoms","silver hand","terenas menethil"},rel={"topic:anduin-lothar","topic:terenas-menethil-ii","topic:second-war","topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:lordaeron","topic:scourge","topic:alterac-kingdom"},src={"Alliance of Lordaeron"}}
E["topic:alterac-kingdom"] = {t="topic",n="Alterac (kingdom)",z="general",s="Alterac was one of the seven historic human kingdoms, nestled high in the rugged Alterac Mountains. During the Second War, its ruler, King Aiden Perenolde, made a treacherous pact with the Old Horde to spare his realm, leading to the kingdom's military downfall and total collapse. Today, its ruined capital lies abandoned in the snow, while dispossessed nobles and outlaws wage a guerrilla war under the banner of the Syndicate.",h="Alterac was one of the seven historic human kingdoms, nestled high in the rugged Alterac Mountains.",sec={{t="A Nation in the Crags",b="Alterac arose thousands of years ago as an Arathorian city-state following the Troll Wars. Settlers capitalized on strategic mountain passes and trading outposts, eventually establishing an independent realm when the empire fractured. Though rugged and mountainous, Alterac bordered the prosperous lands of Lordaeron and Stromgarde, extending as far as the shores of Darrowmere Lake. Historically, it fielded tough mountain fighters, but enduring border friction with Thoras Trollbane's Stromgarde left the kingdom politically isolated from its neighbors long before the Dark Portal ever opened.",sp=0},{t="The Great Betrayal",b="When Sir Anduin Lothar forged the Alliance of Lordaeron, King Aiden Perenolde joined only with deep reluctance. As Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde swept north, Perenolde concluded humanity was doomed and secretly struck a deal with the Warchief. In exchange for the safety of Alterac, Perenolde opened fortified mountain passes, conspired against Alliance forces, and even dispatched soldiers and sailors to assist the Horde's siege of Lordaeron. The treachery allowed the orcs to reach Capital City far ahead of schedule, shaking the Alliance to its core when the truth was laid bare.",sp=0},{t="Ruin and the Syndicate",b="Retribution was swift. Stromgarde's forces marched into the mountains, exposing Perenolde's treason and breaking Alterac's military. King Terenas Menethil II placed the fallen nation under martial law, while arguments over who should inherit the throne paralyzed Alliance diplomats for years. Following the kingdom's collapse, King Perenolde died in disgrace. His son, Lord Aliden Perenolde, rallied the bitter, displaced nobility to form the Syndicate. Wearing the orange cloth of their fallen homeland, these bandits and mercenaries now fight ruthlessly to reclaim their ancestral holdings across Hillsbrad and Alterac.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What happened to the Kingdom of Alterac?",a="Alterac collapsed during the Second War after its king, Aiden Perenolde, betrayed humanity to side with the Old Horde. Alliance armies, spearheaded by Stromgarde, marched in and dismantled the kingdom's military. The crown was dissolved, the realm was placed under martial law, and its people fragmented into ruin.",al={"alterac downfall","why is alterac destroyed","fate of alterac","who destroyed alterac"},it="ruin"},{q="Why did King Perenolde betray the Alliance?",a="Aiden Perenolde believed the Alliance could not defeat the invading Horde. Fearing the total destruction of his mountain realm, he secretly allied with Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer. Doomhammer promised that Alterac would be left untouched and granted sovereign protection once Lordaeron fell, in exchange for open passage through the mountains.",al={"why did perenolde betray","perenolde motive","alterac betrayal reason"},it="motive"},{q="How does the Syndicate relate to Alterac?",a="The Syndicate is the criminal continuation of Alterac's nobility, founded by King Perenolde's son, Lord Aliden Perenolde. The organization seeks to take back their family estates and land across Hillsbrad and Alterac by force. Their signature orange masks match the historic national color of Alterac.",al={"syndicate connection alterac","who leads the syndicate","why orange masks"},it="ties"},{q="Did all Alteraci agree with the betrayal?",a="No. Many Alteraci soldiers initially marched and died fighting loyally under Anduin Lothar in Hillsbrad. When King Thoras Trollbane of Stromgarde confronted him, General Hath reluctantly confirmed Perenolde's treason and sent his troops to help halt the orcish advance through the mountain passes.",al={"were alterac people loyal","did all alterac betray alliance","general hath"},it="conflict"},{q="What are Alterac's traditional colors and symbols?",a="Alterac's national color is orange. This crest and color scheme were worn by its royal military during the Second War and are preserved today by the Syndicate, whose members wear orange masks and hoods.",al={"alterac flag","alterac color"},it="identity"},{q="Who controls Alterac City right now?",a="The ruined capital of Alterac City has no recognized monarch or lawful government. It is largely a shattered ruin overrun by Syndicate outlaws, ogres, and wild mountain beasts, with no formal Alliance garrison capable of holding the high passes.",al={"who rules alterac now","alterac city ruins","who lives in alterac city"},it="in_charge"},{q="What was the Kingdom of Alterac?",a="Alterac was one of the seven historic human nations, settled in the high, rugged peaks of the Alterac Mountains. Originating as a fortified city-state of the ancient empire of Arathor, it grew into an independent kingdom commanding strategic mountain passes and trading routes. Though smaller and militarily weaker than neighbors like Lordaeron and Stromgarde, Alterac held vital territory between the northern kingdoms until its catastrophic treason during the Second War shattered its standing forever.",al={"what is alterac","alterac kingdom overview","who are alterac"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where did the Kingdom of Alterac come from?",a="Alterac began over two thousand seven hundred years ago following the Troll Wars, when the expanding human empire of Arathor founded new outposts beyond Strom. Early settlers built a fortress in the mountains once inhabited by the primitive Alteraci human tribe, guarding valuable trade routes. When Arathor fractured centuries later, Alterac became an autonomous kingdom, often clashing with rival Stromgarde over their ambiguous borders before joining the Alliance of Lordaeron against the Orcish Horde.",al={"alterac history","origin of alterac","how was alterac founded"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who leads the people of Alterac now?",a="The kingdom itself has no recognized sovereign or unified government following the imprisonment and death of King Aiden Perenolde. Its remnants are fractured: many dispossessed nobles and citizens rally under Aiden's son, Lord Aliden Perenolde, who commands the Syndicate in a lawless campaign to reclaim their ancestral lands. Meanwhile, their devastated capital remains contested by wild beasts and rival factions.",al={"who leads alterac","king of alterac","alterac leader"},it="leader",add=true},{q="Who is Alterac allied with?",a="As a recognized nation, Alterac has no allies left. It broke its original pact with the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War to aid Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde, earning the lasting enmity of the other human realms. Today, its noble remnants within the Syndicate operate as outlaws, working with hired cutthroats and rogues while fighting both the Alliance and the new Horde for survival.",al={"alterac allies","who does alterac fight for","who supports alterac"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Why is the kingdom named Alterac?",a="The kingdom took its name from the rugged Alterac Mountains where it was established, as well as the early Alteraci human tribe that inhabited the peaks prior to the expansion of the Arathorian Empire.",al={"why is it called alterac","alterac name origin","meaning of alterac"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="To the Alliance, Alterac is a bitter reminder of treason that nearly cost humanity everything during the Second War. Its memory fuels ongoing hostility toward the Syndicate bandits attempting to reclaim their ancestral estates.",["race:Human"]="For humans of the northern kingdoms, Alterac's fall is a tragic lesson in cowardice and division, showing how easily fear can shatter the unity forged against foreign invaders.",["class:Rogue"]="The mountain valleys of former Alterac are a cutthroat haven for covert operatives, split between the highborn thugs of the Syndicate and the secretive assassins of Ravenholdt Manor."},kw={"aiden","aliden","alterac","betrayal","hath","horde","orgrim doomhammer","perenolde","second war","seven kingdoms","syndicate","treason"},rel={"topic:syndicate","topic:second-war","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","zone:alterac","zone:hillsbrad","npc:orgrim-doomhammer","topic:terenas-menethil-ii","subzone:ravenholdt-manor"},src={"Alterac (kingdom)"}}
E["topic:anduin-lothar"] = {t="topic",n="Anduin Lothar",z="general",s="Sir Anduin Lothar, known as the Lion of Stormwind, was the last descendant of the ancient Arathi bloodline and the Supreme Commander of the Alliance of Lordaeron. A childhood companion of King Llane Wrynn and the Guardian Medivh, Lothar led humanity through the horrors of the First War and rallied the northern nations during the Second War before falling at Blackrock Mountain.",h="Sir Anduin Lothar, known as the Lion of Stormwind, was the last descendant of the ancient Arathi bloodline...",sec={{t="The Lion's Youth",b="Carrying the ancient bloodline of the Arathi kings, Lothar grew up within Stormwind's royal court alongside Prince Llane Wrynn and Medivh. The trio embarked on reckless excursions into the wilderness, skirmishing with Gurubashi trolls in Stranglethorn Vale. Lothar rose rapidly through Stormwind's martial ranks, joining the King's Honor Guard and earning renown as an Armsman of the Brotherhood of the Horse. When troll raids ravaged Westfall, Lothar, Llane, and Medivh defied King Barathen's cautious strategy to slay the troll warlord Jok'non, a clash that triggered an all-out Gurubashi invasion but cemented Lothar's place as one of the kingdom's finest tacticians.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of Stormwind",b="When mysterious green-skinned invaders poured from the Black Morass, King Llane named Lothar the King's Champion to mount the kingdom's defense. Lothar pioneered devastating ambushes against orcish raiding parties across Westfall, the Redridge Mountains, and Elwynn Forest, outmaneuvering Warchief Blackhand and breaking the first siege of Stormwind City. Yet betrayal doomed the kingdom from within: Medivh was revealed as the architect who opened the Dark Portal, compelling Lothar and the apprentice Khadgar to storm Karazhan and execute the corrupted Guardian. Returning to find King Llane assassinated and Stormwind burning, Lothar gathered the survivors—including young Prince Varian—and evacuated them north across the Great Sea.",sp=1},{t="Supreme Commander and Blackrock Mountain",b="Arriving on the shores of Lordaeron, Lothar presented the grim reality of the Horde to King Terenas Menethil II. Invoking his ancestral Arathi lineage and diplomatic gravitas, he unified the human realms, Ironforge dwarves, and high elves into the Grand Alliance of Lordaeron. Named Supreme Commander of the united forces, Lothar spearheaded the campaign that pushed the Horde back south to the foot of Blackrock Mountain. There, in a fateful clash near the end of the Second War, Lothar was slain by Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer. His death did not shatter the Alliance; instead, it galvanized his lieutenant, Turalyon, to lead the charge that broke the Horde forever.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was Anduin Lothar?",a="Sir Anduin Lothar was a knight of Stormwind and the last descendant of the Arathi bloodline. He served as King's Champion during the First War and later became the Supreme Commander of the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War. He is remembered as one of the greatest heroes and tacticians in human history.",al={"who is lothar","who was lothar","lion of stormwind lore"},it="identity"},{q="How did Lothar die?",a="Lothar was slain during the climax of the Second War at the base of Blackrock Mountain. He fought Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer in single combat and fell just before Alliance forces under Turalyon routed the Horde and pursued them to the Dark Portal.",al={"lothar death","how lothar died","who killed lothar"},it="history"},{q="Why is the current prince named Anduin?",a="King Varian Wrynn named his young son, Prince Anduin Wrynn, in honor of Sir Anduin Lothar. Lothar rescued Varian as a child from the ruins of Stormwind, protected him in exile, and reclaimed their homeland through his sacrifice in the Second War.",al={"why is anduin named anduin","anduin wrynn name origin"}},{q="What was Lothar's connection to Medivh and King Llane?",a="Lothar, Prince Llane Wrynn, and the young mage Medivh were inseparable childhood friends in the royal court of Stormwind. They explored Azeroth together in their youth, fighting trolls and beasts. Tragically, Lothar was later forced to lead the strike against Medivh at Karazhan after discovering his friend had betrayed the realm and summoned the Horde.",al={"lothar medivh llane","lothar childhood friends"},it="allies"},{q="Why is Lothar called the 'Last of the Arathi'?",a="Lothar was the final direct scion of the ancient Arathi line, the dynasty that originally united humanity and founded the empire of Arathor millennia ago. This prestigious heritage gave him immense diplomatic standing when uniting the squabbling northern kings under the Alliance banner in Lordaeron.",al={"lothar arathi bloodline","arathi descendant"},it="name"},{q="What was Lothar's role in the Brotherhood of the Horse?",a="Lothar was knighted early in his career and rose to the position of Armsman within the Brotherhood of the Horse, Stormwind's elite order of mounted knights. He utilized their disciplined cavalry charges to repeatedly outflank and disrupt Horde formations throughout the First War.",al={"brotherhood of the horse lothar","knights of stormwind lothar"}}},ang={["race:Human"]="To humanity, Lothar is the ultimate paragon of sacrifice and honor. His Arathi blood brought the disparate human kingdoms together, and his tragic death at Blackrock Mountain sealed the kingdom's liberation.",["faction:Alliance"]="Lothar was the foundational architect and first Supreme Commander of the Alliance, uniting elves, dwarves, and humans to push the Horde out of the Eastern Kingdoms.",["class:Warrior"]="Regarded as one of the finest armsmen and battlefield commanders who ever lived, Lothar's mastery of mounted tactics and sheer martial resolve serve as an inspiration to warriors across Azeroth.",["class:Paladin"]="Lothar embraced the nascent Knights of the Silver Hand during the Second War, placing his trust in paladins like Turalyon, who took up Lothar's shattered sword after his fall to lead the Alliance to victory."},kw={"anduin lothar","arathi","blackrock mountain","brotherhood of the horse","lion of stormwind","llane wrynn","lothar","supreme commander"},rel={"topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","topic:first-war","topic:second-war","topic:medivh","topic:varian-wrynn","subzone:blackrock-mountain","quest:2997","topic:stormwind-kingdom"},src={"Anduin Lothar"}}
E["topic:argent-dawn"] = {t="topic",n="Argent Dawn",z="general",s="The Argent Dawn is a neutral, knightly order dedicated to stamping out malevolent threats across Azeroth, most notably the Scourge and the Burning Legion. Formed by survivors of the Knights of the Silver Hand who rejected the fanatical zealotry of the Scarlet Crusade, they welcome champions of any race or faction who honor the cause of the Light. From their fortified sanctuary at Light's Hope Chapel to far-flung outposts across Kalimdor, they stand on the front lines against darkness.",h="The Argent Dawn is a neutral, knightly order dedicated to stamping out malevolent threats across Azeroth...",sec={{t="A Schism of Faith",b="When Highlord Alexandros Mograine fell and Lordaeron collapsed into ruin, the shattered remnants of the Knights of the Silver Hand broke into bitter disagreement. Many survivors surrendered to grief and paranoia, morphing into the xenophobic Scarlet Crusade. Yet veterans like Lord Maxwell Tyrosus and Pureheart recognized that their former comrades had abandoned the true tenets of the Holy Light. Refusing to succumb to madness or abandon the dead kingdom, they split away to form the Argent Dawn—an order committed to defending Azeroth alongside anyone willing to fight, whether Alliance or Horde.",sp=0},{t="Front Lines of the Plaguelands",b="The Dawn's primary stronghold is Light's Hope Chapel, situated in the blighted expanse of the Eastern Plaguelands. From this consecrated bastion, the order coordinates an unending war against Kel'Thuzad's necromancers and skeletal legions. Operating forward camps at Chillwind Camp and outside the Bulwark in Tirisfal Glades, Argent officers issue enchanted commissions to adventurers, paying bounties in exchange for Scourgestones wrested from fallen undead. Despite lacking the raw manpower of the Scarlet Crusade, the Dawn holds the line through perseverance, unity, and unshakable discipline.",sp=0},{t="Vigilance Across Azeroth",b="While Lordaeron remains their principal battleground, the Argent Dawn maintains an active watch across Kalimdor and the capitals of the world. They keep diplomatic presences in Stormwind City, Darnassus, and the goblin trade town of Everlook. Farther afield, operatives like Delgren the Purifier contest demonic cults in Darkshore and Ashenvale, while scouts in Desolace and the flooded ruins of Blackfathom Deeps investigate sinister threats involving the Shadow Council and the Twilight's Hammer cult.",sp=0},{t="Uneasy Truces and Hidden Foes",b="Facing annihilation by the Scourge, a pragmatic sect within the order known as the Brotherhood of the Light strives to maintain a cold truce with the Scarlet Crusade. Yet relations remain deeply strained. High-ranking officers like Duke Nicholas Zverenhoff actively sanction covert strikes against Scarlet leadership in Stratholme to purge what they view as heretical madness. Compounding their perils, dark forces relentlessly seek to infiltrate the Dawn's ranks—including secret agents who siphon necromantic secrets from the front lines for treacherous masters elsewhere.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the difference between the Argent Dawn and the Scarlet Crusade?",a="Both orders were born from the remnants of the Silver Hand after Lordaeron's fall. The Scarlet Crusade became fanatical and xenophobic, viewing all outsiders and non-humans as potential carriers of the plague. The Argent Dawn refused to abandon the true tenets of the Holy Light, opening their ranks to both Alliance and Horde races to fight evil together.",al={"argent dawn vs scarlet crusade","why did argent dawn split","difference between dawn and crusade"},it="conflict"},{q="What do I do with Scourgestones?",a="Argent Dawn officers accept Scourgestones as evidence of service against the undead. These grisly tokens represent victories over the Scourge and help the order recognize its allies.",al={"what are scourgestones for","argent dawn scourgestones","scourgestone turn in"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Can Horde players join or work with the Argent Dawn?",a="Yes. The Argent Dawn is strictly neutral and operates outside political allegiances. They actively recruit orcs, trolls, tauren, and even Forsaken who are committed to combating the Scourge and the Burning Legion, maintaining recruitment stations like the Bulwark on the Tirisfal border.",al={"can horde join argent dawn","is argent dawn alliance only","argent dawn horde friendly"},it="side"},{q="What are they doing in Kalimdor?",a="The Dawn views its mandate as the protection of all Azeroth, not just Lordaeron. Agents have traveled to Darkshore, Ashenvale, and Desolace to eliminate cults of the Shadow Council, confront Twilight's Hammer conspirators, and guard against demonic incursions.",al={"argent dawn in kalimdor","argent dawn ashenvale","why is argent dawn in darkshore"},it="why_here"},{q="What is the Brotherhood of the Light?",a="The Brotherhood of the Light is a militant sub-faction of the Argent Dawn led by Commander Eligor Dawnbringer. They work to maintain a functional, if tense, working relationship with the Scarlet Crusade so both factions can direct their fury toward Kel'Thuzad rather than each other.",al={"brotherhood of the light","brotherhood of light argent dawn","eligor dawnbringer faction"},it="ties"},{q="What is the Argent Dawn?",a="The Argent Dawn is a neutral, knightly order dedicated to defending Azeroth from destructive evils, chief among them the Scourge and the Burning Legion. Unlike political factions or insular sects, the Dawn welcomes members and champions from any race without distinction, united solely by their devotion to the Light and their refusal to let the world fall to darkness.",al={"who are argent dawn","argent dawn order","what does argent dawn do"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Who leads the Argent Dawn?",a="Lord Maxwell Tyrosus leads the Argent Dawn, directing their crusade against the undead from their primary sanctuary at Light's Hope Chapel in the Eastern Plaguelands. The order was originally founded under Lord Raymond George, but after George fell in battle against the Scourge, Tyrosus assumed command to guide their holy resistance.",al={"argent dawn leader","who is in charge of argent dawn","lord tyrosus argent dawn"},it="leader",add=true},{q="Who are the Argent Dawn allied with?",a="The Argent Dawn maintains strict political neutrality, counting both the Alliance and the Horde as allies in the greater struggle against evil. Their officers recruit heroes from Stormwind to Orgrimmar alike. While they share common undead foes with the Scarlet Crusade and occasionally hold an uneasy truce through the Brotherhood of the Light, they view the Crusade's zealous fanaticism as dangerous heresy.",al={"argent dawn allies","does argent dawn work with horde","who works with argent dawn"},it="allies",add=true},{q="How was the Argent Dawn founded?",a="Following the fall of Lordaeron and the murder of Highlord Alexandros Mograine, the remnants of the Silver Hand fractured. While many knights succumbed to zealous paranoia and formed the Scarlet Crusade, those disgusted by this rising extremism broke away. Determined to uphold the true tenets of the Light without prejudice, they formed the Argent Dawn under Lord Raymond George to fight the Scourge with honor.",al={"argent dawn history","origin of argent dawn","how did argent dawn start"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="For holy warriors, the Argent Dawn represents the uncorrupted legacy of the Silver Hand, holding fast to compassion, sacrifice, and duty when the Scarlet Crusade succumbed to madness.",["faction:Horde"]="Though established by former knights of Lordaeron, the Dawn welcomes Horde champions without prejudice, making them one of the few avenues for Horde heroes to campaign directly in the Plaguelands.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken who despise the Scourge find a rare, pragmatic refuge in the Dawn's camps, where your actions against Kel'Thuzad matter far more than your rotting flesh.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Dawn maintains official embassies in Stormwind and Darnassus, offering Alliance soldiers a righteous path to reclaim Lordaeron from the grasp of the Lich King."},kw={"argent dawn","bulwark","chillwind camp","commission","light's hope chapel","maxwell tyrosus","plaguelands","scarlet crusade","scourge","scourgestone","silver hand"},rel={"subzone:light-s-hope-chapel","npc:lord-maxwell-tyrosus","topic:scarlet-crusade","topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","topic:scourge","zone:easternplaguelands","subzone:chillwind-camp","subzone:bulwark"},src={"Argent Dawn"}}
E["topic:black-dragonflight"] = {t="topic",n="Black dragonflight",z="general",s="Once the stalwart Earth-Warders charged by the titans to safeguard Azeroth's deep subterranean realms, the black dragonflight fell to madness and treachery. Corrupted by the insidious whispers of the imprisoned Old Gods, their leader Neltharion became Deathwing, turning his brood into one of the most destructive and hated forces on the planet. Today, their surviving scions weave plots from the shadows and wage covert wars against mortal kingdoms.",h="Once the stalwart Earth-Warders charged by the titans to safeguard Azeroth's deep subterranean realms, the...",sec={{t="The Fallen Protectors",b="Long ago, the Titan Khaz'goroth bestowed his blessing upon Neltharion, creating the black dragonflight to watch over the earth, raise mountains, and guard Azeroth's deep caverns. For ages, they stood alongside the other flights as patient, unyielding guardians. Yet their intimate bond with the world's subterranean foundations proved their undoing. Dwelling within the deepest hollows, they lay dangerously close to the sunken prisons of the Old Gods. Over centuries, dark whispers eroded the noble Earth-Warder's mind, driving him to madness and twisting his entire brood into vicious instruments of ruin.",sp=0},{t="Betrayal and Fire",b="During the ancient War of the Ancients, Neltharion shattered the unity of dragonkind, turning upon his kin as Deathwing the Destroyer. Ever since, the black flight has waged relentless, spiteful war against the red, blue, green, and bronze flights, who view their cruelty as an absolute profanation of life. Rather than tending the soil, black dragons now wield molten magma, black magic, and searing brimstone to scorch the lands they once nurtured, finding delight in sowing discord, misery, and endless strife across mortal realms.",sp=0},{t="Human Guises and Mortal Intrigues",b="Unlike many beasts that rely purely on claw and flame, the black dragons are masters of manipulation. They frequently adopt humanoid forms—most often cultured, dark-haired humans with piercing eyes—to infiltrate royal courts and council chambers. By steering leaders toward arrogance, sowing division between noble houses, and whispering poisonous council into vulnerable ears, the flight orchestrates the downfall of mortal civilizations from within, preferring that kingdoms tear themselves apart before their drakes arrive to burn the remnants.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why are black dragons evil?",a="Their ancient duty tied them closely to the subterranean depths of Azeroth, directly where the ancient Old Gods were sealed away. Over millennia, the maddening whispers of these entities seeped into Neltharion's mind, twisting his protective instincts into paranoia and malice until his entire brood followed him into corruption.",al={"why did black dragons turn bad","who corrupted deathwing","black dragon madness"},it="hostile"},{q="What powers do black dragons wield?",a="Granted dominance over the earth by the Titan Khaz'goroth, they command stone, magma, and deep subterranean forces. In their corrupted state, they wield volatile earth elemental power known as black magic, exhaling searing fire and molten rock while their heavy scales continually radiate intense heat.",al={"black dragon magic","black dragon flight abilities","what can black dragons do"},it="power"},{q="Do black dragons use disguises?",a="Yes. Black dragons excel at infiltration and political manipulation. Their favored humanoid guise is typically an attractive, noble human with dark hair and dark eyes, allowing them to mingle freely in courts, manipulate monarchs, and spark civil conflicts without revealing their true draconic nature.",al={"do dragons take human form","black dragon mortal guises","black dragons disguised as humans"}},{q="How can you tell a black dragon's name?",a="Members of the black flight commonly follow specific naming traditions: male names frequently end in the suffix '-ion' (such as Neltharion), while female names typically end in '-ia' (such as Onyxia). Many also adopt words evoking ash, shadow, stone, or ruin.",al={"black dragon naming conventions","black dragon names","how do black dragons get named"},it="name"},{q="Why do the other dragonflights hate them?",a="During the War of the Ancients, the Earth-Warder betrayed the other flights, slaughtering thousands of his brethren. The red dragonflight in particular despises them, viewing their fiery malice and corrupting influence as an unnatural blasphemy against life itself.",al={"red dragonflight vs black dragonflight","why do dragons fight each other","dragonflight civil war"},it="conflict"},{q="Who leads the black dragonflight now?",a="Their ancient Aspect, Deathwing, has vanished from public view following past defeats, leaving his dangerous brood scattered into splintered cabals. Today, prominent offspring—such as Nefarian and Onyxia—pursue their own ruthless ambitions to enslave rivals, subvert kingdoms, and breed dark creations.",al={"who is the black dragon leader","where is deathwing","who rules the black dragons"},it="leader"},{q="What is the black dragonflight?",a="The black dragonflight is a brood of dragons, drakonid, and dragonspawn originally blessed by the titan Khaz'goroth to serve as the steadfast warders of the earth. Once revered as the noble defenders of Azeroth's deepest realms, mountains, and subterranean caverns, the entire flight fell into madness when their ancient leader succumbed to the dark whispers of the Old Gods.",al={"who are the black dragons","what are black dragons","what is black dragonflight"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where did the black dragonflight come from?",a="Countless millennia ago, the titans empowered the Great Aspect Neltharion the Earth-Warder and his black brood with dominion over stone and magma. For ages, they shaped mountains and valleys in harmony with the other flights. However, their intimate bond with the deep places of the earth left them exposed to the whispers of the imprisoned Old Gods. Driven utterly mad, Neltharion and his children betrayed their sacred charge, turning their elemental might toward ruin and terror.",al={"black dragonflight history","black dragon origin","history of black dragons"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance must remain constantly vigilant against political subversion, as the black dragonflight has repeatedly sought to corrupt human nobility and exploit aristocratic ambitions in human courts.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde holds an enduring grudge against the flight's machinations, having suffered under their manipulative meddling during previous wars and facing their subterranean broods throughout the crags of Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman view the black dragons with deep sorrow and revulsion, knowing they wield the sacred elemental power of earth not to maintain harmony, but to twist stone and magma into instruments of torment."},kw={"black dragonkin","black dragons","deathwing","dragonflight","earth-warder","khaz'goroth","nefarian","neltharion","onyxia"},rel={"npc:deathwing","topic:titan","topic:old-god","topic:war-of-the-ancients","topic:dragonmaw-clan"},src={"Black dragonflight"}}
E["topic:blackrock-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Blackrock clan",z="general",s="The Blackrock clan is a martial orcish clan renowned for its strict military discipline, unmatched blacksmithing, and central role in the First and Second Wars. Today, the clan is deeply fractured: while some veterans follow Thrall in the New Horde, a vicious and renegade splinter group known as the Dark Horde occupies the upper peaks of Blackrock Mountain under Rend Blackhand.",h="The Blackrock clan is a martial orcish clan renowned for its strict military discipline, unmatched...",sec={{t="The Anvil of the Old Horde",b="Originating in the rugged caverns of Gorgrond on Draenor, the Blackrock clan made its name through rigid hierarchy, heavy armor, and mastery over the forge. Under Chieftain Blackhand, they served as the brutal core of the Old Horde during the invasion of Azeroth, laying siege to Stormwind. When Orgrim Doomhammer slew Blackhand to cleanse the Horde of Gul'dan's direct puppet mastery, leadership of both the clan and the Horde remained firmly in Blackrock hands through the grueling campaigns of the Second War.",sp=0},{t="A Fractured Legacy",b="Following the Horde's catastrophic defeat at Blackrock Mountain, the clan shattered. Many warriors were rounded up and placed in Alliance internment camps, later to be liberated by Thrall and inducted into his reformed New Horde. However, unrepentant elements rallied behind Blackhand's sons, Rend and Maim. This faction retreated into the heights of Blackrock Spire, forming the hostile Dark Horde alongside remnants of the Dragonmaw and other rogue clans.",sp=0},{t="The Threat in the Spire",b="Today, Rend Blackhand claims the mantle of true Warchief from his throne in Blackrock Spire. The Dark Horde maintains an uneasy and treacherous truce with the Dark Iron dwarves who lurk in the molten depths below, all while waging war against both the Alliance and Thrall's Horde. Uncompromising, demon-tainted remnants of the clan have also been spotted lingering in Lordaeron, seeking to revive dark sacrificial rituals.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Are the Blackrock orcs part of Thrall's Horde?",a="Some are, but most encountered in the Eastern Kingdoms are not. While venerable Blackrock veterans like Eitrigg and Varok Saurfang serve Thrall in Kalimdor, the bulk of the clan entrenched at Blackrock Mountain follow Rend Blackhand and consider Thrall's New Horde to be weak traitors.",al={"is blackrock clan horde","are blackrocks friendly","thrall blackrock clan"},it="side"},{q="Who is currently leading the Blackrock clan?",a="In Blackrock Mountain, Dal'rend (Rend) Blackhand leads the Dark Horde splinter faction, claiming leadership over the clan his father once commanded. Those Blackrock orcs who integrated into the New Horde answer directly to Warchief Thrall.",al={"blackrock clan leader","who leads blackrock","rend blackhand leader"},it="leader"},{q="Why are they called the Blackrock clan?",a="They were named after the incredibly durable, black-hued ore found within their ancestral homeland in Gorgrond on Draenor. Their smiths learned how to smelt this impossibly hard mineral, forging superior armaments that made them legendary warriors.",al={"blackrock name origin","why called blackrock","blackrock ore"},it="name"},{q="What is the Dark Horde?",a="The Dark Horde is a rogue faction occupying Upper Blackrock Spire, comprised of unrepentant Blackrock orcs, Black Tooth Grin veterans, and allied Dragonmaw orcs. Led by Rend Blackhand, they reject Thrall's authority and seek to conquer Azeroth under the banner of the Old Horde.",al={"what is dark horde","rend's horde","blackrock dark horde"},it="identity"},{q="What happened to Blackhand?",a="During the First War, Orgrim Doomhammer challenged Warchief Blackhand to a mak'gora duel to strip the Shadow Council of its puppet ruler. Orgrim crushed Blackhand's skull with the Doomhammer, claiming both the title of Warchief and leadership of the Blackrock clan.",al={"how did blackhand die","who killed blackhand","orgrim kills blackhand"},it="history"},{q="Why are there Blackrock orcs in Lordaeron?",a="Scattered splinter forces of the clan fled north or remained hidden in Lordaeron after the Second War. Some of these warbands turned back toward demon worship and dark sacrifices, drawing the ire of the Alliance and later the Scourge during the Third War.",al={"blackrock orcs alterac","blackrocks in lordaeron","demon worshipping blackrocks"},it="why_here"},{q="What is the Blackrock clan?",a="The Blackrock clan is one of the most renowned and militarily disciplined orcish clans from Draenor. Famed for their master blacksmithing and iron will, they formed the central backbone of the Old Horde during the First and Second Wars under Warchiefs Blackhand and Orgrim Doomhammer. While some members eventually followed Thrall into his new Horde, the majority remain rogue traditionalists entrenched in Blackrock Mountain.",al={"who are blackrock orcs","blackrock clan lore","what is blackrock clan"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are the Blackrock orcs hostile to travelers?",a="The free Blackrock orcs still cling to the bloody conquests of the Old Horde and reject Thrall's peaceful ideals. Driven by martial pride and deep hatred for both the Alliance and Thrall's Horde, they treat any trespassers into their lands—particularly around Blackrock Mountain and Redridge—as invaders to be slaughtered or enslaved.",al={"why do blackrock orcs attack","blackrock clan hostile","why are blackrocks evil"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who are the Blackrock clan allied with?",a="The Blackrocks who follow Rend Blackhand lead the Dark Horde from Blackrock Spire. To bolster their numbers against the Dark Iron dwarves and the Alliance, they have allied with remnants of the Dragonmaw clan, local forest trolls, ogres, and the black dragonflight.",al={"blackrock clan allies","who do blackrock orcs work with","dark horde allies"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["race:Orc"]="The Blackrocks produced two of the greatest Warchiefs in orcish history, yet their surviving remnants in the Eastern Kingdoms serve as a bitter reminder of the blood-fueled Old Horde.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="The Blackrock clan's legendary metalworking shaped modern orcish siegecraft and heavy plate armor, born from centuries of smelting unique ores in elemental-powered forges.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance forces have fought the Blackrock clan across three wars, from the burning of Stormwind to the bloody sieges of Blackrock Mountain and Khaz Modan.",["faction:Horde"]="Thrall views Rend's Blackrock extremists as an existential threat to the peace and honor of the New Horde, branding them renegades who cling to demonic servitude."},kw={"blackhand","blackrock","dark horde","doomhammer","eitrigg","gorgrond","rend","saurfang","spire"},rel={"subzone:blackrock-mountain","npc:orgrim-doomhammer","npc:eitrigg","npc:varok-saurfang","topic:old-horde","topic:new-horde","topic:dragonmaw-clan","topic:second-war"},src={"Blackrock clan"}}
E["topic:bronzebeard-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Bronzebeard clan",z="general",s="The Bronzebeard clan is the ruling dwarven royal line and dominant population of Ironforge. Following their victory in the ancient War of the Three Hammers, they forged strong ties with humanity and the gnomes, standing today under King Magni Bronzebeard as a cornerstone of the Alliance.",h="The Bronzebeard clan is the ruling dwarven royal line and dominant population of Ironforge.",sec={{t="The War of the Three Hammers",b="Long ago, dwarven society in Ironforge flourished under High King Modimus Anvilmar. When the king died of old age roughly 230 years ago, simmering rivalries erupted into civil war. Thane Madoran Bronzebeard commanded the traditional garrison and largest standing army within Ironforge, successfully driving both the Wildhammer and Dark Iron clans from under the mountain. When Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan mounted an assault to reconquer Khaz Modan, Madoran Bronzebeard led his clan in a decisive campaign that shattered the Dark Iron host, solidifying Bronzebeard supremacy over Ironforge.",sp=0},{t="A Pillar of the Alliance",b="Human kingdoms first encountered the Bronzebeards nearly two millennia ago, building mutual respect through shared mastery of metalcraft, trade, and a passion for battle. When the Orcish Horde swept through the continent in the Second War, King Magni Bronzebeard brought his realm into the Alliance of Lordaeron. Despite withstanding brutal sieges within their subterranean fortress, Bronzebeard engineers, gunners, and demolitionists provided pivotal firepower and arms that helped turn the tide of the war.",sp=0},{t="Grief at the High Seat",b="Today, King Magni governs from the High Seat in the Great Forge, yet his reign is shadowed by loss. His younger brother, Muradin Bronzebeard, served as an Alliance emissary in Lordaeron and vanished in the frozen wastes of Northrend alongside Prince Arthas Menethil, presumed dead when the prince claimed the cursed blade Frostmourne. Compounding this grief, Magni's daughter, Princess Moira, has gone missing, leaving the lineage facing an uncertain succession while the clan searches ancient Titan excavation sites to divine their origins.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who rules the Bronzebeard clan?",a="King Magni Bronzebeard sits upon the High Seat within the Great Forge. A veteran of the Second War and master smith, he leads both his clan and the allied dwarves of Khaz Modan.",al={"bronzebeard leader","who is king of ironforge","current bronzebeard king"},it="leader"},{q="How did the Bronzebeard clan win control of Ironforge?",a="Following the death of High King Modimus Anvilmar roughly 230 years ago, civil war broke out between the three major clans. Commanding Ironforge's central standing army, Thane Madoran Bronzebeard drove the Wildhammers and Dark Irons from the mountain and later crushed Thaurissan's invading Dark Iron army.",al={"war of the three hammers","dwarf civil war","why bronzebeards rule"},it="history"},{q="What is the difference between Bronzebeard and other dwarven clans?",a="The Bronzebeards inhabit Ironforge and represent the traditional mountain dwarves who excel in heavy plate, firearms, and subterranean architecture. In contrast, the Wildhammers dwell in the crags and skies as gryphon riders, while the hostile Dark Irons master dark sorcery and pyromancy beneath Blackrock Mountain.",al={"dwarf clans difference","ironforge vs wildhammer","types of dwarves"},it="identity"},{q="What happened to Muradin Bronzebeard?",a="Muradin Bronzebeard traveled to Northrend as an Alliance emissary and explorer. He guided Prince Arthas Menethil in the hunt for the runeblade Frostmourne and was reported dead when the cursed weapon was freed from its pedestal.",al={"where is muradin","is muradin alive","muradin fate"}},{q="Why are the Bronzebeards so devoted to archaeological digs?",a="Bronzebeard scholars have uncovered relics linking their ancestry to the ancient, stone-hewn earthen. Driven to discover the roots of their race and the transformation known as the curse of flesh, the clan finances digs and expeditions across Azeroth.",al={"why dwarves dig","dwarven excavations","earthen origins"},it="purpose"},{q="Where is the Bronzebeard royal family's seat of power?",a="The royal throne, known as the High Seat, is situated in the heart of Ironforge along the Great Forge, where King Magni conducts state affairs surrounded by councilors and royal guards.",al={"where is the throne room","high seat location","bronzebeard throne"},it="place"},{q="Who are the Bronzebeard clan's allies?",a="The Bronzebeards are staunch, founding pillars of the Grand Alliance. Their closest bond lies with humanity, dating back roughly eighteen centuries when human explorers from Arathor first journeyed south into Khaz Modan and discovered Ironforge. The two races forged an enduring partnership over shared smithing, engineering, and storytelling. They also share strong ties with the gnomes of nearby Gnomeregan and have long reestablished friendly relations with their Wildhammer cousins in the Aerie Peak, despite past civil conflicts.",al={"bronzebeard allies","who do bronzebeards ally with","ironforge allies"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Every playable dwarf hails from or answers to the Bronzebeard kingdom, serving King Magni as steadfast defenders of Khaz Modan.",["race:Gnome"]="The Bronzebeards offered unconditional refuge in Ironforge's Tinkertown when Gnomeregan fell to the trogg invasion.",["race:Human"]="Humanity and the Bronzebeards have maintained longstanding ties, bonded by shared martial traditions and mutual trade.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="The Bronzebeards are renowned across Azeroth for their unparalleled mastery of smelting, weapon crafting, and heavy armors."},kw={"bronzebeard","high seat","ironforge","khaz modan","madoran","magni","muradin","three hammers"},rel={"npc:magni-bronzebeard","topic:dwarf","zone:ironforge","topic:muradin-bronzebeard","subzone:high-seat","topic:dark-iron-clan","topic:wildhammer-clan","topic:alliance"},src={"Bronzebeard clan"}}
E["topic:brotherhood-of-northshire"] = {t="topic",n="Brotherhood of Northshire",z="general",s="The Brotherhood of Northshire is a holy human clerical order dedicated to the Church of the Holy Light, based out of Northshire Abbey in Elwynn Forest. Originally known as the Clerics of Northshire, the order suffered catastrophic losses during the First War before being reformed under Archbishop Alonsus Faol. Today, the Brotherhood serves as both a spiritual sanctuary and the primary training ground for fledgling Alliance defenders.",h="The Brotherhood of Northshire is a holy human clerical order dedicated to the Church of the Holy Light, based...",sec={{t="The Clerics of the First War",b="Centuries ago, pious missionaries traveled south from Lordaeron into the kingdom of Stormwind, establishing Northshire Abbey to minister to the people and soothe the realm through the Holy Light. Originally known as the Holy Order of Northshire Clerics, their compassion was legendary—they even tended to injured gnolls and jungle trolls during past border wars. When the Orcish Horde crossed through the Dark Portal, the clerics marched bravely to the front lines without armor or weapons to heal fallen soldiers. Because their healing sustained the Stormwind Army, the orcs ruthlessly targeted them. The order was decimated, the abbey was ransacked and burned, and the surviving clerics were forced to flee north across the sea to Southshore with Sir Anduin Lothar.",sp=0},{t="The Spark of the Paladins",b="The slaughter of the Northshire clerics sent shockwaves through the human kingdoms. Realizing that faith alone could not withstand an axe without armor and martial discipline, Archbishop Alonsus Faol resolved to create warriors who possessed both the sacred powers of the Light and the martial mastery of a knight. Drawing upon the surviving clerics and devout knights, Faol formed the Knights of the Silver Hand, giving rise to humanity's first paladins. In this way, the sacrifice of Northshire's fallen healers became the very foundation for the holy warriors who broke the Horde in the Second War.",sp=0},{t="Rebuilding and Present Duty",b="Following the reclamation of Stormwind and the destruction of the Dark Portal, Archbishop Faol oversaw the rebuilding of Northshire Abbey and reorganized the order as the Brotherhood of Northshire. Now in 25 ADP, Northshire Abbey stands as a quiet yet vital center where new recruits gather to begin their service to the realm. Under figures like Priestess Anetta, Brother Sammuel, and Brother Paxton, the Brotherhood attends to the spiritual health of the valley, preserves ancient texts in their library, and trains the next generation of defenders to confront brigands, wolves, and the gathering shadows across Elwynn Forest.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Brotherhood of Northshire?",a="They are a monastic order within the Church of the Holy Light based at Northshire Abbey in Elwynn Forest. Formerly known as the Clerics of Northshire, they provide spiritual guidance, heal the wounded, and train new Alliance adventurers starting their journey.",al={"who are the brotherhood of northshire","brotherhood of northshire lore","clerics of northshire"},it="identity"},{q="What happened to the original clerics during the First War?",a="The clerics entered the First War as unarmored battlefield healers. Recognizing that human soldiers could not easily be beaten while sustained by holy magic, the orcs prioritized butchering the clerics. The abbey was overrun and ruined, and only a remnant escaped across the Great Sea to Lordaeron.",al={"northshire clerics first war","what happened to the abbey","destruction of northshire abbey"},it="history"},{q="How did the Northshire Clerics inspire the paladins?",a="Witnessing how easily unarmored clerics were slain by the Horde, Archbishop Alonsus Faol realized the Alliance needed holy warriors capable of surviving brutal combat. He combined the spiritual devotion of the clerics with the martial prowess of knights, forging the Knights of the Silver Hand.",al={"creation of paladins","silver hand origin","alonsus faol paladins"},it="origin"},{q="Who leads or trains people at Northshire Abbey today?",a="The abbey is home to several notable figures of the Brotherhood, including Brother Paxton, who manages provisions and copies texts for the library, Priestess Anetta, who instructs aspiring priests, and Brother Sammuel, who guides novice paladins.",al={"who is at northshire abbey","northshire abbey trainers","brother paxton"},it="leader"},{q="What was the expedition to the Deadmines during the First War?",a="An ogre chieftain named Turok stole the sacred Tome of Divinity from Northshire. Sir Anduin Lothar led an expedition into the Deadmines of Westfall to retrieve it, but was trapped until Stormwind forces rescued him and secured the book back to the abbey.",al={"tome of divinity deadmines","anduin lothar deadmines","turok deadmines"},it="history"},{q="Are they connected to the Defias Brotherhood?",a="No, they are entirely opposed. While both share the word 'brotherhood', the Brotherhood of Northshire is a holy religious order loyal to Stormwind, whereas the Defias Brotherhood is a criminal syndicate of outlaws and bandits plaguing the countryside.",al={"brotherhood of northshire defias","are they defias","northshire defias"},it="ties"},{q="Why is the Brotherhood based in Northshire Valley?",a="Centuries ago, clerics from the Church of the Holy Light traveled south from Lordaeron and established Northshire Abbey as their spiritual home in Stormwind. Though the original abbey was razed by the Orcish Horde during the First War, Archbishop Alonsus Faol oversaw its reconstruction after the war ended. The valley remains their ancestral sanctuary, now serving as a training and recruitment ground to prepare new defenders for the kingdom.",al={"why are they in northshire","why northshire valley","brotherhood of northshire location"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="Who is the Brotherhood of Northshire allied with?",a="The Brotherhood of Northshire is an order within the Church of the Holy Light and operates directly in service to the Kingdom of Stormwind and the Grand Alliance. Historically, its clerics tended to Stormwind's armies during major conflicts like the Gnoll War and the Gurubashi War, and their restored order now directly recruits and trains fledgling champions to safeguard the realm.",al={"brotherhood of northshire allies","who does the brotherhood work for","brotherhood allegiance"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["class:Priest"]="Northshire Abbey is the cradle of Stormwind's clerical tradition, where ancient disciplines of healing, confession, and the Holy Light were cultivated long before the First War.",["class:Paladin"]="The tragic butchery of Northshire's unarmored clerics during the First War was the direct catalyst for Archbishop Faol to create the Knights of the Silver Hand.",["race:Human"]="For generations of Stormwind citizens, the Brotherhood has offered spiritual comfort in hardship, and its abbey serves as the humble beginning for the kingdom's greatest champions."},kw={"alonsus faol","brother paxton","brotherhood of northshire","church of the holy light","clerics of northshire","elwynn forest","northshire abbey","priestess anetta"},rel={"subzone:northshire-abbey","subzone:northshire-valley","topic:church-of-the-holy-light","topic:first-war","topic:second-war","topic:anduin-lothar","zone:elwynn","quest:1641"},src={"Brotherhood of Northshire"}}
E["topic:burning-blade-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Burning Blade clan",z="general",s="Once an ancient orcish clan renowned for its deadly blademasters and consumed by chaotic frenzy during the early wars, the Burning Blade now largely persists as a sinister demon-worshiping cult. Infiltrating Durotar and the Barrens under the direction of the Shadow Council, its acolytes threaten the fragile peace of the region. Meanwhile, a handful of honorable blademasters have forsaken demonic corruption to serve Warchief Thrall in the New Horde.",h="Once an ancient orcish clan renowned for its deadly blademasters and consumed by chaotic frenzy during the...",sec={{t="From Clan to Elemental Fury",b="Originating in the lands of Draenor alongside clans like the Warsong, the Burning Blade were long respected for the lethal tradition of their blademasters. When they drank the blood of Mannoroth and marched through the Dark Portal into Azeroth during the First War, however, the clan fractured mentally. By the Second War, they were scarcely an organized society, behaving instead like a berserk force of nature. Nomadic and demented, they threw themselves into battle with total disregard for survival, requiring ogres to hold them in check until unleashed. Ultimately, their numbers were battered and routed near the Dark Portal by the Alliance.",sp=0},{t="A Split Legacy",b="Following the Second War, the surviving remnants of the Burning Blade split dramatically. A disciplined contingent of blademasters swore solemn oaths to liberate their people from demonic enslavement, pledging their blades directly to Warchief Thrall as his personal honor guard. Yet a far larger portion of the scattered clan fell entirely under the sway of the Burning Legion and the Shadow Council, morphing into a clandestine cult of warlocks, fel acolytes, and conspirators lurking throughout Kalimdor.",sp=0},{t="The Threat in Durotar",b="The modern cult operates covens across Kalimdor, troubling newly arrived orcs and Darkspear trolls from their first days in the Valley of Trials. Burning Blade warlocks inhabit Thunder Ridge, and garrison a major hideout within Skull Rock near Orgrimmar's eastern gates. Their cultists rally around dark relics like the Demon Seed and harvest souls for demonic rituals, making them a primary internal menace against the fledgling Horde's stability in Durotar and the Barrens.",sp=0},{t="The Traitor in the Cleft",b="While Thrall works to establish the New Horde, the cult's master orchestrator sits directly under his nose. Neeru Fireblade, an ostensible Horde warlock dwelling in the Cleft of Shadows, secretly leads the Burning Blade as an agent of the Shadow Council. Neeru uses pawns, including the expendable Searing Blade of Ragefire Chasm, to maintain his disguise as a benign researcher helping the Warchief. Unbeknownst to Neeru, Thrall has already deduced his treacherous allegiance and watches him closely.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the Burning Blade clan?",a="Originally an orcish clan from Draenor known for elite blademasters, the Burning Blade was consumed by bloodlust during the First and Second Wars. Today, the name primarily refers to a demon-worshiping cult operating across Kalimdor on behalf of the Shadow Council, although a small number of reformed blademasters serve Thrall's Horde.",al={"burning blade lore","who are burning blade","burning blade background"},it="identity"},{q="Who actually leads the Burning Blade?",a="The cult answers to the Shadow Council, and its covens across Durotar and the Barrens are run by warlocks and demon-summoners who keep their true master hidden. Whispers say the trail of command leads uncomfortably close to Orgrimmar itself.",al={"burning blade leader","who is the leader of burning blade"},it="leader"},{q="Why are they attacking new orcs in the Valley of Trials?",a="The Burning Blade established a coven in the northern caves of the Valley of Trials to disrupt young orcs and trolls undergoing their rites of passage, using vile familiars and dark medallions to cultivate fel corruption at the heart of Horde lands.",al={"valley of trials burning blade","why are cultists in valley of trials"},it="why_here"},{q="What is the difference between the Burning Blade and Searing Blade?",a="The Searing Blade is a sect based deep inside Ragefire Chasm beneath Orgrimmar. It is a smaller offshoot, while the Burning Blade is the broader cult operating across Durotar and the Barrens. Investigating the chasm reveals who directs the sect.",al={"searing blade vs burning blade","difference between burning and searing blade"},sp=1,it="conflict"},{q="Are all Burning Blade members demon-worshiping cultists?",a="No. While the cult constitutes the majority, proud blademasters of the original clan broke away by the Third War. They took vows to redeem their people from demonic enslavement and now fight loyally within the New Horde as Thrall's personal honor guard.",al={"are all burning blade bad","burning blade blademasters good"},it="residents"},{q="Why are the Burning Blade hostile to travelers?",a="The Burning Blade serves the demonic will of the Burning Legion and the Shadow Council. Ever since their clan was consumed by demonic bloodlust during the early wars, they have abandoned any true allegiance or honor. Now reorganized as a secretive apocalyptic cult, they view outsiders, loyal Horde subjects, and curious travelers as direct threats to their covens or as fuel for demonic blood sacrifices.",al={"why do burning blade attack","burning blade hostile","why does the burning blade fight"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who are the Burning Blade allied with?",a="The cult acts as a direct arm of the sinister Shadow Council, carrying out their dark designs across Kalimdor. They frequently cooperate with allied demon-worshiping splinter factions like the Searing Blade hiding beneath Orgrimmar and the Argus Wake in the Eastern Kingdoms. Above all, they are sworn in servitude to the Burning Legion, summoning demons and gathering corrupt artifacts to hasten their masters' designs.",al={"burning blade allies","who does the burning blade work for","burning blade shadow council"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Where did the Burning Blade come from?",a="Originally an ancient orcish clan from Draenor known for their fearsome blademasters, the Burning Blade joined the Old Horde and crossed into Azeroth during the First War. Demonic corruption eroded their minds until they became a reckless, mad frenzy during the Second War, eventually breaking apart entirely. While honorable blademasters later swore fealty to Thrall's New Horde, the remaining remnants transformed into the shadowy demonic cult threatening Kalimdor today.",al={"burning blade origin","burning blade lore","history of burning blade clan"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="The Burning Blade represents the persistent specter of the Horde's corrupt demonic past, directly threatening Durotar and the Barrens from within.",["class:Warlock"]="The cult exemplifies the reckless, demonic enslavement that forces Horde warlocks like those studying in the Cleft of Shadows under Neeru Fireblade into hiding and intense suspicion.",["race:Orc"]="The clan's fall is a bitter reminder of the blood curse, standing in direct contrast to the honor-bound blademasters who seek atonement in service to Thrall."},kw={"blademaster","burning blade","cleft of shadows","durotar","searing blade","shadow council","skull rock","zmodlor"},rel={"npc:thrall","quest:5726","quest:829","zone:durotar","zone:ragefire","topic:shadow-council","quest:827","npc:orgnil-soulscar"},src={"Burning Blade clan"}}
E["topic:burning-legion"] = {t="topic",n="Burning Legion",z="general",s="The Burning Legion is an infinite, apocalyptic host of demons led by the fallen titan Sargeras, dedicated to scouring all life from creation. Having already shattered ancient Kalimdor ten thousand years ago, the demons remain Azeroth's most relentless nemesis, orchestrating the corruption of the orcs and the horrors of the Third War.",h="The Burning Legion is an infinite, apocalyptic host of demons led by the fallen titan Sargeras, dedicated to...",sec={{t="Genesis of the Host",b="Long ago, Sargeras stood as the champion of the titan Pantheon, tasked with hunting down chaotic fiends across the Great Dark Beyond. In his travels, he uncovered the horrifying schemes of the Void Lords, who sought to corrupt sleeping worldsouls into engines of utter darkness. Horrified and disillusioned, Sargeras concluded that creation itself was fatally flawed and must be unmade in flame. To enact his crusade, he shattered the prison realm of Mardum, bending its captive demons to his will through fel magic. He augmented their chaos with the intellect of the corrupted eredar—elevating Archimonde and Kil'jaeden as his chief lieutenants—and forged an unstoppable engine of cosmic annihilation.",sp=0},{t="The War of the Ancients",b="Ten millennia ago, the reckless sorceries of the Highborne drew the Legion's gaze to Azeroth. Whispering through the Well of Eternity, Sargeras seduced Queen Azshara and her councilor Xavius with promises of reshaping the world into paradise. Instead, the portal unleashed commanders like Mannoroth and Archimonde alongside swarms of doomguard, felhounds, and flaming infernals. The ensuing war reduced Kalimdor's civilization to ruin before an alliance of night elves, dragonflights, and demigods collapsed the gateway. The catastrophic implosion tore the supercontinent apart in the Great Sundering, casting the ruined lands across vast new seas.",sp=0},{t="Shadows over Azeroth",b="Though cast back into the Twisting Nether, the Legion never abandoned Azeroth. Deciding that direct invasion invited failure, Kil'jaeden orchestrated proxy cataclysms from afar. The deceiver bound the orcs of Draenor through demonic blood pacts, sending the Horde charging through the Dark Portal to weaken mortal defenses. When that failed, the Legion forged the Lich King and unleashed the undead Scourge across Lordaeron, paving the way for Archimonde's return in the Third War. Even after Archimonde fell at Mount Hyjal, remnants of the Legion linger across Felwood, the Blasted Lands, and hidden cults throughout the world.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Burning Legion?",a="The Burning Legion is an infinite army of demons, corrupted races, and destructive horrors forged by the fallen titan Sargeras. Its singular objective is the Burning Crusade: scouring all life and ordered existence from the cosmos so that the Void cannot corrupt it.",al={"who are the legion","what is the burning legion","legion demons","what are demons"},it="identity"},{q="Who leads the Burning Legion?",a="The Legion's ultimate master is the fallen titan Sargeras. Below him serve the demon lords of the eredar, most notably Archimonde the Defiler, who spearheaded military assaults until his demise at Mount Hyjal, and Kil'jaeden the Deceiver, who orchestrates insidious plots across worlds.",al={"who rules the legion","legion leader","who is the demon king","archimonde boss"},it="leader"},{q="Why does the Legion want to destroy Azeroth?",a="Azeroth has repeatedly defied the Legion, standing as the only world known to have repelled their assaults. Furthermore, Sargeras is obsessed with purging the potent world before the shadowy Old Gods and their Void masters can warp it into an unstoppable evil.",al={"why do demons attack","legion motive","why invade azeroth","sargeras goal"},it="purpose"},{q="How did the demons first arrive on Azeroth?",a="Ten thousand years ago, Queen Azshara and her Highborne channeled the vast energies of the Well of Eternity, inadvertently attracting Sargeras. Deceived by promises of limitless power, the Highborne opened a massive portal beneath the palace, allowing the Legion to invade Kalimdor.",al={"first demon invasion","how did legion get here","well of eternity portal","queen azshara demons"},it="history"},{q="What happened to the Legion during the Third War?",a="Summoned back to Azeroth using the book of Medivh, Archimonde marched across Lordaeron and sailed west to devour the World Tree, Nordrassil. At Mount Hyjal, mortal races bought time for Malfurion Stormrage to call upon the ancestral spirits, detonating the tree's power and obliterating Archimonde.",al={"third war demons","mount hyjal invasion","did legion lose third war","what happened to archimonde"},it="history"},{q="Are demons still a threat right now?",a="Yes. Though their main host fractured after Archimonde's fall, dangerous remnants remain entrenched across Azeroth. Dreadlords, infernals, and lingering fiends occupy corrupted zones like Felwood and the Blasted Lands, while mortal cultists such as the Shadow Council work in secret.",al={"are demons still around","legion remnants","demons in classic","where are demons now"},it="threat"},{q="Why are the Burning Legion hostile to everyone?",a="The Burning Legion's ultimate purpose is nothing less than the absolute annihilation of all mortal life and creation across the cosmos. Under the command of the fallen titan Sargeras, demons do not seek conquest, coexistence, or surrender; they view mortal worlds as flawed remnants to be purged in fel fire. Any traveler or soul they encounter on Azeroth is merely fuel for their magic or prey to be wiped out.",al={"why is the legion hostile","why does the burning legion attack","burning legion hostile reasons"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who serves alongside or works for the Burning Legion?",a="The Legion relies on an endless tide of demonic races, including monstrous pit lords, cunning dreadlords, ravenous felhounds, and towering infernals. Beyond pure demons, they corrupt mortal races to act as pawns and vanguards. In the past, they swayed Queen Azshara's Highborne sorcerers, corrupted the orcs of Draenor through demonic blood, and forged the Scourge to soften mortal resistance before invasions.",al={"burning legion allies","who works for the legion","who serves the burning legion"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Why are demonic remnants still found across Azeroth?",a="Following Archimonde's defeat at the Battle of Mount Hyjal, the centralized demonic assault shattered, scattering surviving fiends into hiding. Today, lingering demons and renegade dreadlords lurk in desolate reaches like Desolace, Felwood, and the Blasted Lands. Additionally, corrupt mortal cabals, such as the warlocks of the Shadow Council, secretly summon fresh reinforcements through hidden dark portals to carry on the Legion's work.",al={"why are demons still on azeroth","why are legion demons here","burning legion locations azeroth"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The night elves bear the deepest scars of the Legion's invasions; their ancient empire was shattered in the Sundering, and the recent sacrifice of their World Tree at Mount Hyjal cost them their very immortality.",["race:Orc"]="Orcs carry a bitter legacy with the Legion, having been enslaved through the demonic blood pact of Mannoroth before Grom Hellscream sacrificed himself to break the curse once and for all.",["class:Warlock"]="Warlocks walk a razor's edge, bending the raw fel magic and subservient demons of the Legion to their own mortal will despite constant suspicion and the ever-present threat of corruption.",["class:Paladin"]="Paladins view the Burning Legion as the antithesis of the Holy Light, dedicating their martial vows and righteous power to purging demonic filth wherever it festers."},kw={"archimonde","burning legion","demons","eredar","fel","hyjal","kil'jaeden","mannoroth","sargeras","sundering","twisting nether","well of eternity"},rel={"topic:sargeras","topic:third-war","topic:highborne","topic:night-elf","topic:titan","topic:old-god","topic:lich-king","npc:malfurion-stormrage"},src={"Burning Legion"}}
E["topic:cenarion-circle"] = {t="topic",n="Cenarion Circle",z="general",s="The Cenarion Circle is an ancient, independent order of druids founded by Malfurion Stormrage under the guidance of the demigod Cenarius. Operating from their sacred haven in Moonglade, they strive to maintain the balance of nature, safeguard the Emerald Dream, and mend Azeroth's blighted lands. Though historically composed of night elves, they have recently welcomed the tauren into their ranks, transcending factional divides to defend the living world.",h="The Cenarion Circle is an ancient, independent order of druids founded by Malfurion Stormrage under the...",sec={{t="Origins of the Circle",b="The roots of the Cenarion Circle go back thousands of years to the teachings of Cenarius, the Patron of Druids, who mentored the first mortal druid, Malfurion Stormrage. Following the Great Sundering, Malfurion bound together those who wished to study nature's ways. The organization was formally convened in Moonglade during the ancient War of the Satyr, partly to curb the savage fury of the Druids of the Scythe, who had turned into feral worgen. Armed with the Scythe of Elune, the druids contained the beastform threat, sealing the mad pack within the Emerald Dream. Ever since, the Circle has held sacred vigil over Azeroth's wild places.",sp=0},{t="A Sacred Neutrality",b="While deeply intertwined with night elf history, the Cenarion Circle exists independently of Darnassus and the Sisterhood of Elune. Their heart remains the enchanted valley of Moonglade, centered around the peaceful town of Nighthaven, where weapons are sheathed and the wars of mortals hold no sway. This ancient neutrality was demonstrated anew following the Third War, when Malfurion befriended the tauren elder Hamuul Runetotem. Recognizing the tauren people's profound spiritual reverence for the Earth Mother, the Circle formally inducted Hamuul, reintroducing druidism to his race after thousands of years.",sp=0},{t="Tension and the Absent Archdruid",b="Following the devastation at Mount Hyjal, Malfurion fell into an unnatural, impenetrable coma deep within his barrow den, leaving the druids leaderless. In his absence, Archdruid Fandral Staghelm claimed practical leadership of the Circle. Fandral took advantage of Malfurion's silence to rally support for growing Teldrassil, hoping to restore the kaldorei's lost immortality. However, the Dragon Aspects refused to bless the new tree, and strange corruptions have begun taking root in its branches. Today, internal division simmers within the order as Fandral rules with a stern hand, while ancient guardians sense a creeping darkness spreading from both Silithus and the Emerald Dream.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the Cenarion Circle?",a="The Cenarion Circle is an ancient society of druids dedicated to preserving nature's balance, studying the wild ways, and tending to the Emerald Dream. Named after the demigod Cenarius and founded by Malfurion Stormrage, the order governs druidic teachings across Azeroth from its sanctuary in Moonglade.",al={"cenarion circle lore","who are the cenarion circle","what is the circle"},it="identity"},{q="Where is the headquarters of the Cenarion Circle?",a="The Circle is headquartered in the moonlit, secluded haven of Moonglade, specifically around the village of Nighthaven and the shores of Lake Elune'ara. They maintain smaller outposts and barrow dens across Kalimdor, including a major vigil in the desert wastes of Silithus at Cenarion Hold.",al={"cenarion circle base","where are cenarion druids based","nighthaven cenarion"},it="place"},{q="Why are tauren in the Cenarion Circle?",a="During the Third War, the tauren elder Hamuul Runetotem fought alongside Malfurion Stormrage at Mount Hyjal. The two developed mutual respect, and Malfurion shared deep druidic secrets with Hamuul. Hamuul became the first tauren druid in millennia and was warmly inducted into the Circle, bringing the tauren and night elf druids into rare partnership.",al={"why are tauren druids","hamuul cenarion circle","tauren night elf druids"},it="people"},{q="Who leads the Cenarion Circle?",a="Historically, Malfurion Stormrage led the order alongside elder archdruids. With Malfurion currently trapped in an unexplained barrow coma, leadership has effectively fallen to Archdruid Fandral Staghelm in Darnassus, though respected elders like Hamuul Runetotem and keepers in Moonglade guide their respective factions.",al={"leader of cenarion circle","who is the archdruid","archdruid leader"},it="leader"},{q="Is the Cenarion Circle part of the Alliance?",a="No. The Cenarion Circle is entirely neutral. While its membership was once strictly night elven, their primary oath is to nature, not mortal thrones. In Moonglade and other Cenarion encampments, Alliance and Horde druids walk side by side without bloodshed.",al={"is cenarion circle alliance","cenarion circle faction","are cenarion druids neutral"},it="side"},{q="What happened at the Scarab Wall in Silithus?",a="A thousand years ago during the War of the Shifting Sands, Archdruid Fandral Staghelm rallied the Circle and dragons to combat the insectoid qiraji swarm. Working with the bronze dragon Anachronos, the druids channeled their magic to erupt colossal roots and stone from the earth, forming the Scarab Wall to seal the hive away. The Circle established Cenarion Hold to ensure that seal never fails.",al={"cenarion hold silithus","war of the shifting sands","druids scarab wall"},it="conflict"},{q="Why is it called the Cenarion Circle?",a="The order was named after and blessed by Cenarius, the demigod Lord of the Forest. Cenarius was the patron of the wild who originally tutored Malfurion Stormrage, the first mortal druid of Azeroth. When Malfurion and his fellow practitioners gathered to organize their traditions and safeguard the balance of nature, they took the demigod's name to honor their divine teacher.",al={"cenarion circle name origin","why cenarion","who is cenarion circle named after"},it="name",add=true},{q="Where did the Cenarion Circle come from?",a="The Circle was founded in Moonglade by Malfurion Stormrage following the Great Sundering and the War of the Satyr. Malfurion brought the early night elf druids together to establish strict guidelines and traditions after rogue practitioners, known as the Druids of the Scythe, fell prey to uncontrollable feral rage. For thousands of years during the Long Vigil, the Circle maintained balance and healed demonic scars across Azeroth while walking the Emerald Dream alongside the green dragonflight.",al={"cenarion circle history","origin of the cenarion circle","how was cenarion circle founded"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The Cenarion Circle is your order and spiritual authority. Whether you trace your tutelage to Darnassus or the plains of Mulgore, all druidic power, trials, and callings flow through the Circle's rites in Moonglade.",["race:NightElf"]="For ten millennia your people's men primarily slept in barrows serving the Circle while the Sentinels guarded your borders; the Circle's deep bond with the wild shaped every facet of kaldorei culture.",["race:Tauren"]="Through Hamuul Runetotem's fellowship with Malfurion Stormrage, your people have reclaimed ancient traditions tied directly to the Earth Mother, earning an honored place within the Circle.",["profession:Herbalism"]="The druids of the Cenarion Circle possess unmatched understanding of Azeroth's botanical life and the delicate ecosystems that nurture rare and magical flora."},kw={"cenarion circle","cenarion hold","cenarius","druid","emerald dream","fandral staghelm","hamuul runetotem","malfurion","moonglade","nighthaven"},rel={"quest:5921","npc:malfurion-stormrage","topic:cenarius","quest:1489","npc:fandral-staghelm","subzone:nighthaven","subzone:cenarion-hold","topic:druid"},src={"Cenarion Circle"}}
E["topic:centaur"] = {t="topic",n="Centaur",z="general",s="Centaur are a violent, horse-bodied humanoid race whose nomadic clans dominate the arid plains of central Kalimdor. Born from the tragic union between the demigod Zaetar and the earth princess Theradras, they spent centuries driving the tauren toward extinction before the newly arrived orcish Horde intervened.",h="Centaur are a violent, horse-bodied humanoid race whose nomadic clans dominate the arid plains of central...",sec={{t="A Cursed Lineage",b="Centaur originated roughly eleven hundred years ago beneath the wastes of Desolace. When Princess Theradras, daughter of the elemental lord Therazane, awoke and withered the surrounding land, the demigod Zaetar arrived from the Emerald Dream to investigate. The two formed an unnatural union, and their offspring emerged as the first centaur. Horrified by their misshapen, savage nature, Zaetar could not hide his revulsion. Enraged by their father's disgust, the centaur slaughtered him. Though grief-stricken Theradras entombed Zaetar's spirit within her subterranean sanctuary of Maraudon, she shielded and nurtured her vicious children, who swiftly multiplied across Kalimdor.",sp=0},{t="Centuries of Slaughter",b="Following their emergence, the centaur expanded outward from Desolace, waging a brutal war of attrition against Kalimdor's native populations. The nomadic tauren suffered the most: driven from their fertile ancestral homeland of Mulgore, they were chased relentlessly across the Barrens. Centaur warbands treated the tauren as prey, burning settlements and hunting them for sport. By the time of the Third War, centuries of relentless pursuit had brought the tauren to the precipice of extinction, leaving them desperately fleeing endless marauders across the savannah.",sp=0},{t="The Tide Turns",b="Everything shifted when Warchief Thrall and the newly arrived orcish Horde landed upon Kalimdor. Thrall formed a pact with Cairne Bloodhoof, uniting orcish steel with tauren might. Together, they crushed the aggressive Kolkar warbands, broke their grip on the plains, and enabled the tauren to safely establish Thunder Bluff and reclaim Mulgore. This alliance ended the nomadic exile of the tauren and established a debt of blood and honor that permanently bound them into the Horde.",sp=0},{t="A Fractured Menace",b="Despite their staggering defeats, centaur remain an active threat across central Kalimdor. Their society is divided into warring clans—primarily the Kolkar, Gelkis, Magram, and Maraudine—each led by a khan and locked in perpetual blood feuds over territory and water. While their internal squabbles keep them from uniting into an unstoppable horde, aggressive raiding parties continue to strike at trade caravans and settlements across Durotar, the Barrens, Thousand Needles, and the borders of Mulgore.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where did centaur come from?",a="They were born from the union between Zaetar, a son of Cenarius, and Princess Theradras, an elemental princess of earth. Born misshapen and aggressive, the first centaur killed their father when they saw revulsion in his eyes. Theradras then entombed his spirit in the sacred caverns of Maraudon.",al={"centaur origin","who created the centaur","centaur creation lore"},it="origin"},{q="Why do centaur hate the tauren so much?",a="Centaur expanded aggressively from Desolace and spent over a thousand years raiding the indigenous peoples of the plains. They hunted the tauren for sport and drove them out of Mulgore into an endless nomadic exile across the Barrens, nearly exterminating the entire race before the orcs intervened.",al={"tauren centaur feud","why do centaur hunt tauren","centaur tauren war"},it="conflict"},{q="What clans do the centaur belong to?",a="The primary clans on Kalimdor are the Kolkar, Gelkis, Magram, Maraudine, and Galak. Each trace their leadership back to the original khans who founded them and frequently wage bloody feuds against one another.",al={"centaur tribes","names of centaur clans","centaur factions"},it="people"},{q="Where are centaur encountered today?",a="The heart of their territory is the wasteland of Desolace, where their sacred grounds of Maraudon lie. However, aggressive warbands and outposts roam across the Barrens, Durotar, and Thousand Needles.",al={"where are centaur found","centaur locations","centaur zones"},it="place"},{q="Can centaur be reasoned or allied with?",a="Generally, centaur view all other races as weak prey to be enslaved or destroyed. However, the fierce rivalry between rival clans can sometimes be manipulated; for instance, clans in Desolace like the Magram or Gelkis have occasionally accepted outsider assistance to annihilate their hated kin.",al={"are centaur friendly","can you ally with centaur","friendly centaur"},it="allies"},{q="Who is Princess Theradras to the centaur?",a="Theradras is their mother and revered patron. Deep within Maraudon, she protects the spirit of their murdered father, Zaetar, and has periodically infused her descendants with elemental fury to spur them into conquering new territory across Kalimdor.",al={"centaur mother","theradras and centaur","princess theradras lore"},it="ties"},{q="What are centaur?",a="Centaur are a warlike, primitive race possessing the torso and arms of a human joined to the lower body and four legs of a horse. Savage and nomadic by nature, they roam the arid plains and valleys of central Kalimdor. They are organized into fiercely territorial clans that value brute strength, raiding neighboring settlements, and driving off any outsiders who tread near their hunting grounds and watering holes.",al={"what is centaur","who are the centaur","centaur race"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are centaur so hostile to travelers?",a="Hostility is deeply woven into centaur nature. Born cursed and filled with self-loathing and fury, they murdered their own father shortly after their creation. Ever since, they have viewed the world through violence and conquest. They see central Kalimdor as their rightful territory, treating any passing caravan, traveler, or settlement as trespassers to be slaughtered, enslaved, or driven out.",al={"why do centaur attack","why are centaur aggressive","centaur hostile"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who leads the centaur?",a="The centaur have no single ruler. Instead, they are divided into independent, feuding clans—such as the Kolkar, Gelkis, Magram, Maraudine, and Galak—each governed by its own khan. These khans rule through ruthless physical dominance and cunning, and the clans spend nearly as much effort waging bloody territorial wars against one another as they do raiding foreign enemies.",al={"centaur leader","who rules the centaur","centaur khan"},it="leader",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For your people, centaur are not mere beasts; they are the ancient tormentors who nearly eradicated your ancestors and drove you across the Barrens for centuries until the Horde offered salvation.",["faction:Horde"]="Defending against centaur incursions in the Barrens and Durotar is a matter of survival, honoring the founding pact struck between Thrall and Cairne Bloodhoof.",["class:Druid"]="Centaur represent a tragic perversion of nature's balance, sharing blood with Cenarius through his fallen son Zaetar yet bringing only desolation and slaughter in their wake."},kw={"barrens","centaur","desolace","khan","kolkar","maraudon","theradras","zaetar"},rel={"topic:tauren","zone:desolace","zone:barrens","topic:kolkar-clan","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","npc:thrall","topic:cenarius","quest:850"},src={"Centaur"}}
E["topic:church-of-the-holy-light"] = {t="topic",n="Church of the Holy Light",z="general",s="The Church of the Holy Light is the dominant religious institution of humanity, guiding mortal souls through the virtues of compassion, justice, and devotion. Originally centered in the lost northern kingdom of Lordaeron, its leadership now resides within the grand Cathedral of Light in Stormwind City following the devastation wrought by the Scourge.",h="The Church of the Holy Light is the dominant religious institution of humanity, guiding mortal souls through...",sec={{t="Origins and the Light's Creed",b="The Church's origins reach back over a millennium to the aftermath of the Troll Wars, when early Arathorian mystics, led by Mereldar, experienced profound visions of luminous angelic beings. These visions revealed the existence of the Holy Light, teaching humans to channel sacred power to cure injuries and purge affliction. Driven by tenets of charity, righteousness, and the fundamental belief that the Light resides within all thinking beings, these teachings eventually coalesced into an organized faith. The kingdom of Lordaeron became the religious heartland of this doctrine, establishing revered sanctuaries throughout the north, including Stratholme, Andorhal, Tyr's Hand, and Light's Hope Chapel.",sp=0},{t="A Shield in the Great Wars",b="In the First War, the Brotherhood of Northshire answered the call to battle, healing fallen defenders against the invading Orcish Horde. Because they lacked weapons or heavy armor, these frontline clerics suffered appalling casualties at the hands of the orcs. Recognizing the need for holy warriors who could both channel holy wrath and survive physical combat, Archbishop Alonsus Faol convened with Supreme Commander Anduin Lothar during the Second War to establish the Knights of the Silver Hand. Paladins and priests alike became the moral and martial bulwark of the Alliance of Lordaeron, preserving humanity from total annihilation.",sp=0},{t="Fall of the North and the Southern Haven",b="The Third War brought immense sorrow upon the faithful. When the Plague of Undeath crept across Lordaeron, the Church worked tirelessly to alleviate the sick and stem the contamination, only to be overwhelmed when the kingdom collapsed from within. With Lordaeron transformed into a Scourge-haunted wilderness, the seat of the Church transferred southward. Under the stewardship of Archbishop Benedictus, the clergy reconstituted its hierarchy within the newly constructed Cathedral of Light in Stormwind, offering solace, spiritual guidance, and military purpose to the battered survivors of the Alliance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the core philosophy of the Church of the Holy Light?",a="The Church teaches that a unifying holy energy connects all living things. Practitioners cultivate personal righteousness, altruism, and compassion, seeking to improve both themselves and the world around them. Adherents believe that acting with virtue, justice, and self-sacrifice allows one to reflect the Light's purest essence.",al={"holy light beliefs","what does the church believe","tenets of the light","church philosophy"},it="purpose"},{q="Who leads the Church today?",a="Archbishop Benedictus serves as the spiritual leader of the Church of the Holy Light. Following the passing of Archbishop Alonsus Faol and the fall of the northern kingdom of Lordaeron, Benedictus organized the priesthood from the grand Cathedral of Light situated in the heart of Stormwind City.",al={"church leader","who is the archbishop","head of the church"},it="leader"},{q="How was the order of Paladins formed?",a="During the First War, unarmored clerics from Northshire were deliberately targeted and massacred by the Horde. To ensure holy spellcasters could survive front-line warfare, Archbishop Alonsus Faol founded the Knights of the Silver Hand prior to the Second War, imbuing knightly martial combatants with divine spellcasting abilities.",al={"origin of paladins","how were paladins created","alonsus faol paladins","formation of silver hand"}},{q="Where are the Church's most sacred places?",a="Historically, the Church's oldest holy sites rested in the Eastweald of Lordaeron, including Andorhal, Stratholme, Tyr's Hand, and Light's Hope Chapel. Following the Scourge's destruction of the north, Stormwind's Cathedral of Light became the faith's preeminent sanctuary and seat of authority.",al={"holy sites","sacred places","important shrines"},it="place"},{q="What role did the Church play against the Plague of Undeath?",a="During the Third War, priests and clerics threw themselves into field hospices across Lordaeron, attempting to cleanse the afflicted and halt the plague's spread. Tragically, holy magic proved unable to completely reverse the supernatural corruption, and many clergy fell when their patients succumbed and rose as Scourge monstrosities.",al={"church role in plague","holy light curing the plague","scourge and church"},it="conflict"},{q="Do races other than humans follow the Church?",a="Yes. Ironforge dwarves embraced the Church's teachings centuries ago, establishing their own devout orders of priests and paladins. The high elves of Quel'Thalas also adopted the philosophy, mastering restorative healing magic that they later shared back with human clerics.",al={"non human light worshipers","do dwarves follow the light","high elf priests"},it="people"},{q="What is the Church of the Holy Light?",a="The Church of the Holy Light is the central religious institution of humanity. Its clergy preach that a benevolent Holy Light dwells within all living beings, urging followers to practice compassion, honor, and justice to bring out the best in themselves and the world. Through their devotion, its priests heal the sick and wounded, while the paladins of the Silver Hand serve as the Church's martial arm to protect the weak and vanquish evil.",al={"what is the church of light","who are the church of holy light","church of holy light"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where did the Church of the Holy Light come from?",a="The Church traces its origins back over two thousand years to the Troll Wars. Following visions of angelic beings, the priestess Mereldar and her companions codified the Light's virtues into early scripture. Centuries later, migrants founded the kingdom of Lordaeron, which became a heartland for holy ascetics and pilgrims. Lordaeron's leaders eventually organized these beliefs into a unified church hierarchy, establishing shrines, monasteries, and chapels across the human kingdoms.",al={"origins of the church of light","history of church of holy light","how was the church founded"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who is the Church of the Holy Light allied with?",a="The Church is deeply intertwined with the Grand Alliance. During the Second War, Archbishop Alonsus Faol pledged the Church's spiritual and martial support to repel the Horde, establishing the Knights of the Silver Hand to fight on the front lines. Following the fall of Lordaeron, the Church reconstituted its seat at Stormwind's Cathedral of Light, working directly alongside the Kingdom of Stormwind and its allies.",al={"church of light faction","who does the church of light support","church of light alliance"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Your holy vows stem from Archbishop Faol's covenant during the Second War, marrying knightly chivalry to the restorative and retributive grace of the Light.",["class:Priest"]="Whether providing comfort to the wounded or studying the difficult balance between luminescence and shadow, your vocation belongs to a legacy spanning thousands of years of human devotion.",["race:Human"]="For generations, the Church has blessed human kingdoms through crisis and plague, and its presence in Stormwind remains the spiritual backbone of your civilization.",["race:Dwarf"]="Long allied with the human realms, many Ironforge dwarves find common ground and shared virtue within the sermons and sanctified halls of the Holy Light.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Church of the Holy Light provides the Alliance with moral unity and battlefield hospice care, standing as an enduring symbol of hope against darkness."},kw={"alonsus faol","benedictus","cathedral of light","church","holy light","lordaeron","mereldar","northshire","paladin","priest","silver hand","stormwind"},rel={"subzone:cathedral-of-light","npc:archbishop-benedictus","topic:light","topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","topic:brotherhood-of-northshire","topic:lordaeron","zone:stormwind","topic:plague-of-undeath"},src={"Church of the Holy Light"}}
E["topic:cult-of-the-damned"] = {t="topic",n="Cult of the Damned",z="general",s="The Cult of the Damned is a secretive cabal of mortal zealots, necromancers, and aristocrats devoted to the Lich King. Formed in northern Lordaeron prior to the Third War, its members poisoned the grain supplies that spawned the Scourge and engineered the downfall of humanity's greatest kingdom. Today, the surviving cultists manage the plague cauldrons and necromantic academies across the Plaguelands while actively infiltrating the cities of the living.",h="The Cult of the Damned is a secretive cabal of mortal zealots, necromancers, and aristocrats devoted to the...",sec={{t="A Sickness in the Soul",b="The Cult of the Damned was forged by the exiled Archmage Kel'Thuzad after he answered the telepathic whispers of the Lich King emanating from the Frozen Throne. Returning to Lordaeron with vast wealth and forbidden dark arts, Kel'Thuzad offered two false gospels: to the desperate, impoverished lower classes, he promised social equality and liberation from misery; to the wealthy landowners, such as the noble House of Barov, he promised eternal life and an unshakeable grip upon their domains. In truth, both the destitute and the affluent were cattle intended for slaughter, bound into a death cult designed to betray their homeland from the inside.",sp=0},{t="The Poisoned Harvest",b="Operating in complete secrecy from the dungeons beneath Caer Darrow, the cultists honed their necrotic craft, built hulking abominations, and perfected the Plague of Undeath brought south from Northrend. They struck at Lordaeron's breadbasket, contaminating the grain stores at Andorhal. As tainted flour traveled through regular merchant routes to hearths across the Eastweald, commoners sickened and died within days, only to rise as shambling thralls under the Lich King's telepathic lash. When Prince Arthas slaughtered Kel'Thuzad in Andorhal, the cultist network was already so deeply rooted that the kingdom's doom was sealed.",sp=0},{t="The Present Threat",b="Though Arthas sailed to Northrend and ascended the Frozen Throne, the Cult of the Damned remains fully operational in northern Lordaeron under the command of the resurrected archlich Kel'Thuzad. From the shadows of Stratholme and the halls of Scholomance, living cultists perform dark rites, maintain the toxic plague cauldrons across the ruined farmsteads, and attempt to assassinate Scarlet Crusaders and Forsaken alike. Because they still breathe, these acolytes can walk unnoticed through living settlements, poisoning wells, sabotaging supply lines, and grooming new recruits to feed the Scourge machine.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why would living mortals join a cult that turns people undead?",a="Kel'Thuzad preyed upon mortal weakness. He offered the impoverished lower classes a new order of absolute equality and relief from their hardships, while promising wealthy nobles like Baron Rivendare and the Barov family immortality and everlasting power. Most initiates were kept ignorant of the true, horrific nature of the plague until it was too late to turn back.",al={"why join cult of the damned","cultist motives","why serve scourge"},it="motive"},{q="Why are cultists allowed to stay alive instead of being killed and raised?",a="Living agents possess immense practical value for the Lich King. They can pass through human settlements unnoticed, trade along standard merchant lines, purchase supplies, and subvert kingdoms from within. In cult doctrine, undeath is also treated as a glorious reward that must be earned through devoted service.",al={"why are cultists human","why aren't they undead","living cultists"},it="purpose"},{q="Where is the Cult of the Damned based?",a="Their primary scholastic and spiritual redoubt is Scholomance, located within the catacombs beneath Caer Darrow. Beyond this academy, large concentrations of cultists operate in the ruins of Stratholme, around the plague cauldrons of the Western and Eastern Plaguelands, and across secret cells hidden in living settlements.",al={"cult headquarters","where is the cult","cult main base"},it="place"},{q="Who founded the Cult of the Damned?",a="Archmage Kel'Thuzad, a former high-ranking member of Dalaran's Kirin Tor. After being censured for his forbidden necromantic studies, he traveled north to commune with the Lich King, who granted him immense power and sent him back to Lordaeron to organize the mortal vanguard of the Scourge.",al={"who made cult of damned","cult founder","kel'thuzad cult"},it="origin"},{q="How does the Cult of the Damned spread the plague today?",a="While they initially spread the plague through tainted grain shipments from Andorhal, they now primarily rely on massive plague cauldrons situated across Plaguelands farms. Cultist cauldron lords brew foul reagents to continuously release noxious clouds of blight that choke the land and raise fallen corpses.",al={"how plague spreads","plague cauldrons","how does cult attack"},it="threat"},{q="What is the cult's relationship to the Forsaken?",a="They are bitter enemies. The Forsaken broke free from the Lich King's telepathic command and seek vengeance against the Scourge. Cultists view the Forsaken as stolen property and heretics, constantly seeking to annihilate them or reclaim the ruins of Lordaeron for the Frozen Throne.",al={"cult vs forsaken","forsaken cult of the damned"},it="conflict"},{q="What is the Cult of the Damned?",a="The Cult of the Damned is a clandestine network of living mortals who serve the Lich King and act as the mortal foundation of the Scourge. Composed primarily of fanatical humans alongside members of other races, they infiltrate settlements, study dark necromancy, and work tirelessly to bring death and plague to the living kingdoms in exchange for promises of immortality and power.",al={"what is cult of the damned","who are the cult of the damned","cult of the damned identity"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Who leads the Cult of the Damned?",a="The cult is commanded on Azeroth by the archlich Kel'Thuzad, the former Dalaran archmage who founded the order. Above him reigns the Lich King himself, bound to the Frozen Throne in Northrend, whom all cultists revere as their dark lord and god.",al={"who leads cult of the damned","cult of the damned leader","who is the leader of the cult"},it="leader",add=true},{q="Who are the Cult of the Damned allied with?",a="The cult works directly for the Lich King and serves alongside the legions of the undead Scourge. While they once facilitated the arrival of the Burning Legion during the Third War, their absolute devotion belongs to the throne of Northrend and its lich commander, Kel'Thuzad.",al={"cult of the damned allies","who does the cult work for","who is cult of the damned allied with"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Where did the Cult of the Damned come from?",a="Years before the Third War, the Lich King telepathically reached out from Northrend to Archmage Kel'Thuzad, tempting him with necromantic power. Kel'Thuzad returned to Lordaeron, using his fortune and demagoguery to recruit disillusioned commoners and power-hungry nobles alike. Operating in secret out of Caer Darrow's catacombs, the cult developed the plague of undeath, distributed tainted grain throughout Andorhal and northern Lordaeron, and paved the way for the kingdom's catastrophic downfall.",al={"cult of the damned history","history of cult of the damned","how did the cult start"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why are the Cult of the Damned hostile to travelers?",a="The cultists view all living people outside their ranks as raw materials for their necromantic experiments or future soldiers for the Scourge. They attack outsiders on sight to guard their clandestine laboratories, protect their plague cauldrons, and eliminate anyone who could disrupt their dark rituals.",al={"why are cultists hostile","why does the cult attack you","cult of the damned hostile"},it="hostile",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="Every human remembers the collapse of Lordaeron; the Cult of the Damned serves as a bitter reminder that humanity was not conquered by demons alone, but by traitors from within their own cities and estates.",["race:Undead"]="To the Forsaken, cultists are pathetic lapdogs who willingly sacrifice their freedom to the master whose mental chains the Forsaken fought so hard to shatter.",["class:Paladin"]="The Cult of the Damned represents the ultimate blasphemy against the Holy Light, wielding foul necromancy to corrupt the living and disturb the eternal rest of the dead.",["class:Mage"]="The cult is a painful stain on the honor of the Kirin Tor, having been founded by one of Dalaran's own esteemed archmages, Kel'Thuzad.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance scouts and soldiers must remain constantly vigilant, as cult infiltrators still attempt to sow plague and chaos inside living cities like Stormwind."},kw={"andorhal","caer darrow","cauldron lord","cult of the damned","kel'thuzad","lich king","plague of undeath","plaguelands","scholomance","scourge"},rel={"subzone:scholomance","subzone:andorhal","subzone:caer-darrow","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:scourge","topic:lich-king","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:forsaken"},src={"Cult of the Damned"}}
E["topic:dark-iron-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Dark Iron clan",z="general",s="The Dark Iron clan is an ancient, ash-skinned dwarven faction ruling from the volcanic depths of Blackrock Mountain. Cast out of Ironforge during the War of the Three Hammers, they accidentally summoned Ragnaros the Firelord, who enslaved their empire. Today, under Emperor Dagran Thaurissan, they wage shadow wars against both the Alliance and the Horde while serving their fiery elemental master.",h="The Dark Iron clan is an ancient, ash-skinned dwarven faction ruling from the volcanic depths of Blackrock...",sec={{t="The War of the Three Hammers",b="Long ago, three dwarven factions lived together in Ironforge under High King Modimus Anvilmar. When the king died, civil war erupted. The Bronzebeards defeated both rival factions, casting out the Wildhammers and Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan's Dark Irons. Vowing vengeance, Thaurissan established a settlement in the Redridge Mountains while his sorceress wife, Modgud, led an army against Grim Batol. When both assaults failed and the united Bronzebeard and Wildhammer armies marched on his stronghold, Thaurissan attempted to unleash an apocalyptic subterranean entity to save himself. His ritual breached the core of the world and freed Ragnaros the Firelord.",sp=0},{t="Shattered Lands and Bondage",b="The rebirth of Ragnaros violently shattered the surrounding peaks, carving out the blazing caldera of Blackrock Mountain flanked by the Searing Gorge and the Burning Steppes. The sorcerer-thane perished in the cataclysm, and the surviving Dark Irons found themselves bound into perpetual servitude to the Firelord. Over centuries within the lower chambers of the mountain, they constructed the sprawling subterranean capital of Shadowforge City. Their artisans and sorcerers became masters of stone golems, dark sorceries, and steam-driven siege machinery, weaponizing the molten elements around them under the gaze of flamewakers and elemental overlords.",sp=0},{t="Spreading Shadow",b="In 25 ADP, Emperor Dagran Thaurissan rules the clan from his Imperial Seat deep in Blackrock Depths. While secretly plotting to overthrow Ragnaros using the combined strength of all dwarven lands, Thaurissan obeys the Firelord's commands to prepare for total war. Dark Iron infiltrators and raiding parties strike far from their mountain, launching incursions into Loch Modan, Dun Morogh, the Badlands, and the Wetlands. Their forces and an ambassador can even be found inside Gnomeregan, though their precise role in its fall is unclear.",sp=1},{t="The Stolen Princess",b="Tensions reached a boiling point when Princess Moira Bronzebeard, daughter and sole heir of King Magni, was taken by Dark Iron agents while traveling to Lakeshire. Magni believes his daughter is an enchanted hostage held against her will in Blackrock Depths. Rumors filtering out of Shadowforge City paint a far more complex picture: the princess has become the emperor's bride, embracing a place of power among the very dwarves her father despises.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where is the Dark Iron capital located?",a="Their capital is Shadowforge City, carved deep within the subterranean caverns of Blackrock Depths beneath Blackrock Mountain. It features enormous stonework, Senate chambers, barracks, and the emperor's Imperial Seat.",al={"where do dark iron dwarves live","dark iron home","dark iron capital name","shadowforge city location"},it="place"},{q="Why do the Dark Iron dwarves have dark skin and glowing eyes?",a="Dark Iron dwarves have ash-gray or dark skin and glowing red eyes. They live beneath Blackrock Mountain, but the precise cause of their appearance is not established.",al={"why do dark iron look different","dark iron dwarf appearance","why are they ashen"},it="origin"},{q="Who is Emperor Dagran Thaurissan?",a="Emperor Dagran Thaurissan is the direct descendant of the original Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan. He rules the Dark Iron clan from Blackrock Depths and answers directly to the elemental lord Ragnaros, though he privately dreams of breaking free from the Firelord's bondage.",al={"dagran thaurissan","who leads dark iron clan","dark iron king"},it="leader"},{q="Why did the Dark Iron clan summon Ragnaros?",a="During the War of the Three Hammers, Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan faced certain defeat when the combined armies of Ironforge and Grim Batol marched upon his city. Desperate for a weapon to destroy his enemies, he performed an ancient ritual that accidentally released the slumbering Firelord, shattering the mountains and enslaving his people.",al={"how was ragnaros summoned","thaurissan ragnaros summon","why summoned firelord"},it="motive"},{q="What is the Thorium Brotherhood?",a="The Thorium Brotherhood is a splinter faction of Dark Iron craftsmen who broke away from the clan and the Firelord's dominion. Based out of Thorium Point in Searing Gorge, they trade with outsiders and seek their own fortunes, earning the permanent enmity of their former kin.",al={"thorium brotherhood dark iron","are thorium brotherhood dark irons","friendly dark iron dwarves"}},{q="Why are Dark Iron dwarves fighting in Gnomeregan?",a="Dark Iron forces, including the Dark Iron Ambassador, can be found inside fallen Gnomeregan. Their presence suggests involvement with Sicco Thermaplugg, but their precise role in the disaster is unclear.",al={"dark iron in gnomeregan","dark iron ambassador gnomeregan","why are dark irons with troggs"},it="why_here"},{q="Are the Dark Irons allied with the Blackrock orcs?",a="No. While they once tolerated the Old Horde occupying the upper spire, the Dark Irons and the Blackrock orcs of the upper levels now fight a vicious turf war. The Dark Irons hold the depths, while Rend Blackhand's orcs control the upper citadel.",al={"dark iron orc alliance","dark iron vs blackrock clan","who owns blackrock mountain"},it="conflict"},{q="Why did the Dark Irons attack Grim Batol?",a="During the War of the Three Hammers, Sorceress Modgud led a Dark Iron army to destroy the Wildhammer clan at Grim Batol. Although Modgud was slain by Khardros Wildhammer, her foul sorceries cursed the fortress so deeply that the Wildhammers could never live there again.",al={"modgud grim batol","why grim batol abandoned","curse of grim batol"},it="history"},{q="What is the Dark Iron clan?",a="The Dark Iron clan is one of the three primary dwarven factions, alongside the Bronzebeards and Wildhammers. Renowned for their sorcery, ruthless ambition, and master stonework, they dwell deep within Blackrock Mountain under their capital of Shadowforge City. Bound in servitude to the Firelord Ragnaros, they wage war against both the Alliance and the Horde.",al={"who are dark irons","dark iron dwarves","what are dark irons"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are Dark Iron dwarves hostile to travelers?",a="Driven by centuries of bitter hatred toward Ironforge and ensnared by the fiery tyranny of Ragnaros, the Dark Irons see all outsiders as enemies or tools. Under the Firelord's commands, Emperor Dagran Thaurissan is preparing his armies for total conquest, launching aggressive raids into neighboring lands and slaughtering anyone who trespasses on their territory.",al={"why do dark irons attack","dark iron hostility","are dark irons evil"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who are the Dark Iron clan allied with?",a="The Dark Iron clan serves Ragnaros the Firelord and his elemental armies, bound in harsh servitude ever since their sorcerers mistakenly summoned him. While they maintain a treacherous rivalry with the Blackrock orcs occupying the upper spire, their forces are also present inside fallen Gnomeregan, though their precise role there is unclear.",al={"dark iron allies","who do dark irons work for","dark iron masters"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="For a Bronzebeard, the Dark Irons are ancestral traitors and sorcerers whose ancient grudges still threaten Ironforge's borders from Loch Modan to Dun Morogh.",["race:Gnome"]="The presence of a Dark Iron Ambassador deep inside fallen Gnomeregan raises suspicions about the clan’s involvement in its fall.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Dark Iron smiths are unmatched masters of fiery metallurgy; their specialized Dark Iron anvils and forges deep in Blackrock Depths are required to craft legendary armaments.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance views the Dark Irons as an urgent existential threat, not only for their constant border raids, but for the kidnapping of Ironforge's crown princess Moira Bronzebeard.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde actively clashes with the Dark Iron armies across the Badlands and the Burning Steppes as both seek supremacy over the ruins surrounding Blackrock Spire."},kw={"blackrock","bronzebeard","dark iron","flamelash","modgud","moira","ragnaros","shadowforge","sorcerer-thane","thaurissan"},rel={"topic:dark-iron-dwarf","subzone:blackrock-mountain","topic:bronzebeard-clan","topic:wildhammer-clan","zone:searinggorge","zone:burningsteppes","npc:magni-bronzebeard","npc:dark-iron-ambassador"},src={"Dark Iron clan"}}
E["topic:dark-iron-dwarf"] = {t="topic",n="Dark Iron dwarf",z="general",s="Dark Iron dwarves are a distinct dwarven sub-race distinguished by their ashen skin, blazing red eyes, and innate affinity for fire. Banished from Ironforge following the civil War of the Three Hammers, they dwell deep within Blackrock Mountain where their society has fallen under the tyrannical rule of the Firelord, Ragnaros.",h="Dark Iron dwarves are a distinct dwarven sub-race distinguished by their ashen skin, blazing red eyes, and...",sec={{t="Fiery Heritage and Exile",b="Unlike their Bronzebeard and Wildhammer cousins, Dark Iron dwarves possess dark gray or soot-black skin and eyes that burn like glowing embers. Their history fractured during the War of the Three Hammers, when their Sorcerer-thane Thaurissan sought to conquer Ironforge. Defeated and driven south into the Redridge Mountains, Thaurissan attempted to summon dark powers to avenge his losses, inadvertently releasing Ragnaros the Firelord from the Elemental Plane. The catastrophic summoning shattered the surrounding peaks into the volcanic waste of Blackrock Mountain and bound the Dark Iron race into fiery servitude.",sp=0},{t="Under the Firelord's Shadow",b="Life in the deep subterranean city of Shadowforge is brutal and unforgiving. While the Dark Irons were once merely ambitious and cunning, generations beneath Blackrock Mountain under the oppressive aura of Ragnaros have warped much of their culture into cruelty, paranoia, and tyranny. Dark Iron law knows only one sentence for infractions: trial by combat in the brutal Ring of the Law. Poisoning and political intrigue are so prevalent that Dark Iron travelers customarily pack their own rations rather than trust food from strangers.",sp=0},{t="Splinters and Outcasts",b="Not all Dark Iron dwarves serve Emperor Dagran Thaurissan and the cult of Ragnaros. Dissident factions have broken away to pursue their own ends. The most prominent among them is the Thorium Brotherhood, a guild of master smiths based out of Thorium Point in Searing Gorge who have defected from the main clan to hire outside adventurers against their former masters. Others operate as mercenaries, spies, or trade engineers alongside neutral organizations like the Venture Company.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do Dark Iron dwarves have gray skin and red eyes?",a="Their dark gray skin and fiery, glowing red eyes are natural traits that distinguish them from mountain and hill dwarves. Their deep subterranean lifestyle and centuries living in proximity to the elemental fires of Ragnaros have further emphasized their volcanic nature.",al={"dark iron appearance","why do dark irons look like that","red eyes dark iron"},it="origin"},{q="Are all Dark Iron dwarves evil?",a="Not necessarily. Although imperial Dark Iron society is treacherous and ruled by the will of Ragnaros, historical figures like the legendary architect Franclorn Forgewright and groups like the rebel Thorium Brotherhood demonstrate honor, valor, and independent will apart from the Firelord's corruption.",al={"are all dark irons bad","good dark iron dwarves","can dark irons be good"},it="people"},{q="Where is the capital of the Dark Iron dwarves?",a="Their civilization is centered in Shadowforge City, deep inside the subterranean caverns of Blackrock Depths within Blackrock Mountain. From there, Emperor Dagran Thaurissan oversees his empire under the shadow of Molten Core.",al={"dark iron capital","where do dark irons live","dark iron city"},it="place"},{q="What is the Ring of the Law?",a="The Ring of the Law is the arena where Dark Iron justice is meted out. In Dark Iron society, the penalty for crimes against the clan is death. Accused individuals are forced to pay the blood price by fighting waves of vicious beasts and gladiators, a trial virtually no prisoner ever survives.",al={"ring of the law","dark iron arena","shadowforge trial"}},{q="What is the Thorium Brotherhood?",a="The Thorium Brotherhood is a rogue faction of Dark Iron craftsmen who broke away from the service of Ragnaros. Camped at Thorium Point in Searing Gorge, they trade their unrivaled knowledge of fiery metallurgy and Dark Iron smithing secrets to adventurers willing to undermine the imperial clan.",al={"thorium brotherhood lore","who are the thorium brotherhood","thorium point dwarves"}},{q="What are Dark Iron dwarves?",a="The Dark Irons are a distinct breed of dwarves, predominantly hailing from the Dark Iron clan. Known for their ashen skin, fiery red eyes, and mastery over sorcery and craft, they possess fierce determination and notorious tempers. While most dwell in their subterranean domain beneath Blackrock Mountain, some belong to independent factions like the Thorium Brotherhood or mercenary outfits.",al={"who are dark irons","dark iron dwarves","what is dark iron dwarf"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are Dark Iron dwarves hostile to travelers?",a="Centuries of isolation, internal treachery, and the corrupting presence of the Firelord, Ragnaros, have left the Dark Iron clan manipulative, cruel, and deeply suspicious of outsiders. They view transgressions against their clan with extreme severity, punishing trespassers and criminals with death or bloody combat in the Ring of the Law.",al={"why do dark irons attack","dark iron dwarves aggressive","why are dark irons hostile"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who leads the Dark Iron dwarves?",a="The Dark Iron clan is ruled by Emperor Dagran Thaurissan from his throne in Shadowforge City, deep within Blackrock Depths. Beside him sits Moira Bronzebeard, the daughter of King Magni Bronzebeard of Ironforge.",al={"dark iron dwarf leader","who is emperor of dark irons","dark iron ruler"},sp=1,it="leader",add=true},{q="Who are the Dark Iron dwarves allied with or serving?",a="Most of the Dark Iron clan is bound in servitude to the elemental Firelord, Ragnaros, whose fiery influence permeates their society. Many also operate alongside elemental forces and dark cults, while independent splinter groups like the Thorium Brotherhood work against the clan's leadership.",al={"who do dark irons work for","dark iron allies","dark irons ragnaros"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Where did the Dark Iron dwarves come from?",a="The Dark Irons were once one of the three ruling dwarven clans of Ironforge. Following the War of the Three Hammers, their sorcerer-thane Thaurissan fled south and inadvertently summoned Ragnaros the Firelord into Azeroth. The cataclysm shattered the Redridge Mountains, creating Blackrock Mountain and chaining the Dark Iron clan to the Firelord's will.",al={"dark iron dwarf origin","history of dark irons","war of the three hammers"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="For the Bronzebeard dwarves of Ironforge, the Dark Irons represent an enduring family feud turned existential threat, dating back to their bloody rebellion during the War of the Three Hammers.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Dark Iron artisans are legendary smiths who hold exclusive mastery over Dark Iron ore, requiring the searing heat of Blackrock Mountain's Black Forge to melt and shape.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance scouts watch the Dark Irons warily, recognizing their imperial armies as an aggressive, expansionist menace pressing right at the southern gates of Ironforge's territory.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde views the Dark Irons as dangerous zealots occupying Blackrock Mountain, directly threatening Horde supply routes through the Badlands and adjacent passes."},kw={"blackrock depths","dark iron","dwarf","ironfoe","ragnaros","shadowforge","thaurissan","thorium brotherhood"},rel={"topic:dark-iron-clan","topic:dwarf","subzone:blackrock-mountain","subzone:thorium-point","zone:searinggorge","subzone:grim-batol","zone:ironforge","topic:venture-company"},src={"Dark Iron dwarf"}}
E["topic:darkspear-tribe"] = {t="topic",n="Darkspear tribe",z="multi",s="The Darkspear are a tribe of jungle trolls who joined Thrall's Horde after being driven from their ancestral home in Stranglethorn Vale and rescued from a sea witch in the Great Sea. Led by Shadow Hunter Vol'jin from their temporary refuge at Sen'jin Village, they now serve as core members of the Horde across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms. Despite their alliance, they currently struggle to reclaim their new home, the Echo Isles, from the traitorous witch doctor Zalazane.",h="The Darkspear are a tribe of jungle trolls who joined Thrall's Horde after being driven from their ancestral...",sec={{t="Exile and Salvation",b="Originally a smaller, less ruthless tribe within the broken Gurubashi Empire of Stranglethorn Vale, the Darkspear endured centuries of warfare and famine against brutal neighbors like the Bloodscalp and Skullsplitter tribes. Driven out to sea, they settled on remote islands, only to face aquatic murlocs, Kul Tiran sailors, and the naga sea witch Zar'jira. Their elder chieftain, Sen'jin, was captured and slain in dark sacrificial rites beneath the sea, but before dying he foretold that the orc Warchief Thrall would lead the Darkspear to a new destiny. Rescued by Thrall's passing fleet, the survivors pledged their undying loyalty to the new Horde and sailed westward to Kalimdor.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of the Echo Isles",b="Upon reaching Durotar, the Darkspear established a home on the tropical Echo Isles just off the mainland coast. That peace was short-lived. First, Admiral Daelin Proudmoore's fleet drove them temporarily into the swamps of Dustwallow Marsh before Horde forces destroyed the invaders. Worse betrayal followed from within. A prominent witch doctor named Zalazane embraced twisted voodoo, seizing control of the minds of his kin. To preserve his free people, Sen'jin's son and successor, Vol'jin, ordered another evacuation. Today, the Darkspear dwell in the coastal encampment of Sen'jin Village, sending champions across the water to slay Zalazane and release their thrall-bound brothers.",sp=1},{t="Voodoo and the New Horde",b="Unlike their cannibalistic jungle kin, the Darkspear have adapted rapidly to the Horde's values of honor, establishing enclaves like Malaka'jin in the Stonetalon Mountains, Shadowprey Village in Desolace, and Zoram'gar Outpost in Ashenvale. In Orgrimmar, they make their spiritual home within the Valley of Spirits, where shadow hunters and witch doctors commune with the loa. Though respected for their scouts, bat riders, and powerful hexes, old traditions run deep; some ancient rituals frowned upon by their allies are still practiced quietly in the dark.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are the Darkspear trolls in the Horde?",a="During the Third War, Warchief Thrall's fleet took shelter on the Darkspear islands during a violent storm. When the naga sea witch Zar'jira and her murlocs captured the trolls and orcs alike, Thrall freed them. Though Chieftain Sen'jin died in the escape, his dying vision foresaw Thrall guiding the Darkspear to safety. In gratitude, Sen'jin's son Vol'jin swore the tribe's allegiance to the Horde.",al={"why trolls horde","darkspear thrall origin","how did trolls join horde"},it="allies"},{q="Who leads the Darkspear tribe right now?",a="The tribe is led by Vol'jin, the son of the revered fallen chieftain Sen'jin. Vol'jin is a cunning shadow hunter and one of Warchief Thrall's closest, most trusted confidants, advising the Horde from the Valley of Spirits in Orgrimmar and overseeing the struggle to reclaim the Echo Isles.",al={"darkspear leader","who is troll leader","troll chieftain"},it="leader"},{q="Why do the Darkspear live in Sen'jin Village instead of the Echo Isles?",a="The Echo Isles were meant to be the tribe's permanent sanctuary in Durotar, but they were betrayed from within. A power-mad witch doctor named Zalazane used dark magic to enslave the minds of his fellow trolls. Rather than slaughter their own enchanted kin, Vol'jin ordered the free Darkspear to retreat to the mainland shore, founding Sen'jin Village as a base to fight back.",al={"why senjin village","what happened to echo isles","why are echo isles hostile"},it="why_here"},{q="Where did the Darkspear originally come from?",a="The Darkspear originated in the jungles of Stranglethorn Vale as part of the fractured Gurubashi Empire. Being smaller and less merciless than rival tribes like the Bloodscalp and Skullsplitter, they suffered constant warfare and starvation until they were driven out of the Eastern Kingdoms entirely.",al={"darkspear original home","darkspear stranglethorn","where are darkspear from"},it="history"},{q="Do Darkspear trolls still practice cannibalism?",a="Officially, no. To live in harmony with Thrall's Horde and honor their alliance, the Darkspear renounced cannibalism and headhunting as tribal policy. However, centuries of jungle heritage do not vanish overnight, and whispers persist that darker traditional rites are still observed in secret far from prying eyes.",al={"are darkspear cannibals","troll cannibalism horde","do horde trolls eat people"}},{q="What deities do the Darkspear worship?",a="The Darkspear revere the loa, powerful primal spirits and animal gods. Their faith centers around a vast pantheon, honored through the voodoo rites of their witch doctors and shadow hunters.",al={"darkspear religion","darkspear gods","troll loa"}},{q="What is the Darkspear tribe?",a="The Darkspear are a tribe of jungle trolls who stand as core members of Thrall's Horde. Once driven out of their ancient lands in Stranglethorn Vale, they survived exile, a perilous sea voyage, and betrayal at home. Known for their adaptability and cunning, they have embraced the Horde's code of honor while maintaining their traditions of shadow magic, voodoo, and loa worship.",al={"who are darkspear trolls","what are the darkspear","darkspear tribe"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Troll"]="As a Darkspear troll, you carry the heavy weight of an exiled people rebuilding on foreign shores. Every blow struck against Zalazane on the Echo Isles or against your brutal Gurubashi cousins in Stranglethorn Vale helps secure your tribe's survival.",["race:Orc"]="The bond between orc and troll was forged in the blood of the Third War. Thrall considers Vol'jin his closest brother-in-arms, and the Darkspear's scouts and witch doctors serve faithfully beside the warrior clans of Durotar.",["faction:Horde"]="From bat riders defending the skies to herbalists brewing draughts in Stonetalon, the Darkspear provide mystical cunning and stealth that balance the raw martial might of the Horde.",["class:Shaman"]="Darkspear shamans bridge the ancient worship of primal loa spirits with the elemental teachings learned from their orc and tauren allies, with figures like Je'neu Sancrea representing the tribe within the Earthen Ring."},kw={"darkspear","echo isles","gurubashi","loa","malaka'jin","sen'jin","shadowprey","vol'jin","zalazane"},rel={"npc:vol-jin","subzone:sen-jin-village","subzone:echo-isles","quest:826","subzone:valley-of-spirits","topic:troll","npc:thrall","topic:horde"},src={"Darkspear tribe"}}
E["topic:defias-brotherhood"] = {t="topic",n="Defias Brotherhood",z="general",s="The Defias Brotherhood is a widespread criminal network of bandits, brigands, and assassins waging an underground insurgency against the Kingdom of Stormwind. Originally formed by the artisans of the Stonemasons Guild who were cheated of their pay for rebuilding the capital, they have devolved into violent outlaws terrorizing the southern farmlands. From their hidden redoubt deep beneath Westfall, they aim to overthrow the corrupt House of Nobles and lay waste to the kingdom.",h="The Defias Brotherhood is a widespread criminal network of bandits, brigands, and assassins waging an...",sec={{t="From Masons to Marauders",b="Following the Second War, the Stonemasons Guild was hired to rebuild Stormwind from smoldering ruins into a grand stone capital. When their masterwork was finished, the House of Nobles refused to deliver the promised payment, citing crushing wartime debt. Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef spoke out on behalf of his workers, demanding fair restitution, but the nobles responded by ordering the Stonemasons Guild disbanded. The ensuing riots in the city streets turned tragic when a stray rock struck and killed Queen Tiffin Wrynn. Overcome with grief and rage, King Varian Wrynn drove the laborers out of Stormwind into exile. Stripped of their livelihood and outlawed, VanCleef and the desperate workers banded together, binding their faces in red silk to represent the blood of the wronged, and turned to highway robbery to claim what they believed they were owed.",sp=0},{t="The Shadow Over Westfall",b="With the royal army overextended, VanCleef turned the neglected farmlands of Westfall into the Brotherhood's base of operations. The bandits overran the hamlet of Moonbrook and commandeered the abandoned subterranean tunnels of the Deadmines. Defias agents seized local mines, driven farmers from their homesteads with reprogrammed harvest golems, and salted the earth to starve the region. With the Crown unable or unwilling to send soldiers to protect rural citizens, local farmers formed The People's Militia out of Sentinel Hill to fight a desperate war of attrition against an enemy that now outmans and outguns them.",sp=0},{t="A Web of Conspiracies",b="The Brotherhood has long outgrown a simple band of displaced laborers. VanCleef recruited mercenary goblins to oversee armaments, struck alliances with Riverpaw gnolls and Elwynn kobolds, and traded with cutthroat pirates like the Bloodsail Buccaneers. Defias highwaymen raid gold shipments across Elwynn Forest, and their agents even established a foothold in Duskwood before unearthing a cursed artifact in Roland's Doom. Far worse, the Brotherhood's influence reaches into the very heart of the capital, enjoying covert support from treacherous aristocrats within the House of Nobles while orchestrating kidnappings that strike at the highest offices of the Alliance.",sp=1},{t="The Juggernaught in the Deadmines",b="Beneath Moonbrook lies the Ironclad Cove, where VanCleef's goblin engineers are secretly assembling an enormous iron-plated warship known as the Defias Juggernaught. The guildmaster intends to sail this floating fortress out through the subterranean coastal waterways and bombard Stormwind City from the sea, reducing his own grand architecture to rubble. The conspiracy extends into the royal court itself: corrupt nobles like Lord Gregor Lescovar serve as eyes and ears for the uprising, while the court machinations of Lady Katrana Prestor deliberately keep the kingdom fractured and blind to the rising fleet beneath Westfall.",sp=2}},faq={{q="Why do the Defias wear red bandanas?",a="The red silk bandana was adopted by Edwin VanCleef and the exiled stonemasons as a symbol of defiance. It represents the blood shed by the oppressed working class who rebuilt Stormwind and were met with broken promises, violence, and exile by the nobility.",al={"defias mask meaning","why red silk","red bandanas meaning"},it="name"},{q="Why is the Brotherhood attacking innocent farmers instead of nobles?",a="While the Brotherhood was founded on genuine grievances against the nobility, years of lawless exile transformed it into a brutal criminal empire. Desperation, greed, and the recruitment of ruthless cutthroats and goblins turned them toward pillaging local homesteads, burning crops, and seizing mines to fund their war machine.",al={"why defias attack farmers","why hurt westfall","defias motive"},it="hostile"},{q="Who is Edwin VanCleef?",a="Edwin VanCleef was the brilliant guildmaster of the Stonemasons Guild who oversaw the reconstruction of Stormwind City after the Second War. Trained in stealth and subterfuge, he founded the Defias Brotherhood after the House of Nobles refused to pay the workers. He now orchestrates their operations from deep within the Deadmines.",al={"who is vancleef","edwin vancleef lore","defias leader"},it="leader"},{q="Why doesn't Stormwind send the army to deal with the Defias?",a="The kingdom's finances are heavily drained, and the Stormwind Army is stretched thin garrisoning outposts in distant territories like Stranglethorn. Political gridlock within the House of Nobles further paralyzes the throne, leaving rural Westfall and Duskwood to defend themselves with volunteer militias.",al={"where is the stormwind army","why no guards in westfall","why people's militia"},sp=2,it="conflict"},{q="What is the People's Militia?",a="The People's Militia is a volunteer defense force organized by Gryan Stoutmantle and the beleaguered farmers of Sentinel Hill. Because Stormwind has provided almost no soldiers to clear the Defias out of Westfall, local citizens took up arms to defend their own land.",al={"peoples militia westfall","gryan stoutmantle militia","sentinel hill army"},it="conflict"},{q="What are the Defias building inside the Deadmines?",a="Hidden within the subterranean cavern known as the Ironclad Cove, goblin engineers and rogue artisans are constructing the Defias Juggernaught, a massive iron-plated war galley. VanCleef intends to sail the warship into Stormwind Harbor and obliterate the capital.",al={"deadmines ship","what are they building deadmines","defias juggernaught"},sp=1,it="threat"},{q="Did the Defias have something to do with the King's disappearance?",a="What became of King Varian Wrynn remains a mystery. He vanished while traveling to a peace summit in Theramore, leaving the child Anduin on the throne under regent rule.",al={"did defias kidnap varian","missing king defias","missing diplomat defias"},sp=2,it="ties"},{q="What is the Defias Brotherhood?",a="The Defias Brotherhood is an extensive criminal syndicate and rebel order made up of former stonemasons, thieves, brigands, and mercenaries. Originally born out of a bitter dispute between the craftsmen who rebuilt Stormwind and the corrupt nobility who refused to pay them, the group has evolved into a violent underground army committed to overthrowing the Kingdom of Stormwind.",al={"what is the defias brotherhood","who are the defias","defias brotherhood overview"},it="identity",add=true,nx={9}},{q="How did the Defias Brotherhood form?",a="After the Second War, the Stonemasons Guild restored Stormwind City from ruins. When the work was complete, the House of Nobles, manipulated by Lady Katrana Prestor and burdened by military debt, refused to pay them. The guildmaster, Edwin VanCleef, led riots in protest, during which Queen Tiffin Wrynn was accidentally struck and killed. Driven into exile by King Varian's fury, the stonemasons took refuge in Westfall and turned to banditry to claim their promised wages, steadily swelling their ranks with criminals and outcasts.",al={"defias origin","history of defias","how did defias start","stonemasons guild defias"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why are the Defias Brotherhood operating in Westfall and Elwynn?",a="The Defias base their primary operations beneath the town of Moonbrook in the Deadmines of Westfall, an impoverished region neglected by Stormwind's military. From there, they spread through Westfall, Elwynn Forest, and Duskwood to seize valuable mines, raid supply lines, and keep Stormwind's stretched defenses preoccupied while they secretly prepare a catastrophic strike against the capital.",al={"why are defias in westfall","why are defias in elwynn","where are defias located"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="Who are the Defias Brotherhood allied with?",a="The Brotherhood has forged ties across the southern lands. In Elwynn Forest, they employ kobolds to secure captured mines, while coordinating with Riverpaw gnolls in Westfall. They have engaged goblin engineers to build deadly harvest golems, dealt with the Bloodsail Buccaneers for maritime expertise, and covertly collaborated with treacherous figures inside the capital, including Lord Gregor Lescovar and Lady Katrana Prestor.",al={"defias allies","who works with defias","defias brotherhood partners"},sp=1,it="allies",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="For humans of the kingdom, the Defias are a bitter reminder of Stormwind's internal rot—a tragedy where honest city builders were turned into cutthroats due to aristocratic greed.",["class:Paladin"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand and church leaders like Duthorian Rall call upon paladins to defend defenseless homesteaders like Daphne Stilwell from ruthless Defias brigands in Westfall.",["class:Rogue"]="Master Mathias Shaw and SI:7 keep close watch on the Defias, as VanCleef's operatives match royal scouts in lockpicking, stealth, and lethal subterranean ambushes.",["faction:Alliance"]="The uprising threatens the stability of the entire kingdom, choking grain supplies from Westfall, destabilizing gold mines, and shaking public faith in Stormwind's leadership."},kw={"bandanas","brotherhood","deadmines","defias","elwynn","juggernaught","moonbrook","stonemasons","vancleef","westfall"},rel={"npc:edwin-vancleef","zone:westfall","zone:deadmines","topic:stonemasons-guild","npc:gryan-stoutmantle","topic:house-of-nobles","subzone:moonbrook","quest:65"},src={"Defias Brotherhood"}}
E["topic:dragonmaw-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Dragonmaw clan",z="general",s="The Dragonmaw clan is an infamous orcish faction notorious for subduing and riding red dragons during the Second War. Guided by dark artifacts and foul magic, they imprisoned the Dragonqueen Alexstrasza beneath the cursed halls of Grim Batol until her liberation shattered their dominion. Today, their scattered remnants lurk in the wetlands and mountains of Khaz Modan, remaining hostile to both the Alliance and Thrall's New Horde.",h="The Dragonmaw clan is an infamous orcish faction notorious for subduing and riding red dragons during the...",sec={{t="Beast Tamers of Draenor",b="Before they ever laid eyes on dragons, the Dragonmaw were known in their native tongue as the Nelghor-shomash, or 'Cry of the Beasts.' Hailing from Draenor, they earned renown by taming the vicious, two-headed rylaks that prowled the rocky wastes, referring to their flying mounts as nelghor, or 'loyal beasts.' When starvation ravaged Draenor following the rise of the Old Horde, the clan was forced to butcher and consume the very creatures they had reared. Upon crossing the Dark Portal during the First War, they transferred that ancient talent for subjugation to the winged terrors of Azeroth.",sp=0},{t="The Demon Soul and Grim Batol",b="Guided by whispers from the black dragon Deathwing, the clan's chieftain, Zuluhed the Whacked, recovered an ancient golden disk from the Redridge Mountains: the Dragon Soul. Zuluhed entrusted this relic to his greatest warlock, Nekros Skullcrusher, who tapped into its fel taint and christened it the Demon Soul. Using the artifact's agonizing power, Nekros ensnared Alexstrasza the Life-Binder herself. The Dragonmaw fortified the abandoned dwarven bastion of Grim Batol, keeping the Dragonqueen chained in darkness and forcing her red dragon brood to serve the Old Horde as aerial dreadnoughts during the Second War.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of the Dragon Riders",b="The Dragonmaw reign ended when the Demon Soul was finally destroyed, freeing Alexstrasza and her flight from their psychic torment. Vengeful red dragons incinerated Nekros Skullcrusher and laid waste to Grim Batol, breaking the clan's back. Routed and terrified, the surviving Dragonmaw scattered into the marshy bogs of the Wetlands and the jagged peaks around Grim Batol. While Zuluhed and a portion of the clan remained behind on shattered Outland, the remnants on Azeroth live as desperate, feral renegades, fiercely guarding their dwindling encampments against dwarven patrols and vengeful dragons alike.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are they called the Dragonmaw if they are from Draenor?",a="Their original Orcish name was Nelghor-shomash, meaning 'Cry of the Beasts.' On Draenor, they called their flying rylaks nelghor, meaning 'loyal beasts.' When they arrived on Azeroth and encountered dragons, they applied the same word to them, and the name acquired its association with dragons. The Orcish name itself did not change.",al={"dragonmaw name origin","orcish name","nelghor-shomash"},it="name"},{q="How did the clan manage to control giant red dragons?",a="They utilized the Demon Soul, an ancient artifact provided indirectly by the black Aspect Deathwing. The warlock Nekros Skullcrusher wielded it to inflict torturous agony on the Dragonqueen Alexstrasza, effectively forcing her and her entire flight to obey under threat of her destruction.",al={"how did they control dragons","demon soul enslavement","how was alexstrasza captured"},it="power"},{q="Are the Dragonmaw part of Thrall's Horde?",a="No. The Dragonmaw were loyal to the Old Horde of Blackhand and his sons. Thrall's New Horde has rejected demon worship and the enslavement of dragons, leaving the Dragonmaw as renegade outcasts hostile to both the Horde and the Alliance.",al={"are dragonmaw in horde","dragonmaw new horde","thrall and dragonmaw"},it="side"},{q="What happened to the clan after the Second War?",a="When the Demon Soul was destroyed, the liberated red dragons turned on the Dragonmaw and destroyed their stronghold at Grim Batol. Nekros was slain, and the surviving orcs fled into the Wetlands and the neighboring mountains, where they remain entrenched as aggressive raiders.",al={"where are the dragonmaw now","dragonmaw after second war","what happened to grim batol"},it="history"},{q="Who is Zuluhed the Whacked?",a="Zuluhed was the elder shaman and chieftain of the Dragonmaw clan who first received the visions of dragon riding from Deathwing. While he sent Nekros to oversee Grim Batol, Zuluhed himself ended up stranded on the remnants of Draenor.",al={"zuluhed","chieftain of dragonmaw","who leads dragonmaw"},it="leader"},{q="Where can Dragonmaw orcs be found right now?",a="On Azeroth, they are primarily encountered in the Wetlands, lurking around sites like Angerfang Encampment and the grim approaches to Grim Batol. They skirmish constantly with the dwarves of Menethil Harbor and the Wildhammer clan.",al={"dragonmaw locations","where to find dragonmaw","dragonmaw wetlands"},it="why_here"},{q="What is the Dragonmaw clan?",a="The Dragonmaw clan is an ancient orcish clan originally known for taming winged rylaks on Draenor. Led into Azeroth during the First War, they gained infamy during the Second War after using the Demon Soul to enslave the Dragonqueen Alexstrasza and force the red dragonflight to fight for the Old Horde. Broken after losing control of their dragons at Grim Batol, they remain a dangerous, renegade remnant in the eastern lands.",al={"who are dragonmaw","dragonmaw clan info","what are dragonmaw"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are the Dragonmaw hostile to travelers?",a="The Dragonmaw are unrepentant remnants of the Old Horde who reject Thrall's peaceful new path. Embittered by the loss of their enslaved dragons and driven from Grim Batol, they survive through violent raiding, clashing fiercely with local Wildhammer dwarves, Alliance travelers, and anyone else who ventures near their fortified encampments in the marshlands.",al={"why dragonmaw attack","are dragonmaw evil","why are dragonmaw hostile"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who are the Dragonmaw clan allied with?",a="On Azeroth, the Dragonmaw stand largely isolated. They broke ties with what became Thrall's Horde and historical allies like the Black Tooth Grin. Instead, the surviving clan members in the Wetlands and Twilight Highlands operate as independent renegades, answering only to their own warlords and viewing all other factions as enemies to be crushed or raided.",al={"dragonmaw allies","who do dragonmaw work for","dragonmaw faction"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance remembers the terrifying rain of fire the Dragonmaw brought upon their fleets and armies during the Second War; any sighting of them in Khaz Modan is met with immediate military response.",["race:Dwarf"]="For dwarves, the Dragonmaw are a desecration: they fouled the ancient fortress of Grim Batol and continue to infest the mountain passes of Khaz Modan.",["race:Orc"]="To the orcs of Thrall's New Horde, the Dragonmaw embody the cruelty and fel corruption of the Old Horde that brought ruin upon their people.",["class:Warlock"]="Warlocks study the history of Nekros Skullcrusher, whose mastery over the Demon Soul proved how fel energies could subjugate even the grandest creatures of Azeroth."},kw={"alexstrasza","deathwing","demon soul","dragon soul","dragonmaw","grim batol","nekros skullcrusher","nelghor-shomash","red dragonflight","wetlands","zuluhed the whacked"},rel={"subzone:grim-batol","zone:wetlands","topic:second-war","topic:old-horde","topic:new-horde","npc:deathwing","zone:redridge","npc:orgrim-doomhammer"},src={"Dragonmaw clan"}}
E["topic:druid"] = {t="topic",n="Druid",z="general",s="Druids are guardians of the natural world who draw power directly from the wild spirits, the celestial bodies, and the Emerald Dream. Master shapeshifters, they adopt the forms of primal beasts to protect the land or mend grievous wounds. Practiced primarily by the night elves and newly revived among the tauren, druids place the preservation of nature above all mortal allegiance.",h="Druids are guardians of the natural world who draw power directly from the wild spirits, the celestial...",sec={{t="Wardens of the Wilds",b="Druidism is far more than a martial art; it is an ancient way of life rooted in deep communion with nature and the demigod Cenarius. Druids wield raw natural energies, commanding roots to entangle their foes, directing the celestial brilliance of the moon and stars, and invoking restorative flora to knit bone and flesh. Their duty is to preserve the cosmic balance and protect the unfettered wild from exploitation and corruption.",sp=0},{t="The Gift of Shapeshifting",b="The defining hallmark of the druid is the supernatural art of shapeshifting. Through spiritual devotion, practitioners channel the primal aspects of Azeroth's beasts. They take on the thick hide of the bear to withstand crushing assaults, slip into the lithe form of the feline predator to strike from the shadows, or adopt aquatic and avian shapes to traverse sea and sky. While transformed, their animal mouths can rarely form mortal words, yet their physical prowess rivals the apex predators of the wild.",sp=0},{t="The Cenarion Circle",b="First taught to Malfurion Stormrage by Cenarius ten thousand years ago, mortal druidism was later formalized under the Cenarion Circle. Headquartered in sacred Moonglade, the Circle watches over druidic practices across Kalimdor, standing independent of mortal factions and political squabbles. While long dominated solely by night elf men who spent centuries slumbering in the Emerald Dream, the aftermath of the Third War saw both night elf women and the tauren welcomed into its fold.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who was the first mortal druid?",a="Malfurion Stormrage was the first mortal to master the druidic arts under the direct tutelage of the demigod Cenarius, roughly ten thousand years ago prior to the War of the Ancients.",al={"first druid","who founded druidism","malfurion first druid"},it="origin"},{q="How can tauren become druids?",a="Following the Third War, the tauren leader Hamuul Runetotem befriended Malfurion Stormrage and learned the ways of the wild, becoming the first tauren druid in nearly twenty generations. Despite protests from Fandral Staghelm, the Keeper Remulos welcomed the tauren into the Cenarion Circle.",al={"tauren druid lore","why are tauren druids","hamuul runetotem druid"},it="people"},{q="What is the Cenarion Circle?",a="The Cenarion Circle is the governing order of druids on Azeroth, founded to regulate wild practices and protect nature. Operating independently from both the Alliance and the Horde, its members answer to the Circle's archdruids and gather peacefully in sacred Moonglade.",al={"cenarion circle lore","what is moonglade order"},it="allies"},{q="Can druids speak while in animal form?",a="Generally, no. When shapeshifted into a beast, a druid's vocal anatomy reflects the animal, making normal mortal speech impossible, although a handful of rare, exceptionally powerful individuals have found magical ways around this limitation.",al={"can druids talk in bear form","shapeshift talking lore"}},{q="What is the Emerald Dream?",a="The Emerald Dream is a verdant, pristine realm that mirrors Azeroth as if it had never been touched by civilization. Night elf druids long entered centuries of hibernation to walk its pathways and commune with the spirits of nature.",al={"emerald dream lore","where do druids sleep"},it="place"},{q="Why were druids originally sleeping for centuries?",a="Following the World Tree Nordrassil's blessing, night elf druids entered the Barrow Dens to spend centuries wandering the Emerald Dream, aiding Ysera's green dragonflight in nurturing Azeroth's spiritual ecosystem until the Third War forced them awake.",al={"druid hibernation","barrow dens sleeping","long slumber"},it="history"},{q="What is a Druid?",a="Druids are guardians of the natural world who dedicate their lives to preserving balance and protecting life. Steeped in traditions dating back millennia, druidism is not simply a school of magic, but an all-encompassing way of living in harmony with the wild. Reclusive and wary of the civilized world, druids attune themselves deeply to the plant and animal kingdoms. Through this sacred bond, they can channel the raw restorative powers of nature, command celestial energies, and adopt the forms of primal beasts to safeguard Azeroth.",al={"what is a druid","who are druids","druid lore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does a Druid's power come from?",a="A druid's power flows from communion with nature itself and the teachings of the demigod Cenarius, Lord of the Forest. By entering a spiritual union with the wild and walking the Emerald Dream—the pristine blueprint of Azeroth—druids draw upon primal living energies. This connection allows them to wield restorative life magic, call upon celestial powers such as the moonlight of Elune or solar radiance, and earn the blessing of animal spirits to reshape their mortal bodies into beasts.",al={"druid magic source","where do druids get power","how does druid magic work"},it="power",add=true},{q="What does it mean to walk the path of a Druid?",a="To take up the path of a druid means looking beyond the transient squabbles of mortal nations to serve the eternal cycles of the wild. You must attune your mind and body to nature, learning to commune with beast spirits and heed the balance of life and death. As you commune with the Cenarion Circle and undertake the sacred trials of your elders, you gain the discipline to mend lethal wounds, call down celestial fury, and undergo the transformative gift of shapeshifting.",al={"becoming a druid","path of the druid","what does a druid do"},it="for_you",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="For ten millennia, kaldorei druids served as the quiet, sleeping guardians of Kalimdor, maintaining nature's balance under the guidance of Malfurion Stormrage and the demigod Cenarius.",["race:Tauren"]="Under Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem, the tauren have rekindled an ancient connection to the wilds, joining the Cenarion Circle in sacred Moonglade to walk the path of the Earth Mother.",["class:Druid"]="As a protector of balance, a druid walks the fine line between animal fury, restorative life magic, and celestial devastation, drawing wisdom directly from Cenarius and the Emerald Dream."},kw={"bear form","cat form","cenarion circle","cenarius","druid","emerald dream","hamuul","malfurion","moonglade","shapeshifter"},rel={"topic:cenarion-circle","topic:cenarius","npc:malfurion-stormrage","topic:emerald-dream","quest:5921","quest:1489","npc:remulos","npc:fandral-staghelm"},src={"Druid"}}
E["topic:druids-of-the-fang"] = {t="topic",n="Druids of the Fang",z="general",s="The Druids of the Fang are a splinter sect of night elf druids who succumbed to corruption deep within the Wailing Caverns. Originally disciples of the visionary Archdruid Naralex, they abandoned the Cenarion Circle after their master's connection to the Emerald Dream turned into a foul nightmare. Under the self-proclaimed Lord Serpentis, they now wield serpent-like forms and seek to remake the Barrens into their own twisted paradise.",h="The Druids of the Fang are a splinter sect of night elf druids who succumbed to corruption deep within the...",sec={{t="A Noble Vision Turned Poisonous",b="The Barrens was not always an arid wasteland, and Archdruid Naralex sought to return it to the lush, verdant sanctuary it once was. Naralex led a dedicated order of young druids into the subterranean depths of the Wailing Caverns, tapping into the natural underground fissures and subterranean springs. By entering an induced slumber, Naralex hoped to channel the living energies of the Emerald Dream into the waterways, nursing the scorched savannah above back to vibrant life.",sp=0},{t="Descent into Nightmare",b="Something went horribly awry inside Naralex's trance. Instead of channeling the pristine serenity of the Emerald Dream, Naralex’s slumber was infiltrated by a malevolent, twisting nightmare. This darkness seeped outwards through the caverns, rapidly warping the resident wildlife and poisoning the underground springs. Rather than fleeing to seek aid from the Cenarion Circle, Naralex's acolytes were overwhelmed by the nightmare's influence, their minds and spirits mutating alongside the subterranean ecosystem.",sp=1},{t="The Fang Ascendant",b="Naralex's prize pupil, once a dedicated student, cast aside his master's original teachings and embraced the corruption under the title of Lord Serpentis. Rallying the fallen acolytes to form the Druids of the Fang, Serpentis adopted the primal, venomous aspects of vipers and serpents rather than the traditional bear and cat forms sanctioned by Cenarius. The cult effectively imprisoned their slumbering archdruid, intent on feeding on his nightmare to violently remold the Barrens into their own serpent-ruled paradise.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who are the Druids of the Fang?",a="They are a corrupted order of night elf druids who entered the Wailing Caverns under Archdruid Naralex. Instead of healing the Barrens, they were driven mad when Naralex's trance tapped into a dark nightmare, transforming them into fanatical snake-worshiping renegades.",al={"druids of the fang lore","who are the fang druids","wailing caverns druids"},it="identity"},{q="Why did they enter the Wailing Caverns originally?",a="Naralex and his acolytes sought to tap into the cavern's rich subterranean springs. By channeling the lush vitality of the Emerald Dream into the water system, they hoped to turn the arid Barrens back into a thriving, green forest.",al={"why are they in wailing caverns","naralex goal","why heal the barrens"},it="why_here"},{q="Who leads the Druids of the Fang?",a="They are led by Lord Serpentis. Once the foremost and brightest student of Archdruid Naralex, Serpentis fell wholly into madness when the nightmare struck, declaring himself the leader of the newly formed Druids of the Fang.",al={"leader of the fang","lord serpentis lore","who leads them"},it="leader"},{q="What animal forms do they take?",a="Unlike traditional druids of the Cenarion Circle who adopt the forms of bears, cats, or stormcrows, the Druids of the Fang draw on venomous serpents and vipers, embracing serpentine magic and vicious snake transformations.",al={"snake form druids","fang druid shapeshift","serpent form"}},{q="How does the Cenarion Circle view them?",a="The Cenarion Circle views the Druids of the Fang as a dangerous aberration and a cult. Leaders like Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem and Naralex's remaining loyalists work urgently to eliminate the leaders of the Fang and cleanse the corruption before it destroys the land.",al={"cenarion circle opinion","are they part of the cenarion circle","hamuul on the fang"}},{q="What happened to Naralex?",a="Naralex remains trapped in a deep, agonizing slumber within the heart of the Wailing Caverns. His disciples keep him confined, using his ongoing nightmare as the power source to warp the caverns and their own magic.",al={"is naralex dead","naralex status","where is naralex"}},{q="Why are the Druids of the Fang hostile?",a="When Archdruid Naralex tapped into the Emerald Dream to heal the Barrens, his vision decayed into an encroaching nightmare. That nightmare warped the minds and spirits of his disciples, driving them mad. Now twisted into the Druids of the Fang, they violently attack any intruders to protect their corrupted domain and force the surrounding world to reflect their nightmarish visions.",al={"why do druids of the fang attack","why are druids of the fang aggressive","druids of the fang enemies"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="How did the Druids of the Fang come to exist?",a="They were originally a noble order of night elf druids who followed Archdruid Naralex into the subterranean springs of the Wailing Caverns. Their mission was benevolent: channel the Emerald Dream to restore lush life to the arid Barrens. However, the Dream twisted into madness around Naralex, corrupting his followers and turning his finest student, Lord Serpentis, toward dark fanatical devotion.",al={"druids of the fang origin","how did druids of the fang start","history of druids of the fang"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why are they called the Druids of the Fang?",a="Rather than embracing traditional druidic forms like bears or cats, these fallen druids embraced the aspect of the serpent. Under the guidance of Lord Serpentis and their serpentine leaders, they shapeshift into vipers, employ deadly venom, and keep lethal reptiles as companions, abandoning traditional Cenarion paths to embody the venomous fang.",al={"why druids of the fang","druids of the fang name meaning","why the fang name"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The Druids of the Fang represent the grave peril of the Emerald Dream being corrupted, demonstrating what happens when a druid abandons Cenarion harmony for twisted, unorthodox animal forms.",["race:Tauren"]="Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem and the tauren druids of Thunder Bluff are deeply invested in ending the Fang's threat, viewing their defilement of the Barrens' water as an affront to the Earth Mother.",["race:NightElf"]="Seeing kaldorei brethren degenerate into a feral, nightmare-ridden snake cult in the heart of Kalimdor is a bitter sorrow and embarrassment for night elf traditions."},kw={"barrens","druids of the fang","emerald dream","hamuul runetotem","naralex","nightmare","serpentis","snake form","wailing caverns"},rel={"npc:lord-serpentis","npc:naralex","zone:wailing","quest:914","topic:emerald-dream","topic:druid","quest:1489","zone:barrens"},src={"Druids of the Fang"}}
E["topic:dwarf"] = {t="topic",n="Dwarf",z="general",s="Dwarves are a stout, resilient race carved from the living stone of ancient titan-forged earthen, now inhabiting the snow-capped mountain kingdoms of Khaz Modan and beyond. Known for their mastery of stonework, metallurgy, and devastating frontline combat, they stand as one of the Alliance's fiercest and most dependable pillars. Under the reign of King Magni Bronzebeard, their sprawling subterranean capital of Ironforge serves as the beating heart of dwarven society.",h="Dwarves are a stout, resilient race carved from the living stone of ancient titan-forged earthen, now...",sec={{t="Sons of the Mountain",b="Long ago, the subterranean titan-forged known as earthen awoke from an eons-long slumber within the vaults of Uldaman, transformed by the Curse of Flesh into beings of blood and bone. Taking the name dwarves, they reclaimed the high crags of Khaz Modan—named in honor of the titan Khaz'goroth—and carved the majestic subterranean stronghold of Ironforge beneath the peaks of Dun Morogh. Endowed with incredible natural fortitude, a sharp eye for stone and ore, and a penchant for roaring hearths and heady ale, dwarves approach craftsmanship and warfare with identical tenacity.",sp=0},{t="The War of the Three Hammers",b="Dwarven unity shattered centuries ago following the death of High King Modimus Anvilmar. Civil war erupted across Ironforge among three rival factions: the Bronzebeard clan, the Wildhammer clan, and the Dark Iron clan. After the Bronzebeards secured the mountain and drove out their kin, Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan of the Dark Irons launched a disastrous war of vengeance against both rivals. Driven to desperation beneath the Redridge Mountains, Thaurissan accidentally summoned Ragnaros the Firelord, immolating his own realm into the volcanic wastes of Searing Gorge and the Burning Steppes. The surviving Dark Irons were enslaved beneath Blackrock Mountain, while the devastated Wildhammers migrated north to Aerie Peak in the Hinterlands.",sp=0},{t="Steadfast Shield of the Alliance",b="When the orcish Horde surged across the continent during the Second War, King Magni Bronzebeard sealed Ironforge's gates against brutal sieges while dwarven forces took up arms alongside the Alliance of Lordaeron. Renowned for heavy plate armor, deadly muskets, and fearless gryphon riders, dwarven warriors played a vital role in shattering the Horde. Today, the Kingdom of Ironforge remains one of the Alliance's most stalwart defenders, welcoming their displaced gnome cousins into their halls while actively unearthing the titan ruins of Khaz Modan to unravel the mysteries of their ancient origins.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where did the dwarves come from originally?",a="Dwarves are descended from the earthen, an ancient race of living stone beings created by the titan keepers. Sealed away inside the subterranean vaults of Uldaman, the earthen succumbed to the Curse of Flesh over millennia, awakening as mortal dwarves who then ventured out to settle the mountains of Khaz Modan.",al={"dwarf origin","dwarf lore","how were dwarves made","earthen"},it="origin"},{q="Who rules the dwarves right now?",a="King Magni Bronzebeard rules the Kingdom of Ironforge and the Bronzebeard clan from the Great Forge. He has led his people through the Second and Third Wars and remains one of the core founding pillars of the Grand Alliance.",al={"who is dwarf king","dwarf leader","king of ironforge","magni bronzebeard"},it="leader"},{q="What are the three main dwarven clans?",a="The three major clans are the Bronzebeards, who rule Ironforge and Dun Morogh; the Wildhammers, who dwell in the open air of the Hinterlands and ride gryphons; and the Dark Irons, who dwell deep beneath Blackrock Mountain under the fiery dominion of Ragnaros.",al={"dwarf clans","three hammers","bronzebeard wildhammer dark iron","clans of ironforge"},it="people"},{q="Why are the Dark Iron dwarves enemies?",a="During the War of the Three Hammers, the Dark Irons attempted to annihilate both the Bronzebeard and Wildhammer clans. Facing defeat, their sorcerer-thane summoned Ragnaros the Firelord, which shattered their homeland and bound the clan in servitude to elemental fire and shadow beneath Blackrock Mountain.",al={"why are dark irons evil","dark iron clan lore","shadowforge city dwarves"},it="hostile"},{q="Why are gnomes living inside Ironforge?",a="The dwarves and gnomes have shared a close bond since their first meeting centuries ago, exchanging stoneworking secrets for mechanical engineering. When Gnomeregan fell to a trogg invasion and toxic radiation, King Magni gave High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque and his refugees asylum in Tinker Town.",al={"why are gnomes in ironforge","ironforge gnomes","what happened to gnomeregan"},it="ties"},{q="Do dwarves worship the Holy Light?",a="Yes. Early human expeditions from the Empire of Arathor introduced the philosophy of the Holy Light to the dwarves centuries ago. Dwarves embraced it wholeheartedly, establishing orders of paladins and priests that rival the devotion of Lordaeron and Stormwind.",al={"dwarf religion","dwarves holy light","do dwarves believe in the light"}},{q="What is the story of the dwarves?",a="Dwarves are a stout, resilient people descended from the earthen, titan-forged beings made of living stone who succumbed to the Curse of Flesh while sleeping within Uldaman. Awakening transformed into creatures of flesh, they carved out the kingdom of Khaz Modan beneath the snow-covered mountains, naming it after the titan Khaz'goroth. Renowned for masterful smithing, engineering, and fierce courage in battle, they survived a bitter civil war among their three great clans and stand today as one of the most stalwart pillars of the Grand Alliance.",al={"dwarf lore","who are the dwarves","history of dwarves"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do the dwarves stand in the world today?",a="Under King Magni Bronzebeard, the Kingdom of Ironforge holds Khaz Modan and remains a staunch cornerstone of the Alliance alongside Stormwind. They have taken in their gnomish allies following the catastrophic fall of Gnomeregan. However, threats press in from every direction: hostile troggs and frost trolls overrun the snowy passes, Dark Iron dwarves scheme from the volcanic depths of Blackrock Mountain, and King Magni's own daughter, Princess Moira, remains missing, feared captive by their subterranean kin.",al={"dwarves current situation","dwarves today","where are dwarves now"},it="today",add=true},{q="What does it mean to be a dwarf adventurer?",a="Stepping out from Coldridge Valley into Dun Morogh, you carry the pride and indomitable grit of your mountain kin. Whether swinging a warhammer, wielding the Holy Light, or hunting across frozen crags, your folk rely on you to defend the kingdom from rampaging troggs, unruly beasts, and treacherous Dark Iron incursions. You also share in the thrill of uncovering your race's forgotten heritage, seeking ancient titan relics buried in ruins like Uldaman to uncover the mystery of your creation.",al={"being a dwarf","dwarf adventurer meaning","playing a dwarf"},it="for_you",rc="Dwarf",add=true}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Every dwarf carries the weight of a stoneborn legacy, navigating ancient blood feuds between clans while excavating titan secrets that define your very existence.",["race:Gnome"]="The dwarves are your oldest and truest allies, having helped build Gnomeregan's initial stonework and now offering your people shelter in Tinker Town.",["faction:Alliance"]="Ironforge's vast mineral wealth, unmatched siege engineering, and unyielding infantry form the northern bulwark of the Alliance.",["class:Paladin"]="Dwarven paladins carry the Holy Light with mountain-hewn resolve, blending human theology with the unbreakable martial discipline of Khaz Modan.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Dwarves are renowned as the finest smiths in Azeroth, utilizing the volcanic heat of the Great Forge to temper legendary arms and armor.",["profession:Mining"]="Born from living stone, dwarves possess an innate eye for veins of rich ore and ancient gemstones buried beneath the subterranean deeps."},kw={"bronzebeard","dark iron","dun morogh","dwarf","dwarves","earthen","ironforge","khaz modan","magni","uldaman","wildhammer"},rel={"zone:ironforge","zone:dunmorogh","npc:magni-bronzebeard","topic:bronzebeard-clan","topic:wildhammer-clan","topic:dark-iron-clan","subzone:uldaman","topic:alliance"},src={"Dwarf"}}
E["topic:earth-mother"] = {t="topic",n="Earth Mother",z="general",s="The Earth Mother is the supreme, benevolent creator deity worshipped by the tauren, revered as the living embodiment and architect of Azeroth's land and elements. In tauren myth, she shaped the mountains, plains, and seasons before sacrificing her physical form to shield her mortal children from dark whispers rising from the earth's depths. Her presence permeates everyday tauren culture, guiding their reverence for nature, duty to the elements, and hopes for the afterlife.",h="The Earth Mother is the supreme, benevolent creator deity worshipped by the tauren, revered as the living...",sec={{t="The Dawn of Creation",b="Tauren creation myths tell that before day and night existed, the Earth Mother braved the void alone, untouched by the whispering shadows of the Old Ones trapped beneath the surface. To provide a safe haven for her coming offspring, she set about shaping Azeroth. Scraping her nails across newly raised mountains sparked the world's first fires, while her hands carved rivers and ocean beds. Her laughter gave breath to the wind, and by lifting the primordial fogs into the sky, she brought the first rains, which coaxed grass, forests, and grain from the soil.",sp=0},{t="Sun, Moon, and the Shu'halo",b="Aided by the friendly elements, the Earth Mother brought forth twins: An'she, the sun, and Mu'sha, the moon. To rest while keeping them safe, she placed the radiant twins into her eyes, creating day, night, and the cycle of the seasons as she opened and closed each lid. Later, she pressed her heart against the fertile earth, breathing the song of life into the land to create the shu'halo, the first tauren. She charged the twins and the elements with teaching the young mortals how to hunt, build, dance, and live in harmony with the wilds.",sp=0},{t="The Great Sorrow and Sacrifice",b="While the Earth Mother slept, the shadowy Old Ones crept into the hearts of some tauren, teaching them hatred, violence, and warfare. Devastated by the corruption of her children, she wept a single tear that became Lo'sho, the Blue Child. Fearful that she might one day fall to the darkness herself, she tore An'she and Mu'sha from her eyes so they would never be tainted, casting them into the sky to watch over the land. She then threw herself across the world, rooting her essence into the soil to trap the shadows forever, sacrificing her mobility so the shu'halo could survive.",sp=0},{t="Living Legacy",b="Although the Earth Mother gave up her physical presence, tauren believe her spirit cradles Azeroth still. Her ear is attuned to the winds, hearing the prayers of her children, and her heart beats within the deep stone. Tauren hunters, shamans, and druids direct their thanks to her for every kill and harvest, believing that all life originates from her embrace and that every tauren's spirit returns to her soil upon death.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is the Earth Mother?",a="The Earth Mother is the benevolent creator deity at the heart of tauren religion. According to their myths, she crafted the physical landscape of Azeroth, birthed the sun and the moon, and created the tauren race from the soil.",al={"what is the earth mother","earthmother lore","tauren god"},it="identity"},{q="What are her eyes in the sky?",a="Her right eye is An'she, the sun, who brings light and warmth to the world. Her left eye is Mu'sha, the White Lady moon, who brings peace and slumber. Tauren myth states she tore them from her brow and placed them in the heavens to protect them from corruption.",al={"anshe and musha","tauren sun and moon","earth mother eyes"},it="power"},{q="What is the Blue Child in tauren myth?",a="The Blue Child, known as Lo'sho, is Azeroth's smaller celestial body. Tauren mythology holds that it was born from a single, radiant tear the Earth Mother wept upon witnessing her children fall to the corrupting whispers of the Old Ones.",al={"losho","blue moon tauren","earth mother tear"},it="origin"},{q="Is the Earth Mother an actual living entity?",a="To the tauren, she is an active, loving spiritual reality whose essence permeates the natural world, stone, and winds. Shamans and druids channel nature and elemental spirits through her blessings, though whether she was a primordial giant, a world-soul, or an allegory remains a matter of faith.",al={"is earth mother real","does earth mother exist","who is the earthmother"}},{q="Do other races worship the Earth Mother?",a="While the tauren are her primary worshippers, other peoples who commune with the land acknowledge her. Some orc shamans and centaur invoke her name respectfully, and her blessings are frequently exchanged throughout the Horde.",al={"orcs earth mother","who worships earth mother","do trolls believe in earth mother"},it="ties"},{q="What do tauren believe happens after death?",a="Tauren believe that because all living things were born from the Earth Mother's loving touch, every soul returns to her upon death. Traditional rites commit the deceased back to the land, trusting their spirits to rest peacefully in her eternal embrace.",al={"tauren afterlife","tauren burial beliefs","returning to the earth mother"},it="history"},{q="How did the Earth Mother create the world and the tauren?",a="According to tauren creation myths, the Earth Mother wandered Azeroth before the dawn of memory and shaped the mountains, seas, and skies to make a refuge from the dark whispers beneath the earth. After birthing her twin children, An'she and Mu'sha, she gave life to the plains and fashioned the first tauren, whom she loved dearly. When malicious shadows corrupted many of her children with war and hatred, she gave of herself completely, pressing her spirit into the earth to hold back the darkness and safeguard the surviving tauren forever.",al={"earth mother creation story","how did earth mother make world","earth mother history"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Every aspect of shu'halo society—from the nomad's respect for prey to the council of chieftains—is rooted in the law, sacrifice, and enduring love of the Earth Mother.",["class:Druid"]="Tauren druids view their sacred bond with nature as a direct communion with the Earth Mother's living body, answering her ancient call to preserve the land's balance.",["class:Shaman"]="Tauren shamans honor the Earth Mother alongside the elemental spirits, remembering that the wind, water, fire, and stone were her first companions.",["faction:Horde"]="Through their alliance with Mulgore, orcs and jungle trolls have grown familiar with tauren reverence for the Earth Mother, often invoking her name as a respectful blessing."},kw={"an'she","creation","earth mother","earthmother","lo'sho","mu'sha","mulgore","shu'halo","tauren"},rel={"topic:tauren","topic:horde","topic:centaur","zone:feralas","quest:860","quest:938","quest:6381","topic:light"},src={"Earth Mother"}}
E["topic:elemental-plane"] = {t="topic",n="Elemental Plane",z="general",s="The Elemental Plane is an interconnected pocket dimension forged by the titan-forged Keepers to imprison Azeroth's chaotic elementals. Divided into four distinct realms—Skywall, Deepholm, the Abyssal Maw, and the Firelands—it confines the primordial spirits away from the mortal world. Though sealed long ago, cracks in its barriers allow violent elemental incursions and catastrophic summonings into Azeroth.",h="The Elemental Plane is an interconnected pocket dimension forged by the titan-forged Keepers to imprison...",sec={{t="A Prison for Primordial Chaos",b="In the ancient days following the fall of the Old Gods, the titan-forged Keepers faced a dilemma: Azeroth's chaotic elementals could not truly be destroyed, as their essences were fundamentally tied to the world. Highkeeper Ra and the sorceress Helya devised an enduring solution by weaving an isolated pocket dimension known as the Elemental Plane. Within it, they crafted four interlinked domains tailored to each primal force: Skywall for air, Deepholm for earth, the Abyssal Maw for water, and the Firelands for fire. The elemental lords and their myriad servants were banished into this realm, and powerful wards were set to sever the prison from the material world, allowing Azeroth's surface to finally know peace.",sp=0},{t="Endless Strife",b="Banishment did not temper the fury of the elementals. Confined within their pocket dimensions, the primordial powers waged endless wars against one another. The most terrible conflict, the Elemental Sundering, pitted the domains of fire and air against each other in a war that raged for millennia across the core of their prison. Meanwhile, a handful of elementals left behind on Azeroth gradually lost their fiery tempestuousness over ages, their forms softening into creatures of flesh and blood.",sp=0},{t="Breaching the Wards",b="The barriers separating Azeroth from the Elemental Plane are formidable, but not unbreakable. Two centuries ago, during the desperate climax of the War of the Three Hammers, Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan pierced the veil to summon Ragnaros the Firelord from the Firelands, an apocalyptic event that ruptured the Redridge Mountains and forged Blackrock Mountain. In recent days, volatile cracks have opened across Kalimdor. Dimensional rifts repeatedly manifest in Silithus, Un'Goro Crater, Azshara, and Winterspring, disgorging waves of hostile elementals eager to test the defenses of the mortal world.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Elemental Plane?",a="The Elemental Plane is an artificial pocket dimension created by the titan-forged to contain the chaotic elementals of Azeroth. It is composed of four distinct domains corresponding to the four primary elements: Skywall for air, Deepholm for earth, the Abyssal Maw for water, and the Firelands for fire.",al={"what is elemental plane","elemental plane explained","elemental realms"},it="identity"},{q="Who created the Elemental Plane and why?",a="Highkeeper Ra and the sorceress Helya crafted the Elemental Plane after the war with the Old Gods. Because the elementals were bound directly to Azeroth and would simply reform if slain, the Keepers imprisoned them within this separate dimension so nature on the surface could flourish in peace.",al={"who made elemental plane","why was elemental plane made","how was elemental plane made"},it="origin"},{q="What are the four realms within the plane?",a="The four interlinked domains are Skywall (the realm of air), Deepholm (the realm of earth), the Abyssal Maw (the realm of water), and the Firelands (the realm of fire). Each realm is shaped as an ideal home and prison for its respective elemental spirits.",al={"four elemental planes","elemental domains","names of elemental realms"},it="place"},{q="Can creatures travel between Azeroth and the Elemental Plane?",a="Travel between realms is strictly barred by ancient wards, but powerful sorcery and dimensional instability can create breaches. Mortal spellcasters have summoned entities across the veil, such as Thaurissan summoning Ragnaros, and volatile rifts periodically tear open in remote regions of Kalimdor.",al={"portal to elemental plane","visiting elemental plane","how to get to elemental plane"},it="power"},{q="Why are elementals invading Kalimdor right now?",a="Periodic incursions have begun erupting across Silithus, Un'Goro Crater, Azshara, and Winterspring. Portals tearing through the veil allow waves of invaders from the Elemental Plane to push into mortal territory until beaten back by the defenders of the Horde and Alliance.",al={"elemental invasions","why are elementals attacking","elemental rifts kalimdor"},it="conflict"},{q="How was Blackrock Mountain created by the plane?",a="During the War of the Three Hammers, Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan attempted to conjure a fiery power to save his clan. He inadvertently tore open a gateway into the Firelands, wrenching Ragnaros into Azeroth. The Firelord's explosive arrival shattered the surrounding mountains, carving out the Burning Steppes, Searing Gorge, and Blackrock Mountain.",al={"thaurissan ragnaros","creation of blackrock mountain","how did ragnaros get here"},it="history"},{q="Who rules the Elemental Plane?",a="The plane has no single ruler, as its four domains are governed independently by the four mighty Elemental Lords: Ragnaros the Firelord commands the Firelands, Therazane the Stonemother rules Deepholm, Al'Akir the Windlord holds Skywall, and Neptulon the Tidehunter oversees the Abyssal Maw. Rather than cooperating, these entities and their armies have spent millenia locked in endless war against one another across the boundaries of their respective realms.",al={"elemental plane leaders","who leads the elemental plane","elemental plane lords"},it="leader",add=true},{q="Who are the elementals allied with?",a="In primordial times, the elementals were bound into service by the Old Gods, warring against the titans and their forged armies on their masters' behalf. In the current age, however, most elementals fight strictly for their respective Elemental Lords. They remain intensely hostile to nearly all mortals, though specific factions or summoners—such as the Dark Iron dwarves who accidentally bound themselves to Ragnaros—occasionally serve or bargain with them.",al={"elemental plane allies","who do elementals serve","elemental allegiances"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Shaman seek harmony with spirits whose ancient rulers remain imprisoned across the veil. Understanding the division between Azeroth and the Elemental Plane is central to grasping why chaotic elements lash out when summoned.",["class:Mage"]="Conjuring elemental familiars requires tapping into the fringe energies of these distant realms, demanding strict control to avoid tearing wider breaches into the material world.",["race:Dwarf"]="The dark history of the Dark Iron clan serves as a grim warning of what happens when mortal ambition pierces the Elemental Plane to summon beings like Ragnaros.",["race:Skyborne"]="Zephras Isle floats within the towering expanse of Skywall, serving as a sanctuary within the windy domain of the Elemental Plane."},kw={"abyssal maw","deepholm","elemental plane","elemental sundering","firelands","helya","highkeeper ra","invasions","ragnaros","skywall"},rel={"topic:titan","topic:old-god","topic:skywall","topic:skyborne","subzone:blackrock-mountain","zone:burningsteppes","zone:searinggorge","npc:highlord-demitrian"},src={"Elemental Plane"}}
E["topic:elune"] = {t="topic",n="Elune",z="general",s="Elune is the revered moon goddess of Azeroth, worshipped primarily by the night elves as their benevolent protector and mother of the forest demigod Cenarius. Associated with the larger moon, the White Lady, she governs peace, healing, and lunar fury across the world. In tauren myth, she is honored as Mu'sha, the gentle left eye of the Earth Mother.",h="Elune is the revered moon goddess of Azeroth, worshipped primarily by the night elves as their benevolent...",sec={{t="The White Lady",b="Elune is one of the few true deities acknowledged across Azeroth, bound inseparably to the night sky and the larger of the world's two moons, the White Lady. While never appearing in a physical form on the world, night elven artisans portray her as a kaldorei of transcendent grace, bathed in luminescence with alabaster skin, silver hair, and eyes of pure moonlight. Ancient legends among the kaldorei claim she once rested in the depths of the Well of Eternity during the daylight hours, whispering wisdom to the early night elves and teaching them the speech of the world.",sp=0},{t="The Sisters and the Forest",b="Night elven spiritual and civil life is anchored in devotion to Elune through the Sisterhood of Elune, an ancient matriarchal priesthood led by High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind. The Sisters invoke Elune's grace to mend lethal wounds, but they can also channel her radiant wrath to call down searing starlight upon their enemies. Her divine union with the Great White Stag, Malorne, produced Cenarius, binding Elune's heavenly mysteries directly to the druidic traditions that tend the wildlands of Kalimdor.",sp=0},{t="Aspects and Names",b="Though renowned for peace, harmony, and tranquil restoration, Elune is not a gentle pacifist. As the Night Warrior, she acts as a fierce patron of battle who gathers the spirits of fallen heroes from the field and places them in the heavens to ride across the night as stars. Beyond the kaldorei, the nomadic tauren know her as Mu'sha, the radiant left eye of the Earth Mother, who illuminates the night and guides wandering hunters alongside her fiery twin brother, An'she the sun.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Elune?",a="Elune is the primary deity of the night elves, a moon goddess associated with healing, nature, peace, and the White Lady—Azeroth's larger moon. She is widely revered as one of the few true divine beings in the world.",al={"what is elune","elune lore","who is the moon goddess"},it="identity"},{q="Has Elune ever appeared in person?",a="Elune has never been observed in physical form on Azeroth. Kaldorei artisans depict her as a radiant, platinum-crowned night elf maiden with long silver hair and glowing moonlight eyes, though her true cosmic shape remains a mystery.",al={"has elune taken physical form","what does elune look like"}},{q="What is the Night Warrior?",a="The Night Warrior is Elune's fiercest aspect. Rather than offering gentle peace and healing, this manifestation represents her righteous wrath in combat, lifting the spirits of courageous fallen warriors into the night sky to ride among the stars.",al={"elune night warrior","wrath of elune"},it="power"},{q="How is Elune related to Cenarius?",a="Elune conceived Cenarius with the wild stag demigod Malorne. Through this union, Cenarius inherited both his mother's profound love for Azeroth's living creatures and his father's deep connection to the natural wild and the Emerald Dream.",al={"is elune cenarius mother","cenarius parents"},it="ties"},{q="Do the tauren worship Elune?",a="Yes, though by a different name. Tauren myth refers to her as Mu'sha, the gentle left eye of the Earth Mother, who watches over the world by night alongside her solar brother, An'she.",al={"musha","tauren moon goddess","who is musha"},it="people"},{q="Who leads the worship of Elune in Kalimdor?",a="The Sisterhood of Elune is led by High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind from the Temple of the Moon in Darnassus. The Sisterhood serves as the primary spiritual and civic authority among the night elves.",al={"priestesses of elune leader","sisters of elune"},it="leader"},{q="Where did Elune come from?",a="Elune's true origins remain shrouded in myth, as she is one of the few true deities worshipped across Azeroth. According to night elven belief, the early kaldorei first sensed her presence after being transformed by the Well of Eternity, where they believed she rested during the day. Ancient legends tell of her communing with the primordial dark trolls, teaching them words in the Titan tongue, and taking the Great White Stag, Malorne, as her consort to bring forth Cenarius.",al={"elune origins","where did elune come from","history of elune"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Every aspect of kaldorei civilization—from the ruling Sisterhood to the moonlight bathing your woodland settlements—flows from your eternal devotion to the Mother Moon.",["race:Tauren"]="The moon in the night sky is Mu'sha, the left eye of the Earth Mother, praised in your ancestral myths for watching over the tribes and the creatures of the dark.",["class:Priest"]="Night elf priests draw their divine spells and celestial fury directly from Elune's guidance, wielding radiant moonlight rather than the holy light worshipped in the eastern kingdoms.",["class:Druid"]="As the divine mother of Lord Cenarius, Elune's celestial influence is woven directly into your astral powers and ancient kinship with the living wild."},kw={"cenarius","elune","mother moon","mu'sha","night warrior","sisterhood of elune","tyrande","white lady"},rel={"npc:tyrande-whisperwind","topic:cenarius","topic:earth-mother","npc:malfurion-stormrage","topic:druid","topic:tauren","topic:emerald-dream"},src={"Elune"}}
E["topic:emerald-dream"] = {t="topic",n="Emerald Dream",z="general",s="The Emerald Dream is a vast, ever-shifting spirit realm representing Azeroth in its primordial state, completely untouched by civilization. Watched over by the Dragon Aspect Ysera and her green flight, it serves as the spiritual blueprint of nature and the sacred domain where druids commune with the cycle of life.",h="The Emerald Dream is a vast, ever-shifting spirit realm representing Azeroth in its primordial state...",sec={{t="A Mirror of Primordial Azeroth",b="Existing outside the physical plane yet tethered to its heartbeat, the Emerald Dream reflects what Azeroth would look like had intelligent beings never cleared its forests or built cities upon its stone. Sculpted during the ancient ordering of the world, it is an ever-shifting wilderness of pristine forests, boundless plains, and primordial rivers. Time and distance hold little meaning within the Dream; days in the physical world can feel like decades in the spirit, and landmarks change shape like mist before mortal eyes.",sp=0},{t="The Realm of the Dreamer",b="Following the ordering of the world, the titans entrusted this verdant plane to Ysera the Dreamer, the great Aspect of the green dragonflight. Charged with guiding the evolution and natural rhythm of Azeroth, Ysera dwells in an unending trance, gazing through enchanted dreamsight that perceives both the waking world and its mirrored spirit plane. The green dragons guard the rare, natural portals linking the physical plane to the Dream, maintaining eternal vigilance over the boundary between sleep and waking.",sp=0},{t="Druids and the Dreaming",b="To mortal druids, the Emerald Dream is the wellspring of their craft. By channeling nature magic, druids can project their spirits into the Dream, leaving their corporeal bodies resting safely in barrow dens for years or even centuries. In this untamed realm, they commune with ancient nature spirits and the Wild Gods, gaining profound insight into the Great Cycle of life and death. Skilled walkers of the Dream learn to transcend physical laws while in their dreamforms, traversing vast distances or slipping through solid barriers at will.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Emerald Dream?",a="The Emerald Dream is an ethereal, spiritual reflection of Azeroth. It preserves a pristine, untamed blueprint of the world as it existed before civilized races altered the landscape, serving as a sanctuary for nature spirits and the green dragonflight.",al={"what is emerald dream","emerald dream lore","the dream explained"},it="identity"},{q="Who rules the Emerald Dream?",a="The Dragon Aspect Ysera watches over the Dream alongside the green dragonflight. However, she does not absolute control it. The Dream's wild, underlying structure operates on its own ancient rhythm, guiding Ysera's stewardship rather than submitting to her will.",al={"who controls emerald dream","emerald dream ruler","ysera dream"},it="leader"},{q="How do mortals enter the Emerald Dream?",a="Most mortals enter through deep, druidic slumber, projecting their spirit into a dreamform while leaving their physical bodies protected in barrows. Rare, physical portals guarded by the green dragonflight also exist across Azeroth, though stepping through them is perilous.",al={"visiting emerald dream","how to enter the dream","dreamform"}},{q="Can you change things inside the Dream?",a="Dreamers can shape and alter the local environment temporarily with their thoughts and magic, but these changes never last. The Dream's primordial blueprint inevitably reasserts itself, erasing any permanent mortal imprint.",al={"changing emerald dream","can you alter the dream"}},{q="Why do druids sleep for centuries?",a="Druids enter prolonged hibernation to wander the Emerald Dream, communing with nature spirits, learning from the Wild Gods, and helping the green dragonflight maintain the balance of Azeroth's natural cycles across generations.",al={"why druids hibernate","druid slumber","barrow dens sleeping"}},{q="Where do animal spirits go when they die?",a="The spirits of many beasts, Wild Gods, and nature beings depart for the Emerald Dream upon death. Places like G'Hanir, the Mother Tree, serve as celestial sanctuaries where wild spirits find rest within the Great Cycle.",al={"animal afterlife","wild god spirits","beast spirits"}},{q="Where did the Emerald Dream come from?",a="During the ordering of Azeroth, the titan-forged Keeper Freya molded the Emerald Dream to serve as an underlying blueprint for the planet. Some believe she wove it out of nothing, while others say it was a primordial dream slumbering within the world itself that she shaped and ordered.",al={"emerald dream origin","who made emerald dream","how was emerald dream created"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why is it called the Emerald Dream?",a="It is named for its lush, pristine verdure and its spiritual nature. Charged to Ysera and the green dragonflight, this verdant realm mirrors an untouched, flourishing Azeroth that mortals can only walk in dreams and spirit forms.",al={"why emerald dream","emerald dream name meaning"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The Emerald Dream is the sacred source of your power, where your order communes with the Wild Gods and studies the delicate balance of the Great Cycle.",["race:NightElf"]="For ten thousand years, your male druids slept in sacred barrows to walk the Dream alongside Ysera, leaving the Sentinels and Priestesses to guard the waking world.",["race:Tauren"]="Your druids walk the land of dreams to commune with the spirits of the earth, seeing it as the untamed cradle of the Earth Mother's creation."},kw={"cenarius","dreaming","druid slumber","emerald dream","ghanir","green dragonflight","wild gods","ysera"},rel={"topic:druid","topic:titan","topic:night-elf","quest:1490","quest:480","quest:1599","quest:6381"},src={"Emerald Dream"}}
E["topic:explorers-league"] = {t="topic",n="Explorers' League",z="general",s="The Explorers' League is a renowned dwarven archaeological guild headquartered in Ironforge's Hall of Explorers. Founded under King Magni Bronzebeard and led by his wandering brother Brann, the League seeks to uncover the forgotten origins of the dwarven race. Their expansive digs span across Azeroth, uncovering ancient titan ruins while frequently igniting regional conflicts.",h="The Explorers' League is a renowned dwarven archaeological guild headquartered in Ironforge's Hall of...",sec={{t="A Shift in Dwarven Purpose",b="For centuries, the dwarves of Khaz Modan focused their formidable energies on mining, stonework, and engineering. That focus changed drastically when ancient ruins were unearthed, hinting at a forgotten legacy connecting dwarves to mythical titan creators. Eager to solve the mystery of his people's beginnings, King Magni Bronzebeard decreed that dwarven industry pivot heavily toward archaeology. Magni, alongside his brother Brann, established the Explorers' Guild—now widely known as the Explorers' League—to scour the world for relics, forgotten cities, and traces of their ancient ancestry.",sp=0},{t="The Hall and the Digs",b="The League's operations are directed from the cavernous Hall of Explorers in Ironforge, oversaw on a day-to-day administrative level by High Explorer Muninn Magellas while Brann Bronzebeard explores distant reaches. Across Khaz Modan and beyond, prospectors manage extensive digsites. Sites like Ironband's Excavation in Loch Modan and Whelgar's Excavation in the Wetlands uncover immense fossil records and ancient architecture. However, prospectors often find themselves under siege by displaced wildlife, troggs, and rival factions interested in subterranean relics.",sp=0},{t="Frontier Clashes",b="The League's aggressive expeditions have sparked fierce resistance outside dwarven homelands. In Kalimdor, League operations such as the Bael Modan dig in the southern Barrens drove out the native Stonespire tauren through heavy industrial blasting, stirring vengeance and angering local earth spirits. In the arid Badlands, League prospectors clash openly with the Dark Iron clan over the vast titan vaults of Uldaman. League agents also quietly share intelligence with Stormwind's covert SI:7 network, demonstrating that their worldwide excavations frequently intersect with high-stakes Alliance politics.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Explorers' League?",a="The Explorers' League is an Ironforge-based organization dedicated to surveying, archaeology, and researching the origins of the dwarven race. They maintain excavation sites across the Eastern Kingdoms and Kalimdor to unearth relics of the ancient world.",al={"explorers guild","dwarf explorers","who are the explorers league","league of explorers"},it="identity"},{q="Who leads the Explorers' League?",a="King Magni Bronzebeard helped found the guild, but his youngest brother, the renowned wanderer Brann Bronzebeard, serves as its official leader. In Brann's frequent absences across the globe, High Explorer Muninn Magellas oversees business from the Hall of Explorers in Ironforge.",al={"leader of explorers league","brann bronzebeard role","muninn magellas"},it="leader"},{q="Why did King Magni start the League?",a="After strange subterranean ruins revealed evidence that the dwarves were not merely born of ordinary mountain rock, King Magni shifted a massive portion of dwarven industry toward archaeology to discover the true, mythical heritage of his people.",al={"why do dwarves dig","dwarven origins","why archaeology"},it="history"},{q="Can non-dwarves join the League?",a="Yes. While the League remains predominantly dwarven in membership, leadership, and aesthetic, individuals of other Alliance races do join, including adventurous gnomes like Clopper Wizbang, along with trusted field assistants across the continents.",al={"gnomes in explorers league","other races in league","who can join"},it="people"},{q="Why are the tauren fighting the Explorers' League?",a="In their race to excavate Kalimdor, dwarven prospectors at Bael Modan used heavy explosives and displaced the native Stonespire tauren tribe, decimating their settlement and outraging the local earth spirits through wanton destruction of sacred ground.",al={"bael modan tauren","dwarves vs tauren","stonespire feud"},it="hostile"},{q="What is Uldaman and why does the League care about it?",a="Uldaman is a massive underground titan facility buried beneath the Badlands. The League views it as the crown jewel of their research, believing its sealed chambers hold definitive proof regarding the transformation and ancient creation of the dwarven race.",al={"uldaman excavation","league badlands","titan ruins uldaman"},it="purpose"},{q="Why are Explorers' League camps found across the world?",a="King Magni turned dwarven industry toward archaeology after discovering ancient ruins tied to their heritage. Since then, the League has dispatched prospectors and excavators to anywhere ancient titan relics, mysterious ruins, or historical secrets might lie buried. You will find their dig sites scattered across Khaz Modan, deep in Kalimdor's wilderness like Darkshore and the Barrens, and throughout ancient subterranean complexes like Uldaman.",al={"why is explorers league everywhere","explorers league dig sites locations","why is the league in kalimdor"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="Who is the Explorers' League allied with?",a="The League is an official arm of the dwarven kingdom of Ironforge, placing them firmly within the Grand Alliance. Beyond open military ties with dwarven and Alliance troops who guard their digs, the League maintains a discreet, unofficial relationship with Stormwind's SI:7 intelligence service, quietly trading encoded dispatches and findings gathered across Azeroth's remote frontiers.",al={"explorers league alliance ties","who does the explorers league work for","explorers league si7"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Every digsite and titan artifact brings the dwarven race closer to understanding their ancient creator roots and their departure from stone.",["race:Tauren"]="The League's careless use of dynamite in Kalimdor desecrated Stonespire ancestral lands, proving their relentless digging often comes at the expense of the earth.",["faction:Alliance"]="Beyond academic curiosity, the League acts as an unofficial intelligence-gathering arm across remote territories, quietly passing encrypted findings to Stormwind's SI:7.",["profession:Mining"]="Dwarven prospectors rely heavily on mining traditions to shore up tunnels, survey strata, and extract delicate titan machinery from solid rock."},kw={"archaeology","brann bronzebeard","excavation","explorers guild","explorers' league","hall of explorers","magni bronzebeard","muninn magellas","prospector","uldaman"},rel={"subzone:hall-of-explorers","npc:brann-bronzebeard","npc:magni-bronzebeard","subzone:uldaman","subzone:ironband-s-excavation-site","subzone:whelgar-s-excavation-site","subzone:bael-modan","quest:2300"},src={"Explorers' League"}}
E["topic:first-war"] = {t="topic",n="First War",z="general",s="The First War was the devastating five-year conflict in which the Orcish Horde emerged through the Dark Portal and completely destroyed the human Kingdom of Stormwind. Orchestrated behind the scenes by the demon-possessed Guardian Medivh and the warlock Gul'dan, it concluded with the assassination of King Llane Wrynn I and the flight of human survivors north to Lordaeron.",h="The First War was the devastating five-year conflict in which the Orcish Horde emerged through the Dark...",sec={{t="Opening of the Portal",b="Before the war, the Kingdom of Stormwind enjoyed long years of peace following the Gnoll War, guarded by its Knights and the Clerics of Northshire Abbey. That stability shattered when the Guardian Medivh, secretly possessed by the demon lord Sargeras, contacted the orc warlock Gul'dan on Draenor. Together, they forged the Dark Portal in the swampy Black Morass. Driven by demon blood and the slow death of their homeworld, the Orcish Horde crossed into Azeroth in force. Early rumors dismissed the invaders as vengeful spirits or strange offshoots of trolls, but Sir Anduin Lothar's scouting patrols quickly confirmed the arrival of a disciplined, bloodthirsty army.",sp=0},{t="The War for Stormwind",b="Led openly by Warchief Blackhand and manipulated from the shadows by Gul'dan's Shadow Council, the Horde overran the southern reaches of the kingdom. While an attempt by the orcs to push into Stranglethorn Vale against the Gurubashi trolls failed, their campaign across human territory proved catastrophic. The Horde conquered outward until only Elwynn Forest remained under human control. Blackhand launched a first direct assault on Stormwind City, but Sir Anduin Lothar repelled the invasion with a brilliant, surprise flanking counterattack by sea, temporarily saving the capital from annihilation.",sp=0},{t="Treachery and the Fall",b="The war turned when Lothar and the young mage Khadgar uncovered Medivh's betrayal, invading Karazhan and slaying the Guardian. Medivh's demise cast Gul'dan into a coma, creating an opportunity for Orgrim Doomhammer to challenge and kill Blackhand in a mak'gora duel. Taking absolute command, Doomhammer crushed the Shadow Council and marched on Stormwind for a second siege. During the onslaught, the half-orc assassin Garona murdered King Llane Wrynn I within his keep. Stormwind was sacked and burned to ash, forcing Lothar to gather the surviving populace and flee north across the Great Sea to Lordaeron.",sp=1}},faq={{q="How did the orcs get to Azeroth in the first place?",a="The warlock Gul'dan on Draenor and the Guardian Medivh on Azeroth collaborated using dark fel magic to breach the dimensional fabric between worlds, erecting the Dark Portal in the Black Morass.",al={"how did orcs get here","dark portal origin","first war start"},it="origin"},{q="Why didn't the other human kingdoms help Stormwind?",a="Stormwind stood alone because the distant northern kingdoms viewed rumors of green-skinned invaders as far-fetched exaggerations or local squabbles. It was only after Stormwind fell and Lothar arrived in Lordaeron with boats full of refugees that the northern kings united.",al={"why no alliance","did lordaeron help","reinforcements for stormwind"},it="conflict"},{q="Who killed King Llane?",a="King Llane Wrynn I was assassinated inside his own keep by Garona Halforcen, an emissary whom the king had trusted, striking a fatal blow to human morale during the final siege.",al={"who murdered llane","king llane death","garona killed king"},it="history"},{q="Did the orcs fight anyone besides humans in the First War?",a="Yes. Early in the conflict, the Horde pushed southward into Stranglethorn Vale, but they were repelled in a fierce clash with the Gurubashi jungle trolls.",al={"did orcs fight trolls","gurubashi in first war","other races first war"},it="history"},{q="Who won the First War?",a="The Orcish Horde won a total victory. The Kingdom of Stormwind was razed, King Llane was slain, and the surviving human population was forced into a massive sea evacuation north to Lordaeron.",al={"first war victor","who won wc1","outcome of first war"},it="history"},{q="What happened to Warchief Blackhand?",a="When Gul'dan was incapacitated following Medivh's death, Orgrim Doomhammer challenged Blackhand to mak'gora—a duel of honor—slew him, and seized leadership of the Horde.",al={"how did blackhand die","orgrim doomhammer challenge","warchief killed"},it="leader"},{q="What was the First War?",a="The First War, also known as the first Great War, was the devastating five-year conflict fought between the Orcish Horde and the human Kingdom of Stormwind. Orchestrated in secret by the warlock Gul'dan and the corrupted Guardian Medivh, the orcs crossed from their dying homeworld of Draenor through the Dark Portal into the Black Morass. What began as scattered frontier clashes escalated into an all-out invasion that destroyed the kingdom's towns, culminated in the assassination of King Llane Wrynn, and ended with the total sack of Stormwind City.",al={"what is first war","first war summary","the first great war"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why is it called the First War?",a="It is named the First War because it marked the very first clash between humanity and the Orcish Horde on Azeroth. Before the Dark Portal opened, humans had never seen orcs and had enjoyed generations of relative peace following the Gnoll War. The conflict shattered that isolation, and because a second grand confrontation soon followed when the Horde invaded the northern human nations, historians and survivors refer to this initial struggle for Stormwind as the First War.",al={"why first war","first war name meaning","why is it called the first war"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="The First War represents the darkest hour of Stormwind's history, when the kingdom fell, its king was murdered, and its survivors fled across the sea as penniless refugees.",["race:Orc"]="For the orcs, the First War marked their initial bloody conquest on Azeroth, tainted by the blood-curse of Mannoroth and the hidden machinations of Gul'dan's Shadow Council.",["class:Paladin"]="The slaughter of the Clerics of Northshire Abbey during this conflict demonstrated that holy faith alone was not enough to survive brutal melee combat, directly inspiring the founding of the Knights of the Silver Hand.",["faction:Alliance"]="The total annihilation of Stormwind served as the grim warning that convinced the northern nations to abandon their isolation and unite into the Alliance of Lordaeron.",["class:Warlock"]="Gul'dan and his Shadow Council directed the Horde's conquests through fel sorceries, demonstrating the immense, world-breaking devastation demonic pacts can unleash."},kw={"anduin lothar","black morass","blackhand","dark portal","first war","gul'dan","llane wrynn","medivh","northshire abbey","orgrim doomhammer"},rel={"topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:llane-wrynn-i","topic:medivh","npc:orgrim-doomhammer","subzone:dark-portal","topic:second-war","subzone:northshire-abbey"},src={"First War"}}
E["topic:forsaken"] = {t="topic",n="Forsaken",z="general",s="The Forsaken are a free-willed faction of intelligent undead who broke away from the Scourge following the Third War. Led by the Banshee Queen Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, they dwell within the subterranean Undercity carved beneath the ruins of Lordaeron's capital. Though deeply distrusted across Azeroth, they maintain a tenuous alliance of convenience with the Horde.",h="The Forsaken are a free-willed faction of intelligent undead who broke away from the Scourge following the...",sec={{t="Broken Shackles",b="When the Lich King's grip over his northern armies slipped after the Third War, thousands of undead in the blighted ruins of Lordaeron suddenly regained their sapience and memories. Horrified by the butchery they had been forced to carry out while enslaved to his will, these former citizens found themselves reviled and hunted by the living. Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the fallen ranger-general of Quel'Thalas who had been transformed into an unholy banshee, rallied these adrift spirits, claimed the catacombs beneath Capital City, and dubbed her new nation the Forsaken.",sp=0},{t="An Alliance of Convenience",b="Hemmed in by relentless enemies—chief among them the fanatic Scarlet Crusade and the Scourge—Sylvanas dispatched emissaries seeking allies. Those sent to Stormwind were slaughtered before breaching the gates. Across the Great Sea in Kalimdor, however, Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem and elder tauren counseled compassion, believing the cursed people might yet find redemption or a cure through the earth. Despite intense misgivings from Warchief Thrall and Cairne Bloodhoof, the Horde admitted the Forsaken, gaining a vital northern foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms.",sp=0},{t="Dark Laboratories",b="While presenting a cooperative front to their Kalimdor allies, the Forsaken pursue their own grim survival. Beneath the Undercity in the Apothecarium, the Royal Apothecary Society works incessantly to concoct a 'New Plague' potent enough to extinguish both the Scourge and living threats alike. Meanwhile, Forsaken champions like Nathanos Blightcaller wage bitter wars across the Plaguelands, Hillsbrad Foothills, and the Scarlet Monastery to secure their sovereign borders.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Are the Forsaken part of the Scourge?",a="No. The Forsaken broke free from the Lich King's mental domination after the Third War. They loathe the Scourge and consider the Lich King Arthas their absolute mortal enemy, fighting bitterly against his minions throughout the Plaguelands.",al={"scourge difference","are forsaken scourge","are undead evil"},it="origin"},{q="Where is the Forsaken capital?",a="Their capital is the Undercity, a vast subterranean sprawl built beneath the ruins of Capital City in Tirisfal Glades. Sylvanas's forces expanded the royal crypts and sewers into a fortified sanctuary safe from the light and outdoor threats.",al={"undead capital","forsaken city","where do undead live"},it="place"},{q="Why did the Horde let the Forsaken join?",a="Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem and the tauren advocated strongly for them, seeing potential for redemption and hoping the earth's powers might cure their affliction. Magatha Grimtotem also supported the pact. Warchief Thrall accepted because the Horde urgently needed a strategic military foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms.",al={"why are undead horde","how did forsaken join horde","thrall forsaken alliance"},it="motive"},{q="Who rules the Forsaken?",a="Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the Banshee Queen. Formerly the high elven ranger-general who fell defending Silvermoon, she liberated free-willed undead and rules from the Undercity alongside her dreadlord advisor, Varimathras.",al={"undead leader","forsaken queen","who leads forsaken"},it="in_charge"},{q="Are the Forsaken all former humans?",a="The vast majority are former humans from the fallen kingdom of Lordaeron who were slain and raised during the Scourge invasion. However, their leadership includes former elves like Sylvanas, and anyone who bends the knee to the Dark Lady is welcomed into their ranks.",al={"undead races","are forsaken only humans","what race are forsaken"},it="people"},{q="Why does the Scarlet Crusade hate the Forsaken so much?",a="The Scarlet Crusade makes zero distinction between mindful Forsaken and mindless Scourge. Their zealots view all undead as abominations that must be purified through fire and holy wrath, regardless of free will or allegiances.",al={"scarlet crusade undead","crusade vs forsaken","why scarlet crusade attacks"},it="conflict"},{q="What is the story of the Forsaken?",a="The Forsaken are former victims of the Scourge who broke free of the Lich King's mental grip when his power waned after the Third War. Regaining their free will and the memories of the atrocities they were forced to commit, they were rallied by Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, a fallen ranger-general turned banshee. Together, they drove rival forces from the ruins of Lordaeron, finished carving out the Undercity beneath its shattered capital, and banded together to survive in a world where the living and the Scourge alike seek their utter destruction.",al={"forsaken origin","who are the forsaken","forsaken lore summary"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do the Forsaken stand in the world today?",a="Currently, the Forsaken serve as the Horde's vital foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms through an alliance of convenience backed by the tauren. From the Undercity, they wage a brutal multi-front struggle: fighting off Scourge holdouts in the Plaguelands, contesting resources across Hillsbrad, Alterac, and Arathi Basin, and battling the zealous Scarlet Crusade, who seek to eradicate all undead indiscriminately. Meanwhile, the Royal Apothecary Society works covertly in the shadows to concoct a new plague capable of eradicating both their Scourge enemies and anyone else who threatens their survival.",al={"forsaken current situation","where are the forsaken now","forsaken standing"},sp=1,it="today",add=true},{q="What does it mean for a new adventurer to be one of the Forsaken?",a="Rising as a freshly freed corpse in Deathknell, you awaken into a world that despises and hunts you. You bear the horrific memories of your time as a mindless Scourge puppet, yet Lady Sylvanas Windrunner offers you refuge, purpose, and vengeance under her banner. As an adventurer of the Forsaken, your allegiance belongs first and foremost to the Dark Lady and your fellow undead. You fight to secure Lordaeron, push back human zealots and Scourge remnants, and advance the pragmatic interests of a budding nation determined never to be enslaved again.",al={"playing a forsaken","forsaken player role","what does it mean to be undead"},it="for_you",rc="Undead",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This is your people: cursed to walk the earth in rotting flesh, spurned by former kin, yet bound together under the Dark Lady to carve out an empire of your own.",["race:Tauren"]="Your elders, especially Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem, championed the Forsaken's entry into the Horde out of sympathy, hoping your shamanic and druidic rites might one day soothe or heal their tragic condition.",["faction:Alliance"]="To the Alliance, the Forsaken are nightmarish parodies of their fallen friends and family from Lordaeron, holding former imperial lands and answering overtures with poison and steel.",["faction:Horde"]="Thrall forged an alliance of convenience with the Undercity for territory in the East, but many orcs and trolls view their grim apothecary experiments and necromantic culture with deep suspicion."},kw={"apothecary","forsaken","lordaeron","plaguelands","sylvanas","tirisfal","undead","undercity"},rel={"npc:sylvanas-windrunner","zone:undercity","topic:scourge","topic:horde","topic:scarlet-crusade","quest:1489","npc:varimathras","npc:nathanos-blightcaller"},src={"Forsaken"}}
E["topic:furbolg"] = {t="topic",n="Furbolg",z="general",s="Furbolgs are a hulking, reclusive race of bear-men native to northern Kalimdor who lived in harmony with nature and the night elves for millennia. In the wake of the Third War, demonic corruption and lingering fel magic have driven nearly every tribe into a frenzied madness. Today, former friends have become vicious territorial predators, with only a tiny handful of tribes clinging to their sanity.",h="Furbolgs are a hulking, reclusive race of bear-men native to northern Kalimdor who lived in harmony with...",sec={{t="Ancient Kinship",b="Furbolgs trace their ancestry back to the jalgar of ancient Kalimdor and have long revered the wild god Ursoc. During the War of the Ancients ten thousand years ago, tribal packs united under chieftain Unng Ak to fight alongside night elves and tauren against the first demonic invasion. Following the Great Sundering, they chose seclusion deep in the northern woodlands, maintaining peaceful ties with night elf druids and forest wardens while living in simple harmony with the wild.",sp=0},{t="The Scourge of Madness",b="The Third War shattered the furbolgs' tranquil existence. The passage of the Burning Legion through Kalimdor left lingering fel taint across the soil and canopy of Ashenvale and Felwood. Simple-minded and deeply attuned to the land, the bear-men absorbed this corruption directly. Their wizened shamans were overthrown or silenced by bloodthirsty firebrands who channeled fel moss, dark talismans, and corrupt alchemy, transforming peaceful gatherers into rabid raiders who now attack travelers and night elf settlements on sight.",sp=0},{t="The Unbroken Line",b="Across northern Kalimdor, nearly every major tribe has succumbed: the Gnarlpine across Teldrassil, the Blackwood of Darkshore, the Thistlefur and Foulweald in Ashenvale, and the Deadwood and Winterfall of the northern highlands. Yet a few hold the line. Deep within the fortified tunnels connecting Felwood, Winterspring, and Moonglade, the Timbermaw tribe fiercely guards its sanity. Wary and isolated, the Timbermaw defend their hold against their crazed northern kin, desperate to preserve what remains of their uncorrupted culture.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are the furbolgs attacking everyone?",a="The aftermath of the Third War left lingering demonic corruption throughout Kalimdor's northern forests. Because furbolgs are deeply attuned to nature, this taint seeped into their minds, driving their tribes into paranoid, violent frenzy and replacing their peaceful elders with aggressive chieftains.",al={"why are furbolgs hostile","furbolg madness","why furbolgs went crazy"},it="hostile"},{q="Were furbolgs always enemies of the night elves?",a="No. For thousands of years, furbolgs and night elves were peaceful neighbors and close allies. They fought shoulder to shoulder against the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients, and night elf druids maintained gentle relations with tribal settlements until the recent wave of madness took hold.",al={"furbolg night elf history","did furbolgs like night elves"},it="ties"},{q="Are there any furbolgs who are still friendly?",a="Yes. The Timbermaw tribe maintains an uncorrupted enclave inside Timbermaw Hold, the crossroads tunnel system between Felwood, Moonglade, and Winterspring. Though deeply suspicious of outsiders, they will tolerate travelers who prove their enmity toward the tainted Deadwood and Winterfall tribes.",al={"friendly furbolgs","good furbolg tribe","uncorrupted furbolgs"},it="residents"},{q="What is wrong with the furbolgs on Teldrassil?",a="The Gnarlpine tribe on Teldrassil has fallen under the sway of fel moss, an insidious corruption spreading through the world tree's boughs. Under Chieftain Ursal the Mauler, the corrupted Gnarlpine abandoned their traditional villages and overran sacred sites like the Ban'ethil Barrow Den and Starbreeze Village.",al={"teldrassil furbolgs","gnarlpine corruption"},it="ruin"},{q="Who is behind the corruption of the Winterfall in Winterspring?",a="The Winterfall are secretly manipulated by a satyr named Xandivious, who lurks within their chieftain's den. The tribe's frenzy is sustained by Winterfall Firewater, brewed from tainted materials inside Felwood's Deadwood Cauldron and ferried north through the mountain passes.",al={"winterfall tribe lore","who corrupted winterfall","winterfall firewater origin"},sp=1},{q="Can corrupted furbolgs ever be cured?",a="Night elf sentinels and druids have attempted to cleanse individual tribes using moonwell waters and sacred relics, but broad recovery has proven nearly impossible. With satyrs and dark relics actively driving the corruption deeper, defenders often have no choice but to cull hostile packs to protect settlements.",al={"cure furbolgs","can furbolgs be saved"},it="threat"},{q="What are furbolgs?",a="Furbolgs are a race of hulking, bear-like humanoids native to the ancient forests of northern Kalimdor. Traditionally simple-minded, deeply spiritual, and reclusive, they lived peacefully alongside nature for millennia. Though powerful in stature and capable of fierce combat, they possessed no natural appetite for war or cruelty until recent tragedies twisted their minds.",al={"what is a furbolg","who are furbolgs","furbolg race"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where did the furbolgs come from?",a="Furbolgs descend from an ancient race of bear-men known as the jalgar. Long before the Great Sundering, conflicts in the ancient north pushed them southward into the central forests of Kalimdor. During the War of the Ancients ten thousand years ago, furbolg packs led by Unng Ak stood shoulder to shoulder with night elves, tauren, and earthen against the Burning Legion, cementing an alliance that lasted peacefully for generations.",al={"furbolg origin","history of furbolgs","where do furbolgs come from"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who leads the furbolgs?",a="Furbolgs do not have a single overarching leader or ruler. Instead, they are divided into independent tribes, each led by its own chieftains, shamans, and elders. In recent times, many of these tribes' wise elders have been overthrown, slain, or replaced by aggressive firebrands and corrupt chieftains who lead their crazed kin into violence.",al={"furbolg leader","who rules the furbolgs","furbolg king"},it="leader",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="For night elves, the fall of the furbolgs is a heartbreaking tragedy. Tribes who were trusted woodland allies for ten thousand years have become feral predators threatening Astranaar and the sacred barrows of Teldrassil.",["class:Druid"]="Druids mourn the bear-men as kindred spirits of Ursoc whose connection to nature has been weaponized by fel taint, making tribal unrest a direct symptom of the land's broader sickness.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde views the hostile tribes of Ashenvale as territorial roadblocks, clearing aggressive packs like the Foulweald and Thistlefur to secure supply routes and outposts along the forest fringe."},kw={"ashenvale","corruption","deadwood","foulweald","furbolg","gnarlpine","thistlefur","timbermaw","ursoc","winterfall"},rel={"subzone:timbermaw-hold","zone:ashenvale","zone:felwood","zone:winterspring","topic:night-elf","topic:war-of-the-ancients","quest:486","quest:991"},src={"Furbolg"}}
E["topic:gnoll"] = {t="topic",n="Gnoll",z="general",s="Gnolls are a savage, hyena-like race of humanoid scavengers and raiders found scattered across both the Eastern Kingdoms and Kalimdor. Renowned for their vicious infighting, brutal strength, and animal cunning, they survive by pillaging settlements, butchering wildlife, and ambushing unwary travelers. Left unchecked, their sprawling packs and warlords pose a constant threat to frontier hamlets and trade roads alike.",h="Gnolls are a savage, hyena-like race of humanoid scavengers and raiders found scattered across both the...",sec={{t="Hyenas of the Wilds",b="Standing slightly shorter than the average human but possessing explosive physical power, gnolls are bipedal hyena-kin known for their spiky teeth, thick pelts, and distinct barking laugh. While fully capable of walking upright, they often drop to all fours to lope swiftly through brush or track prey by scent. Gnoll culture values raw physical dominance above all else. They organize into fractious packs led by the strongest, cruelest alpha, only yielding when battered into submission by a superior force.",sp=0},{t="The Gnoll War",b="Though usually fractured by petty squabbles, united gnolls once brought the kingdom of Stormwind to its knees. Decades before the First War, a packlord named Garfang subjugated the tribes around Redridge and launched coordinated assaults on Stormwind City. While city defenders were pinned down, gnoll raiders laid waste to Westfall's farms. The crisis only ended when King Barathen Wrynn led a daring strike into the Redridge Mountains and severed Garfang's head. Deprived of leadership, the gnolls turned on each other in bloody infighting, shattering their grand coalition forever.",sp=0},{t="Raiders of the Southern Kingdoms",b="In the south, gnoll packs run rampant in the absence of Stormwind's full military might. In Elwynn Forest and Westfall, the Riverpaw pack—marshaled by the notorious Hogger—raids homesteads and forces subjugated kobolds to dig for gold, secretly colluding with the Defias Brotherhood. Further east in the Redridge Mountains, the Redridge pack snatches horses outside Lakeshire and sabotages bridge repairs, while the Shadowhide clan in Galardell Valley serves the dark will of the rogue wizard Morganth.",sp=1},{t="Pestilence and Territory",b="Gnoll menace is not limited to the human south. In northern Lordaeron, the Scourge necromancer Thule Ravenclaw raises undead Rot Hide gnolls to dig up corpses across Tirisfal and Silverpine. Near Hillsbrad, the Mudsnout harvest foul, toxic blossoms among ruined farmsteads. Across the Great Sea in Kalimdor, the Palemane pack butchers game across Mulgore despite tauren efforts to make peace, while the Woodpaw of Feralas wage a desperate, frenzied war against encroaching silithid swarms.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What exactly is a gnoll?",a="Gnolls are hyena-like humanoid carnivores known for their violent nature, physical endurance, and low cunning. They live in nomadic or semi-permanent camps, surviving mostly on plunder, hunting, and carrion.",al={"what are gnolls","gnoll race","are gnolls hyenas"},it="identity"},{q="Why don't gnolls form a proper kingdom?",a="Gnolls are fiercely belligerent and driven by immediate hierarchy. Without an exceptional and tyrannical warlord beating them into line, packs inevitably splinter into bloody territorial feuds, preventing long-term organization or civil progress.",al={"why are gnolls scattered","gnoll empire","gnoll unity"},it="conflict"},{q="What was the Gnoll War?",a="Roughly seventy-five years before the First War, Packlord Garfang united the Redridge gnolls and laid siege to Stormwind while burning Westfall. King Barathen Wrynn rode into the mountains and personally slew Garfang, sparking suicidal infighting that broke the gnoll horde.",al={"king barathen gnoll war","garfang invasion","stormwind gnoll war"},it="history"},{q="Why are gnolls working with the Defias Brotherhood?",a="The Riverpaw pack in Westfall and Elwynn collaborates with the Defias Brotherhood in exchange for arms, coordination, and shared spoils. The bandits use the gnolls as brute muscle to overrun farmlands and distract Stormwind's dwindling garrisons.",al={"defias gnoll alliance","riverpaw defias","hogger defias"},sp=1,it="allies"},{q="Are the Rot Hide gnolls alive?",a="No. The Rot Hide gnolls prowling Tirisfal Glades and Silverpine Forest are undead thralls. They answer to Thule Ravenclaw, an exiled human necromancer using Fenris Isle as a redoubt to gather corpses for the Scourge.",al={"rot hide undead","silverpine gnolls","fenris isle gnolls"}},{q="Why do tauren fight the Palemane gnolls?",a="When the tauren settled Mulgore, they attempted to coexist and communicate with the indigenous Palemane pack. The gnolls rebuffed all peaceful gestures, slaughtering the plains wildlife without restraint and threatening tauren villages.",al={"palemane mulgore","tauren and gnolls","why kill palemane"},it="motive"},{q="Why are gnolls hostile to travelers?",a="Gnolls are brutish, predatory scavengers who view other races primarily as prey, trespassers, or sources of plunder. Belligerent and driven by basic animal cunning, they value physical strength above all else and respect little else. Because they rely heavily on raiding, kidnapping, and stealing livestock from nearby settlements, gnoll packs reflexively attack anyone who wanders into their territory or crosses their hunting grounds.",al={"why do gnolls attack","are gnolls hostile","gnolls attack on sight"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who leads the gnolls?",a="Gnolls have no unified leader or central king. Instead, they are splintered into scattered, squabbling packs and tribes across both continents. In each clan, leadership falls to the alpha or packlord—the largest, most brutal brute capable of beating rivals into submission. Notable warlords include Hogger of the Riverpaw in Elwynn Forest, Packlord Yowler in Redridge, and Lieutenant Fangore among the Shadowhide.",al={"who is gnoll leader","gnoll king","gnoll pack leader"},it="leader",add=true},{q="Where did gnolls come from?",a="Gnolls are an ancient race of hyena-like humanoids whose origins are lost to history. They have inhabited both Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms for millennia, long before the rise of modern nations. Early human settlers encountered vicious gnoll packs in Tirisfal Glades thousands of years ago, and troll tribes fought them in southern forests. Wherever wild lands exist, gnolls have carved out a savage existence.",al={"gnoll origin","where do gnolls come from","history of gnolls"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="For humans of Stormwind, gnolls are an ancient enemy that once burned the kingdom's heartlands; today, packs like the Riverpaw and Shadowhide threaten the kingdom's vulnerable frontier roads.",["race:Tauren"]="The Palemane tribe in Mulgore treats the land with careless cruelty, forcing young tauren braves to drive them back to preserve the balance of the plains.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance marshals across Elwynn, Westfall, Redridge, and the Wetlands rely heavily on freelance champions to thin out encroaching gnoll populations.",["race:Undead"]="The Forsaken view gnolls either as an annoyance or a resource: the Mudsnout cultivate restorative fungi in Hillsbrad, while Sylvanas previously enslaved northern packs through banshee possession."},kw={"garfang","gnoll","hogger","mosshide","mudsnout","palemane","riverpaw","rot hide","shadowhide","woodpaw"},rel={"topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:defias-brotherhood","zone:elwynn","zone:redridge","zone:westfall","zone:silverpine","zone:mulgore","quest:124"},src={"Gnoll"}}
E["topic:gnome"] = {t="topic",n="Gnome",z="general",s="Gnomes are a diminutive, eccentric, and astonishingly intelligent race renowned across Azeroth for their inventive engineering and mastery of the arcane. Despite their cheerful demeanor, they recently suffered a catastrophic tragedy when a subterranean trogg invasion and a disastrous defense plan irradiated their capital of Gnomeregan. Now living as refugees in Ironforge's Tinker Town, the gnomes remain staunch Alliance allies as they fight to reclaim their lost home.",h="Gnomes are a diminutive, eccentric, and astonishingly intelligent race renowned across Azeroth for their...",sec={{t="Ingenuity and Optimism",b="Small in stature but towering in intellect, gnomes approach the world with boundless curiosity and scientific ambition. While other races focus on recording histories and preserving traditions, gnomes look relentlessly forward, dedicating their lives to mechanical engineering, arcane studies, and technological innovation. Their inventions—ranging from clockwork devices and flying machines to complex subterranean trains—have shaped the modern world. Even after devastating hardships, gnomefolk maintain an irrepressible cheerfulness and an unshakable drive to rebuild and solve the impossible.",sp=0},{t="Forged in the Mountains",b="Millennia ago, the ancient ancestors of the gnomes emerged from the titan vaults of Uldaman, afflicted by the Curse of Flesh. Forgetting their titan-forged origins over generations, they settled in the frozen crags of Dun Morogh. When the dwarves arrived centuries later, the two peoples discovered an immediate kinship. Dwarven brawn and gnomish ingenuity united to carve out the marvelous subterranean techno-city of Gnomeregan in western Dun Morogh. The gnomes abandoned hereditary monarchy four centuries ago when their last king departed, electing visionary leaders known as High Tinkers instead.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of Gnomeregan",b="During the Third War, the gnomes sent armaments to Lordaeron but withheld troops, drawing confusion from their allies. In truth, an apocalyptic catastrophe had struck their doorstep: hordes of savage troggs, stirred by excavations near Uldaman, surged into Gnomeregan's lower sectors. Desperate to purge the invaders without burdening an embattled Alliance, High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque followed the council of his chief advisor and vented radioactive waste into the lower city. The toxic gambit failed utterly. The troggs survived mutated and frenzied, while nearly eighty percent of the gnomish population perished.",sp=1},{t="Exile in Tinker Town",b="The surviving gnomes fled their poisoned home, finding sanctuary among their dwarven cousins in Ironforge. High Tinker Mekkatorque and his people established a bustling enclave in Ironforge's Tinker Town, continuing to support the Alliance and oversee the Deeprun Tram linking Ironforge to Stormwind. From this adopted quarter, gnomish commandos and brave volunteers constantly mount expeditions into Gnomeregan's irradiated depths, striving to clear out the troggs, deal with mechanical abominations, and someday reclaim their grand city.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are gnomes living in Ironforge instead of their own city?",a="Gnomes are currently refugees residing in Tinker Town within Ironforge. Their ancestral home of Gnomeregan was overrun by a sudden invasion of subterranean troggs. During the crisis, a desperate plan to irradiate the troggs went wrong, bathing the city in lethal fallout and killing the vast majority of its inhabitants. King Magni Bronzebeard offered the survivors shelter in the dwarven capital.",al={"why in ironforge","where do gnomes live","gnome refugee city"},it="ruin"},{q="Who leads the gnomes?",a="The gnomes are led by High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque. Rather than maintaining a hereditary royal line, gnomes elect their leaders based on merit, engineering prowess, and visionary thinking. Mekkatorque carries the heavy burden of having ordered the irradiation of Gnomeregan to stop the troggs, and now directs the war effort to take back their home.",al={"gnome leader","gnome king","who rules gnomes","high tinker"},it="leader"},{q="Why didn't the gnomes help fight the Scourge in Lordaeron?",a="While the gnomes provided siege weapons and technology to the Alliance of Lordaeron, they could not spare soldiers because Gnomeregan was fighting for its survival against troggs at the exact same time. The gnomes chose not to ask their allies for help, believing the Alliance had enough to handle against the undead Scourge.",al={"gnomes in third war","why gnomes skipped third war","gnomes third war help"},it="conflict"},{q="What is the Deeprun Tram?",a="The Deeprun Tram is an extraordinary subterranean railway designed by High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque and built by gnomish engineers. It connects Tinker Town in Ironforge directly to the dwarven district of Stormwind City, allowing rapid transport of troops, supplies, and travelers between the two allied capitals.",al={"who built the tram","stormwind ironforge train","deeprun tram origin"},it="place"},{q="What is the rivalry between gnomes and goblins?",a="Gnomes and goblins possess an intense cultural and technological rivalry that flared during the Second War. Gnomish engineering focuses on precision, stability, and intricate, safe mechanisms. In contrast, goblin engineering prioritizes raw explosive power, cheap production, and swift results regardless of volatility. Both races constantly attempt to out-invent and mock one another.",al={"gnomes vs goblins","gnome goblin engineering","why do gnomes hate goblins"},it="conflict"},{q="What is the story of the gnomes?",a="Gnomes are a diminutive, brilliant, and irrepressibly cheerful race famed for their mastery of engineering, science, and the arcane. Long ago, their ancestors succumbed to the Curse of Flesh and abandoned Uldaman, settling in the snowy peaks of Dun Morogh. Together with their dwarven neighbors, they built the grand subterranean wonder of Gnomeregan. Although small in stature, their intellect and inventive ambition have reshaped warfare across Azeroth.",al={"gnome lore","who are the gnomes","gnome history"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do the gnomes stand in the world today?",a="Gnomes currently face their darkest hour as refugees. After a subterranean trogg invasion and a catastrophic radiation purge devastated Gnomeregan—killing up to eighty percent of their population—survivors fled to Ironforge. Living primarily out of the Tinker Town district under High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque, they remain staunch allies of the Alliance, repaying dwarven hospitality with tireless technical support while planning to reclaim their ruined home.",al={"gnomes present situation","current state of gnomes","where are gnomes now"},it="today",add=true},{q="What does it mean for a new adventurer to be a gnome?",a="Stepping out as a gnome means carrying an unyielding spirit in the shadow of tragedy. Your people have lost their beloved homeland, yet despair finds no purchase against gnomish curiosity and optimism. Whether wielding cutting-edge inventions or deadly arcane mastery, you venture into the world to prove your ingenuity, aid your Alliance allies, and help pave the way toward one day taking back Gnomeregan.",al={"playing a gnome","why play gnome","gnome adventurer"},it="for_you",rc="Gnome",add=true}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="Your people carry the tragic loss of Gnomeregan with quiet resolve, using brilliance, optimism, and explosive firepower to fight for your stolen home from exile in Tinker Town.",["race:Dwarf"]="The gnomes are your oldest and dearest mountain neighbors; opening Ironforge's gates to their survivors in Tinker Town was an easy choice after generations of shared brotherhood.",["profession:Engineering"]="Gnomish engineering is world-renowned for sophisticated contraptions, ray devices, and mind-boggling precision that values meticulous design over volatile goblin detonations.",["class:Mage"]="Despite their diminutive size, gnomes are exceptionally gifted arcanists who have served as foundational scholars and members of Dalaran's councils since ancient times.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance owes immense strategic mobility and siege technology to the gnomes, who faithfully supply machines and maintain the Deeprun Tram despite their own displacement."},kw={"deeprun tram","gnomanity","gnome","gnomeregan","gnomes","high tinker","mechagnome","mekkatorque","tinker town","trogg"},rel={"zone:gnomeregan","npc:gelbin-mekkatorque","subzone:tinker-town","zone:ironforge","subzone:deeprun-tram","topic:trogg","topic:goblin","subzone:uldaman"},src={"Gnome"}}
E["topic:goblin"] = {t="topic",n="Goblin",z="general",s="Goblins are diminutive, cunning humanoids originating from the Isle of Kezan, renowned across Azeroth for their relentless pursuit of profit, explosive engineering, and aggressive trade cartels. While most maintain a stance of strict neutrality to maximize commerce between the Alliance and Horde, mercenary and cutthroat outfits like the Venture Company and Steamwheedle Cartel leave an indelible mark on every corner of the world.",h="Goblins are diminutive, cunning humanoids originating from the Isle of Kezan, renowned across Azeroth for...",sec={{t="Kaja'mite and the Trade Wars",b="Long ago on the Isle of Kezan, primitive goblins were subjugated by Zandalari trolls and forced to excavate deposits of a potent, mind-expanding mineral known as kaja'mite. Breathing in the subterranean ore dust dramatically heightened their intellect. Developing devastating firearms, explosives, and traps in secret, the goblins overthrew their troll masters in a sudden uprising. Once liberated, their society fractured into competing economic syndicates known as cartels. When kaja'mite veins dwindled and their inspired genius turned erratic and volatile, goblin leaders channeled their ambitions directly into cutthroat mercantilism and global industrial dominance.",sp=0},{t="Mercenaries and Mercantilism",b="During the Second War, Trade Prince Steamwheedle allied his cartel with Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde, supplying maps, siege craft, and fleet-building expertise in exchange for Stormwind's captured plunder. Following the Horde's eventual defeat, the cartels learned that open allegiance cost them lucrative markets. In the modern era, the major cartels claim official neutrality, maintaining bustling trade havens like Booty Bay in Stranglethorn Vale and Gadgetzan in the Tanaris desert. Here, Alliance and Horde travelers cross paths without blades drawn—so long as port bruisers keep the peace.",sp=0},{t="Hired Hands and Industry",b="A goblin's primary loyalty belongs to gold, and many operate outside the neutral cartels as hired specialists or ruthless corporate barons. The Venture Company pursues unchecked deforestation and resource stripping across continents, heedless of local populations. Elsewhere, skilled artificers sell their talents directly to criminal enterprises: deep inside the Deadmines, goblin engineers like Sneed, Gilnid, and Captain Greenskin oversee smelters and shipwrights constructing a massive ironclad juggernaut for the Defias Brotherhood.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Are goblins aligned with the Alliance or the Horde?",a="Neither. The primary trade syndicates, including the Steamwheedle Cartel, adhere to strict neutrality to trade freely with both sides. Individual goblins and private enterprises happily contract out their services to the Horde, Alliance, or third parties like the Defias Brotherhood for the right price.",al={"are goblins horde","goblin faction","who do goblins fight for"},it="side"},{q="Why are goblin inventions so prone to exploding?",a="Their sudden technological leap was spurred by inhaling kaja'mite ore dust. As Kezan's mineral reserves dwindled over generations, goblin intelligence became more erratic, leaving behind an engineering philosophy prioritizing raw power, haste, and high explosive yields over structural safety.",al={"goblin engineering dangerous","why do goblin machines blow up","goblin vs gnome tech"}},{q="What is the Steamwheedle Cartel?",a="The Steamwheedle Cartel is the most influential mercantile cartel operating on the mainland. They manage vital neutral trading ports and cross-continental transit hubs, including Booty Bay in southern Stranglethorn and Gadgetzan in the Tanaris desert.",al={"steamwheedle cartel","who rules booty bay","goblin ports"}},{q="What is the Venture Company?",a="The Venture Company is a ruthless goblin-run industrial conglomerate notorious for aggressive deforestation, strip-mining, and oil extraction. Unlike the Steamwheedle Cartel, they operate with little regard for local law or environmental balance, often clashing violently with native populations.",al={"venture co","who are venture company","what is venture co"}},{q="Why are there goblins working with the Defias in the Deadmines?",a="The Defias Brotherhood possessed abundant plundered coin and raw ore from the Moonbrook mines, allowing them to hire top goblin specialists. Craftsmen like Sneed, Gilnid, and Captain Greenskin were contracted to oversee heavy smelting and build Edwin VanCleef's ironclad juggernaut.",al={"deadmines goblins","why are goblins in deadmines","gilnid sneed deadmines"},sp=1,it="why_here"},{q="Where is the goblin homeland?",a="Goblins hail from the tropical Isle of Kezan in the South Seas. It was there that they were enslaved by trolls, unearthed kaja'mite, threw off their chains, and built an empire of commerce and industrial smog.",al={"where do goblins come from","goblin home island","kezan"},it="origin"},{q="What are goblins?",a="Goblins are small, green-skinned humanoids renowned across Azeroth for their shrewd mercantile cunning, mastery of volatile technology, and love of explosives. Driven by an insatiable appetite for profit, they view almost every endeavor through the lens of trade, mining, and industry. While physically small, their ingenuity and willingness to weaponize raw engineering make them both formidable and unpredictable.",al={"what is a goblin","who are goblins","goblin race"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Who leads the goblins?",a="Goblins have no single ruler. Instead, their society is divided into competing commercial cartels and syndicates, each commanded by a wealthy Trade Prince or executive mogul. Prominent figures like Trade Prince Steamwheedle lead massive trading syndicates, while local barons and overseers—such as Baron Revilgaz in Booty Bay—direct operations and enforce order across individual ports and settlements.",al={"goblin leader","who is in charge of goblins","goblin ruler"},it="leader",add=true},{q="Why are some goblins hostile to travelers?",a="While major ports like Booty Bay and Gadgetzan maintain strict armed neutrality to protect trade, many goblins belong to cutthroat organizations that view travelers as obstacles or competition. Entities like the Venture Company aggressively defend their illicit logging and mining claims with brute force, while other goblins readily hire themselves out as mercenaries, technicians, or pirates to criminal factions.",al={"hostile goblins","why do goblins attack","are goblins enemies"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Where did the goblins come from, and what is their history?",a="Originally a primitive race on the Isle of Kezan, goblins gained tremendous intellect after being exposed to the rare mineral kaja'mite. Enslaved by Zandalari trolls to mine the ore, the goblins secretly engineered advanced weapons and staged a massive uprising roughly a century ago, driving the trolls out. After winning their freedom, the rival cartels turned their energy toward economic warfare, trade, and worldwide technological enterprise.",al={"goblin history","origin of goblins","where did goblins come from"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["profession:Engineering"]="Goblin engineering prioritizes extreme explosive force, offensive gadgetry, and high-risk mechanical contraptions, standing in sharp contrast to the precise, defensive designs favored by gnomes.",["profession:Mining"]="The goblin ascent from primitive islanders to industrial pioneers was driven by the mining of kaja'mite, and modern cartels remain fiercely protective of valuable mineral claims across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance authorities remain deeply suspicious of goblin neutrality, remembering well their mercenary service providing warships and sappers to the Old Horde during the Second War.",["faction:Horde"]="While Thrall's Horde utilizes goblin mechanics and trades with neutral hubs, memory remains fresh of the mercenary contracts forged between Orgrim Doomhammer and Trade Prince Steamwheedle."},kw={"booty bay","cartel","deadmines","engineering","gadgetzan","goblin","kaja'mite","kezan","steamwheedle","venture company"},rel={"topic:steamwheedle-cartel","topic:venture-company","subzone:booty-bay","subzone:gadgetzan","npc:baron-revilgaz","npc:captain-greenskin","topic:second-war","zone:deadmines"},src={"Goblin"}}
E["topic:grimtotem-tribe"] = {t="topic",n="Grimtotem tribe",z="general",s="The Grimtotem tribe is an aggressive, fiercely traditionalist tauren clan that refuses to formally swear allegiance to Thrall's Horde. Distinguishable by their dark hides and fierce warpaint, they seek to purge foreign races from Kalimdor and retake ancestral lands by any means necessary. Even as their matriarch, the Elder Crone Magatha Grimtotem, resides atop Thunder Bluff playing regional politics, her kin actively raid settlements across Kalimdor.",h="The Grimtotem tribe is an aggressive, fiercely traditionalist tauren clan that refuses to formally swear...",sec={{t="A Tribe Apart",b="Unlike their peaceful cousins who honor the Earth Mother through reverence, the Grimtotem believe Kalimdor belongs exclusively to the tauren. From youth, they are trained not in the patient harmony of the Great Hunt, but in ruthlessness, stealth, and lethal combat. They harbor immense disdain for the other races that have settled upon their ancient soils. Though Cairne Bloodhoof has welcomed them into Thunder Bluff in an effort to unite all tauren people, the Grimtotem clan stands alone as the only major tribe that has never formally joined Thrall's Horde, maintaining their own insular and violent agenda.",sp=0},{t="Wildland Aggressors",b="In the wilderness far from Thunder Bluff's watchful eyes, Grimtotem hostility flares openly into violence. In Stonetalon Mountains, they butcher local settlements and threaten Horde supply outposts. Across the towering mesas of Thousand Needles, centered around Darkcloud Pinnacle, they constantly menace Freewind Post and plot to seize the canyon. Encounters in Feralas prove equally perilous: the Grimtotem attack Horde travelers, Alliance caravans, and fellow tauren without hesitation. Horde garrisons at Sun Rock Retreat and Camp Mojache regularly post bounties and dispatch warriors to cut down these violent outcasts.",sp=0},{t="Shadows and Apothecaries",b="Beneath Magatha's veneer of spiritual counsel sits an intricate web of intrigue. The Elder Crone is an ardent sponsor of the tauren's controversial ties with the Forsaken, publicly claiming she wishes to heal the undead, while secretly seeking out toxic plagues and Highborne artifacts. In Thousand Needles, scouts from Freewind Post intercept guarded correspondence between cloaked couriers and Grimtotem chieftains in Darkcloud Pinnacle.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why are the Grimtotem hostile if their leader lives in Thunder Bluff?",a="The Grimtotem tribe never formally joined the Horde. While Cairne Bloodhoof tolerates Magatha Grimtotem on the high mesas of Thunder Bluff in hopes of keeping peace among all tauren, the tribe's roaming warriors reject outside diplomacy. In Stonetalon, Thousand Needles, and Feralas, they attack anyone trespassing on ancestral territory, including other tauren.",al={"why grimtotem red","why do grimtotem attack horde","are grimtotem friendly"},it="hostile"},{q="How can you tell a Grimtotem apart from other tauren?",a="Grimtotem tauren almost universally possess pitch-black fur. They are also known for painting their bodies and faces with distinctive, sharp warpaint in stark white, deep red, or blue.",al={"grimtotem appearance","grimtotem fur color"}},{q="Who leads the Grimtotem?",a="They are ruled by the Elder Crone, Magatha Grimtotem, a powerful shaman residing on the Elder Rise in Thunder Bluff. She attained rule after marrying the previous chieftain, who perished shortly afterward in an unexplained climbing mishap.",al={"grimtotem leader","elder crone"},it="leader"},{q="What is the Grimtotem goal?",a="The Grimtotem aim to purge Kalimdor of what they view as lesser foreign races—including orcs, humans, and night elves—and reclaim all ancestral tauren territories. Unlike Cairne's people, they believe this land should be seized purely through force, deception, and elemental dominion.",al={"what do grimtotem want","grimtotem motive"},it="purpose"},{q="Why does Magatha Grimtotem work with the Forsaken?",a="Publicly, Magatha claims she champions the Forsaken because tauren shamanism and earth wisdom can help cure their affliction and return their humanity. Privately, she seeks their expertise in virulent poisons, rot, and dark magic to eliminate her enemies and consolidate power across Kalimdor.",al={"magatha and undead","grimtotem forsaken"},sp=1,it="allies"},{q="What is the Grimtotem tribe?",a="The Grimtotem are one of the most powerful and aggressive tauren tribes in Kalimdor. Unlike the generally peaceful and spiritual clans united under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, the Grimtotem are militaristic extremists. From childhood, their warriors are trained in ruthless combat, eschewing traditional honorable hunting rites in favor of learning to kill swiftly with any weapon at hand.",al={"who are the grimtotem","grimtotem clan","what is grimtotem"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are the Grimtotem found throughout Kalimdor?",a="You will encounter Grimtotem outposts and warbands across the Stonetalon Mountains, Thousand Needles, and Feralas. They have established fortified positions in these regions to assert dominance over ancestral lands, actively raiding rival settlements and encroaching on both Horde and Alliance territories.",al={"where are grimtotem found","why are grimtotem in stonetalon","grimtotem locations"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Grimtotem are an open shame to the Bloodhoof and other tribes; though Cairne offers them shelter in Thunder Bluff in the name of tauren unity, their warmongering threatens to tear that very unity apart.",["faction:Horde"]="Do not mistake their black hides for allies: out in the barrens, mountains, and marshes of Kalimdor, Grimtotem warriors will ambush Horde scouts on sight.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Grimtotem despise all who cross into Kalimdor, attacking Alliance traders and night elf enclaves indiscriminately.",["race:Undead"]="Magatha Grimtotem is one of the few tauren leaders in Thunder Bluff who openly courts the Forsaken, brokering research into exotic plagues alongside Royal Apothecary Society specialists."},kw={"darkcloud pinnacle","elder crone","freewind post","grimtotem","magatha","tauren tribe","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:magatha-grimtotem","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","subzone:darkcloud-pinnacle","zone:thousandneedles","zone:stonetalon","quest:5064","quest:6548"},src={"Grimtotem tribe"}}
E["topic:harpy"] = {t="topic",n="Harpy",z="general",s="Harpies are predatory, avian humanoids resembling winged night elves who dwell in foul-smelling roosts across Kalimdor and the high mountain passes of Alterac. Infamous for their shrill shrieks and tempestuous elemental magic, they raid unwary travelers and blight the environments they inhabit. Though night elf folklore links them to cursed betrayers of Queen Azshara, scholars trace their lineage to the avian Wild God Aviana.",h="Harpies are predatory, avian humanoids resembling winged night elves who dwell in foul-smelling roosts across...",sec={{t="Winged Scourge of Kalimdor",b="Harpies bear the upper torsos and heads of night elf women, joined to broad feathered wings and wicked talons. Despite their delicate, elven visage—which ironically grows more striking as they age—their nature is thoroughly savage. Originally roosting among the crags of the Stonetalon Mountains, they have aggressively migrated across Kalimdor, terrorizing the arid canyons of Durotar, the sacred boughs of Teldrassil, and the heights of Mulgore. Unlike the wild creatures they hunt, harpies do not live in harmony with nature; they despoil surrounding flora and fauna, turning their nesting grounds into foul, fetid middens littered with the picked-clean bones of their prey.",sp=0},{t="Avian Lineage and Dark Nature",b="Night elf myth often claims harpies originated as treacherous handmaidens cursed by Queen Azshara before the Great Sundering. However, deeper research into antiquity indicates they are instead the wayward progeny of Aviana, the ancient avian Wild God. Following Aviana's fall during the War of the Ancients and the burning of the Mother Tree G'Hanir, many of her feathered brood succumbed to chaos and bitter isolation, abandoning their sacred stewardship of the sky. Over millennia, this despair curdled into pure spite. Today, harpies operate as ruthless matriarchies, where aspiring queens must betray, slaughter, and torment rivals to maintain command.",sp=0},{t="Tempest Magic and Wicked Habits",b="Beyond rending flesh with razor-sharp claws, harpies command dangerous wind and lightning magic, channeling shrieking gales to batter foes from above. They are deeply cunning hunters capable of spoken language, occasionally trading scavenged oddities with subterranean creatures like kobolds in exchange for bright gems and shiny trinkets. Unwary explorers are sometimes beguiled by their elven forms, but physical contact carries grave danger: a harpy's kiss is venomous, rapidly inflicting a violent, wasting fever upon those foolish enough to let their guard down.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where do harpies originally come from?",a="Harpies originally hailed from the jagged peaks of the Stonetalon Mountains in central Kalimdor. From these ancestral heights, their populations expanded rapidly, establishing nests in Teldrassil, the Barrens, Durotar, Mulgore, Feralas, the Thousand Needles, and even across the Great Sea in the snowy crags of Alterac Valley.",al={"harpy origins","harpy homeland","where do harpies live"},it="origin"},{q="Are harpies actually related to night elves?",a="While Kalimdor folklore claims they were once night elf women cursed for betraying Queen Azshara, dwarven research and ancient lore establish that harpies are actually descended from Aviana, the ancient bird goddess who fell during the War of the Ancients.",al={"are harpies night elves","harpy night elf connection","curse of azshara"},it="ties"},{q="Why are harpy nests so infamous?",a="Unlike wild birds, harpies defile the land around them. Their nests are filthy, stench-ridden middens composed of trampled foliage, noxious waste, and the butchered bones and remnants of unwary travelers and beasts they drag from nearby trails.",al={"harpy smell","what are harpy nests like","harpy roosts"},it="danger"},{q="How do harpy covens choose their leaders?",a="Harpy leadership is fiercely contested. To claim and hold the title of queen or matriarch, a harpy must prove herself far more ruthless than her roost-mates, employing betrayal, murder, and relentless cruelty against enemies and rivals alike.",al={"harpy matriarch","harpy queen","who leads harpies"},it="leader"},{q="What powers and combat tactics do harpies use?",a="Common harpies dive from above using crude weapons or razor-sharp talons. Their spellcasters, however, wield tempestuous storm magic, striking foes with crackling lightning, swirling wind gusts, and shrill, disorienting shrieks.",al={"harpy abilities","harpy magic","how do harpies fight"},it="power"},{q="Is it true that a harpy's kiss is dangerous?",a="Yes. Beyond their claws and storm magic, harpies carry a toxic kiss. Travelers who are lured by their elven features into letting one close risk suffering from a potent poison that induces a sudden, debilitating fever.",al={"harpy kiss","harpy poison","harpy fever"},it="threat"},{q="What are harpies?",a="Harpies are vicious, intelligent avian creatures resembling a hybrid of a female night elf and a predatory bird, possessing graceful wings for arms and wicked talons for feet. Rather than harmonizing with the wilderness, they actively despoil their surroundings, slaughtering local wildlife and building foul, noxious nests. They wield primal storm magic alongside their talons and crude weaponry, causing misery wherever they roost.",al={"what is a harpy","who are harpies","harpy lore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are harpies hostile to travelers?",a="Harpies attack on sight because they are territorial, predatory, and thrive on causing havoc and sorrow. They expand aggressively from their roosts, ambushing travelers, raiding supply caravans, and killing nearly every creature that wanders into their lands to litter their nests with the grisly remains of their victims.",al={"why do harpies attack","are harpies friendly","why are harpies aggressive"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Do harpies have any allies?",a="Harpies rarely tolerate outsiders, but they are known to maintain loose ties with kobolds. Because harpies covet shiny gems and ores, kobolds trade these scavenged treasures in exchange for items like harpy earwax, and in some regions, kobolds offer tribute to harpies in exchange for protection.",al={"who do harpies work with","harpy allies","who aligns with harpies"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Why are harpies found all across Kalimdor?",a="Harpies were once confined to their ancestral roosts in the crags of the Stonetalon Mountains. Over time, their growing numbers and aggressive nature drove covens to migrate outward across the continent. You can now encounter their foul roosts anywhere from the forests of Teldrassil to the mesas of Mulgore, Durotar, and the Barrens.",al={"where do harpies live","why are harpies in kalimdor","harpy territory"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Though they bear an uncanny resemblance to night elf women, harpies represent a perversion of the natural skies, despoiling sacred boughs around Teldrassil's Oracle Glade and mocking the memory of the ancient goddess Aviana.",["race:Tauren"]="The Windfury harpies of Mulgore and the Screeching brood of Thousand Needles have plagued tauren caravans and blighted the plains for generations, making them reviled enemies of the tribes.",["race:Orc"]="The Dustwind harpies roosting in Drygulch Ravine and Razorwind Canyon persistently menace supply routes between Sen'jin Village, Razor Hill, and Orgrimmar, requiring constant armed patrols.",["class:Druid"]="Druids hold particular sorrow and anger toward harpies, recognizing them as the degraded, chaotic descendants of Aviana who abandoned the sacred balance to pollute the wilderness with tempest magic."},kw={"aviana","bloodfeather","dustwind","harpy","kalimdor","stonetalon","talons","windfury","witchwing"},rel={"zone:stonetalon","quest:876","zone:durotar","zone:mulgore","zone:barrens","subzone:oracle-glade","topic:night-elf","topic:tauren"},src={"Harpy"}}
E["topic:highborne"] = {t="topic",n="Highborne",z="general",s="The Highborne, or quel'dorei, were the aristocratic sorcerer caste of the ancient Kaldorei Empire who served Queen Azshara. Their reckless manipulation of the Well of Eternity brought the Burning Legion to Azeroth, leading to the devastating War of the Ancients and the Great Sundering.",h="The Highborne, or quel'dorei, were the aristocratic sorcerer caste of the ancient Kaldorei Empire who served...",sec={{t="Aristocrats of the Well",b="Before the Sundering, the Highborne arose as the favored upper class surrounding Queen Azshara. Claiming superiority over common kaldorei through selective bloodlines, intelligence, and opulent privilege, they strictly controlled access to the Well of Eternity. They discovered how to tap and shape its primal arcane energies, erecting magnificent cities across ancient Kalimdor. Their endless thirst for greater sorcery led them to draw deeper from the Well, turning its calm waters turbulent and dark.",sp=0},{t="The Betrayal and the Sundering",b="The ripples of arcane power created by Highborne spellcraft caught the attention of the dark titan Sargeras and the Burning Legion. Convinced by Azshara and her counselor Xavius that the demons would remake the world into an untainted paradise, the Highborne opened a massive gateway within the Well itself. This unleashed the catastrophic War of the Ancients. As demonic hordes slaughtered the populace, rebel forces rallied by Malfurion Stormrage fought to close the portal, causing the Well of Eternity to implode and tear the supercontinent apart.",sp=0},{t="Scattered Heirs and Remnants",b="The Highborne fate splintered after the Sundering. Those dragged beneath the waves alongside Azshara were twisted into serpentine naga, while others fell to demonic curses and became satyrs. A faction under Dath'Remar Sunstrider aided the resistance but refused to abandon arcane magic, ultimately suffering exile across the sea to found the high elven kingdom of Quel'Thalas. Today, isolated enclaves like the Shen'dralar remain cloistered in Eldre'Thalas, shunned relics of an empire that nearly destroyed Azeroth.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does 'Highborne' mean?",a="In Darnassian, they are known as the quel'dorei, which translates directly to 'children of noble birth.' They chose the name to distinguish their aristocratic bloodlines and magical aptitude from the rest of kaldorei society.",al={"queldorei meaning","what does queldorei mean","highborne translation"},it="name"},{q="Are modern night elves related to the Highborne?",a="Yes. The Highborne were merely the noble, magic-wielding ruling caste of the ancient night elves. Aside from being slightly paler and prone to flamboyant dress, they were physically the same race. After the Sundering, modern night elf society banned arcane magic and abolished the caste entirely.",al={"are night elves highborne","difference between night elves and highborne","highborne night elf connection"},it="people"},{q="How did the Highborne cause the War of the Ancients?",a="Their excessive and unchecked use of the Well of Eternity sent magical ripples across the cosmos, attracting the Burning Legion. Enticed by Sargeras's promises of unmatched power and an idealized world, Azshara and her nobles channeled the Well to create a portal, inviting the demon army directly into Kalimdor.",al={"why did burning legion come","how did highborne summon demons","highborne war of ancients role"},it="history"},{q="Did all Highborne side with Queen Azshara?",a="No. Several factions broke away when they saw the demonic devastation. Sorcerers led by Dath'Remar Sunstrider defected to join Malfurion's Kaldorei Resistance, while groups like the Shen'dralar in Eldre'Thalas focused on surviving and defending their own regional holdings.",al={"good highborne","did any highborne fight azshara","highborne resistance"},it="side"},{q="What happened to the surviving Highborne after the war?",a="Their lineage scattered across several destinies. Many who sank with Zin-Azshari became the naga, others were twisted into satyrs, and those who followed Dath'Remar were exiled to the Eastern Kingdoms, evolving into high elves. A few, like the Shen'dralar in Dire Maul, remained cloistered in ancient ruins.",al={"what became of the highborne","where are the highborne now","highborne descendants"},it="origin"},{q="Who are the Shen'dralar?",a="The Shen'dralar are an ancient sect of Highborne scholars and arcanists who successfully defended the southern city of Eldre'Thalas during the Great Sundering. Cut off from Queen Azshara and mainstream kaldorei society, they retreated into the city—now called Dire Maul—relying on dark sorceries to preserve their immortality.",al={"dire maul highborne","eldre thalas elves","who lives in dire maul"},it="residents"},{q="Who were the Highborne?",a="The Highborne—or quel'dorei—were the wealthy aristocratic caste of the ancient night elven empire, serving as the favored courtiers and sorcerers of Queen Azshara. Distinguishing themselves through hereditary privilege and relentless study of arcane magic from the Well of Eternity, they grew arrogant and detached from ordinary kaldorei. Their reckless magical experimentation and pact with Sargeras brought about the Burning Legion's first invasion ten thousand years ago.",al={"what are highborne","who are the highborne","quel'dorei night elves"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Who leads the Highborne?",a="In ancient times, the Highborne answered directly to Queen Azshara, their beloved and tyrannical monarch. Following the Great Sundering and the collapse of the Kaldorei Empire, the caste fragmented into scattered remnants. Surviving enclaves like the Shen'dralar in Dire Maul follow their own local rulers, while the Highborne who fled across the sea formed new lineages, leaving no single leader over the ancient caste today.",al={"highborne leader","who rules the highborne","queen azshara highborne"},it="leader",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The memory of the Highborne caste remains a deep scar on night elven culture, serving as the sole reason arcane magic is outlawed under pain of exile or death across Kalimdor.",["class:Mage"]="The Highborne were the first mortal practitioners of mortal arcane magic on Azeroth, whose breathtaking achievements and catastrophic mistakes established the dangerous rules of modern spellcasting.",["class:Druid"]="Druidism arose as the natural philosophy that opposed the Highborne's reckless exploitation of the Well of Eternity, providing the foundation for healing Kalimdor after the empire fell.",["class:Warlock"]="The Highborne were the first mortals on Azeroth to treat with the Burning Legion, bartering cosmic secrets and demonic pacts that paved the way for modern fel studies."},kw={"azshara","dath'remar","dire maul","eldre'thalas","highborne","quel'dorei","sargeras","shen'dralar","well of eternity","zin-azshari"},rel={"topic:war-of-the-ancients","topic:night-elf","subzone:dire-maul","topic:burning-legion","topic:naga","topic:satyr","npc:malfurion-stormrage","zone:azshara"},src={"Highborne"}}
E["topic:horde"] = {t="topic",n="Horde",z="general",s="The Horde is a coalition of outcast nations united under Warchief Thrall to secure their mutual survival and freedom. Anchored by the shamanic bond of the orcs, Darkspear trolls, and tauren across Kalimdor, the faction also maintains an uneasy alliance of convenience with the Forsaken in Lordaeron. Beset by historical grievances and hostile neighbors, the Horde values strength, action, and honor over political maneuverings.",h="The Horde is a coalition of outcast nations united under Warchief Thrall to secure their mutual survival and...",sec={{t="A Reborn Union",b="The modern Horde bears little resemblance to the demonic army that marched through the Dark Portal in the First and Second Wars. After shattering the Burning Legion's grasp upon his people, Warchief Thrall led the orcs across the Great Sea to Kalimdor to build a spiritual homeland founded on ancestral traditions and honor. Along the way, Thrall forged lasting ties with Vol'jin's displaced Darkspear trolls and Cairne Bloodhoof's tauren tribes, fighting side by side to drive off centaur and demonic incursions during the Third War.",sp=0},{t="The Kalimdor Core",b="In Kalimdor, the orcs carved out the nation of Durotar and built the fortress capital of Orgrimmar, offering the Darkspear refuge upon the nearby Echo Isles. Just south, in the lush plains of Mulgore, the nomadic tauren unified around Thunder Bluff under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. Sharing shamanic reverence, a deep bond with nature, and mutual survival needs, these three races view each other not as tactical assets, but as an enduring family bound by spilled blood and spiritual respect.",sp=0},{t="An Alliance of Convenience",b="Across the sea in the ruined lands of Lordaeron, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner led the free-willed Forsaken out of the Scourge's grasp. Desperate for allies against surviving human factions and remnants of the Scourge, Sylvanas dispatched emissaries to both Stormwind and the Horde. While her messengers were turned away by the humans, Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem argued the undead could be spiritually redeemed. Persuaded by Cairne Bloodhoof, Thrall admitted the Forsaken into the Horde, establishing a vital military outpost in the Eastern Kingdoms despite persistent misgivings among Kalimdor's ranks.",sp=0},{t="A Cold War",b="Despite Thrall's hopes to build an enduring peace with the Alliance, the legacy of the Old Horde casts an inescapable shadow. To human and dwarven eyes, orcs remain bloodthirsty invaders, and undead can never be trusted. Clashes over vital lumber in Ashenvale and skirmishes along territorial borders have plunged both factions into a tense, fragile cold war. Thrall actively directs campaigns against demon cults like the Burning Blade to prove the Horde has severed ties with the Legion, but deep-seated hatred threatens to reignite all-out war at any spark.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the difference between the Old Horde and the New Horde?",a="The Old Horde was a blood-crazed conqueror host manipulated by the Burning Legion into invading Azeroth during the First and Second Wars. The New Horde, founded by Warchief Thrall, rejected demonic corruption, returned to ancestral shamanism, and reorganized as a defensive alliance of outcasts seeking survival, land, and spiritual renewal.",al={"old horde vs new horde","how is this horde different","is this the same horde"},it="history"},{q="Who leads the Horde?",a="The faction is led supreme by Warchief Thrall, based in the Valley of Wisdom within Orgrimmar. While Thrall directs the military and diplomatic affairs of the entire Horde, he relies on the counsel and leadership of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof of the tauren, Shadow Hunter Vol'jin of the Darkspear, and Lady Sylvanas Windrunner of the Forsaken.",al={"who is the horde leader","horde warchief","who rules the horde"},it="leader"},{q="Why are the Forsaken part of the Horde?",a="Surrounded by hostile Alliance remnants and the Scourge in Lordaeron, Sylvanas Windrunner sent ambassadors seeking survival allies. Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem believed the Forsaken could be spiritually healed and urged Cairne Bloodhoof to advocate on their behalf. Cairne convinced a reluctant Thrall to accept them, gaining the Horde a critical defensive foothold in the Eastern Kingdoms.",al={"why are undead in horde","how did forsaken join horde","tauren helped undead join"},it="ties"},{q="Where is the capital of the Horde?",a="The fortified city of Orgrimmar, built into the crags of northern Durotar in Kalimdor, serves as the Horde's administrative and military center. It houses Thrall's throne as well as enclaves for troll, tauren, and Forsaken delegates.",al={"horde capital","what is the horde main city","where does thrall rule"},it="place"},{q="Are the Horde and Alliance actively at war right now?",a="Formally, the factions exist under an uneasy truce negotiated following the Battle of Mount Hyjal. However, border conflicts over lumber in Ashenvale, territorial friction in the Alterac Mountains, and deep-seated historical hatred keep both sides locked in a simmering cold war marked by localized skirmishes.",al={"are we at war with alliance","horde alliance war classic","is it peace or war"}},{q="Do all trolls belong to the Horde?",a="No. The Darkspear tribe is the primary troll member of the Horde, having sailed to Kalimdor with Thrall. Most other troll tribes across Azeroth are hostile, though certain groups, like the Revantusk tribe of the Hinterlands, maintain a separate pact of friendship and mutual defense without formally joining.",al={"which trolls are horde","are forest trolls horde","trolls in horde"},it="people"},{q="What is the Horde?",a="The Horde is a coalition of four disparate races—orcs, tauren, Darkspear trolls, and the Forsaken—banded together for mutual survival in an unforgiving world that would see them destroyed. Reborn under Warchief Thrall after breaking free from demonic corruption, this New Horde values strength, honor, and freedom. Rather than conquering, these outcasts and spiritual allies stand united to protect their newfound homes in Kalimdor and Lordaeron from enemies on all sides.",al={"what is horde","who are the horde","horde faction explained"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why does the Horde fight the Alliance?",a="Deep-seated mistrust and painful history keep both sides locked in a tense cold war. The Alliance remembers the brutality of the demonic Old Horde and struggles to believe the orcs have genuinely changed, while also viewing the undead Forsaken as monstrous abominations. Territorial skirmishes, scarce resources, and old grudges flare up constantly across the world. Even though leaders like Thrall seek peace and distance from the past, the Alliance's lingering suspicion makes open conflict an ever-present reality.",al={"why hate alliance","horde alliance war reason","why fight the alliance"},it="conflict",add=true},{q="What does it mean to serve the Horde?",a="Serving the Horde means fighting for the survival, freedom, and honor of peoples cast aside by the rest of Azeroth. You are expected to stand beside your comrades as family and answer threats with action and strength rather than empty diplomacy. Whether securing vital timber in contested forests, hunting alongside tauren braves, or defending new settlements from hostile neighbors, your blade protects an alliance of outcasts striving to carve out a permanent place in the world.",al={"serving the horde","horde duty","what does the horde expect"},it="for_you",add=true}},ang={["race:Orc"]="The New Horde is your people's path to redemption, casting off the legacy of the demon blood to reclaim ancestral shamanic honor under Thrall's banner.",["race:Tauren"]="By standing shoulder-to-shoulder with the orcs during the centaur wars, the tribes preserved their sacred lands in Mulgore and found steadfast spiritual brothers.",["race:Troll"]="Rescued from destruction on their island home by Thrall's fleet, the Darkspear tribe pledged their spears and shadows to the survival of the Horde.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken membership within the Horde is a vital shield against the Living who would purge Lordaeron, though many in Kalimdor eye your methods with quiet caution.",["faction:Alliance"]="To human, dwarven, and elven eyes, the Horde remains an assembly of monstrous savages and treacherous corpses that cannot be trusted to keep any peace."},kw={"cairne bloodhoof","darkspear","durotar","forsaken","horde","kalimdor","new horde","orgrimmar","tauren","thrall","vol'jin","warchief"},rel={"npc:thrall","zone:orgrimmar","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","npc:vol-jin","npc:sylvanas-windrunner","topic:old-horde","topic:alliance","quest:787"},src={"Horde"}}
E["topic:house-of-nobles"] = {t="topic",n="House of Nobles",z="general",s="The House of Nobles is the hereditary aristocratic council that helps administer the kingdom of Stormwind from Stormwind Keep. Holding immense leverage over regional land, taxation, and trade, their refusal to pay the rebuilt city's laborers ignited the bloody riots that gave birth to the Defias Brotherhood.",h="The House of Nobles is the hereditary aristocratic council that helps administer the kingdom of Stormwind...",sec={{t="Aristocrats of the Realm",b="Composed of Stormwind's most powerful patrician bloodlines, the House of Nobles serves as the primary advisory body to the Crown. Because the aristocracy owns the majority of the arable land throughout Elwynn, Westfall, and Duskwood, they wield outsized authority over commerce, regional magistrates, and royal decrees. While the boy-king Anduin Wrynn and Highlord Bolvar Fordragon officially rule the kingdom, the council routinely ties the throne's hands in red tape, asserting the power to stall trials, block executions, and meddle in provincial administration.",sp=0},{t="The Labor Revolt",b="Following the Second War, the House of Nobles contracted Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef and the Stonemasons Guild to rebuild Stormwind City from its ashes. However, the nobility simultaneously incurred staggering debts while expanding the kingdom's military frontier south into Stranglethorn. When the gleaming capital was finished, the nobles balked at the invoice and refused to pay the workers their full wages. Enraged by the betrayal, the masons rioted in the streets. A stone thrown during the chaos struck and killed Queen Tiffin Wrynn, prompting the nobility to banish the guild, driving the ruined workers into Westfall to form the Defias Brotherhood.",sp=0},{t="Bureaucratic Paralysis",b="With King Varian missing, the nobles have largely abandoned the kingdom's outlying territories to fend for themselves while exploiting royal authority for petty squabbles. Officials in Darkshire seethe because the House of Nobles countermanded their death sentence for the grave robber Dextren Ward, ordering him spared and transferred to the Stormwind Stockade instead. Similarly, the execution of the Blackrock Orc chieftain Targorr the Dread has been indefinitely delayed simply because a scheming noble wishes to use the monster as a political pawn, leaving local sentries to watch Stormwind rot from within.",sp=1},{t="Whispers of Treason",b="The council's obstinacy is far from simple greed. In truth, Lady Katrana Prestor—secretly the black dragon Onyxia—has systematically manipulated the House of Nobles to paralyze the kingdom. By stoking the aristocracy's arrogance during the stonemason negotiations and encouraging them to hoard royal funds, she orchestrated both the realm's internal collapse and the King's kidnapping. High-ranking aristocrats like Lord Gregor Lescovar actively conspire alongside the Defias Brotherhood in the shadow of the throne, ensuring no imperial troops march to save the outlying towns.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is the House of Nobles?",a="The House of Nobles is Stormwind's ruling aristocratic assembly, composed of prominent landowning families whose titles and seats are passed down through inheritance. They serve as advisors to the monarch and control much of the kingdom's domestic policy, taxes, and labor laws.",al={"what is the house of nobles","who are the nobles in stormwind","house of nobles lore"},it="identity"},{q="Why did the House of Nobles refuse to pay the Stonemasons?",a="The nobility heavily overextended Stormwind's treasury by financing costly military expeditions southward into Elwynn and Stranglethorn. When Edwin VanCleef presented the bill for rebuilding Stormwind City, the nobles could not pay, offering a fraction of what was promised and eventually ordering the guild disbanded.",al={"why didn't stonemasons get paid","why did nobles stiff vancleef","defias unpaid"},it="conflict"},{q="Who killed Queen Tiffin Wrynn?",a="During the stonemason riots sparked by the House of Nobles' refusal to pay, an unidentified rioter threw a paving stone into the crowd. It struck Queen Tiffin Wrynn in the head, killing her instantly and permanently derailing any peaceful negotiations between the crown and the workers.",al={"how did tiffin die","queen tiffin death","who threw the rock at the queen"}},{q="Why won't Stormwind send troops to help Westfall or Duskwood?",a="The House of Nobles controls the realm's purse strings and administrative authorizations. The nobility continuously blocks funding, military deployments, and municipal aid to outlying territories, leaving local militias like the People's Militia and the Night Watch entirely isolated.",al={"why no help for duskwood","why does stormwind ignore westfall","why won't stormwind send army"},sp=1,it="motive"},{q="Who was Lord Baurles K. Wishock?",a="Lord Baurles K. Wishock is an influential noble lobbying for an investigation of the warlocks beneath The Slaughtered Lamb. The consequences of his inquiry are uncovered in the warlock quest chain.",al={"lord wishock","baurles wishock","noble murdered by warlocks"},sp=1},{q="Did all the nobles turn against the Stonemasons?",a="No. Queen Tiffin Wrynn originally supported the masons, as did Count S. J. Erlgadin, who lobbied fiercely to improve their wages and working conditions. Erlgadin grew so disgusted by his peers' betrayal of the guild that he renounced his title and seat entirely, fleeing society to join Hemet Nesingwary's expedition in Stranglethorn.",al={"did any nobles help stonemasons","count erlgadin","nobles who supported masons"}},{q="What is the history of the House of Nobles?",a="The House of Nobles predates the First War, representing the kingdom's wealthiest aristocratic landholders. Most of its bloodlines were nearly wiped out when the Old Horde sacked Stormwind City. After the Second War, Prince Varian Wrynn restored the assembly with ministers and advisors to oversee domestic recovery. The House oversaw the rebuilding of Stormwind by hiring the Stonemasons' Guild, but ran up heavy debts through military ventures, sparking bitter disputes, the death of Queen Tiffin, and years of political paralysis.",al={"history of house of nobles","house of nobles origins","where did the nobles come from"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who leads the House of Nobles?",a="The House of Nobles has no single presiding ruler; it is a hereditary council of powerful aristocrats that advises the throne. With King Varian missing, the boy-king Anduin Wrynn and Highlord Bolvar Fordragon hold formal executive authority, but court counselor Lady Katrana Prestor commands immense sway over the nobility. Her whispers guide much of their policy, keeping the assembly divided, fearful, and hesitant to commit royal troops to the outlying frontiers.",al={"leader of house of nobles","who controls the house of nobles","who is in charge of the nobles"},it="leader",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="For humans of Westfall and Duskwood, the House of Nobles represents arrogant highborn who sit comfortably in their marble palace while common folk bleed to defend their homesteads from bandits and monsters.",["class:Rogue"]="The nobility's bitter squabbles, corrupt dealings, and dirty secrets provide ample work for fixers, cutpurses, and the master cheesemonger Elling Trias.",["class:Warlock"]="Stormwind's practitioners of the dark arts beneath The Slaughtered Lamb face unwanted inquiries from influential nobles.",["faction:Alliance"]="While powerful within Stormwind City's court, the House of Nobles holds little sway over the wider Grand Alliance, though their stinginess constantly threatens the southern human territories' stability."},kw={"baurles wishock","defias","erlgadin","gregor lescovar","house of nobles","katrana prestor","nobles","stonemasons guild","tiffin wrynn","vancleef"},rel={"topic:defias-brotherhood","topic:stonemasons-guild","topic:stormwind-kingdom","subzone:stormwind-keep","npc:edwin-vancleef","npc:onyxia","npc:lord-baurles-k-wishock","quest:434"},src={"House of Nobles"}}
E["topic:human"] = {t="topic",n="Human",z="general",s="Humans are an adaptable and resilient mortal race who have shaped much of the Eastern Kingdoms' history. Once unified under the ancient Empire of Arathor, their civilization fractured into the Seven Kingdoms, with Stormwind now standing as humanity's primary surviving bastion within the Alliance.",h="Humans are an adaptable and resilient mortal race who have shaped much of the Eastern Kingdoms' history.",sec={{t="The Empire of Arathor",b="Millennia ago, early humans lived as scattered, nomadic tribes constantly threatened by the Amani forest trolls. Recognizing that disunity would mean extinction, Warlord Thoradin of the Arathi tribe united the disparate clans through diplomacy and conquest, establishing the Empire of Arathor with its fortress capital at Strom. During the Troll Wars, the beleaguered high elves of Quel'Thalas allied with Arathor, teaching one hundred humans the arts of arcane magic. Together, they broke the troll armies, securing humanity's dominion over the central continents and laying the foundation for future mortal alliances.",sp=0},{t="Fracture into Seven Kingdoms",b="As Arathor grew secure and prosperous, its outlying settlements became powerful, self-sufficient city-states. Over time, the centralized empire splintered into seven distinct realms: Gilneas, Alterac, Dalaran, Lordaeron, Kul Tiras, Stormwind, and Stromgarde (the original home of Strom). While these nations traded vigorously with dwarves, gnomes, and elves, peace was fragile. Territorial disputes, border skirmishes—such as the perennial mountain feuds between Alterac and Stromgarde—and courtly intrigue consumed the kingdoms, creating deep rivalries that persisted until external catastrophes forced their hand.",sp=0},{t="Invasion and Ruin",b="The arrival of the orcish Horde through the Dark Portal shattered humanity's internal squabbles. Stormwind was razed during the First War, its refugees fleeing north to warn the other realms. In response, King Terenas Menethil II helped forge the Alliance of Lordaeron, combining the strength of mankind, dwarves, gnomes, and high elves to defeat the Horde in the Second War. Though Stormwind was painstakingly rebuilt, tragedy struck again when the Scourge plague ravaged Lordaeron and Dalaran fell during the Third War, leaving the northern human realms in smoking ruin.",sp=0},{t="A Precarious Present",b="Today, Stormwind stands as the strongest remaining human kingdom and the linchpin of the grand Alliance. However, peace remains elusive. King Varian Wrynn has mysteriously vanished while en route to a diplomatic summit, leaving his ten-year-old son, Anduin Wrynn, crowned king while Highlord Bolvar Fordragon serves as regent. To the north, the survivors of Lordaeron are scattered or dead, while Gilneas hides behind its colossal Greymane Wall, leaving Stormwind to bear the weight of mankind's defense.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What were the Seven Kingdoms of humanity?",a="The Seven Kingdoms arose when the ancient Empire of Arathor fractured into independent city-states: Lordaeron, Stormwind, Stromgarde, Dalaran, Kul Tiras, Gilneas, and Alterac. Each developed its own culture, from the seafaring fleets of Kul Tiras to the arcane colleges of Dalaran.",al={"seven human kingdoms","human nations","arathor kingdoms"},it="history"},{q="Where did humans originally come from?",a="Humans emerged thousands of years ago as nomadic tribes roaming the forests and highlands of the northern Eastern Kingdoms, particularly around Tirisfal Glades, before uniting into the first human nation under Warlord Thoradin.",al={"human origin","ancient humans","human ancestors"},it="origin"},{q="What happened to the kingdom of Lordaeron?",a="Lordaeron was devastated during the Third War by the Scourge's plague of undeath and betrayed by its own prince, Arthas Menethil. Today, its ruined capital and surrounding lands are contested between the undead Forsaken, the Scourge, and remnants like the Scarlet Crusade.",al={"why is lordaeron destroyed","fall of lordaeron","fate of lordaeron"},it="ruin"},{q="Who currently rules humanity and Stormwind?",a="With King Varian Wrynn missing under suspicious circumstances, his young son Anduin Wrynn sits upon the throne as a figurehead, while Highlord Bolvar Fordragon rules as Regent alongside royal advisor Lady Katrana Prestor.",al={"who leads stormwind","king of stormwind","stormwind ruler"},it="leader"},{q="Why did the high elves teach humans magic?",a="During the ancient Troll Wars, the high elves of Quel'Thalas faced annihilation by the Amani trolls. Desperate for military reinforcements, they made a treaty with Thoradin, ruler of Arathor, agreeing to teach one hundred humans the arcane in exchange for human armies.",al={"elves taught humans magic","first human mages","troll wars magic"},it="allies"},{q="What role did humans play in forming the Alliance?",a="After Stormwind fell to the orcs in the First War, human refugees rallied the northern kingdoms. Under King Terenas of Lordaeron and Lord Lothar of Stormwind, the human realms united with Ironforge dwarves, gnomes, and high elves to create the Alliance of Lordaeron.",al={"formation of alliance","alliance of lordaeron origin","how did alliance start"},it="history"},{q="What is the story of humanity?",a="Humans began as scattered, nomadic tribes throughout the Eastern Kingdoms. Faced with the threat of the Amani troll empire, the Arathi tribe united mankind into the great Empire of Arathor. In exchange for military aid against the trolls, high elven magi taught the first one hundred humans the arts of the arcane. Over centuries of expansion, Arathor eventually fractured into the Seven Kingdoms: Stormwind, Lordaeron, Stromgarde, Kul Tiras, Gilneas, Dalaran, and Alterac. Despite their internal rivalries, humans became the driving force behind the Grand Alliance when the orcish Horde invaded.",al={"human lore","who are humans","history of humans"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do humans stand in the world today?",a="Mankind is reeling from unimaginable catastrophe. The Scourge and the Third War obliterated northern kingdoms like Lordaeron and Dalaran, leaving millions dead or turned into undead thralls. To the south, Stormwind rebuilt from its First War ruin to become humanity's greatest surviving bastion and the heart of the Alliance. Yet even Stormwind is troubled: King Varian Wrynn has mysteriously vanished, leaving his young son Anduin on the throne under Highlord Bolvar Fordragon's regency while internal strife, bandits, and monsters threaten its borders.",al={"humanity state today","where are humans now","state of humans"},it="today",add=true},{q="What does it mean for a new adventurer to be human?",a="As a human adventurer, you step into a fractured realm that relies entirely on your courage and resilience to endure. With northern realms like Lordaeron lost to the Scourge, Stormwind stands as humanity's last great sanctuary, but its military is stretched desperately thin. Starting in Northshire Valley, you take up arms under the banner of the Lion. Your duty is to defend local towns from defias outlaws, gnolls, and orcs, proving that mankind will adapt, rebuild, and survive any darkness that threatens it.",al={"playing a human","human adventurer role","why play human"},it="for_you",rc="Human",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="As a human, you inherit the legacy of fallen Arathor and ruined Lordaeron; the defense and survival of the mortal realms rests squarely on the shoulders of Stormwind.",["faction:Alliance"]="Human diplomacy and resolve forged the Alliance of Lordaeron, and Stormwind remains the faction's political and military heart.",["class:Mage"]="Human wizardry dates back to the Troll Wars, when the high elves taught the first One Hundred human mages, eventually leading to the founding of Dalaran.",["class:Paladin"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand were founded by human holy men and knights in Lordaeron to combat the horrors of the Second War.",["faction:Horde"]="Humans were the first enemies the orcs encountered upon crossing through the Dark Portal, instigating three brutal wars for survival on Azeroth."},kw={"alliance","arathor","human","lordaeron","mankind","stormwind","thoradin","troll wars","wrynn"},rel={"topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:alliance","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","topic:first-war","topic:second-war","topic:third-war","topic:lordaeron","zone:stormwind"},src={"Human"}}
E["topic:hunter"] = {t="topic",n="Hunter",z="general",s="Masters of wilderness survival and deadly marksmanship, hunters roam the untamed reaches of Azeroth alongside fierce beast companions. Armed with bows, rifles, or crossbows, they excel at harassing enemies from a distance while relying on clever traps and primal aspects to control the flow of battle. Whether tracking elusive quarry across snowcapped peaks or stalking prey through dense forests, they live by their wits and connection to nature.",h="Masters of wilderness survival and deadly marksmanship, hunters roam the untamed reaches of Azeroth alongside...",sec={{t="Kinship with the Wild",b="At the heart of every hunter lies an instinctual empathy for the primal world. Unlike those who exploit nature or isolate themselves within stone bastions, hunters immerse themselves entirely in untamed lands. They study the habits, movements, and strengths of predatory beasts, earning their respect rather than breaking their spirits. Through patient bonding, a hunter tames dangerous creatures—such as fierce prowling cats, stubborn mountain bears, and sharp-eyed raptors—turning wild animals into steadfast allies that defend them to the death.",sp=0},{t="Master Marksmen and Trackers",b="On the battlefield, hunters dictate the terms of engagement from a safe distance. Armed with bows, heavy crossbows, or calibrated rifles, they unleash deadly volleys that pierce through heavy armor before an attacker can even draw near. Their finely honed senses make them peerless trackers, capable of tracing the faintest footsteps, scents, and disturbances left by quarry. When foes attempt to close the distance, hunters deploy frost and flame traps, disorienting ambushes, or agile evasive maneuvers to keep enemies right where they want them.",sp=0},{t="Traditions Across Cultures",b="The path of the hunter manifests differently among the various cultures of Azeroth. Night elf Sentinels embody ancient marksmanship and stealth beneath the canopy of Ashenvale, utilizing bows and shadowcraft in quiet harmony with the wilderness. Dwarven riflemen brave the freezing crags of Dun Morogh alongside hardy bears, mastering the engineering of gunpowder. Across Kalimdor, Tauren and Orc trackers honor ancient nomadic customs, running down their prey with sacred respect and calling upon bestial spirits to guide their shots.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What weapons do hunters use?",a="Hunters specialize in ranged weapons, including bows, crossbows, and rifles. In close quarters, they are also skilled in wielding melee weapons such as polearms, staves, axes, and daggers, often engaging in dual-wielding or using traps to create space and resume firing from a distance.",al={"hunter weapons","what do hunters equip","hunter bows rifles"},gp=true},{q="How do hunters tame beasts?",a="Hunters rely on an innate empathy and deep understanding of wild creatures. By observing their behavior, approaching without malice, and demonstrating strength and respect, they can soothe dangerous predators and form a lifelong companion bond, training them to fight at their side.",al={"how to tame pets","beast taming","tame animal"},gp=true},{q="What armor do hunters wear?",a="Hunters begin their journeys wearing light, flexible leather armor to maintain their agility while traversing rugged terrain. As they grow in experience and face deadlier threats, they learn to wear durable mail armor, providing greater protection without severely impeding their movement.",al={"hunter armor type","can hunters wear mail","hunter gear"},gp=true},{q="What are hunter aspects?",a="Aspects are primal techniques where a hunter attunes their senses and physical reflexes to the innate strengths of wild creatures. By invoking these aspects, a hunter can run with the fleetness of a cheetah, focus their aim like a hawk, or adopt the defensive resilience of a monkey.",al={"hunter aspects","aspect of the cheetah","hunter animal aspects"},gp=true},{q="What happens if an enemy gets too close to a hunter?",a="Hunters are at a distinct disadvantage at close range, where firing a ranged weapon becomes difficult. To survive, they rely on frost and immolation traps, concussive tools, and melee strikes to slow, wing, or disorient attackers while they or their beasts break away to establish distance.",al={"hunter deadzone","close combat hunter","hunter melee"},gp=true},{q="Which races practice the ways of the hunter?",a="Hunting is practiced across nearly all cultures, though it takes varied forms. In the Alliance, Night Elves and Dwarves take up the bow and rifle. In the Horde, Orcs, Tauren, and Trolls revere tracking and beast mastery as essential ancestral traditions of survival.",al={"who can be a hunter","hunter races","playable hunter races"},gp=true},{q="What is a Hunter in the lore?",a="Hunters are masters of the wilderness, expert marksmen, and rugged trackers who heed the call of the wild. Uniquely attuned to nature, they survive in uncharted territories by their wits and knowledge of the creatures they pursue. While deadly with bows, crossbows, rifles, and traps, a hunter is defined by a deep empathy for beasts, forging lifelong bonds with feral companions who fight and scout alongside them against the encroaching threats of the world.",al={"what is a hunter","hunter lore","hunter class lore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does a Hunter's power come from?",a="A hunter's prowess comes from an intimate connection to the natural world, rigorous martial training, and keen observation of wild quarry. Rather than drawing upon arcane study or divine worship, they channel strength through attuning themselves to the primal aspects of wild beasts. Combined with unmatched marksmanship, herbal venoms, and an innate kinship that commands loyalty from ferocious predators, their power lies in complete harmony with the wilderness.",al={"where do hunters get their power","hunter source of power","hunter magic source"},it="power",add=true},{q="What does it mean for a new adventurer to become a Hunter?",a="Stepping out as a hunter means answering an inner call to leave civilized comforts behind for the untamed wilds. You rely entirely on your cunning, your keen senses, and your ranged weapons to survive alone in hostile lands. As you learn to read trails and set ambushes, you will earn the respect of a wild beast, gaining a loyal partner to guard your back as you defend the wilderness from corruption and exploitation.",al={"becoming a hunter","what does it mean to be a hunter","starting as a hunter"},it="for_you",add=true}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="Your strength lies in your connection to the wilds and your companion beast. Master your ranged arsenal, maintain distance, and let your pet hold the enemy's attention.",["race:NightElf"]="For millennia, Kaldorei Sentinels have patrolled ancient forests with bows and trusted sabers, defending sacred groves from outside corruption.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarven marksmen are renowned for their marksmanship with heavy rifles, braving the treacherous mountain passes of Khaz Modan alongside fierce polar bears.",["race:Tauren"]="To the Tauren, the hunt is a sacred spiritual trial of endurance and reverence, honoring the Earth Mother by respecting every beast that feeds and clothes the tribe.",["race:Orc"]="Orcish beastmasters honor ancestral survival traditions forged on harsh worlds, forming unbreakable combat bonds with loyal wolves and boars."},kw={"archery","aspects","beasts","hunter","marksmanship","pet","rifle","survival","tracking","traps"},rel={"topic:sentinels","topic:tauren","topic:dwarf","topic:night-elf","topic:horde","topic:alliance","topic:war-of-the-ancients","topic:gnome"},src={"Hunter"}}
E["topic:juggernaught"] = {t="topic",n="Juggernaught",z="general",s="A Juggernaught is an enormous, heavily armored battleship bristling with heavy naval cannons. Originally engineered by ogres to serve as the iron core of the Old Horde's fleet during the Second War, these floating fortresses were built to pulverize coastal strongholds and trade routes. Their massive iron plating and siege-grade broadsides make them legendary instruments of naval warfare.",h="A Juggernaught is an enormous, heavily armored battleship bristling with heavy naval cannons.",sec={{t="Terror of the Second War",b="Orcs were not traditionally a seafaring culture, so when the Horde required a fleet capable of crossing the Great Sea and contesting the Alliance's naval superiority during the Second War, they relied heavily on ogre ingenuity. The resulting craft—the ogre juggernaught—became the backbone of the Horde armada. Though vastly slower and less nimble than swift troll destroyers, these behemoths carried immense plating and devastating broadside batteries that could shatter Alliance hulls and smash shore fortifications to rubble.",sp=0},{t="Ironclad Cove",b="Hidden beneath the hills of Westfall, deep within the subterranean caverns of the Deadmines, lies Ironclad Cove. There, secluded away from prying eyes and royal inspectors, Edwin VanCleef's craftsmen labored secretly to construct a fully armed juggernaught. By assembling a heavy warship in an underground cavern connected directly to the open sea, the Defias planned to bypass Stormwind's overland defenses and launch a surprise naval strike right into the heart of the kingdom.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is a juggernaught?",a="A juggernaught is a massive, heavily armored naval battleship armed with heavy bombardment cannons. Developed originally by the Horde with ogre aid during the Second War, it functions as a floating fortress built to overpower enemy fleets and shatter coastal defenses.",al={"juggernaut warcraft","orc battleship","ogre juggernaut"},it="identity"},{q="Why is there a huge ship inside the Deadmines?",a="Edwin VanCleef and the Defias Brotherhood used the secluded cavern of Ironclad Cove inside the Deadmines as a hidden shipyard. Constructing the juggernaught underground kept it safe from Alliance patrols while they worked on the vessel.",al={"ship in deadmines","why boat in mines","deadmines ship"},sp=1,it="why_here"},{q="What did Edwin VanCleef intend to do with the ship?",a="The ship is part of VanCleef's activities beneath Westfall. Investigating the Defias Brotherhood reveals what he intended for it.",al={"vancleef boat plan","why did defias build a ship","defias juggernaught purpose"},sp=1,it="motive"},{q="Who originally invented the juggernaught?",a="The Old Horde built juggernaughts with the help of ogres during the Second War. The orcs had little seafaring experience, and these massive warships became the heavy armament of their fleet.",al={"who made juggernauts","ogre ships lore","horde navy origin"},it="origin"},{q="How does the ship get out of the Deadmines cavern?",a="Ironclad Cove connects to the open waters of the Great Sea through a concealed coastal channel along the rocky shores of Westfall, permitting the warship to sail directly out into the ocean once completed.",al={"how does deadmines ship leave","ironclad cove exit","deadmines water exit"},sp=1},{q="Does the Horde still use juggernaughts?",a="Orcish battleships were still known as juggernaughts during the Third War. Their role was to provide the heavy firepower of the Horde fleet; the current deployment of such vessels is uncertain.",al={"modern horde juggernauts","horde battleships"},it="today"},{q="What is the history of the juggernaught?",a="Juggernaughts were first constructed during the Second War to serve as the iron core of the Old Horde's naval armada. Because the orcs possessed little naval tradition, they relied heavily on ogre craftsmanship and brute force to fashion these gargantuan floating fortresses. Bristling with heavy cannons and thick armor, they terrorized the seas of Azeroth and inflicted devastating losses upon the Alliance fleet. Though many were sunk or abandoned following the Horde's ultimate defeat, surviving vessels and salvageable designs continue to be prized by outlaws and warlords alike.",al={"juggernaught history","where did juggernaughts come from","second war juggernaughts"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The discovery of a juggernaught under construction beneath Westfall exposes a grave threat to the safety of Stormwind and King Anduin's court.",["faction:Horde"]="Juggernaughts represent a storied chapter of the Horde's naval heritage from the Second War, proving that raw brute engineering could contest Alliance mastery of the seas.",["race:Human"]="Stormwind's rebuilders, betrayed and turned outlaw under VanCleef, applied their finest architectural and stonework skills into assembling a monstrous warship hidden beneath the hills of Westfall.",["class:Rogue"]="The juggernaught in the Deadmines represents the ultimate audacity of Edwin VanCleef—crafting a capital battleship right beneath the noses of SI:7 and the Stormwind guard."},kw={"battleship","deadmines","defias","ironclad cove","juggernaught","juggernaut","ogre juggernaught","ship","stormwind","vancleef"},rel={"subzone:ironclad-cove","zone:deadmines","npc:edwin-vancleef","topic:defias-brotherhood","zone:stormwind","topic:second-war","topic:horde","quest:65"},src={"Juggernaught"}}
E["topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand"] = {t="topic",n="Knights of the Silver Hand",z="general",s="The Knights of the Silver Hand are the legendary order of holy paladins founded after the First War to combine martial combat with the healing and smiting power of the Holy Light. Though their original northern chapter was broken during the fall of Lordaeron, surviving knights carry on their sacred vows in Stormwind and Ironforge, while scattered remnants battle the undead in the northern Plaguelands.",h="The Knights of the Silver Hand are the legendary order of holy paladins founded after the First War to...",sec={{t="Creation of the Paladins",b="Following the destruction of Stormwind during the First War, Archbishop Alonsus Faol recognized a dire flaw in the Alliance's defenses: the clerics of Northshire were devoted and skilled healers, but they lacked martial training and fell easily on the front lines. To ensure holy warriors could endure the brutal combat of the Second War, Faol and his premier student, Uther the Lightbringer, founded the Order of the Knights of the Silver Hand in Stratholme. Taking inspiration from the legend of Keeper Tyr's silver hand as an emblem of selfless sacrifice, Faol recruited both battle-hardened knights willing to learn devotion to the Light and devout clerics willing to bear plate armor and warhammers.",sp=0},{t="Glory in the Second War",b="The original five paladins—Uther the Lightbringer, Turalyon, Saidan Dathrohan, Tirion Fordring, and Gavinrad the Dire—proved instrumental to the Alliance of Lordaeron. First riding into combat during the Battle of Hillsbrad, the paladins lifted the morale of weary soldiers and countered the sinister necromancy of the Horde's death knights. Throughout the war, the Silver Hand broke peasant rebellions, reclaimed Caer Darrow, and helped defend Capital City against the siege of the orcs. In the years of peace that followed, the paladins guarded Lordaeron's borders, settled civil disputes, and served as shining exemplars of honor, humility, and justice.",sp=0},{t="The Fall and Fractured Remnants",b="Tragedy struck the order from within during the Third War. Prince Arthas Menethil, a decorated knight of the Silver Hand, clashed bitterly with his mentor Uther outside Stratholme and unlawfully suspended the paladins when Uther refused to butcher the infected citizenry. When Arthas later succumbed to corruption and led the Scourge against Lordaeron, Uther and Gavinrad were slain, shattering the order's central leadership. The surviving knights in the north fractured under differing doctrines, eventually birthing the zealous Scarlet Crusade and the vigilant Argent Dawn, while the surviving chapters in Stormwind and Ironforge continue to train new paladins under the classic banner of the Silver Hand.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who founded the Knights of the Silver Hand?",a="The order was created by Archbishop Alonsus Faol in Stratholme following the First War, with Uther the Lightbringer chosen as its very first knight.",al={"who made the silver hand","founder of paladins","who created paladins"},it="origin"},{q="Who were the first five paladins?",a="The first five inducted into the order were Uther the Lightbringer, Turalyon, Saidan Dathrohan, Tirion Fordring, and Gavinrad the Dire, each blessed with a sacred libram by Archbishop Faol.",al={"first paladins","original silver hand members","first five"},it="people"},{q="Why is the order called the Silver Hand?",a="The name and emblem are derived from the ancient legend of Keeper Tyr's silver hand, revered as a profound symbol of selfless sacrifice in the face of evil.",al={"silver hand meaning","why silver hand name","silver hand origin"},it="name"},{q="Does the Silver Hand still exist?",a="Yes. While its northern high command was decimated during the Scourge's destruction of Lordaeron, thriving chapters continue to serve the Alliance out of Stormwind's Cathedral of Light and the dwarven halls of Ironforge.",al={"is silver hand alive","silver hand today","are paladins still around"},it="today"},{q="How are the Scarlet Crusade and Argent Dawn related to the Silver Hand?",a="After the death of Uther and Highlord Alexandros Mograine, the surviving paladins in Lordaeron fractured over ideological differences. Hardline fanatics formed the Scarlet Crusade to eliminate all outsiders, while more moderate veterans founded the Argent Dawn.",al={"scarlet crusade silver hand","argent dawn silver hand","how did the silver hand split"},it="ties"},{q="Can non-humans join the Knights of the Silver Hand?",a="Yes. While initially composed of human knights and clerics from Lordaeron, dwarven priests and warriors of Ironforge were welcomed into the order during and after the Second War, bearing the Silver Hand's insignia and rites.",al={"dwarf paladins silver hand","who can be a silver hand paladin","can dwarves be silver hand"},it="residents"},{q="What is the Order of the Silver Hand?",a="The Knights of the Silver Hand are an order of holy warriors, commonly known as paladins. Formed as the martial branch of the Church of the Holy Light, they blend martial prowess with divine power. Armed with heavy plate, warhammers, and sacred incantations, they serve as paragons of virtue, dedicated to protecting the innocent, healing the wounded, and vanquishing evil.",al={"what are the knights of the silver hand","who are the silver hand","paladins of the silver hand"},it="identity",add=true},{q="What is the history of the Knights of the Silver Hand?",a="Archbishop Alonsus Faol established the order during the Second War after the clergy suffered heavy losses in the First War. He sought to create champions who could fight on the front lines while channeling the Light. Under Uther the Lightbringer, the paladins turned the tide against the Horde. For decades, they defended Lordaeron until Prince Arthas shattered their ranks during the Third War.",al={"history of silver hand","how did the silver hand start","silver hand origin story"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who leads the Knights of the Silver Hand?",a="The order lacks a single supreme leader. Lord Uther the Lightbringer originally commanded the knights until his tragic demise at the hands of Arthas. Highlord Alexandros Mograine briefly rallied the survivors in Lordaeron until his assassination. Today, the remnants in Lordaeron have splintered, while paladin leaders in Stormwind and Ironforge guide the brothers fighting under the Alliance banner.",al={"silver hand leader","who commands the silver hand","leader of paladins"},it="leader",add=true},{q="Who are the Knights of the Silver Hand allied with?",a="The Silver Hand was established to defend humanity and fight for the Grand Alliance. While its original northern chapter fell into ruin during the Third War, active chapters in Stormwind and Ironforge remain staunch champions of the Alliance of Lordaeron's successor nations, fighting alongside humans, dwarves, and high elves against threats across Azeroth.",al={"who does the silver hand fight for","silver hand factions","silver hand alliance"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Every human and dwarven paladin serving the Alliance traces their sacred oaths, librams, and martial tradition directly back to the laws laid down by Archbishop Faol and Uther the Lightbringer.",["class:Priest"]="The Silver Hand was born out of the priesthood's vulnerability during the First War, bridging the gap between divine ministry and heavy warfare.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand were the ideological anchor of the Alliance of Lordaeron, inspiring soldiers across every human realm to stand firm against darkness.",["race:Human"]="Humanity views the Silver Hand as the pinnacle of virtue, honor, and courage, mourning the fall of its great northern masters while honoring their memory in Stormwind."},kw={"alonsus faol","arthas","holy light","libram","paladin","silver hand","stratholme","tirion fordring","turalyon","uther"},rel={"topic:church-of-the-holy-light","topic:light","npc:tirion-fordring","subzone:stratholme","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:second-war","topic:argent-dawn","topic:scarlet-crusade"},src={"Knights of the Silver Hand"}}
E["topic:kobold"] = {t="topic",n="Kobold",z="general",s="Kobolds are diminutive, rat-like subterranean humanoids found scuttling through mines and caverns across Azeroth. Notorious for their obsessive devotion to the tallow candles strapped to their heads, they are individually cowardly but dangerous in swarms, fiercely defending their dig sites against any who threaten their light.",h="Kobolds are diminutive, rat-like subterranean humanoids found scuttling through mines and caverns across...",sec={{t="Miners of the Dark",b="Standing barely four feet tall, kobolds are filthy, subterranean scavengers who spend their lives delving into the earth. Though they possess low-light vision, the deepest shafts are pitch-black, compelling every kobold to affix a crude candle to their head. While cowardly when cornered alone, kobolds breed in massive numbers and work with relentless energy. When agitated, their rat-like tails whip violently, and they overwhelm intruders in frantic mobs, beating foes to death with heavy iron mining picks.",sp=0},{t="You No Take Candle!",b="To a kobold, a candle is far more than a light source; it is an existential shield. They harbor a deep, primal terror of the pitch-black tunnels, believing that the unlit darkness will actively consume them. Their leaders are frequently chosen by the length of the candle they bear, and their craftsmen harvest earwax and rendered fat to keep their wicks lit. Kobold folklore even treats the sun as the ultimate myth: the 'Wickless Candle,' a great eternal flame suspended from a vast blue cavern ceiling.",sp=0},{t="Subterranean Pests of the Kingdoms",b="Across the Eastern Kingdoms, kobolds frequently overrun commercial mines. In Elwynn Forest, a clan displaced from the Redridge Mountains seized the Echo Ridge, Fargodeep, and Jasperlode mines, driven in part by Riverpaw gnolls and the Defias Brotherhood. In Loch Modan, the Tunnel Rat clan seized the Silver Stream Mine from the dwarven Miners' League. Even on Kalimdor, kobolds plague industrial digs, laboring for the Venture Company in Stonetalon or uncovering strange minerals in Thousand Needles.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do kobolds care so much about their candles?",a="Kobolds have a paralyzing terror of complete darkness. In their folklore, darkness is an active predator that snuffs out life and swallows miners whole. A candle's flame is their only defense against the shadowy deep, making them obsessively defensive of their wicks.",al={"why candles","kobold candles","you no take candle meaning"},it="motive"},{q="Where do kobolds come from?",a="According to the dwarven explorer Brann Bronzebeard, kobolds originally evolved from troggs. Over generations of subterranean adaptation, they became smaller, rodent-like, and adapted to burrowing through narrow crawlspaces.",al={"kobold origins","where do kobolds come from","kobold evolution"},it="origin"},{q="Are kobolds allied with the Defias Brotherhood?",a="Not willingly as equal partners. The kobolds infesting mines in Elwynn Forest and Westfall were largely bullied and enslaved by the Riverpaw gnolls, who were hired by the Defias Brotherhood to disrupt regional mining and secure mineral wealth.",al={"kobold defias","are kobolds working for defias","fargodeep defias"},it="allies"},{q="How do kobolds choose their leaders?",a="Outside of distant isolated tribes, kobolds generally determine their leadership through candle size. The digger bearing the tallest, longest-burning candle on their head is recognized by the clan as the chief.",al={"kobold leader","kobold boss","how do kobolds pick chiefs"},it="leader"},{q="Are kobolds found in Kalimdor as well as the Eastern Kingdoms?",a="Yes. Kobolds infested the underground caverns beneath Orgrimmar during the city's founding, work in the Venture Company's Windshear Mine in the Stonetalon Mountains, and burrow throughout Thousand Needles.",al={"kobolds in kalimdor","stonetalon kobolds","orgrimmar kobolds"},it="place"},{q="What do kobolds eat?",a="Kobolds survive on whatever subterranean vermin they can scavenge. They actively hunt and farm rats, harvest cave grubs, and gather glowing cavern fungi, sometimes pressing mushrooms into crude cakes eaten with syrup.",al={"kobold diet","what do kobolds eat"}},{q="What are kobolds?",a="Kobolds are diminutive, rat-like subterranean humanoids standing barely four feet tall. Infamous for their cowardice and foul odor, they make their homes tunneling through caverns and abandoned mines. While individually weak and fearful of larger races, they reproduce rapidly and rely on overwhelming numbers when forced into a fight, swarming intruders with crude mining picks.",al={"what is a kobold","who are the kobolds","kobold info"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are kobolds found infesting so many mines?",a="Kobolds are natural diggers and miners who prefer subterranean dens far from populated surface settlements. They naturally flock to pre-existing caves, quarries, and rich mineral seams. When humanoid settlements leave excavations unattended or lightly defended—such as the mines across Elwynn, Loch Modan, and Stonetalon—opportunistic kobolds quickly burrow in, expand the tunnels, and claim the veins for themselves.",al={"why are kobolds in mines","kobold mines why","where do kobolds live"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="Why are kobolds hostile to travelers?",a="Kobolds are skittish, paranoid, and distrustful of anything larger than themselves. Deep underground, they constantly fear cave-ins, starvation, and the absolute dark. When outsiders enter their territory, kobolds perceive them as an immediate threat come to rob their claims or snuff out their precious head-candles, driving them to attack in frantic, desperate mobs.",al={"why do kobolds attack","are kobolds always hostile","why do kobolds fight"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="What is known about the history of kobolds?",a="Kobolds have inhabited underground Azeroth for millennia, with Arathi settlers encountering them in Tirisfal Glades thousands of years ago. Dwarven scholar Brann Bronzebeard theorizes they evolved from ancient troggs. While largely splintered into local clans, they regularly collide with other races—such as when goblin excavators accidentally breached a subterranean den during the construction of Orgrimmar.",al={"kobold history","how long have kobolds existed","where did kobolds come from history"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["profession:Mining"]="Any miner operating in human or dwarven lands will inevitably run into kobold infestations, as the creatures naturally gravitate toward rich veins of copper, tin, and silver.",["race:Human"]="For the Kingdom of Stormwind, kobolds represent a constant economic headache, having overrun vital local operations at Fargodeep, Jasperlode, and Echo Ridge.",["race:Dwarf"]="The Miners' League regularly employs adventurers to clear Tunnel Rat kobolds out of mountain digs like the Silver Stream Mine in Loch Modan.",["faction:Horde"]="During Orgrimmar's founding, kobold warrens beneath the dry earth caused early construction chaos, and clans like the Gravelsnouts continue to harass frontier outposts in Kalimdor."},kw={"candle","echo ridge","fargodeep","jasperlode","kobold","silver stream","trogg","tunnel rat","windshear"},rel={"quest:60","subzone:fargodeep-mine","subzone:jasperlode-mine","subzone:silver-stream-mine","topic:defias-brotherhood","topic:trogg","npc:brann-bronzebeard","quest:21"},src={"Kobold"}}
E["topic:kolkar-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Kolkar clan",z="general",s="The Kolkar are an aggressive clan of centaur native to central Kalimdor, claiming descent from the legendary first Khan, Kolk. They maintain strongholds in Desolace and across the Barrens, frequently raiding Horde settlements and fighting anyone who crosses their path.",h="The Kolkar are an aggressive clan of centaur native to central Kalimdor, claiming descent from the legendary...",sec={{t="Sons of Kolk",b="The Kolkar take their name from Khan Kolk, the legendary first centaur khan born from the union of Zaetar and Theradras. Driven by a deep hatred for other races and their fellow centaur clans alike, the Kolkar distinguish themselves by their red markings and an absolute refusal to form alliances. Historically based out of their ancient village in eastern Desolace, the restless spirit of Khan Kolk still wanders the subterranean depths of their ancestral lands.",sp=0},{t="The Scourge of the Barrens",b="For generations, the nomadic tauren endured brutal raids from the Kolkar as they traversed the arid Barrens. When the orcs arrived on Kalimdor during the Third War, Thrall allied with Cairne Bloodhoof to push the marauding centaur back and escort the tauren to safety in Mulgore. Defeat did not break the clan; instead, it fueled a lingering blood feud. The Kolkar entrenched themselves around all three vital water sources in the Barrens: the Forgotten Pools, the Lushwater Oasis, and the Stagnant Oasis.",sp=0},{t="Encroachment on Durotar",b="Emboldened by their fortified positions across the savanna, the Kolkar have pushed eastward past the Southfury River into Durotar itself. From Kolkar Crag in the south, raiding parties plot assaults against Sen'jin Village, the Valley of Trials, and the paths approaching Orgrimmar. While senior Horde leadership focuses on larger regional threats, young recruits guided by veterans like Lar Prowltusk are dispatched to cull the centaur advance before their raiding parties can strike the nascent orc capital.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who are the Kolkar centaur?",a="The Kolkar are one of five major centaur clans in Kalimdor. Named after their founding leader, Khan Kolk, they are a fierce and xenophobic tribe recognized by their red armor. They maintain settlements in Desolace, control the Barrens' primary water oases, and run raiding camps in southern Durotar.",al={"kolkar clan","who are kolkar","kolkar lore","centaur in barrens"},it="identity"},{q="Why do the Kolkar hate the Horde so much?",a="The Kolkar hunted and harassed the tauren for generations across the Barrens. When Thrall's Horde arrived, the orcs joined Cairne Bloodhoof to drive the centaur back so the tauren could settle Mulgore. This alliance humiliated the Kolkar Khans, turning centuries of predatory hunting into a bitter war against both orcs and tauren.",al={"why do kolkar attack orcs","kolkar tauren feud","centaur vs horde"},it="hostile"},{q="Why are the Kolkar occupying the Barrens oases?",a="In the arid Barrens, controlling water means controlling survival. The Kolkar have occupied the Forgotten Pools, Lushwater Oasis, and Stagnant Oasis with command tents, tribal totems, and hyena packs, denying these precious watering holes to travelers, merchants, and the Horde.",al={"kolkar at oases","forgotten pools centaur","lushwater oasis centaur"},it="why_here"},{q="Who leads the Kolkar clan?",a="The clan's first ruler was Khan Kolk, whose spirit lingers in Maraudon. In recent times, leadership in the Barrens fell to Khan Dez'hepah and his lieutenants. In Desolace, the clan's traditional stronghold is overseen by Khan Leh'Prah, while local warbands answer to regional khans like the fallen Khan Gragtor.",al={"kolkar leader","kolkar khan","who is the kolkar boss"},it="leader"},{q="What is Kolkar Crag?",a="Kolkar Crag is a rocky canyon outpost in southern Durotar. Centaur warbands established it as a forward staging ground to harass Horde initiates in the Valley of Trials, menace Sen'jin Village, and test the defenses of Orgrimmar.",al={"where is kolkar crag","durotar centaur camp","kolkar in durotar"},it="place"},{q="Do the Kolkar work with other centaur clans?",a="No. The Kolkar share no kinship or treaties with the other four centaur clans native to Desolace. Centaur society is violently fragmented, and the Kolkar wage war against other clans just as readily as they fight the Horde and Alliance.",al={"centaur clans alliance","kolkar allies","do centaur cooperate"},it="allies"},{q="Where did the Kolkar clan come from?",a="The Kolkar are one of the original centaur clans born in Desolace, tracing their bloodline to Khan Kolk, the son of Zaetar and Princess Theradras. Long before the Third War, their roaming warbands pushed east into the Barrens, violently driving the nomadic tauren away from their sacred hunting grounds. When Thrall's newly arrived orcs allied with Cairne Bloodhoof, the united Horde shattered the centaur army and forced them into Mulgore's fringes. Now fractured across Desolace, Durotar, and the Barrens, the Kolkar nurse a bitter grudge and wage constant territorial wars to reclaim their lost dominance.",al={"kolkar history","origin of kolkar","kolkar clan origins"},it="history",add=true},{q="How did the Kolkar clan get their name?",a="The clan takes its name from its founding father, Khan Kolk. As the legendary eldest son of the demigod Zaetar and the earth elemental Princess Theradras, Kolk stood as the first of the great centaur khans who led their brutish descendants out of Maraudon and claimed the barren reaches of central Kalimdor.",al={"why are they called kolkar","kolkar name origin","who is kolk"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Kolkar hunted your people nearly to extinction across the Barrens before the orcs intervened, making every encounter with them a matter of ancestral survival.",["race:Orc"]="Your first pact with the tauren was forged in blood against Kolkar marauders, and their scouts at Kolkar Crag now threaten the Valley of Trials where young orcs come of age.",["faction:Horde"]="Kolkar control over the three Barrens oases cuts off critical trade and water supplies between Durotar, Mulgore, and the Crossroads.",["class:Hunter"]="Kolkar raiding parties rely heavily on trained, semi-domesticated hyenas to track intruders and guard the perimeters of their watering holes."},kw={"barrens","centaur","desolace","dezhepah","durotar","khan","kolk","kolkar","kolkar crag","lehprah","maraudon","oasis"},rel={"topic:centaur","zone:barrens","zone:desolace","subzone:kolkar-crag","topic:tauren","topic:horde","quest:1362","subzone:lushwater-oasis"},src={"Kolkar clan"}}
E["topic:kul-tiras"] = {t="topic",n="Kul Tiras",z="general",s="Kul Tiras is an insular maritime human kingdom situated on an island archipelago in the Great Sea, west of Dun Morogh and Baradin Bay. Renowned for possessing the mightiest navy in Azeroth, it served as a cornerstone of the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War under Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore. Following the fracturing of the old Alliance and tragic losses in Kalimdor, the island realm has largely receded into isolated waters.",h="Kul Tiras is an insular maritime human kingdom situated on an island archipelago in the Great Sea, west of...",sec={{t="A Nation Born of the Sea",b="Centuries ago, human sailors striking out from Gilneas discovered a rugged, mountainous island cluster across the Great Sea rich in metal ores and timber. Settling the coastlines and subduing the native threats of the rugged interior, the colonists forged a thriving maritime society. Kul Tiras blossomed into an unmatched seafaring power, sustained by an industrious merchant marine, rich fishing grounds, and a naval armada without peer in human history.",sp=0},{t="The Second War and Isolation",b="When the Orcish Horde swept through the Eastern Kingdoms, Kul Tiras answered the call of King Terenas Menethil II and committed its armada to the Alliance of Lordaeron. Lord Admiral Daelin Proudmoore commanded the naval forces of humanity, waging bloody warfare across the open ocean to sever the Horde's sea lanes and protect coastal shores. However, following the war's conclusion and the eventual splintering of the northern kingdoms, Kul Tiras pulled back its fleets, withdrawing into heavy coastal defense and quiet insularity.",sp=0},{t="Shadows over the Proudmoores",b="Kul Tiras has felt the bitter sting of distant wars across the sea. When Lady Jaina Proudmoore led refugees across the ocean to Kalimdor to build Theramore, her father, Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore, took a massive contingent of the Kul Tiran fleet to pursue the Horde. His obsessive crusade brought ruin, culminating in his death on the shores of Durotar. Now, Kul Tiras remains isolated and mourning, harboring immense grief and burning hostility toward the Horde and the daughter who failed to save her father.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where is Kul Tiras located?",a="Kul Tiras is an archipelago situated out in the Great Sea, west of Baradin Bay and the coast of Dun Morogh. It is a rugged, cold, and mountainous cluster of islands fortified by massive seawalls and heavy coastal garrisons.",al={"where is kul tiras","kul tiras map","kul tiras location"},it="place"},{q="Is Kul Tiras still part of the Alliance?",a="Kul Tiras was one of the core founding nations of the original Alliance of Lordaeron. However, after the Third War and the catastrophic demise of Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore in Kalimdor, the island kingdom largely withdrew from continental affairs and maintains a posture of stern isolation.",al={"is kul tiras alliance","kul tiras alliance member"},it="allies"},{q="Who rules Kul Tiras?",a="Kul Tiras is governed by the Proudmoore family through the title of Lord Admiral. Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore led the nation for decades until his fateful campaign in Kalimdor, leaving the island nation in deep turmoil and isolation under the remaining Admiralty.",al={"kul tiras leader","who leads kul tiras","king of kul tiras"},it="leader"},{q="Why is Kul Tiras famous across Azeroth?",a="Kul Tiras is legendary for having the greatest maritime fleet, shipwrights, and sailors in the known world. Its warships and vast merchant convoys have dominated the sea lanes for centuries, and its navy was essential in holding off the Horde during the Second War.",al={"what is kul tiras known for","why is kul tiras famous"},it="fame"},{q="How is Kul Tiras connected to Jaina Proudmoore?",a="Lady Jaina Proudmoore is a daughter of Kul Tiras, being the child of Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore. Although born to the Admiralty, she studied in Dalaran and later established the independent port of Theramore Isle on Kalimdor, distancing her from her homeland's leadership.",al={"jaina kul tiras","is jaina from kul tiras"},it="ties"},{q="What threats face Kul Tiras?",a="Beyond their deep-seated enmity for the Horde, the waters around Kul Tiras are continuously plagued by renegade pirate crews and hostile coastal beasts, requiring constant vigilance and fortified seagates to protect shipping lanes.",al={"kul tiras enemies","threats to kul tiras"},it="threat"},{q="What is Kul Tiras?",a="Kul Tiras is an island nation and archipelago situated in the Great Sea west of the Eastern Kingdoms. Renowned for their peerless maritime mastery and trading vessels, the people of Kul Tiras built one of the seven historic human kingdoms. They command the mightiest naval fleet in the world, built on a long tradition of shipwrighting, fishing, and open-ocean exploration.",al={"what is kul tiras","kul tiras kingdom","kul tiras nation"},it="identity",add=true},{q="What is the history of Kul Tiras?",a="Nearly three millennia ago, human sailors from Gilneas ventured west across the Great Sea and discovered an island archipelago rich with metal ores and timber. After surviving a war against the island's native Drust inhabitants, the settlers founded a maritime outpost that grew into a powerful city-state. Kul Tiras established far-reaching merchant lanes across the world and provided the core naval strength of the Alliance during the Second War.",al={"history of kul tiras","how was kul tiras founded","kul tiras origin"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance of Lordaeron owed its survival at sea to the Kul Tiran armada, though the island nation currently remains distant and guarded following the losses of the Third War.",["faction:Horde"]="Kul Tiran sailors under Admiral Proudmoore fought bitterly against the Horde across the Eastern Kingdoms and chased Thrall's fleet all the way to the shores of Durotar, cementing a deep and enduring blood feud.",["race:Human"]="Originating from Gilnean seafaring pioneers, Kul Tirans developed a distinct, hardy island culture famed for shipbuilding, trade, and resilience against the open ocean.",["profession:Mining"]="When early sailors first settled the archipelago, it was the island's immense veins of heavy metal ores that catalyzed its rapid industrial growth and armored shipbuilding."},kw={"admiralty","alliance of lordaeron","daelin proudmoore","drustvar","great sea","jaina proudmoore","kul tiras","stormsong","tiragarde"},rel={"npc:jaina-proudmoore","topic:second-war","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","topic:alliance","topic:horde","subzone:baradin-bay","zone:dunmorogh","topic:pirate"},src={"Kul Tiras"}}
E["topic:leper-gnome"] = {t="topic",n="Leper gnome",z="general",s="Leper gnomes are the tragic, mutated survivors of Gnomeregan's catastrophic irradiation during the Third War. Maddened by toxic fallout and lingering fumes, most have sworn fealty to the usurper Sicco Thermaplugg, fiercely defending their ruined city against all outsiders.",h="Leper gnomes are the tragic, mutated survivors of Gnomeregan's catastrophic irradiation during the Third War.",sec={{t="The Fall of the Great City",b="When trogg invaders breached Gnomeregan during the Third War, Mekgineer Sicco Thermaplugg advised High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque to flood the city with radioactive toxins. Thermaplugg claimed the poison would exterminate the troggs while sparing the gnomish population. Instead, the catastrophe wiped out eighty percent of the city's inhabitants. Those who survived within the sealed lower levels were warped in mind and flesh by toxic fallout, mutating into pale, pustulant wretches dubbed leper gnomes.",sp=0},{t="Thermaplugg's Enclave",b="Thermaplugg himself succumbed to the poisonous fallout, losing both his complexion and his sanity. Naming himself techno-overlord and King of Gnomeregan, he gathered the crazed survivors in the Tinkers' Court. In their fractured mental state, many leper gnomes perceive all surface dwellers as trogg intruders, driving them to attack returning rescue parties with frantic savagery. They pilot salvaged mechanical constructs and assemble volatile ordnance, guarding Thermaplugg's twisted kingdom with fanatical devotion.",sp=0},{t="Scattered Across Azeroth",b="While the majority remain trapped within Gnomeregan's contaminated corridors, a handful of leper gnomes have found their way into the wider world. Driven by desperation or dark ambition, individuals have turned up among the Dark Iron dwarves in Blackrock Depths, operated machinery for the Venture Company in Stonetalon, or taken up service as lab assistants under the Forsaken's Royal Apothecary Society in the bowels of the Undercity.",sp=0},{t="The Search for a Cure",b="Gnome scholars exiled in Ironforge refuse to abandon their irradiated kin. In Dun Morogh, engineers and tinkers such as Ozzie Togglevolt and Razzle Sprysprocket work tirelessly on 'Operation Recombobulation,' attempting to devise alchemical or mechanical means to reverse the mutation. Whether a mind broken by toxic fallout can truly be restored remains a painful and unanswered question.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What turned the gnomes into leper gnomes?",a="During the trogg invasion of Gnomeregan, Sicco Thermaplugg urged High Tinker Mekkatorque to release radioactive vents to purge the caverns. The toxic fallout failed to kill the troggs and instead irradiated the trapped gnomish citizenry, mutating their skin and destroying their sanity.",al={"why are leper gnomes green","how did they become leper gnomes","gnome radiation cause"},it="origin"},{q="Are leper gnomes undead?",a="No. Leper gnomes are living gnomes who have suffered severe radiation poisoning and physical mutation. While some serve alongside the Forsaken in Lordaeron due to their affinity for blight and toxins, their affliction stems from irradiated gnomish engineering, not necromancy.",al={"are leper gnomes zombies","are they undead","leper gnome scourge"}},{q="Can leper gnomes be cured?",a="Allied gnome inventors like Ozzie Togglevolt and Razzle Sprysprocket are actively testing treatments, such as recombobulation devices and alchemical washes. While some experimental devices show minor promise, no reliable cure currently exists to reverse both the physical rot and the deep mental degradation.",al={"can leper gnomes be saved","leper gnome cure","operation recombobulation"}},{q="Who leads the leper gnomes?",a="Mekgineer Sicco Thermaplugg acts as their despotic techno-overlord. Mutated by the very radiation he insisted on unleashing, he rules from the Tinkers' Court inside Gnomeregan, manipulating the survivors' fractured minds to maintain absolute control over the ruins.",al={"leper gnome leader","king of gnomeregan","thermaplugg boss"},it="leader"},{q="Why do leper gnomes attack anyone who enters Gnomeregan?",a="The prolonged exposure to radioactive gasses shattered their minds. In their paranoia and delirium, most leper gnomes view any outsider—even an uncorrupted gnome from the surface—as an invading trogg bent on their destruction.",al={"why are leper gnomes hostile","why do they fight us"},it="hostile"},{q="Why are some leper gnomes working with the Forsaken?",a="A few wandering leper gnomes, such as Apprentice Crispin in Tirisfal Glades, offered their service to Lady Sylvanas Windrunner. The Royal Apothecary Society tolerates and employs them as research assistants, recognizing a shared appreciation for plague, blighted alchemy, and survival despite severe bodily degradation.",al={"leper gnomes in undercity","forsaken leper gnomes","apprentice crispin"},it="allies"},{q="What are leper gnomes?",a="Leper gnomes are gnomes who were twisted, diseased, and driven mad by the toxic radiation released within Gnomeregan. Marked by pale, greenish, lesion-covered skin and violent instability, most have devolved into deranged wretches who wander the ruined halls of their former capital, completely severed from civilized gnome society.",al={"what is a leper gnome","who are leper gnomes","leper gnome info"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where did the leper gnomes come from?",a="During the Third War, troggs invaded Gnomeregan from subterranean depths. To repel them, High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque—advised by Sicco Thermaplugg—opened the city's radioactive waste tanks. The plan proved disastrous: toxic radiation blanketed the underground metropolis, slaughtering eighty percent of the gnome population and horrifically mutating the survivors into the maddened leper gnomes seen today.",al={"leper gnome history","origin of leper gnomes","how did leper gnomes happen"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why are they called leper gnomes?",a="They earn their name from their sickly, rotting appearance. Heavy exposure to irradiated waste left their flesh discolored, peeling, and pocked with vile sores and lesions, resembling the afflictions of classic leprosy. Combined with their slow physical and mental degradation, the title reflects their tragically diseased state.",al={"why leper gnomes","leper gnome name meaning","why are they named leper gnomes"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="Every leper gnome is a tragic reminder of Gnomeregan's downfall and the heavy cost of Mekkatorque's trust in Thermaplugg. Exiled gnomes in Tinker Town view them with a mixture of profound sorrow and determination to find a cure.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken apothecaries find common ground with these irradiated outcasts, viewing their diseased forms without the disgust typical of living surface races and putting their mechanical skills to work in Undercity labs.",["profession:Engineering"]="Even in their state of utter madness, leper gnomes retain an uncanny aptitude for assembly lines, automated defense turrets, and high-voltage machinations."},kw={"dun morogh","gnomeregan","leper gnome","mekkatorque","radiation","recombobulation","thermaplugg","toxic"},rel={"zone:gnomeregan","npc:sicco-thermaplugg","topic:gnome","npc:gelbin-mekkatorque","quest:412","subzone:tinkers-court","topic:trogg","zone:undercity"},src={"Leper gnome"}}
E["topic:lich-king"] = {t="topic",n="Lich King",z="general",s="The Lich King is the lord and psychic master of the undead Scourge, ruling from the Frozen Throne far to the north in Icecrown. Originally forged by the demon Kil'jaeden from the spirit of the orc shaman Ner'zhul, the entity eventually lured Prince Arthas Menethil into corruption, culminating in their fateful union atop Northrend's icy peaks.",h="The Lich King is the lord and psychic master of the undead Scourge, ruling from the Frozen Throne far to the...",sec={{t="Birth of the Frozen God",b="When the orc shaman Ner'zhul attempted to flee the dying world of Draenor, he fell directly into the clutches of Kil'jaeden the Deceiver. The demon lord tore Ner'zhul's body to shreds but bound his tormented soul into a block of diamond-hard ice cast out of the Twisting Nether. Flung into Azeroth, the glacial monolith struck the northern wastes of Northrend, forming the Frozen Throne. Kil'jaeden intended the Lich King to forge an unstoppable undead vanguard that would dismantle the mortal kingdoms without succumbing to the infighting that ruined the Orcish Horde. Instead, armed with immense telepathic power, Ner'zhul immediately began plotting his own bid for freedom.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of Lordaeron",b="Watching dreadlords kept close vigil over the entity, forcing Ner'zhul to act in secret. Reaching out with his mind, he ensnared the exiled Kirin Tor archmage Kel'Thuzad, who established the Cult of the Damned in Lordaeron. Through cauldrons of tainted grain, the Scourge unleashed the Plague of Undeath across the northern kingdoms. The crisis drew in Prince Arthas Menethil, whose desperation to save his people led him into ruthless brutality, culminating in the infamous slaughter of Stratholme. Blinded by vengeance against the dreadlord Mal'Ganis, Arthas pursued his quarry into Northrend, where the cursed runeblade Frostmourne waited to steal his soul and turn him into the Scourge's premier death knight.",sp=0},{t="Treachery and Ascendance",b="Though forced to summon Archimonde and hand temporary command to the Burning Legion, the Lich King anticipated the demons' hubris. Arthas manipulated the demon hunter Illidan Stormrage into claiming the Skull of Gul'dan and slaying Tichondrius, ultimately contributing to Archimonde's ruin at Mount Hyjal. In the aftermath, a desperate Kil'jaeden tasked Illidan with destroying the Frozen Throne. As cracks in the icy spire weakened Ner'zhul's power—allowing Sylvanas Windrunner and her Forsaken to break free—the Lich King summoned his champion home. Arthas returned to Northrend, defeated Illidan at the foot of Icecrown, ascended the spire, and shattered the ice to merge with Ner'zhul into a single terrible consciousness.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is the Lich King right now?",a="The Lich King is the merged entity of Prince Arthas Menethil and the orc shaman Ner'zhul. Arthas shattered the glacial prison atop the Frozen Throne in Icecrown and donned the Helm of Domination, uniting his mortal form and dark soul with Ner'zhul's spirit into a single absolute ruler of the Scourge.",al={"current lich king","who rules the scourge","is arthas the lich king"},it="identity"},{q="Why did the Burning Legion create him?",a="Kil'jaeden created the Lich King to weaken Azeroth's defenders before a full demonic invasion. The previous invasion failed due to internal squabbling among the mortal Orcish Horde; a telepathically controlled army of the dead promised absolute obedience and complete exhaustion of mortal armies.",al={"why make lich king","kiljaeden purpose","legion scourge origin"},it="purpose"},{q="Where is the Lich King located?",a="He sits upon the Frozen Throne atop the icy glacier of Icecrown, deep within the remote and frozen continent of Northrend, far across the Great Sea north of Lordaeron.",al={"where is frozen throne","lich king location","where does he live"},it="place"},{q="How did Sylvanas break free from his control?",a="When Illidan Stormrage attacked the Frozen Throne with powerful sorcery, the magical ice fractured and the Lich King's power began leaking out. As his telepathic grip weakened, a faction of undead regained their free will, rallied under Sylvanas Windrunner, and founded the Forsaken.",al={"how did forsaken break free","why sylvanas free","scourge loss of control"},it="history"},{q="What is Frostmourne?",a="Frostmourne is the cursed, soul-stealing runeblade sent out from the Frozen Throne to lure a mortal champion. It lured Prince Arthas Menethil into claiming it in Northrend, devouring the prince's soul and transforming him into the foremost death knight of the Scourge.",al={"what sword did arthas use","frostmourne lore","runeblade"},it="item"},{q="Who was Ner'zhul?",a="Ner'zhul was an elder orc shaman of Draenor. When he attempted to flee the dying homeworld through dimensional rifts, he was captured by the demon Kil'jaeden, who tore his flesh away and warped his spirit into the all-seeing consciousness that became the Lich King.",al={"nerzhul lore","who was nerzhul","original lich king"},it="history"},{q="Why are the Lich King and his Scourge hostile to all living travelers?",a="The Lich King seeks the utter extinction and subjugation of mortal life. Every living creature slain by his plague or his unholy legions can be reanimated under his absolute mental command, expanding his power exponentially. He sees mortal races not as neighbors, but as raw material to swell his eternal Scourge.",al={"why is lich king hostile","scourge hostility","why does the lich king attack the living"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who is allied with the Lich King?",a="The Lich King works with no living nations. He commands the Undead Scourge directly through psychic domination, supported by mortal necromancers and zealots in the Cult of the Damned like Kel'Thuzad. Though created by the demon lord Kil'jaeden, he secretly betrayed the Burning Legion to pursue his own supreme dominion over Azeroth.",al={"lich king allies","who works for lich king","who does the lich king serve"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The Forsaken exist solely because the Lich King's telepathic grip faltered, and Queen Sylvanas drives her people with a burning hatred toward their former master in the frozen north.",["race:Human"]="For humans, the Lich King represents the ultimate tragedy: Prince Arthas, heir to the throne of Lordaeron and paladin of the Silver Hand, murdered his own father and annihilated his kingdom in the entity's name.",["class:Paladin"]="The Order of the Silver Hand was shattered by the Lich King's corrupting power, which transformed their greatest champion into a death knight who struck down Uther the Lightbringer.",["class:Priest"]="The Scourge represents a grim perversion of the Light's sanctity, reminding holy practitioners of the terrible cost paid across Lordaeron's fallen parishes."},kw={"arthas","cult of the damned","frostmourne","frozen throne","helm of domination","icecrown","kiljaeden","lich king","nerzhul","scourge"},rel={"topic:arthas-menethil","topic:scourge","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:cult-of-the-damned","npc:sylvanas-windrunner","topic:lordaeron","topic:burning-legion","subzone:stratholme"},src={"Lich King"}}
E["topic:light"] = {t="topic",n="Light",z="general",s="The Light is a boundless primal cosmic force whose energy connects and suffuses all living things across creation. Manifested in the mortal realm as Holy magic, it responds to willpower, meditation, and unyielding faith to mend flesh, shield allies, and purge darkness.",h="The Light is a boundless primal cosmic force whose energy connects and suffuses all living things across...",sec={{t="A Primal Force",b="The Light is an omnipresent cosmic ocean of living energy that predates the physical universe. In its pure state beyond the boundary of reality, it knows neither malice nor benevolence; it does not distinguish between love and righteous hatred. When brought into Azeroth, it manifests as Holy magic. Its power flows through living souls as a connective tissue, binding mortal hearts together. While mortals often personify it as a conscious protector, the Light fundamentally answers the absolute conviction of the soul calling upon it.",sp=0},{t="Instruments of Faith",b="To channel the Light requires intense self-abnegation, disciplined meditation, and unshakable willpower. Practitioners—most famously the priests of the Church of the Holy Light and the holy paladins—train for years to become clear, empty vessels for its radiance. Through their devotion, the Light manifests as restorative warmth to mend mortal wounds, brilliant wards to shelter the innocent, or searing fire to strike down demons and the risen undead.",sp=0},{t="Many Faces of Radiance",b="While humanity and the dwarves revere the Light through formal scripture, other cultures access its brilliance through different spiritual traditions. The night elves channel sacred moonlight through devotion to the goddess Elune, weaving holy magic that cleanses and protects. The tauren honor the sun deity An'she in their own spiritual traditions.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Light?",a="The Light is a fundamental cosmic energy that exists across all creation. It is the primal source of life and positive energy, binding sentient souls together. Mortals call upon it to produce Holy magic for healing, protection, and warfare.",al={"holy light lore","what is the holy light","the light explained"},it="identity"},{q="Does the Light have a conscious will or mind?",a="The Light is a primal cosmic force rather than a god. While mortals frequently speak of the 'will of the Light' and treat it as a guiding patron, it does not possess mortal morality. It responds directly to the certainty, willpower, and faith of the caster.",al={"is the light alive","does light have a mind","is the light a god"}},{q="Why can evil zealots still use the Light?",a="Because the Light answers conviction, not abstract righteousness. If a caster possesses absolute faith that their cause is just and their spirit remains undivided, the Light will flow through them—even if their deeds are ruthless or misguided.",al={"why do scarlet crusade have light","can bad guys use the light","evil paladins light"}},{q="How does the Light affect the undead?",a="Holy magic is violently reactive to the corruptive state of undeath. Channeling or touching the Light burns undead flesh and scourges the dark magic animating them, making it the premier weapon against the Scourge.",al={"light vs undead","why does light hurt scourge","can undead use light"}},{q="How do priests and paladins learn to wield it?",a="Wielding the Light requires rigorous mental discipline, emotional clarity, and years of meditation. The caster must quiet their personal ego and focus their spirit entirely, acting as a clear conduit through which the Holy magic can pour into reality.",al={"how to use holy magic","paladin magic source","priest light training"}},{q="Is Elune's magic connected to the Light?",a="Yes. The moonlight channeled by the priestesses of Elune manifests as a form of holy magic, sharing the same restorative, protective, and purifying capabilities as the spells wielded by paladins and church priests.",al={"is elune holy magic","elune and the light","moonlight holy light"}},{q="Where did the Light come from?",a="Before the universe or mortal life existed, the Light was a boundless, shimmering sea of living energy swelling across existence. As this boundless sea expanded, cold voids appeared, giving rise to its opposing cosmic force, the Void. The tension between Light and Void ignited cataclysmic cosmic explosions that birthed the physical universe, the Great Dark Beyond. Shards of fractured Light scattered across the cosmos, infusing newly formed worlds with the spark of life and bringing forth living beings.",al={"origin of the light","where did light come from","how was the light created","history of the holy light"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Paladins do not merely cast spells; they infuse their martial discipline and mortal bodies with the Light, wielding its incandescent fury against the Scourge and demonic evil.",["class:Priest"]="Priests serve as spiritual shepherds and conduits, practicing profound meditation and prayer to mend flesh, steady broken spirits, and shield allies.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Church of the Holy Light forms the spiritual bedrock of Stormwind and Ironforge, offering hope in an era darkened by the fall of Lordaeron.",["race:Undead"]="The Light burns undead flesh and rebukes necrotic magic, serving as a searing reminder of the mortal warmth the Forsaken were torn from.",["race:NightElf"]="Night elves channel holy energy not through church doctrine, but through reverence for the moon goddess Elune and her silver light.",["race:Tauren"]="Tauren revere An'she, the golden right eye of the Earth Mother, through their traditional sun worship."},kw={"an'she","church","elune","faith","healing","holy light","holy magic","light","paladin","priest","scourge"},rel={"topic:church-of-the-holy-light","npc:tirion-fordring","topic:elune","topic:lordaeron","topic:tauren","topic:alliance","quest:99134","topic:burning-legion"},src={"Light"}}
E["topic:llane-wrynn-i"] = {t="topic",n="Llane Wrynn I",z="general",s="King Llane Wrynn I was the ruler of Stormwind during the catastrophic First War, whose reign ended with the fall of the kingdom and his own tragic murder. A beloved and proud monarch, his steadfast refusal to believe his friend Medivh had betrayed humanity ultimately doomed his defense of the capital.",h="King Llane Wrynn I was the ruler of Stormwind during the catastrophic First War, whose reign ended with the...",sec={{t="A Prince of Reckless Valor",b="Born to King Barathen Wrynn, young Prince Llane grew up alongside Anduin Lothar and Medivh. When the Gurubashi trolls staged gruesome raids against Westfall settlements, Llane defied his father's defensive policies and led his companions into the jungles of Stranglethorn Vale to slay the warlord Jok'non. This bold defiance ignited an all-out Gurubashi invasion. Though Barathen perished leading a desperate counterattack outside the city gates, Medivh intervened with devastating sorcery to annihilate the troll vanguard. In the wake of this costly victory, Llane ascended to the throne as Stormwind's king.",sp=0},{t="The First War",b="When mysterious, green-skinned creatures emerged from the southern swamps, King Llane dispatched Lothar to investigate the Black Morass. What began as minor skirmishes erupted into the First War as the Horde overran Brightwood, Westfall, and Redridge. Messengers sent to Lordaeron and other northern human kingdoms were dismissed, their pleas undermined by Stormwind's historic isolationism and aristocratic intrigue. Despite these disadvantages, Llane organized an audacious counterattack alongside Lothar that broke the Horde's initial siege of Stormwind, dealing the invaders their first major defeat.",sp=0},{t="Betrayal in the Keep",b="Llane's steadfast nature ultimately blinded him to the true danger within his inner circle. Even when Medivh's apprentice Khadgar and the emissary Garona revealed that the Guardian himself had opened the Dark Portal, Llane refused to accept his childhood friend's treachery. Soon after, Orgrim Doomhammer unified the Horde and breached Stormwind's outer defenses. As Llane directed the defense from Stormwind Keep, Garona—compelled by the magical influence of the Shadow Council—murdered the King in his own throne room, carving out his heart and sealing the city's doom.",sp=2}},faq={{q="Who was King Llane Wrynn I?",a="King Llane Wrynn I was the ruler of the Kingdom of Stormwind during the First War. He was the father of King Varian Wrynn and grandfather of young Prince Anduin Llane Wrynn. He died defending his people during the initial fall of Stormwind.",al={"who is llane","llane wrynn lore","king llane"},it="identity"},{q="How did King Llane die?",a="Llane was assassinated inside Stormwind Keep during the final siege of the First War. He was slain by Garona Halforcen, a half-orc emissary whom he considered a trusted friend and confidante, but who was mind-controlled by the Shadow Council.",al={"who killed king llane","llane death","how did llane die"},it="conflict"},{q="What was Llane's relationship to Medivh and Lothar?",a="Llane, Medivh, and Anduin Lothar were inseparable childhood companions who adventured together across southern Azeroth. Their lifelong bond proved tragic: Lothar became Llane's greatest champion, while Llane's total refusal to believe Medivh could betray him led to the monarch's downfall.",al={"llane medivh lothar","llane childhood friends"},it="allies"},{q="Why didn't the other human kingdoms help Llane during the First War?",a="Stormwind had long maintained an isolationist foreign policy that northern nations viewed as arrogant. When Llane sent warnings of the green-skinned invaders, leaders in Lordaeron dismissed the reports as an exaggerated civil rebellion, leaving Stormwind to fight alone.",al={"why didn't lordaeron help llane","alliance during first war"}},{q="How did Varian escape the fall of Stormwind?",a="Following King Llane's assassination and the breach of Stormwind Keep, Champion Anduin Lothar gathered the surviving citizens and Llane's young son, Prince Varian Wrynn. Lothar led them to the harbor and fled north across the Great Sea to Lordaeron.",al={"what happened to varian after llane died","varian escape stormwind"},it="history"},{q="What did Medivh give Llane at his ascension?",a="When Llane came of age as Prince of Stormwind, Medivh emerged from a long coma and presented him with an enchanted obsidian hourglass. The sand within trickled endlessly, with the upper chamber never emptying, symbolizing Stormwind's eternal prosperity.",al={"medivh gift to llane","llane hourglass"},it="item"}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="King Llane's tragic murder and the fall of Stormwind forced the survivors north, directly provoking the creation of the Grand Alliance of Lordaeron to counter the Horde.",["race:Human"]="For the people of Stormwind, King Llane remains a tragic hero whose sacrifice is commemorated across the restored city, including in the naming of King Varian's son, Anduin Llane Wrynn.",["class:Warrior"]="King Llane fought alongside his Champion, Anduin Lothar, personally directing the martial defense of Stormwind from the front lines of the Keep.",["class:Rogue"]="The regicide of King Llane by the assassin Garona remains one of the most famous and devastating blade strikes in Azeroth's history."},kw={"barathen","first war","garona","gurubashi","kingslayers","llane","lothar","medivh","shadow council","stormwind","wrynn"},rel={"topic:varian-wrynn","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:first-war","topic:medivh","topic:stormwind-kingdom","subzone:stormwind-keep","topic:shadow-council","npc:orgrim-doomhammer"},src={"Llane Wrynn I"}}
E["topic:lordaeron"] = {t="topic",n="Lordaeron",z="general",s="Lordaeron is the great northern subcontinent of the Eastern Kingdoms, once the glorious heart of human civilization and the Alliance. Following the devastation of the Third War, its former kingdoms lie in ruins, contested by the undead Scourge, Sylvanas Windrunner's Forsaken, and desperate remnants of the living.",h="Lordaeron is the great northern subcontinent of the Eastern Kingdoms, once the glorious heart of human...",sec={{t="Cradle of Northern Humanity",b="Lordaeron takes its name from Lordain, a revered general of the ancient Empire of Arathor who sacrificed his life during the Troll Wars. Stretching from the rugged Arathi Highlands in the south across scenic forests and waterways up toward Quel'Thalas, the region nurtured thriving kingdoms like Lordaeron, Dalaran, Gilneas, and Alterac. It was historically celebrated for fertile farmlands in Tirisfal and Hillsbrad, mountain strongholds, and the sacred cradle of the Holy Light.",sp=0},{t="The Scourge and the Fall",b="During the Third War, Lordaeron became ground zero for the Lich King's invasion. Through tainted grain, the Cult of the Damned transformed countless northern citizens into mindless undead monstrosities. Prince Arthas Menethil fell to darkness, returning from Northrend to slay his father, King Terenas Menethil II, and orchestrate the total slaughter of the Capital City. The thriving heartland quickly rotted into the blighted nightmare known as the Plaguelands.",sp=0},{t="A Contested Wasteland",b="Today, Lordaeron is a brutal battleground defined by fractured loyalties. Beneath the ruins of the Capital City, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken have carved out the Undercity, establishing a sovereign realm of free-willed undead aligned with the Horde. To the east, the Lich King's mindless Scourge clings to corrupted keeps and strongholds. Meanwhile, human zealots of the Scarlet Crusade, surviving Alliance settlements in Hillsbrad, and wild forest trolls clash relentlessly over what remains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What exactly is Lordaeron?",a="Lordaeron refers both to the entire northern subcontinent of the Eastern Kingdoms and specifically to the former human kingdom that once ruled its central territories. The land stretches from the Thandol Span at the Arathi border north toward Quel'Thalas, encompassing mountains, forests, and inland lakes.",al={"is lordaeron a continent","what does lordaeron mean","lordaeron geography"},it="identity"},{q="Who controls Lordaeron right now?",a="No single power controls the entire subcontinent. Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken hold Tirisfal Glades and Silverpine from their subterranean Undercity. The Scourge retains immense power in the Plaguelands under Kel'Thuzad, while the Scarlet Crusade, the Kingdom of Stormwind's allies in Hillsbrad, and various troll tribes control isolated frontiers.",al={"who rules lordaeron","who owns lordaeron now","current ruler of lordaeron"},it="in_charge"},{q="What happened to the original living humans of Lordaeron?",a="Most of Lordaeron's population perished during the Third War, struck down by the plague of undeath or Arthas's Scourge army. Many of those dead were raised into mindless servitude, and later regained their minds to become the Forsaken. The surviving living fled south to Stormwind, sailed to Kalimdor with Jaina Proudmoore, or joined militant enclaves like the Scarlet Crusade.",al={"where did the people of lordaeron go","did lordaeron humans die","what happened to lordaeron"},it="people"},{q="Why was the subcontinent named Lordaeron?",a="It was named in memory of Lordain, a general of the ancient Empire of Arathor. During the ancient Troll Wars between humanity, high elves, and the Amani Empire, Lordain sacrificed himself alongside his warriors to protect his comrades, forever etching his name into northern lore.",al={"origin of name lordaeron","who is lordaeron named after","lordain name"},it="name"},{q="Where is the Capital City of Lordaeron?",a="The Capital City of Lordaeron stands along the northern shore of Lordamere Lake in Tirisfal Glades. Though its surface streets and palace courtyards lie in silent ruins, the sprawling catacombs beneath have been converted by Sylvanas Windrunner into the Undercity, the Forsaken capital.",al={"where was lordaeron city","capital of lordaeron","ruins of lordaeron location"},it="place"},{q="Is Lordaeron part of the Alliance or the Horde?",a="It is bitterly contested. Historically, the Kingdom of Lordaeron founded and led the original Alliance of Lordaeron. Today, however, the Undercity is an official capital of the Horde, making western Lordaeron a Horde stronghold, while Alliance forces desperately fight to hold their few remaining outposts in Hillsbrad and Arathi.",al={"is lordaeron alliance or horde","who claims lordaeron"},it="side"},{q="What is the history of Lordaeron?",a="Originally settled by human tribes following the era of the Empire of Arathor, Lordaeron grew into one of humanity's most powerful kingdoms and gave its name to the entire northern continent. For generations, its realm encompassed rich farmlands and strong cities, standing as the bulwark of the Alliance during the Second War. That glory crumbled in the Third War, when the Scourge unleashed the plague of undeath upon its lands, tearing the kingdom apart and reducing its once-thriving territories to the blighted Plaguelands and contested ruins you see today.",al={"history of lordaeron","lordaeron lore","where did lordaeron come from"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="This was once your homeland in life. You walk the ruined courtyards of the Capital City not as visiting travelers, but as the rightful heirs of Lordaeron claiming what is yours under Lady Sylvanas.",["race:Human"]="For humans of the Alliance, Lordaeron is a sacred tragedy. It was the cradle of the Holy Light and the leader of the original Alliance; seeing its halls overrun by the undead is a constant source of grief and fury.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance once took its very name from this continent. Today, reclaiming footholds in Hillsbrad and combating the Scourge in the Plaguelands represents an agonizing struggle to recover human heritage.",["faction:Horde"]="Through their Forsaken allies, the Horde maintains a vital and contested foothold in the northern Eastern Kingdoms, surrounding Alliance outposts and confronting the remnants of the Scourge.",["class:Paladin"]="The Order of the Silver Hand was born in Lordaeron at the Chapel of Alonsus Faol. Every road and ruin in these northern lands is steeped in the history of the earliest paladins."},kw={"hillsbrad","lordaeron","lordain","menethil","plaguelands","scourge","silverpine","terenas","tirisfal","undercity"},rel={"topic:third-war","topic:lich-king","zone:tirisfal","zone:westernplaguelands","zone:easternplaguelands","zone:hillsbrad","zone:silverpine","zone:arathi"},src={"Lordaeron"}}
E["topic:mage"] = {t="topic",n="Mage",z="general",s="Mages are scholarly spellcasters who bend the fabric of reality through arcane study, hurling destructive fire, controlling frost, and raw arcane energy. While physically fragile and cloaked only in cloth, their mastery over teleportation, conjuration, and battlefield crowd control makes them formidable allies and terrifying foes. Their discipline requires immense mental dedication, balancing immense destructive potential against the perilous history of arcane misuse.",h="Mages are scholarly spellcasters who bend the fabric of reality through arcane study, hurling destructive...",sec={{t="Scholars of the Arcane",b="The path of the mage demands intellect, focus, and years of rigorous study. Rather than communing with spirits or relying on divine faith, magi treat magic as a precise cosmic science. By drawing upon arcane energies, a practitioner can weave raw destructive power, summon searing bursts of flame, or chill the surrounding air into solid ice and howling blizzards. Because heavy armor disrupts the delicate somatic gestures and mental focus required for incantations, mages wear light cloth, relying instead on wards, mana barriers, and evasive spells to protect themselves.",sp=0},{t="The Legacy of Dalaran and Arathor",b="Human magical heritage began nearly three millennia ago during the Troll Wars, when high elven sorcerers instructed one hundred humans of the Empire of Arathor to turn the tide against the Amani. As the number of human practitioners multiplied, fearful mundane citizens drove many magi to establish the magocracy of Dalaran. Under the governance of the Kirin Tor, Dalaran became the central archive of magical study for the northern kingdoms, formalizing the traditions of combat magic that later supported the Alliance of Lordaeron through the First and Second Wars.",sp=0},{t="Suspicion and Necessity",b="Arcane practitioners have never enjoyed uncomplicated trust. From the ancient cataclysm wrought by the Highborne to the catastrophic fall of Dalaran during the Third War, magic's volatile nature evokes widespread wariness. The Church of the Holy Light has historically treated wizardry with skepticism, and conservative knights have often viewed arcane spellcrafters as damned souls or a necessary evil on the battlefield. Despite this suspicion, civil and military leaders recognize that a capable magus offers invaluable battlefield supremacy, swift mass transportation, and essential countermeasures against supernatural threats.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What schools of magic do mages specialize in?",a="Mages focus on three core schools: Arcane, Fire, and Frost. Arcane magic alters time, space, and reality, allowing for polymorphing, teleportation, and focused bursts of pure power. Fire spells focus on swift, overwhelming combustive damage to incinerate targets. Frost magic slows, snares, and shatters foes, providing critical battlefield control to keep enemies from closing into melee distance.",al={"mage specs","mage magic types","arcane fire frost"}},{q="Why do mages only wear cloth armor?",a="Casting complex spells requires uninhibited physical movement, precise hand gestures, and uninterrupted mental focus. Heavy materials like leather, chainmail, or plate interfere with a spellcaster's somatic components and channel of magical energy. To compensate for their physical vulnerability, magi employ magical barriers, frost shielding, and spells that keep enemies at bay.",al={"why cloth","mage armor type","can mages wear leather"}},{q="How did humans first learn to cast arcane magic?",a="During the ancient Troll Wars roughly 2,800 years ago, the human Empire of Arathor allied with the high elves of Quel'Thalas against the Amani trolls. In exchange for military aid, elven magi trained one hundred humans in the fundamentals of the arcane. These original hundred pioneers proved devastating in battle and founded the human magical lineage that eventually established Dalaran.",al={"first human mages","troll wars mages","human magic origin"},it="history"},{q="What is the relationship between mages and the Church of the Holy Light?",a="The relationship is tense. For centuries, orthodox members of the Church of the Holy Light viewed arcane studies and the Kirin Tor with suspicion, sometimes treating them as heretical. Paladins and devout priests often consider wizardry an unpredictable, morally dubious practice, though one accepted out of necessity due to its strategic military power.",al={"church views on mages","paladins and mages","holy light arcane heresy"},it="ties"},{q="Are Stormwind's mages part of Dalaran's Kirin Tor?",a="Historically, Stormwind had its own traditions, originally led by court conjurers who operated largely independent of Dalaran. While Dalaran's Kirin Tor was the undisputed center of magical academia in the north, southern wizards developed their own enclaves and royal advisory roles, though communication and common ties endured across both realms.",al={"stormwind mages dalaran","conjurers of azeroth","stormwind wizards"},it="ties"},{q="Can mages create food and transport people instantly?",a="Yes. Beyond destructive spells, mages possess extensive utility magic. They can weave arcane energy to conjure fresh food and water from thin air, enchant allies' intellects, and bend space to instantly open portals connecting major cities across the world.",al={"mage utility","mage portals","conjure water food"},it="power"},{q="What is a Mage?",a="Mages, also known as magi or wizards, are spellcasting savants who master the arcane to alter the fabric of reality itself. Unlike priests or shamans who appeal to deities or elemental spirits, mages achieve their power through rigorous intellectual study, research, and iron mental discipline. They wield destructive bursts of fire, immobilizing frost, and raw arcane energy from afar, trading physical armor for powerful defensive wards and unmatched tactical versatility.",al={"what is a mage","who are mages","mage identity","lore of mages"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does a Mage get their power?",a="Mages draw their power by tapping into the cosmic energies of the Twisting Nether and manipulating raw arcane magic. By expending their own internal mana through focused mental discipline, a mage reshapes these volatile ambient forces into physical elements like blazing fire and piercing frost, or channels raw arcane energy to warp space, conjure sustenance, and transform reality.",al={"where do mages get power","source of mage magic","mage arcane power source"},it="power",add=true},{q="What does it mean to become a Mage?",a="Walking the path of the mage demands a keen intellect and unwavering dedication. You are an apprentice entering a tradition of perilous knowledge, undertaking mental regimens that strain both body and soul. Because arcane power is volatile and viewed with widespread suspicion, you must learn strict control, relying on light cloth robes, tactical distance, and sheer wit to demolish threats before they ever reach striking range.",al={"becoming a mage","starting as a mage","what does being a mage mean"},it="for_you",add=true}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Your craft is one of meticulous intellectual discipline, mastering fire, frost, and arcane force while safeguarding the fragile balance of reality.",["race:Human"]="Humanity's arcane legacy traces back to the hundred original magi taught during the Troll Wars, whose descendants built Dalaran into a grand center of magical study.",["race:NightElf"]="Kaldorei society strictly shuns arcane magic, remembering all too well how Queen Azshara and her Highborne magi brought ruin upon ancient Kalimdor.",["class:Paladin"]="While you fight beside magi on the battlefield, the Knights of the Silver Hand have long regarded their worldly arcane methods with deep caution.",["class:Warlock"]="Many warlocks began their journey as mages before abandoning academic restraint and strict Dalaran edicts in pursuit of demonic power."},kw={"arcane","conjuration","dalaran","fire","frost","kirin tor","mage","magus","mana","spellcaster","teleport","wizard"},rel={"subzone:dalaran","topic:highborne","npc:jaina-proudmoore","topic:medivh","topic:church-of-the-holy-light","topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","zone:stormwind"},src={"Mage"}}
E["topic:medivh"] = {t="topic",n="Medivh",z="general",s="Medivh was the last Guardian of Tirisfal, an ancient order tasked with defending Azeroth from demonic corruption. Bound from before his birth by the spirit of the Dark Titan Sargeras, he opened the Dark Portal and brought the orcish Horde into the world, setting off decades of catastrophic warfare.",h="Medivh was the last Guardian of Tirisfal, an ancient order tasked with defending Azeroth from demonic...",sec={{t="A Cursed Heritage",b="Medivh was born to the powerful Guardian Aegwynn and the court conjurer Nielas Aran. Centuries earlier, Aegwynn had fought the fallen Titan Sargeras in Northrend, unaware that his dark essence slipped into her body to await an heir. When Medivh reached his fourteenth birthday, the vast magical inheritance bestowed by his mother manifested in a terrifying explosion of power, plunging the boy into a decade-long coma under the watch of the clerics at Northshire Abbey.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of Karazhan",b="Awakening from his slumber, Medivh took residence in the isolated tower of Karazhan, serving as court magus to his childhood friend King Llane Wrynn of Stormwind. Yet the dark spirit within him slowly eroded his sanity. Seeking to unleash devastation, Medivh made contact across the stars with the orc warlock Gul'dan and the Shadow Council. Together, they channeled tremendous magic to tear open the Dark Portal, delivering the Horde upon the Eastern Kingdoms and sparking the First War. Medivh was ultimately slain in his tower by his closest friend, Anduin Lothar, and his apprentice, Khadgar.",sp=0},{t="The Prophet's Redemption",b="Freed from Sargeras by his mortal death, Medivh returned from beyond death to atone for his crimes. During the Third War, he disguised himself as an enigmatic raven-cloaked Prophet, whispering warnings of the Burning Legion's return to King Terenas Menethil and Warchief Thrall. Though Lordaeron ignored his pleas, he guided Thrall's Horde and Jaina Proudmoore's refugees across the Great Sea to Kalimdor, uniting orcs, humans, and night elves for the decisive stand at Mount Hyjal before fading into myth.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who was Medivh?",a="Medivh was the last human Guardian of Tirisfal, endowed with immense arcane power to protect Azeroth. He was the son of Magna Aegwynn and the architect behind the opening of the Dark Portal.",al={"who is medivh","medivh lore","the last guardian"},it="identity"},{q="Why did Medivh open the Dark Portal?",a="Medivh was possessed before birth by the spirit of the Dark Titan Sargeras. Under this malevolent influence, he bargained with the warlock Gul'dan, offering the location of the Tomb of Sargeras in exchange for the Horde invading Azeroth to bring ruin to the mortal kingdoms.",al={"why did medivh betray stormwind","medivh dark portal","why bring orcs"},it="motive"},{q="Who killed Medivh?",a="During the First War, Medivh was confronted in Karazhan by his close friend Anduin Lothar, his apprentice Khadgar, and the half-orc assassin Garona. Khadgar pierced Medivh's heart and Lothar severed his head, ending his life and casting Sargeras's spirit back into the Nether.",al={"how did medivh die","who killed the guardian","medivh death"},it="conflict"},{q="What was Medivh's relationship with Stormwind's royalty?",a="Medivh grew up alongside Prince Llane Wrynn and Anduin Lothar in the royal court of Stormwind. The trio fought side by side against Gurubashi trolls in their youth, and King Llane trusted Medivh completely, making his eventual betrayal devastating to the kingdom.",al={"medivh and llane","was medivh friends with lothar"},it="ties"},{q="Is Medivh still alive?",a="After guiding the mortal races to victory over Archimonde at the Battle of Mount Hyjal, Medivh left to take his place among the legends of the past. His whereabouts since are unknown.",al={"where is medivh now","is the prophet medivh","medivh status"},sp=1,it="today"},{q="What is Karazhan?",a="Karazhan is a spire built atop a potent intersection of ley lines in Deadwind Pass. Originally established by Aegwynn and later inherited by Medivh, the tower became heavily warped by arcane and demonic energies following the Guardian's fall and remains a haunted ruin.",al={"medivh tower","what happened to karazhan"},it="place"},{q="Where did Medivh come from?",a="Medivh was born to Magna Aegwynn, the Guardian of Tirisfal, and Nielas Aran, a mage of the Kirin Tor. Unknown to his parents, the dark titan Sargeras possessed Medivh in his mother's womb. Raised among the court of Stormwind as a close childhood friend to Prince Llane Wrynn and Anduin Lothar, his inherited magical powers violently manifested on the eve of his fourteenth birthday, killing his father and casting Medivh into a decade-long coma.",al={"medivh history","medivh origin","medivh past"},it="history",add=true},{q="What does the name Medivh mean?",a="Medivh means \"keeper of secrets\" in Thalassian, the tongue of the high elves. Given to him at birth by his mother, Aegwynn, the name proved chillingly fitting for a Guardian who concealed both the boundless power of Tirisfal and the insidious corruption of Sargeras within his soul.",al={"what does medivh mean","medivh name meaning","meaning of medivh"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Medivh represents both the zenith and the ultimate peril of arcane mastery; every Kirin Tor scholar studies his catastrophic fall as a cautionary tale of unchecked power.",["class:Warlock"]="Medivh's communion with Gul'dan and his role as the mortal conduit for Sargeras remain legendary demonstrations of fel summoning and interplanar manipulation.",["faction:Alliance"]="The people of Stormwind view Medivh with bitter sorrow, remembering him as a beloved royal companion who ultimately invited the ruinous Horde into their lands.",["faction:Horde"]="To Thrall's Horde, Medivh is the human sorcerer whose bargain with Gul'dan opened the Dark Portal and first brought the orcs into this world."},kw={"aegwynn","atiesh","dark portal","guardian","karazhan","khadgar","lothar","medivh","sargeras","tirisfal"},rel={"subzone:karazhan","subzone:dark-portal","topic:sargeras","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:first-war","topic:third-war","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:burning-legion"},src={"Medivh"}}
E["topic:muradin-bronzebeard"] = {t="topic",n="Muradin Bronzebeard",z="multi",s="Muradin Bronzebeard was the brother of King Magni Bronzebeard and Brann Bronzebeard, as well as a renowned warrior, diplomat, and co-founder of the Explorers' League. Revered across Khaz Modan and Lordaeron alike, he is believed to have died in the frozen wastes of Northrend when Prince Arthas Menethil claimed the cursed runeblade Frostmourne.",h="Muradin Bronzebeard was the brother of King Magni Bronzebeard and Brann Bronzebeard, as well as a renowned...",sec={{t="A Brother and Ambassador",b="Muradin fought with distinction during the Second War, acting as the vital liaison between the mountain halls of Khaz Modan and the Alliance of Lordaeron. He helped hold Ironforge during the Horde's siege and marched alongside Alliance forces all the way through Blackrock Spire to the Dark Portal. In the peaceful years that followed, Muradin settled into Capital City as Ironforge’s official ambassador. Jovial yet formidable, he became a fixture in the palace, growing close to King Terenas Menethil, winning over the royal cooks, and taking it upon himself to tutor young Prince Arthas in the arts of swordplay and combat.",sp=0},{t="The Explorers' League and Northrend",b="Beyond politics and war, Muradin possessed a restless curiosity. Alongside his brothers, he helped establish the dwarven Explorers' League, dedicated to uncovering lost titan relics and chronicling the distant corners of the world. He fell in love with the desolate, windswept continent of Northrend, making frequent journeys north to sketch ancient architecture and document its fierce inhabitants. It was Northrend's rugged mysteries that ultimately drew him on his final expedition, seeking out lore, relics, and the whispered legend of a forgotten runeblade.",sp=0},{t="The Claiming of Frostmourne",b="When Prince Arthas arrived in Northrend obsessed with hunting down the dreadlord Mal'Ganis, he reunited with Muradin and enlisted his dwarven expedition. Arthas sought the power of Frostmourne to turn the tide, ignoring Muradin's rising alarm as the prince betrayed his own mercenaries and abandoned all honor. At the altar of Frostmourne, Muradin read the warning inscribed upon its icy pedestal and pleaded with his former pupil to turn back. Arthas refused. As the prince seized the runeblade, the explosion of unholy ice struck Muradin down, leaving him apparently dead upon the cavern floor.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who was Muradin Bronzebeard?",a="Muradin Bronzebeard was the middle brother of King Magni Bronzebeard and the explorer Brann. He was a veteran hero of the Second War, Ironforge's ambassador to Lordaeron, and a founding member of the Explorers' League.",al={"who is muradin","muradin lore","magni brother muradin"},it="identity"},{q="What happened to Muradin in Northrend?",a="Muradin accompanied Prince Arthas to the cavern containing Frostmourne. When Arthas ignored Muradin's warnings and shattered the icy prison holding the runeblade, an exploding shard of enchanted ice struck Muradin, seemingly killing him on the spot. Arthas abandoned his body and took the blade.",al={"how did muradin die","muradin death","did arthas kill muradin"},it="history"},{q="What was Muradin's relationship with Prince Arthas?",a="While serving as Ironforge's ambassador in Capital City, Muradin took Arthas under his wing. He trained the young prince in bladed combat and went on many adventures with him, viewing Arthas almost like a surrogate son or beloved nephew until Arthas's dark descent in Northrend.",al={"did muradin know arthas","muradin arthas teacher","muradin and arthas"},it="ties"},{q="What was Muradin's role in the Second War?",a="Muradin served as the Alliance liaison between Lordaeron and Khaz Modan. He defended Ironforge during the Horde's siege, then marched south with the united Alliance army to fight at Blackrock Spire and the Dark Portal.",al={"muradin second war","muradin battles","did muradin fight the horde"},it="history"},{q="Why did Muradin go to Northrend in the first place?",a="As a founder of the Explorers' League, Muradin was deeply fascinated by Northrend's archaeology and ancient ruins. He returned there on an expedition to uncover forgotten history and search for relics, which ultimately led him to the trail of Frostmourne.",al={"why was muradin in northrend","muradin expedition","what was muradin looking for"},it="motive"},{q="How did King Magni react to Muradin's death?",a="King Magni and the entire Bronzebeard clan were devastated by Muradin's loss. Muradin's apparent demise severed the once-warm bond between Ironforge and Prince Arthas, turning grief into bitter fury toward the Scourge.",al={"magni muradin reaction","ironforge muradin grief"}},{q="Who was Muradin Bronzebeard allied with?",a="Muradin was fiercely loyal to the Alliance of Lordaeron and his own people of Khaz Modan. As a prince of the Bronzebeard clan, he answered directly to his brother, King Magni Bronzebeard, while serving as Ironforge's official ambassador to the royal court of Lordaeron. There, he developed strong ties with King Terenas Menethil II and Prince Arthas. Muradin also helped found the dwarven Explorers' League, leading intrepid teams of dwarven archaeologists and adventurers into the world's most remote corners under Ironforge's banner.",al={"muradin bronzebeard allies","who did muradin work for","muradin factions"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="Muradin is remembered by the dwarves of Ironforge as a paragon of their people—brave, fiercely loyal, and driven by an insatiable curiosity for the history of their makers.",["faction:Alliance"]="Muradin forged the bonds of camaraderie between Ironforge and the northern human kingdoms, standing as one of the Alliance's foundational heroes.",["class:Warrior"]="Muradin was considered one of the finest dwarven duelists and weapon masters of his era, having instructed Prince Arthas Menethil himself in the art of the blade."},kw={"arthas","bronzebeard","explorers league","frostmourne","ironforge","magni","muradin","northrend"},rel={"npc:magni-bronzebeard","npc:brann-bronzebeard","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:bronzebeard-clan","topic:explorers-league","zone:ironforge","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","topic:second-war"},src={"Muradin Bronzebeard"}}
E["topic:murloc"] = {t="topic",n="Murloc",z="general",s="Murlocs are amphibious, bipedal humanoids famous for their gurgling battle cries, territorial coastal villages, and sudden swarming tactics. Though historically dwelling in the deep ocean, they have steadily migrated inland along the shores, lakes, and rivers of Azeroth, acting with far greater coordination and cunning than their primitive appearance suggests.",h="Murlocs are amphibious, bipedal humanoids famous for their gurgling battle cries, territorial coastal...",sec={{t="Creatures of the Shallows",b="Murlocs dwell in densely populated settlements along coastlines, riverbanks, and freshwater lakes. Averaging roughly four feet in height, they possess bulbous, slime-coated bodies, wide mouths filled with sharp fangs, and keen vision capable of piercing pitch-black water. While individuals are rarely seen wandering far from their kin, encountering one murloc invariably means a dozen more lurk just beneath the surface. They rely on overwhelming numbers and sharp crude weaponry.",sp=0},{t="The Inland March",b="Though ancient lineages suggest murlocs have existed beneath the waves since before recorded history, their widespread encroachment onto the landmasses of Lordaeron and Kalimdor is relatively recent. Historians note that this inland migration coincided suspiciously with the reemergence of the serpentine naga from the ocean depths. Some tribes appear to worship deep-sea monstrosities waiting in the oceanic fathoms. Whether fleeing darker terrors or preparing the world for their masters' awakening, their coordinated movement into freshwaters poses a rising threat.",sp=0},{t="A Hidden Intellect",b="Because their garbled language sounds like nonsensical bubbling to outsiders, many dismiss murlocs as mere feral pests. However, their coordinated ambushes, mastery of earth and water magic, and communal structures hint at a sharper intellect. Renowned explorer Brann Bronzebeard has suggested that murlocs are far smarter than civilized races realize, intentionally concealing their cunning. Their impact on global history is undeniable: it was a murloc raid upon the Darkspear tribe that spurred Thrall to intervene, forging the alliance between the orcs and trolls that built the modern Horde.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do murlocs attack all at once?",a="Murlocs rarely fight alone. They live in tight-knit, crowded settlements and respond in groups to intruders, swarming enemies with overwhelming numbers.",al={"murloc swarming","why do murlocs group up","murloc aggro"},it="danger"},{q="Are murlocs actually intelligent?",a="Yes, far more than their comical appearance implies. While their guttural, gurgling dialect is incomprehensible to most, murlocs craft weapons, organize strategic ambushes, and wield elemental water and earth magic. Scholars like Brann Bronzebeard believe murlocs intentionally allow outsiders to underestimate their intellect.",al={"are murlocs smart","murloc intelligence","can murlocs think"},it="power"},{q="Why are murlocs moving inland from the sea?",a="Originally creatures of the ocean floor, murlocs have steadily advanced into freshwater lakes and riverbeds. Historians suspect this migration was triggered by the resurgence of the naga or by deep-sea entities slumbering in the abyss whose awakening commands murlocs to secure the land.",al={"why are murlocs on land","murloc migration","why are murlocs everywhere"},it="why_here"},{q="How did murlocs help form the Horde?",a="During the Third War, a tribe of murlocs serving the sea witch Zar'jira ambushed the Darkspear trolls on their island home. Warchief Thrall and his orc warriors fought alongside Sen'jin to defend the trolls. This battle forged the unbreakable bond between orcs and the Darkspear tribe, laying the foundation of the modern Horde.",al={"murloc horde origin","murlocs and darkspear","thrall murloc island"},it="conflict"},{q="What are mur'gul?",a="Mur'gul are murlocs encountered during the Third War. Despite their strange appearance and name, they are living creatures.",al={"mur'gul","warcraft iii murlocs","murgul identity"}},{q="What is that iconic sound murlocs make?",a="When engaging prey or raising the alarm, murlocs let out a loud, wet, undulating gargle (often rendered as 'Aaaaaughibbrgubugbugrguburgle' or 'RwlRwlRwlRwl'). It serves as both an aggressive psychological weapon and an instant call for their pack to converge.",al={"murloc noise","murloc battle cry","rwlrwlrwl"}},{q="What are murlocs?",a="Murlocs are a bipedal, amphibious race found clustering along shores, lakes, and rivers across Azeroth. Averaging around four feet in height, they possess bulbous bodies, slimy skin, clawed digits, and mouths lined with razor-sharp fangs. While often dismissed as primitive fish-beasts due to their gurgling tongue, they construct communal settlements, wield handcrafted weaponry, and channel earth and water magic.",al={"what is a murloc","murloc race","who are the murlocs"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are murlocs hostile to travelers?",a="Murlocs are deeply insular creatures that shun dry-land mortals and hold almost no tolerance for other races. They fiercely protect their nesting grounds, egg caches, and food stores from outsiders. Combined with their habit of living in tightly packed encampments, any trespasser venturing near their shoreline settlements is quickly swarmed and driven off by the entire tribe.",al={"why do murlocs attack","murloc aggression","why are murlocs aggressive"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Where did murlocs come from?",a="Though they only recently invaded Azeroth's inland shores, murlocs are an ancient people. Their early forebears inhabited the abyssal depths of the world's oceans long before surface-dwellers ever knew of them, possibly predating even the trolls. In recent years, they have migrated steadily out of the oceanic abyss and adapted to freshwater waterways across the continents.",al={"murloc origin","history of murlocs","where do murlocs come from"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who are murlocs allied with?",a="Murlocs generally isolate themselves, but scholars suspect their emergence is tied to darker ocean powers. Some tribes revere sea witches or possibly slumbering deep-sea monstrosities waiting in the oceanic fathoms, carrying out rituals in their name.",al={"who do murlocs work for","murloc masters","murloc allies"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["race:Troll"]="The Darkspear tribe shares a bitter history with murlocs, having faced capture and ritual sacrifice by coastal tribes before joining Warchief Thrall.",["race:Orc"]="Orcs first encountered murlocs when rescuing the Darkspear trolls at sea, learning early on that their goofy demeanor hides savage brutality.",["faction:Alliance"]="Settlers across Westfall, Darkshore, and Lakeshire frequently find their shipping, farms, and docks besieged by encroaching murloc tribes.",["class:Shaman"]="Murloc mystics possess an innate, if crude, connection to water and earth elementals, bending the local spirits to bolster their shoreline villages."},kw={"brann bronzebeard","coastal","fin","mur'gul","murloc","rwlrwlrwl","shoreline","tadpole"},rel={"topic:naga","topic:darkspear-tribe","npc:thrall","zone:westfall","subzone:westfall-lighthouse","npc:brann-bronzebeard","topic:scourge","topic:gnoll"},src={"Murloc"}}
E["topic:naga"] = {t="topic",n="Naga",z="general",s="The naga are a monstrous, serpentine race dwelling beneath the ocean's depths, ruled by their ancient empress, Queen Azshara. Once the Highborne elite of the Kaldorei Empire, they were warped into amphibious horrors during the Great Sundering ten thousand years ago. Recently emerging from the dark waters, they have seized shorelines across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms to reclaim their forgotten surface empire.",h="The naga are a monstrous, serpentine race dwelling beneath the ocean's depths, ruled by their ancient...",sec={{t="Fall of the Highborne",b="Ten millennia ago, the Highborne nobility of the Kaldorei Empire ruled supreme from their grand capital of Zin-Azshari. Consumed by an obsession with arcane power, Queen Azshara and her inner court tapped the Well of Eternity to draw the gaze of the Dark Titan Sargeras, inviting the catastrophic invasion known as the War of the Ancients. When the Well of Eternity collapsed into ruin, triggering the Great Sundering, the palace slipped into the crushing abyss. Facing utter annihilation, Azshara bargained with the malevolent Old Gods deep below. In exchange for survival, she and her followers were transformed into cold, scaly, serpentine beings bound to the deeps.",sp=0},{t="Depths of Nazjatar",b="Submerged at the seafloor beneath the swirling fury of the Maelstrom, the survivors erected the aquatic empire of Nazjatar. Nursing a ten-thousand-year grudge against the surface world, the naga preserved their Highborne arrogance while mutating into distinct casts: hulking, brute-like warriors and multi-armed, spell-weaving sea witches. Under Azshara's unyielding gaze, they perfected their mastery over sea creatures, storm magic, and frost sorcery, slowly rebuilding their strength in wait for an opportunity to strike back against land-dwellers.",sp=0},{t="The Resurfacing",b="The naga broke their ten-thousand-year silence during the Third War. Thrall's Horde first encountered the sea witch Zar'jira in the Great Sea, while Lady Vashj answered the summons of Illidan Stormrage, marching naga forces across the ruins of Dalaran. Across the world today, naga encampments encroach aggressively upon coastlines in Azshara, Darkshore, Ashenvale, Desolace, Feralas, Dustwallow Marsh, Hillsbrad, Arathi, and Stranglethorn. Driven by ancient contempt and whispers of the Old Gods, they forge quiet alliances with the Twilight's Hammer and savage satyrs to claim what was once theirs.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What were the naga before they were transformed?",a="The naga were once the Highborne, the aristocratic upper class of the ancient night elven empire. Ten thousand years ago, during the Great Sundering that destroyed the Well of Eternity, Queen Azshara and her courtiers drowned in the collapsing sea. The Old Gods offered them salvation, mutating their bodies into serpentine, aquatic abominations.",al={"what are naga","naga origin","who were the naga"},it="origin"},{q="Who rules the naga?",a="The naga are ruled absolute by Queen Azshara, the former empress of the Kaldorei. From her underwater city of Nazjatar beneath the Maelstrom, she directs her sea witches and warriors. Even after thousands of years of monstrous transformation, the naga venerate her with fanatical devotion.",al={"naga leader","naga queen","who leads the naga"},it="leader"},{q="Why are naga attacking the beaches and coasts now?",a="After hiding beneath the waves for ten millennia, the naga resurfaced during the Third War. They believe all of ancient Kalimdor rightfully belongs to them as the true heirs of the Kaldorei Empire. Working under the guidance of Queen Azshara and the Old Gods, they seek to subjugate coastal territories and conquer the surface races.",al={"why are naga on land","naga invasion","why did naga return"},it="why_here"},{q="Why do male and female naga look so different?",a="Naga society has severe physical dimorphism. Naga females retain the intellectual and magical cunning of their Highborne ancestry, often possessing four arms and immense talent for arcane, frost, and storm spellcasting. Naga males are larger, hulking brutes covered in heavy scales and fins, built primarily for physical warfare and brute violence.",al={"male vs female naga","naga differences","naga sea witches"}},{q="What is the relationship between the naga and Illidan Stormrage?",a="Following the Third War, Illidan summoned deep-sea allies using powerful magic. Lady Vashj answered his call with an army of naga, honoring ancient Highborne ties and executing the covert will of the Old Gods, who wished to disrupt the surface world. Naga forces assisted Illidan in the Broken Isles, Lordaeron, and beyond.",al={"why did naga help illidan","illidan naga","lady vashj illidan"},it="allies"},{q="Where can naga be encountered right now?",a="Naga hold beachheads along numerous coastlines. In Kalimdor, they heavily infest Azshara, Darkshore, Ashenvale, Desolace, Feralas, and Dustwallow Marsh. In the Eastern Kingdoms, their raiding parties and altars can be discovered along the shores of Hillsbrad Foothills, Arathi Highlands, and Stranglethorn Vale.",al={"naga locations","where to find naga","naga zones"},it="place"},{q="What are the naga?",a="The naga are a cruel, serpentine race of aquatic beings who dwell in the ocean depths and have recently invaded the coasts of Azeroth. Once the aristocratic Highborne of the ancient night elf empire, they were transformed ten thousand years ago after the Great Sundering drowned their civilization. They possess formidable mastery over water, frost, and arcane sorcery, wielding heavy tridents and slithering upon muscular, scaled tails.",al={"who are the naga","what is a naga","naga race"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are naga hostile to land dwellers?",a="The naga harbor bitter resentment over their ancient banishment beneath the waves. Having lost the empire they once ruled as Highborne nobility, their minds and hearts were warped with hatred toward the surface world. They view air-breathing races as inferior usurpers of their rightful lands. Now driven to reclaim their place and serve their dark masters, they show no mercy to anyone trespassing upon the shores they claim.",al={"why do naga attack","why are naga aggressive","why do naga hate us"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="What is the history of the naga?",a="Over ten thousand years ago, Queen Azshara's Highborne courtiers brought about the War of the Ancients by reckless use of the Well of Eternity. When the Well collapsed during the Great Sundering, their capital sank into the ocean depths. Facing drowning, Azshara struck a pact with the Old God N'Zoth, who twisted the survivors into serpentine horrors. From the underwater city of Nazjatar beneath the Maelstrom, they spent millennia plotting their return.",al={"where did naga come from","naga history","history of naga"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="To the night elves, naga are a grotesque and agonizing reminder of the Highborne hubris that shattered Ancient Kalimdor. Seeing Azshara's former court slithering onto the shores of Darkshore and Ashenvale is a bitter call to purge their ancient kin.",["race:Troll"]="The Darkspear trolls hold an intense hatred for the naga ever since the sea witch Zar'jira drove them from their island home during the Great Sea voyage, an attack that claimed the life of their elder leader, Sen'jin.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance remembers the naga from the ruins of Dalaran, where Lady Vashj's creatures captured survivors and incited the division between the blood elves and Grand Marshal Garithos's forces.",["faction:Horde"]="Thrall and the Horde fought off naga raiders from the Darkspear Isles to the shores of Dustwallow Marsh, where Lady Darkscale butchered human settlements and assaulted Durotar's outskirts."},kw={"azshara","highborne","naga","nazjatar","old gods","sea witch","sundering","vashj"},rel={"topic:highborne","topic:night-elf","topic:war-of-the-ancients","topic:old-god","zone:azshara","zone:darkshore","zone:dustwallow","topic:third-war"},src={"Naga"}}
E["topic:new-horde"] = {t="topic",n="New Horde",z="general",s="The New Horde is the modern coalition forged under Warchief Thrall, departing from the bloodthirsty, demon-corrupted Old Horde of the First and Second Wars. Founded upon shamanic tradition, honor, and mutual survival, it unites the liberated orcs, the Darkspear trolls, the tauren tribes of Kalimdor, and a tenuous pact with the Forsaken in Lordaeron.",h="The New Horde is the modern coalition forged under Warchief Thrall, departing from the bloodthirsty...",sec={{t="A Legacy Reborn",b="The original Horde was a weapon crafted by the Burning Legion and the Shadow Council, fueling the orcs with demonic bloodlust and driving them across the Dark Portal. After the Second War ended in devastation, surviving orcs were rounded up into Alliance internment camps, sinking into a soul-draining lethargy. Through the efforts of Orgrim Doomhammer, Grommash Hellscream, and the young shaman Thrall, the orcs cast off their demonic shackles, rediscovered their ancestral shamanism, and fought their way to freedom.",sp=0},{t="Crossing the Great Sea",b="Following prophetic visions from the mysterious Prophet, Thrall led his people across the Great Sea to the untamed continent of Kalimdor. Along the way, the Horde welcomed Sen'jin's displaced Darkspear trolls. Upon arriving on Kalimdor, Thrall allied with Cairne Bloodhoof and the nomadic tauren, helping drive back hostile centaur tribes. Together, these disparate peoples fought alongside humans and night elves at Mount Hyjal, breaking the Burning Legion's invasion and carving out their new home of Durotar.",sp=0},{t="The Modern Coalition",b="Today, the New Horde strives to build a durable society from their fortified capital of Orgrimmar. While anchored by the strong bond between orcs, Darkspear trolls, and tauren, survival has forced Thrall to accept uncomfortable alliances—chief among them Sylvanas Windrunner and the Forsaken in the ruins of Lordaeron. Thrall pursues an uneasy peace with the Alliance, but old enmities, resource shortages, and hawkish elements among both factions keep borders simmering on the brink of war.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What makes the New Horde different from the Old Horde?",a="The Old Horde was ruled by demonic corruption, dark warlocks, and brutal conquest during the First and Second Wars. The New Horde, founded by Thrall, explicitly rejected the Burning Legion, renewed the orcs' lost shamanic roots, and emphasizes honor, cultural survival, and self-determination.",al={"old horde vs new horde","how is this horde different","thrall horde difference"}},{q="Who leads the New Horde?",a="Thrall, son of Durotan, serves as Warchief. He governs from the Valley of Wisdom in Orgrimmar, working in close council with High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof of the tauren and Shadow Hunter Vol'jin of the Darkspear trolls.",al={"warchief","who is the leader","thrall's role"},it="leader"},{q="Which races belong to the New Horde right now?",a="The core of the New Horde consists of the orcs, the Darkspear tribe of trolls, and the tauren of Mulgore. Across the sea in the Eastern Kingdoms, the undead Forsaken under Lady Sylvanas Windrunner hold membership through a pragmatic military alliance.",al={"horde races","who is in the horde","horde members"},it="allies"},{q="Why did the Forsaken join the New Horde?",a="Surrounded by the Scarlet Crusade, hostile humans, and Scourge remnants, Sylvanas Windrunner sought powerful allies for survival. The tauren, led by Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem, advocated for them in Orgrimmar, believing the Forsaken could be redeemed, convincing Thrall to accept them into a mutual pact of convenience.",al={"undead in the horde","why forsaken horde","why are undead with thrall"}},{q="Are the orcs still corrupted by demon blood?",a="No. When Grommash Hellscream and Thrall confronted the pit lord Mannoroth in Ashenvale, Grom struck down the demon at the cost of his own life. Mannoroth's demise shattered the blood curse once and for all, liberating every orc from demonic enslavement.",al={"demon blood","blood curse","are orcs still evil"}},{q="Is the New Horde currently at war with the Alliance?",a="Open, all-out war is officially held in check by an uneasy truce established between Thrall and Lady Jaina Proudmoore of Theramore. However, bitter skirmishes rage across Ashenvale, Alterac Valley, and the Arathi Basin, as mistrust and resource disputes continually threaten to erupt into open conflict.",al={"are horde and alliance at war","peace with alliance","horde alliance conflict"},it="conflict"},{q="What is the New Horde?",a="The New Horde is the coalition of races gathered under Warchief Thrall. Built upon the ideals of honor, shamanism, and mutual survival, it stands distinct from the bloodthirsty demonic puppet of the First and Second Wars. Today, its core consists of the free orcs who broke their demonic lethargy, the Darkspear trolls, the tauren of Kalimdor, and a tenuous pact with the Forsaken of Lordaeron.",al={"what is new horde","who are new horde","new horde faction"},it="identity",add=true,nx={8}},{q="Where did the New Horde come from?",a="The seeds of the New Horde were sown when Thrall, Orgrim Doomhammer, and Grommash Hellscream liberated the lethargic orcs from human internment camps across Lordaeron. Guided by a prophetic vision, Thrall led his people across the Great Sea to the forgotten continent of Kalimdor. Along their journey, they allied with Sen'jin's Darkspear trolls and later Cairne Bloodhoof's tauren, establishing a permanent home in the harsh lands of Durotar.",al={"history of new horde","origin of new horde","how was new horde founded"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why is it called the New Horde?",a="The name distinguishes Thrall's renewed alliance from the Old Horde that invaded Azeroth through the Dark Portal. While the Old Horde was bound to the Burning Legion's fel corruption and endless slaughter, the New Horde seeks redemption through ancient shamanic traditions, honor, and freedom from demonic dominion.",al={"why new horde","new horde name meaning"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="The New Horde is a sanctuary of outcasts. Membership offers mutual defense, cultural survival, and a shared oath of honor under Warchief Thrall.",["faction:Alliance"]="Many in the Alliance still view the New Horde through the blood-soaked memories of the First and Second Wars, struggling to believe orcs can ever abandon their violent past.",["race:Orc"]="The New Horde represents redemption: a chance to shed the lethargy of the internment camps, honor the sacrifice of Grom Hellscream, and live as free shamanic warriors.",["class:Shaman"]="Shamanism is the spiritual backbone of the New Horde, reviving ancient traditions connecting mortals with the elements that were almost lost during the demonic corruption."},kw={"darkspear","doomhammer","durotar","new horde","orgrimmar","shamanism","tauren","thrall","warchief"},rel={"topic:horde","npc:thrall","topic:old-horde","topic:tauren","topic:troll","topic:grommash-hellscream","npc:orgrim-doomhammer","quest:787"},src={"New Horde"}}
E["topic:night-elf"] = {t="topic",n="Night elf",z="general",s="The night elves, or kaldorei, are an ancient and reclusive race native to the forests of northern Kalimdor. Once the masters of a sprawling magical empire, they abandoned arcane magic following the catastrophic War of the Ancients and turned to the worship of Elune and the path of druidism. In the wake of the Third War, having sacrificed their immortality to stop the Burning Legion, they joined the Alliance to defend their ancestral lands from Horde encroachment.",h="The night elves, or kaldorei, are an ancient and reclusive race native to the forests of northern Kalimdor.",sec={{t="Children of the Stars",b="Millennia ago, an inquisitive nocturnal tribe gathered around the shores of the Well of Eternity, a vast font of raw arcane energy. Over generations, the Well's cosmic magic transformed them into tall, violet-skinned, long-lived beings who called themselves the kaldorei—'children of the stars.' Guiding their early society were the moon goddess Elune and the forest demigod Cenarius, who nurtured their deep connection to the living wilds of Kalimdor.",sp=0},{t="The Empire and the Sundering",b="As centuries passed, the ruling caste—the Highborne, led by Queen Azshara—became obsessed with unlocking the Well's infinite arcane secrets. Their reckless use of sorcery shattered the troll empires and built breathtaking wonders, but it ultimately caught the attention of the Burning Legion. The ensuing demonic invasion ended in the Great Sundering: the Well imploded, shattering ancient Kalimdor into fragmented continents and destroying Azshara's realm. Horrified by the calamity, the surviving kaldorei strictly outlawed arcane magic under penalty of death or exile.",sp=0},{t="The Long Vigil and the New Age",b="For ten thousand years, the night elves retreated into seclusion. Druids slumbered in the Emerald Dream to heal the world while the Priestesses of Elune and Sentinels stood guard over Kalimdor's woodlands beneath the World Tree Nordrassil. This isolation ended during the Third War when the Legion returned to attack Mount Hyjal. The kaldorei sacrificed their immortality to defeat Archimonde. Now, led by High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind and Archdruid Fandral Staghelm, the night elves dwell in their young World Tree, Teldrassil, and fight alongside the Alliance to safeguard Ashenvale.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does 'kaldorei' mean?",a="In their native language of Darnassian, kaldorei translates to 'children of the stars.' It was the name adopted by the early night elves after settling around the Well of Eternity and dedicating themselves to the moon goddess Elune.",al={"kaldorei meaning","what is kaldorei","night elf native name"},it="name"},{q="Where did the night elves originate?",a="Roughly fifteen thousand years ago, a reclusive tribe of dark trolls settled along the radiant banks of the Well of Eternity. Exposed to the lake's raw magical energies over generations, their bodies and minds transformed, turning them into the graceful, long-lived kaldorei.",al={"night elf origins","where do night elves come from","are elves evolved trolls"},it="origin"},{q="Why don't night elves practice arcane magic?",a="Ten thousand years ago, Queen Azshara and her Highborne sorcerers drew the demonic Burning Legion to Azeroth through their reckless manipulation of the Well of Eternity. The resulting war shattered the world during the Great Sundering. Afterwards, the night elves outlawed arcane practice to prevent demons from ever finding Azeroth again.",al={"why no night elf mages","night elf arcane ban","why did night elves ban magic"},it="motive"},{q="Are night elves still immortal?",a="No. The night elves gained their immortality and agelessness from the World Tree Nordrassil, which had been blessed by the Dragon Aspects. During the Battle of Mount Hyjal in the Third War, the kaldorei detonated Nordrassil's ancestral power to destroy the demon lord Archimonde, forfeiting their immortality in the process.",al={"are night elves immortal","did night elves lose immortality","how long do night elves live"},it="power"},{q="Where is the night elf capital located?",a="The night elf capital is Darnassus, constructed atop the massive canopy of Teldrassil, a colossal World Tree planted off the northwest coast of Kalimdor following the Third War.",al={"night elf capital","where do night elves live","darnassus location"},it="place"},{q="Why did the night elves join the Alliance?",a="After centuries of isolation, the kaldorei fought alongside humans and dwarves against the Burning Legion at Mount Hyjal. Following the war, the Horde began heavily logging Ashenvale's sacred forests for lumber, prompting the night elves to formalize an alliance with the Eastern Kingdoms to defend their borders.",al={"why are night elves alliance","night elf alliance allegiance","why join alliance"},it="allies"},{q="What is the story of the night elves?",a="The night elves, or kaldorei, are an ancient race transformed millennia ago by the magical waters of the Well of Eternity. Once masters of a vast empire centered around arcane mastery, their reckless reliance on magic drew the demonic Burning Legion to Azeroth, shattering the continent in the Great Sundering. Devastated by the catastrophe, the survivors abandoned the arcane and turned to druidism and the worship of Elune. Guided by Malfurion Stormrage and High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind, they spent ten thousand years guarding the natural world in watchful isolation.",al={"night elf lore","history of the night elves","who are the kaldorei"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do the night elves stand in the world today?",a="Their ten-thousand-year isolation, the Long Vigil, ended during the Third War when they allied with outlanders at the Battle of Mount Hyjal. While the world tree Nordrassil was saved, its sacrifice cost the night elves their immortality. Today, they have planted a new World Tree, Teldrassil, off Kalimdor's northwest coast and built their capital, Darnassus, amidst its branches. Finding their sacred woodlands of Ashenvale encroached upon by the Horde's lumber camps, they have formally cast their lot with the Alliance to defend their ancestral lands.",al={"night elf current state","night elves today","where are the kaldorei now"},it="today",add=true},{q="What does it mean for a new adventurer to be a night elf?",a="As a night elf stepping into the world, you bear the weight of an ancient civilization adapting to profound vulnerability. Your people have lost the gift of immortality and must now navigate unfamiliar alliances across the sea while protecting their fragile new World Tree, Teldrassil. Whether you serve the Moon Goddess as a priestess, safeguard nature as a druid, or stalk the shadows as a sentinel, you are called to defend your ancient forests and people against the creeping corruption of demons and the axes of the encroaching Horde.",al={"starting as a night elf","being a night elf","night elf adventurer"},it="for_you",rc="Night Elf",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Your people carry the wisdom and burdens of ten millennia, navigating a perilous new reality where mortality is real, your ancestral forests are under siege, and ancient traditions are tested.",["race:Troll"]="Troll history remembers the kaldorei as the sorcerous upstarts who shattered the great Amani and Gurubashi empires thousands of years ago, cementing an ancient, bitter rivalry.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde's aggressive lumber operations in Ashenvale have made the fierce Sentinels and watchful druids of the kaldorei some of your most relentless and deadly adversaries on Kalimdor.",["class:Druid"]="Night elf tradition shaped modern druidism under the demigod Cenarius, binding kaldorei guardians to the Emerald Dream and the defense of the natural balance."},kw={"ashenvale","cenarius","darnassus","elune","kaldorei","night elf","nordrassil","sundering","teldrassil","well of eternity"},rel={"zone:darnassus","topic:elune","topic:cenarius","topic:nordrassil","topic:highborne","zone:ashenvale","npc:tyrande-whisperwind","npc:malfurion-stormrage"},src={"Night elf"}}
E["topic:nordrassil"] = {t="topic",n="Nordrassil",z="general",s="Nordrassil, the 'Crown of the Heavens,' is the original World Tree planted atop Mount Hyjal to shield the second Well of Eternity. Blessed by the Great Dragon Aspects, it granted the night elves ageless immortality and a deep connection to the Emerald Dream for ten thousand years. Its sacrifice during the climax of the Third War broke Archimonde's assault, leaving the tree wounded and the night elves mortal.",h="Nordrassil, the 'Crown of the Heavens,' is the original World Tree planted atop Mount Hyjal to shield the...",sec={{t="Crown of the Heavens",b="Ten thousand years ago, in the ashes of the Great Sundering, Illidan Stormrage cast vials of the original Well of Eternity into a mountaintop lake atop Mount Hyjal, birthing a new font of arcane power. To prevent this raw magic from corrupting the world or drawing the Burning Legion anew, the Dragon Aspect Alexstrasza planted an acorn from the Mother Tree, G'Hanir, directly over the waters. From it grew Nordrassil. The roots spread deep beneath Azeroth, sealing the Well's volatile energies and distributing gentle, life-giving vigor across the continents.",sp=0},{t="The Aspects' Gifts",b="Nordrassil became the spiritual and cultural heart of night elf civilization through the blessings of three Great Dragon Aspects. Alexstrasza imbued the tree with peerless vigor, extending vitality to the kaldorei. Ysera bound the tree to the Emerald Dream, allowing night elven druids to enter her slumbering realm with effortless clarity. Crucially, Nozdormu the Timeless One cast his blessing across its boughs: as long as the colossal tree stood, the night elves would never age or fall prey to natural disease, gifting an entire race with enduring immortality.",sp=0},{t="The Fall at Mount Hyjal",b="Nordrassil's tranquility ended with the Third War, when the demon lord Archimonde marched upon Mount Hyjal to tear the tree apart and claim the Well's infinite power. With mortal defenders pushed to the brink, Malfurion Stormrage made the ultimate choice. Sounding the Horn of Cenarius, he called upon thousands of nature spirits and unleashed the stored enchantments within Nordrassil itself. The ensuing cataclysmic blast obliterated Archimonde and decimated the Legion. Though Nordrassil survived to heal slowly, its enchantments shattered—stripping the night elves of their beloved immortality.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does Nordrassil mean?",a="In Darnassian, Nordrassil translates to 'Crown of the Heavens.' It earned this name due to its monumental height, towering high above the summit of sacred Mount Hyjal.",al={"nordrassil translation","what does nordrassil mean in darnassian","nordrassil name meaning"},it="name"},{q="Where is Nordrassil located?",a="Nordrassil stands at the summit of Mount Hyjal in northern Kalimdor, rooted directly inside the waters of the second Well of Eternity created by Illidan Stormrage.",al={"nordrassil location","where is the original world tree","where is nordrassil"},it="place"},{q="Why did the night elves lose their immortality?",a="To defeat the demon lord Archimonde at the Battle of Mount Hyjal, Malfurion Stormrage unleashed the magical wards and blessings bound within Nordrassil. The resulting detonation obliterated Archimonde but burned away the blessings of the Dragon Aspects, ending Nozdormu's spell of eternal life.",al={"why aren't night elves immortal anymore","night elf mortality","loss of immortality"},it="power"},{q="How is Nordrassil related to Teldrassil?",a="Nordrassil is the original World Tree atop Mount Hyjal. Teldrassil is a much younger World Tree grown off the coast of Kalimdor by Archdruid Fandral Staghelm and the Circle of the Ancients, planted in a desperate bid to replace Nordrassil and reclaim their lost immortality.",al={"nordrassil vs teldrassil","difference between teldrassil and nordrassil","is teldrassil nordrassil"},it="ties"},{q="Did Nordrassil die when Archimonde was defeated?",a="No. While the blast severely scorched and wounded the World Tree, Nordrassil's roots survived. The tree remains standing atop Mount Hyjal, slowly mending its wounds over time, even though its ancient dragon blessings were dispelled.",al={"is nordrassil dead","did nordrassil survive","nordrassil destroyed"},it="today"},{q="Which dragons blessed Nordrassil?",a="Nordrassil was blessed by three Dragon Aspects: Alexstrasza the Life-Binder, who planted it and gave it vitality; Ysera the Dreamer, who linked it to the Emerald Dream; and Nozdormu the Timeless One, who granted the night elves eternal youth.",al={"who blessed nordrassil","dragon blessings world tree","which aspects blessed nordrassil"},it="allies"},{q="What is Nordrassil?",a="Nordrassil is the original World Tree of the night elves, standing atop Mount Hyjal above the second Well of Eternity. Grown to shield and contain the Well's raw arcane magic, its massive roots spread life-giving energy throughout Azeroth. For millennia, it served as the sacred heart of night elf civilization.",al={"what is nordrassil","who is nordrassil","nordrassil identity"},it="identity",add=true},{q="What is the history of Nordrassil?",a="After the Great Sundering, the dragon Alexstrasza planted an acorn from the Mother Tree, G'Hanir, into the second Well of Eternity created atop Mount Hyjal. The Great Aspects Alexstrasza, Ysera, and Nozdormu blessed the growing tree, binding the night elves to nature, granting druids direct access to the Emerald Dream, and bestowing immortality upon the Kaldorei. Nordrassil stood unharmed for ten thousand years until the Third War, when Malfurion Stormrage detonated the tree's ancestral energies to obliterate the demon lord Archimonde.",al={"history of nordrassil","where did nordrassil come from","how was nordrassil created"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Nordrassil was the cradle of your civilization for ten millennia. Its sacrifice saved the world from Archimonde, but left you grappling with aging, sickness, and mortality for the very first time.",["class:Druid"]="Rooted above the second Well of Eternity, Nordrassil's bond with Ysera allowed druids to effortlessly cross into the Emerald Dream. Its scarring remains a stark reminder of the cost of defending nature.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance's presence at Mount Hyjal forged the desperate coalition that shielded Nordrassil from the Burning Legion, securing Kalimdor's salvation.",["faction:Horde"]="Warchief Thrall led the Horde to the slopes of Mount Hyjal alongside humans and night elves, buying Malfurion the time needed to spring the trap at the base of Nordrassil."},kw={"alexstrasza","archimonde","hyjal","immortality","malfurion","nordrassil","nozdormu","teldrassil","well of eternity","world tree","ysera"},rel={"quest:940","npc:malfurion-stormrage","topic:third-war","topic:emerald-dream","npc:fandral-staghelm","topic:druid","npc:tyrande-whisperwind","topic:cenarius"},src={"Nordrassil"}}
E["topic:old-god"] = {t="topic",n="Old God",z="general",s="The Old Gods are colossal, parasitic horrors of flesh and madness that plummeted from the cosmos during Azeroth's primordial dawn. Though vanquished and bound deep beneath the crust by the Titans, their ancient whispers still corrode mortal minds, twist nature, and drive doomsday cults to unearth their prisons.",h="The Old Gods are colossal, parasitic horrors of flesh and madness that plummeted from the cosmos during...",sec={{t="The Black Empire",b="Long before mortal records began, Azeroth was dominated by the Black Empire—a terrifying civilization ruled by the Old Gods: Y'Shaarj, C'Thun, Yogg-Saron, and N'Zoth. These colossal mountains of chaotic flesh embedded themselves into the planet's crust, commanding elemental armies and insectoid swarms. When the Titans arrived, their armies fought an apocalyptic war to uproot them. However, when the Titan Aman'Thul ripped Y'Shaarj from the surface, it tore a catastrophic wound into the world's crust, causing the planet's arcane lifeblood to hemorrhage. Realizing that slaying the entities outright would destroy Azeroth itself, the Titans chose to bind the surviving Old Gods in vast, subterranean prisons to keep them dormant forever.",sp=0},{t="Whispers from the Deep",b="Even chained beneath miles of stone and enchanted wards, the Old Gods have never truly slept. Their psychic malice seeps continually into the world, corrupting the vulnerable. It was their collective malice that slowly drove Neltharion the Earth-Warder mad during the War of the Ancients, twisting the noble dragon into Deathwing. When Queen Azshara and her Highborne drowned beneath the raging waters of the Great Sundering, an Old God answered their desperation, transforming them into the serpentine naga. Furthermore, their insidious taint has breached the Emerald Dream, twisting druidic realms into the creeping terror known as the Emerald Nightmare.",sp=0},{t="Stirrings in the Sands",b="In the modern era, mortal civilizations treat the Old Gods as myth, yet their prisons are fracturing. Nearly a millennium ago, the insectoid qiraji swarmed out of the southern desert of Silithus in the War of the Shifting Sands, serving their master C'Thun until the night elves and dragons sealed them behind the Scarab Wall. Today, ominous cults like the Twilight's Hammer gather in dark corners across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms, performing foul rites to shatter the Titans' bindings and hasten the end of all mortal life.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What exactly are the Old Gods?",a="They are ancient, eldritch monstrosities of flesh, tentacles, and void energy that crashed onto Azeroth eons ago. They feed on chaos and seek to corrupt the nascent world, driving mortals and elementals toward madness.",al={"what is an old god","old gods lore","who are the old gods"},it="identity"},{q="How many Old Gods were there on Azeroth?",a="Four great Old Gods ruled primordial Azeroth: Y'Shaarj, C'Thun, Yogg-Saron, and N'Zoth. Y'Shaarj was ripped apart by the Titans, while the remaining three were chained in subterranean vaults across the globe.",al={"how many old gods","list of old gods","names of old gods"}},{q="Why didn't the Titans just kill them all?",a="The Old Gods burrowed so deeply into Azeroth's bedrock that ripping them out proved catastrophic. When Aman'Thul wrenched Y'Shaarj out, it tore an immense, bleeding wound in the planet's crust. Fearing the world's total destruction, the Titans built prisons instead.",al={"why didn't titans kill old gods","why imprison old gods","why spare old gods"}},{q="Are the Old Gods connected to Deathwing and the naga?",a="Yes. The Old Gods whispered into the mind of Neltharion the Earth-Warder until he snapped and became Deathwing. When Queen Azshara and her Highborne sank during the Great Sundering, an Old God answered their cries and remade them into the naga.",al={"did old gods make naga","old gods deathwing","who corrupted neltharion"},it="ties"},{q="Who are the Twilight's Hammer?",a="The Twilight's Hammer is a nihilistic, apocalyptic cult dedicated to serving the Old Gods. They seek to unbind the imprisoned entities, unleash the elemental lords, and plunge the world into total ruin.",al={"twilights hammer cult","who worships old gods","old god cultists"},it="allies"},{q="Where are the Old Gods imprisoned?",a="Their known prisons are scattered across the world, entombed deep beneath Titan ruins. For instance, C'Thun lies trapped beneath Ahn'Qiraj in Silithus, while others were bound beneath the northern reaches and deep subterranean foundations.",al={"where are old gods","old god locations","c'thun location"},it="why_here"},{q="Why are the Old Gods hostile to mortals?",a="The Old Gods exist solely to corrupt reality and claim worlds for the Void. To these primordial horrors, mortal life is little more than insects to be twisted, driven mad, or utterly enslaved. They hold no concept of peaceful coexistence; their dark whispers seek only to undermine your sanity and bend every living soul to their will.",al={"why do old gods attack","old god hostility","why are old gods evil"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Where did the Old Gods come from?",a="The Old Gods were spawned by the void lords—malevolent beings from beyond physical reality who sought to corrupt slumbering titan world-souls. Flung blindly across the Great Dark Beyond, these mountains of flesh plummeted into primordial Azeroth. Embedding deep within the planet's crust, they forged the tyrannical Black Empire until the Titans arrived to defeat and seal them beneath the earth.",al={"old god origins","origin of the old gods","how were the old gods created"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The kaldorei fought the qiraji minion hordes in the War of the Shifting Sands, sealing Silithus behind the Scarab Wall, while their druids now battle the Old Gods' taint creeping into the Emerald Dream.",["class:Priest"]="Priests who delve into shadow magic often brush against the cold, maddening whispers of the Void, a dangerous power closely tied to the ancient horrors chained beneath the earth.",["class:Druid"]="Druids hold a sacred duty to cleanse the Emerald Nightmare, an unnatural corruption born from the Old Gods' subterranean tendrils piercing the Dream.",["class:Warlock"]="Though warlocks primarily draw from fel magic and demons of the Burning Legion, some foolishly seek the forbidden curses and sacrificial rites left behind by the Old Gods' servants."},kw={"ahn'qiraj","black empire","c'thun","n'zoth","old god","qiraji","silithus","twilight's hammer","void","y'shaarj","yogg-saron"},rel={"subzone:scarab-wall","zone:silithus","topic:twilight-s-hammer","topic:titan","topic:emerald-dream","topic:naga","npc:deathwing","topic:war-of-the-ancients"},src={"Old God"}}
E["topic:old-horde"] = {t="topic",n="Old Horde",z="general",s="The Old Horde was the blood-crazed coalition of orcish clans manipulated by demonic power into conquering Draenor and invading Azeroth. Led first by Blackhand and later by Orgrim Doomhammer, its path of destruction shaped the First and Second Wars until its decisive defeat by the Alliance of Lordaeron. Today, Thrall's New Horde seeks to overcome that dark legacy, while isolated remnants like the Dark Horde still cling to its bloody past.",h="The Old Horde was the blood-crazed coalition of orcish clans manipulated by demonic power into conquering...",sec={{t="Rise and Deception",b="Before it crossed into Azeroth, the Horde was forged on Draenor through demonic manipulation. The demon lord Kil'jaeden used the warlock Gul'dan and the elder shaman Ner'zhul to deceive the proud, spiritual orc clans into believing the draenei plotted their extermination. As elemental spirits abandoned the warring orcs, shamanistic traditions were cast aside for warlock magic. Sealed by the consumption of the blood of Mannoroth, the clans surrendered to bloodlust, slaughtering the draenei and draining their homeland of life.",sp=0},{t="Invasion of Azeroth",b="With Draenor's lands withering away and resources exhausted, the warlock Gul'dan and the possessed human guardian Medivh opened the Dark Portal into Azeroth. The Horde poured through, igniting the First War against the Kingdom of Stormwind. Under Warchief Blackhand—and the covert strings of Gul'dan's Shadow Council—the Horde battered the southern realm. Toward the war's conclusion, Orgrim Doomhammer slew Blackhand and purged the Shadow Council, taking command of the Horde and razing Stormwind to the ground.",sp=0},{t="Collapse and Demonic Hangover",b="Orgrim Doomhammer drove the Horde north across the seas into the Second War, forging alliances with forest trolls and goblin mercenaries. However, at the height of the siege of Lordaeron, Gul'dan deserted the war effort with his loyalists to seek the Tomb of Sargeras. Fractured and reeling from the betrayal, the Horde was pushed back to Blackrock Mountain and crushed by the Alliance of Lordaeron. Defeated, their portal destroyed, surviving orcs were herded into internment camps, afflicted with lethargy as the demonic blood haze faded.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the difference between the Old Horde and the New Horde?",a="The Old Horde was driven by demonic corruption, warlock magic, and conquest under the influence of the Burning Legion and Gul'dan's Shadow Council. Thrall's New Horde, established after escaping the internment camps, returned to traditional shamanism and honor, forming a coalition of survivors rather than an invading army.",al={"old horde vs new horde","difference in hordes","thrall's horde vs old horde"},it="identity"},{q="Who led the Old Horde?",a="Warchief Blackhand initially led the Horde during the genocide of the draenei and most of the First War, though he served as a figurehead for Gul'dan. Orgrim Doomhammer slew Blackhand in a duel, assumed the mantle of Warchief, and led the Horde through the end of the First War and the Second War.",al={"who was warchief","old horde leader","who led the first horde"},it="leader"},{q="Why did the orcs drink demon blood?",a="Gul'dan offered the blood of the pit lord Mannoroth to the clan chieftains before their final siege on the draenei city of Shattrath. He promised them overwhelming power and victory, concealing the fact that drinking it bound their souls to the Burning Legion in reckless bloodlust.",al={"why did orcs drink fel blood","mannoroth blood","orc corruption reason"},it="motive"},{q="What was the Shadow Council?",a="The Shadow Council was a secret order of warlocks, necrolytes, and assassins founded by Gul'dan. It controlled Warchief Blackhand and steered the Horde from the shadows according to the will of the Burning Legion until Orgrim Doomhammer uncovered and purged them.",al={"who were the shadow council","gul'dan council","shadow council role"}},{q="How was the Old Horde defeated?",a="During the Second War, Gul'dan abandoned the Horde at the gates of Lordaeron to claim godhood at the Tomb of Sargeras, fracturing the army. The Alliance of Lordaeron rallied, pushing Doomhammer back to Blackrock Mountain, capturing him, and herding the demoralized remnants into internment camps.",al={"how did the old horde lose","second war ending","defeat of the orcs"},it="history"},{q="Do any remnants of the Old Horde still exist?",a="Yes. Splinter factions such as the 'Dark Horde' holding Blackrock Spire, led by Rend Blackhand, reject Thrall's New Horde and cling to the old brutal ways of conquest, allying with dragons and forest trolls.",al={"is the old horde still around","dark horde","remnants of old horde"},it="today"},{q="Why is it called the Old Horde?",a="During its existence, it was simply known as the Horde or the Orcish Horde. Historians and travelers now call it the Old Horde or the first Horde to distinguish it from the New Horde founded by Thrall. Thrall's Horde broke free from demonic corruption and returned to the spiritual ways of shamanism, leaving behind the dark legacy of the original faction.",al={"why old horde","old horde name meaning","why called old horde"},it="name",add=true},{q="Who did the Old Horde ally with?",a="Originally, the Old Horde consisted solely of the orcish clans of Draenor, though they were soon joined by ogres from their homeworld. During the Second War on Azeroth, their ranks expanded further to include the forest trolls of the Amani Empire and mercenary goblins who provided engineering, Zeppelins, and munitions to bolster their conquest.",al={"old horde allies","who fought with the old horde","old horde races"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["race:Orc"]="For orcs, the Old Horde represents a shameful era of demonic slavery and ruin, whose bitter legacy Thrall strives to overcome through honor and shamanism.",["race:Human"]="Humanity still bears the deep scars of the Old Horde's invasion, which leveled Stormwind City and ravaged the lands of Lordaeron during the First and Second Wars.",["class:Shaman"]="The formation of the Old Horde led the elemental spirits of Draenor to turn their backs on the orcs, a spiritual breach healed only recently under Thrall's guidance.",["class:Warlock"]="The Old Horde was the cradle of modern mortal warlockcraft, birthed by Gul'dan and his Shadow Council through the corrupting powers of the Burning Legion.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance of Lordaeron was created specifically to unite the disparate mortal kingdoms against the existential threat posed by the Old Horde."},kw={"blackhand","dark portal","draenor","first war","gul'dan","mannoroth","old horde","orgrim doomhammer","second war","shadow council"},rel={"npc:orgrim-doomhammer","topic:first-war","topic:second-war","topic:shadow-council","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","subzone:dark-portal","topic:blackrock-clan","topic:new-horde"},src={"Old Horde"}}
E["topic:orc"] = {t="topic",n="Orc",z="general",s="Originally a shamanistic clan people hailing from the alien world of Draenor, the orcs were deceived and corrupted into a bloodthirsty conquering force by the demonic Burning Legion. After invading Azeroth and suffering eventual defeat and internment, they were rallied by Warchief Thrall to reclaim their spiritual heritage. In this new era, the orcs lead the Horde from their newly carved nation of Durotar, striving for honor while grappling with the lingering shadow of their demonic past.",h="Originally a shamanistic clan people hailing from the alien world of Draenor, the orcs were deceived and...",sec={{t="Children of Draenor",b="Before their fall, orcs lived as nomadic hunter-gatherers and spiritual clans across the rugged landscapes of Draenor. Their culture was grounded in ancestral reverence and deep communion with elemental spirits, guided primarily by revered shamans. The disparate clans—such as the Frostwolves, Warsongs, and Shadowmoon—maintained a fragile peace mediated at biannual gatherings known as the Kosh'harg festival. Though physically formidable and deeply proud, traditional orcish society was defined by survival, honor, and harmony with the natural elements rather than mindless conquest.",sp=0},{t="Corruption and the Great Wars",b="The fate of the orcs shattered when Kil'jaeden and the warlock Gul'dan orchestrated their corruption. Tricked into drinking the blood of the demon Mannoroth, the orcs were seized by a supernatural bloodlust that warped their natural brown skin to a sickly green. Forged into the original Horde, they nearly eradicated Draenor's draenei before pouring through the Dark Portal into Azeroth. The First and Second Wars saw human kingdoms fall and orcish armies reach Lordaeron's gates, but internal treachery and the unified Alliance of Lordaeron eventually crushed the invaders, herding survivors into miserable internment camps.",sp=0},{t="A New Beginning in Kalimdor",b="Imprisonment stripped the orcs of their demonic frenzy, leaving them lethargic and broken until Thrall, an orc raised in human captivity, escaped. Reconnecting with shamanism, Thrall rallied the Frostwolf clan, freed his kin from the camps, and established a renewed Horde. Heeding prophetic warnings, he led his people across the Great Sea to Kalimdor. There, the sacrifice of Grom Hellscream finally broke Mannoroth's blood curse forever. Today, the orcs have carved out the arid realm of Durotar and built the fortress-city of Orgrimmar, striving to maintain their hard-won honor amidst renewed tensions with the Alliance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are orcs green?",a="Originally, orcs had brown skin. Their green complexion is the result of pervasive exposure to fel magic and demonic corruption, sparked by the warlocks of the Shadow Council and solidified when chieftains drank the blood of the pit lord Mannoroth.",al={"why is orc skin green","orc green skin origin","brown orcs vs green orcs"},it="origin"},{q="Where did the orcs come from?",a="Orcs are not native to Azeroth; they originated on the alien world of Draenor. They traveled to Azeroth through the Dark Portal when Gul'dan and the demon-possessed human sorcerer Medivh opened the rift between worlds.",al={"orc homeworld","are orcs native to azeroth","where are orcs from"},it="origin"},{q="Who is the leader of the orcs?",a="The orcs are led by Warchief Thrall, son of Durotan. Raised by humans as a gladiator slave, Thrall rediscovered the ancestral art of shamanism, liberated the orc internment camps, and guided the new Horde to Kalimdor.",al={"orc warchief","orc faction leader","who leads the orcs"},it="leader"},{q="What is the blood curse?",a="The blood curse was the demonic enslavement inflicted upon the orcs when they drank the blood of the annihilan Mannoroth. It instilled an uncontrollable bloodlust that fueled their wars, but it was finally shattered when Grom Hellscream sacrificed his life to slay Mannoroth.",al={"blood of mannoroth","orc bloodlust curse","mannoroth blood"},it="threat"},{q="Why do the orcs live in Durotar?",a="Following the Third War, Thrall settled the orcs in the harsh, dry coastal realm he named Durotar in honor of his father, Durotan. Its punishing terrain serves as both penance for past sins and a forge to keep his people strong and resilient.",al={"why is durotar a desert","why orgrimmar","why durotar"},it="why_here"},{q="Are all orcs part of Thrall's Horde?",a="No. While Thrall leads the New Horde, rogue clans remain. The Dragonmaw clan still clings to their enslaver traditions in the Wetlands, while the Blackrock clan under Rend Blackhand continues to wage war from Blackrock Mountain, rejecting Thrall's peace.",al={"evil orcs","hostile orcs","are all orcs friendly to horde"},it="ties"},{q="What is the story of the orcs?",a="Originally a proud, shamanistic people living in independent clans on Draenor, the orcs were deceived and corrupted by the demon lord Kil'jaeden and the warlock Gul'dan. Drunk on demonic blood, they forged the first Horde, conquered their homeworld, and marched through the Dark Portal to invade Azeroth. Defeated by the Alliance of Lordaeron, many were herded into internment camps until the shaman Thrall united the scattered clans. He guided his people to rediscover their noble shamanic heritage, broke the demonic curse on Kalimdor, and founded a new sovereign homeland.",al={"orc history","who are the orcs","story of the orcs"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do the orcs stand in the world today?",a="The orcs now stand as the heart and backbone of the modern Horde. Under Warchief Thrall, they govern from their capital city of Orgrimmar in the arid land of Durotar, standing shoulder-to-shoulder with their Darkspear troll and tauren allies. Though free from demonic servitude and focused on rebuilding their society through honor and spiritual wisdom, they face fragile truces with the Alliance, hostile neighbors, and internal disputes with lingering renegade clans who reject Thrall's peaceful ideals.",al={"orcs today","current state of orcs","where are the orcs now"},it="today",add=true},{q="What does it mean to be an orc adventurer?",a="Taking up arms as an orc means carrying the weight of a turbulent past and the honor of a redeemed people. You are sworn to uphold Thrall's New Horde, trading mindless savagery for the disciplined path of the warrior or the spiritual guidance of the spirits. Whether carving out the rugged frontier of Durotar or defending your allies across Kalimdor, your strength and valor directly shape the future of a nation striving to prove its honor to the world.",al={"playing an orc","orc meaning","what does it mean to be an orc"},it="for_you",rc="Orc",add=true}},ang={["faction:Horde"]="Orcs form the military and ideological backbone of the modern Horde, championing honor and survival alongside their troll, tauren, and Forsaken allies.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance races still view orcs with deep suspicion and lingering resentment, remembering the slaughter of the First and Second Wars and the destruction of Stormwind.",["class:Shaman"]="For orcs, shamanism is not merely magic—it represents their reclaimed cultural soul and spiritual redemption after decades of demonic servitude.",["class:Warlock"]="Orc warlocks are viewed with deep distrust and revulsion by their kin, as fel magic is the exact force that corrupted and enslaved their race."},kw={"bloodlust","draenor","durotar","fel","koshharg","mannoroth","orc","orcs","orgrimmar","shamanism","thrall"},rel={"npc:thrall","topic:new-horde","zone:orgrimmar","zone:durotar","topic:burning-legion","subzone:dark-portal","topic:warsong-clan","topic:dragonmaw-clan"},src={"Orc"}}
E["topic:paladin"] = {t="topic",n="Paladin",z="general",s="Paladins are holy knights of the Alliance who blend martial prowess with divine magic. Clad in heavy plate armor, they wield weapons empowered by the Holy Light to protect the innocent, heal the wounded, and strike down wicked foes.",h="Paladins are holy knights of the Alliance who blend martial prowess with divine magic.",sec={{t="Origins in Lordaeron",b="The paladins were originally established during the Second War by Archbishop Alonsus Faol. Having witnessed the devastation wrought upon the pious Clerics of Northshire during the First War, Faol recognized that healers alone could not survive the brutality of orcish warfare. He gathered virtuous knights of Lordaeron and trained surviving clerics in the ways of martial combat and divine magic. Baptized as the Knights of the Silver Hand and led by Uther the Lightbringer, these champions provided both physical defense and spiritual renewal to the kingdoms of humanity.",sp=0},{t="Champions of the Holy Light",b="Where priests act as the voice of the Holy Light—preaching faith and tending the spirits of the common folk—paladins serve as its martial arm. Their profound conviction grants them miraculous powers: they project emboldening auras, seal sanctified blessings upon their allies, and mend fatal injuries. In combat against unholy horrors, the Light burns through their strikes, allowing paladins to incinerate undead and demonic entities with righteous fury.",sp=0},{t="A Legacy in the South",b="The fall of Lordaeron during the Third War scattered the original order, yet the ideals of the Silver Hand endure. Today, the mantle has been embraced by the humans of Stormwind and the stalwart dwarves of Ironforge. Operating out of sanctuaries like the Cathedral of Light and the Hall of Mysteries, these new paladins pledge their swords and shields to uphold justice and defend the Alliance from the gathering shadows.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the difference between a priest and a paladin?",a="Priests serve as the voice of the Light, ministering to the faithful and offering pastoral guidance through prayer and ritual. Paladins serve as the hand and hammer of the Light, donning heavy plate armor and taking up weapons to directly enforce divine justice and defend the weak on the battlefield.",al={"paladin vs priest","difference between priest and paladin","how are paladins different from priests"}},{q="Who created the first paladins?",a="Archbishop Alonsus Faol created the order during the Second War. After seeing the Clerics of Northshire slaughtered during the First War, he recruited devout knights and trained surviving clerics to unite martial discipline with holy magic under the banner of the Knights of the Silver Hand.",al={"who made paladins","founder of paladins","origin of the silver hand"},it="origin"},{q="Which races can be paladins?",a="Currently, only humans and dwarves train as paladins within the Alliance. While the order was originally exclusively human, the dwarves of Ironforge embraced the teachings of the Holy Light and were welcomed into the Knights of the Silver Hand.",al={"can horde be paladins","who can be a paladin","paladin races"},it="people"},{q="Why are paladins effective against the undead?",a="The Holy Light is anathema to necrotic corruption and demonic energy. When paladins channel divine power, it purges blight and incinerates unholy creatures, making them the foremost champions against the Scourge and other foul beings.",al={"paladins vs undead","why does holy magic hurt undead","paladin scourge counter"},it="danger"},{q="What happened to the original Knights of the Silver Hand?",a="The order suffered catastrophic losses during the Third War when the plague ravished Lordaeron. The brotherhood fragmented amid internal betrayal and political strife, though surviving members and new initiates in Stormwind and Ironforge continue to carry the title and ideals forward.",al={"what happened to silver hand","is silver hand gone","fall of the silver hand"},it="history"},{q="Can members of the Horde become paladins?",a="No. The paladin traditions of Azeroth are deeply rooted in the church and knightly orders of the Alliance. The races of the Horde turn to shamans, witch doctors, and warriors rather than the knightly path of the Holy Light.",al={"horde paladins","can orcs be paladins","why no horde paladin"},it="side"},{q="What is a paladin?",a="Paladins are holy knights, protectors of the weak, and dispensers of justice. While a priest serves as a voice of the Holy Light preaching to the faithful, a paladin is the hand of the Light, enforcing divine law in martial combat. Clad in heavy plate armor and brandishing shields or crushing two-handed hammers, paladins take to the front lines to defend their companions, heal the wounded, and purge evil from the world.",al={"paladin lore","who are paladins","what are paladins"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does a paladin's power come from?",a="A paladin's power flows from the Holy Light, channeled through absolute conviction and unwavering faith in the righteous cause of protecting life and order. By dedicating themselves to divine doctrine and purity of purpose, paladins become living conduits of this energy. The Light rejuvenates them, radiates outward to empower nearby allies, shields them from harm, and immolates profane beings such as the undead and demons.",al={"paladin source of power","how do paladins get magic","paladin holy light"},it="power",add=true},{q="What does it mean for an adventurer to become a paladin?",a="Taking the vows of a paladin means dedicating your sword, your armor, and your life to safeguarding the innocent and confronting the forces of shadow. You step onto the battlefield not merely as a soldier, but as a bastion of order and moral fortitude. Whether healing fallen comrades, holding back ferocious monsters behind heavy plate, or striking down the corrupt with holy wrath, you serve the greater good.",al={"becoming a paladin","calling of the paladin","what is a paladins role"},it="for_you",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="Every novice paladin walks in the footsteps of Uther the Lightbringer and Alonsus Faol, binding martial readiness to an oath of selfless duty.",["class:Priest"]="Priests share a deep spiritual kinship with paladins, though they shepherd the flock with sermons and faith while paladins protect it with steel.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarves from Ironforge recently embraced the tenets of the Silver Hand, finding that the discipline of the Light aligns naturally with their unyielding nature.",["race:Human"]="For humans, paladins represent the enduring soul of the Alliance, carrying on the sacred crusade forged during the horrors of the Second War.",["faction:Alliance"]="Paladins stand as the moral anchor of the Alliance, rallying troops with sacred auras and inspiring hope across the battlefield."},kw={"alonsus faol","auras","blessings","cleric","crusader","holy light","paladin","plate","silver hand","uther"},rel={"topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","topic:light","topic:priest","topic:warrior","topic:alliance","topic:second-war","zone:stormwind","zone:ironforge"},src={"Paladin"}}
E["topic:pirate"] = {t="topic",n="Pirate",z="general",s="Pirates and buccaneers are lawless seafaring plunderers operating across the Great Sea and coastal waters of Azeroth. Preying on Alliance, Horde, and neutral merchant vessels alike, they band together under loose flags to seize wealth, capture cargo, and control coastal coves. Their violent presence drives constant naval skirmishes from the waters of Kalimdor to the shores of the Eastern Kingdoms.",h="Pirates and buccaneers are lawless seafaring plunderers operating across the Great Sea and coastal waters of...",sec={{t="Lords of the High Seas",b="Pirates—often called buccaneers, corsairs, or freebooters—are maritime raiders of nearly every race who make their living seizing ships and raiding coastal settlements. Their operations stretch far beyond simple robbery; crews regularly engage in smuggling, kidnappings, and mercenary contracts for whoever has coin. With the chaos left behind by the Third War and the collapse of northern kingdoms, naval enforcement has fractured, giving pirate factions the freedom to carve out fortified footholds on neglected islands and coastal coves.",sp=0},{t="A Ruthless Life",b="Despite common tales painting pirate life as one of romantic adventure, life aboard a buccaneer vessel is brutal and short. Crews endure squalid, rat-infested holds, harsh discipline, and the ever-present threat of scurvy, fought off only with supplies of limes. Betrayal is an everyday hazard, and captains maintain their command solely through strength and fear of punishment, which ranges from immediate execution to being marooned or fed to sharks. When battles end, buccaneers celebrate with roaring bouts of dice, song, and heavy rum.",sp=0},{t="Factions and Havens",b="While independent crews roam every sea lane, major organizations wield enough ships to contest sovereign navies. The Southsea Freebooters harass shipping throughout the waters surrounding Kalimdor, while the notorious Bloodsail Buccaneers menace the southern seas and wage open war against the Steamwheedle Cartel in Booty Bay.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the difference between a pirate, buccaneer, and freebooter?",a="The titles are largely used interchangeably along the sea lanes of Azeroth. While some groups prefer particular banners—such as the Southsea Freebooters or the Bloodsail Buccaneers—they all engage in maritime raiding, robbery, smuggling, and coastal plundering.",al={"buccaneer vs pirate","freebooter difference","pirate titles"},it="identity"},{q="Where are the main pirate bases located?",a="Pirate crews congregate in lawless anchorages and hidden harbors across the world. Major havens include Plunder Isle and coastal camps scattered throughout the shores of Tanaris, the Barrens, and Stranglethorn Vale.",al={"pirate towns","where do pirates live","pirate ports"},it="place"},{q="Who do pirates attack?",a="Most pirate crews are indiscriminate predators. They hunt merchant vessels, civilian transports, and military supply craft belonging to the Alliance, the Horde, and the neutral Steamwheedle Cartel alike. Any ship laden with gold, timber, or trade goods is a potential target.",al={"who do pirates target","are pirates alliance or horde"},it="target"},{q="How do pirate crews recruit new members?",a="Pirates expand their numbers by hiring desperate cutthroats and mercenaries in port towns, but they also rely heavily on press-ganging. Captured sailors and travelers are frequently given the choice between swearing fealty to the crew or being cast into the sea.",al={"joining pirates","how do they get crew"}},{q="How does Kul Tiras deal with pirates?",a="Kul Tiran law treats piracy as one of the most vile offenses a sailor can commit. Those captured by Kul Tiran patrols face immediate death by public execution, though rare crews have managed to secure royal pardons by undertaking perilous, high-stakes tasks for the admiralty.",al={"kul tiras piracy","kul tiran pirate law"}},{q="Are there non-human pirates?",a="Yes. While human crews are common, buccaneer crews accept anyone willing to raid and follow orders. Crews include orcs, goblins, trolls, dwarves, and even tauren who were recruited or impressed into service along the coasts of Kalimdor.",al={"tauren pirates","orc pirates","goblin pirates"},it="people"},{q="What is a pirate?",a="Pirates are cutthroat seafaring raiders who plunder ships along the high seas and coastlines of Azeroth. Rather than flying national banners, they operate independently for personal gain through looting, smuggling, kidnapping, and mercenary work. Crews consist of ruthless renegades from across the mortal races, feared by merchants and hunted as criminals by both the Alliance and Horde navies.",al={"what is a pirate","who are pirates","define pirate"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are pirates hostile to travelers?",a="Pirates view anyone crossing their territory or sea lanes as fresh prey. They take what they want through bloodshed and intimidation, caring little for the lives or allegiances of those they ambush. Any passing adventurer or merchant vessel represents plunder, hostage ransoms, or cargo to strip and sell.",al={"why do pirates attack","why are pirates hostile","are pirates dangerous"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Where do pirates come from?",a="Piracy has troubled the Great Sea for generations, thriving wherever kingdoms falter or fail to patrol the waters. In the aftermath of the Second and Third Wars, the destruction of coastal kingdoms and the breakdown of naval enforcement allowed lawless outcasts, deserters, and opportunists to seize abandoned outposts and terrorize maritime trade routes.",al={"pirate history","origin of pirates","where did pirates come from"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="The cutthroat tactics, blinding powder, and swift blade work of pirate crews closely mirror the practical, lethal fighting styles favored by roguish mercenaries.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance transport lines between Stormwind, Ironforge, and Theramore are under constant threat from buccaneers who prowl coastal trade routes.",["faction:Horde"]="The Southsea Freebooters maintain aggressive encampments along the coast of Durotar and the Barrens, raiding Horde caravans and landing parties.",["race:Tauren"]="Though plains dwellers by tradition, some tauren traveling Kalimdor's shores have ended up aboard pirate vessels, either impressed into crews or seeking fortune."},kw={"bloodsail","booty bay","buccaneer","corsair","freebooter","great sea","pirate","plunder","southsea"},rel={"subzone:booty-bay","subzone:baradin-bay","quest:887","topic:kul-tiras","topic:goblin","topic:alliance","topic:horde","topic:tauren"},src={"Pirate"}}
E["topic:plague-of-undeath"] = {t="topic",n="Plague of Undeath",z="general",s="The Plague of Undeath is a supernatural contagion concocted by the Lich King under the direction of the Burning Legion to scourge northern Eastern Kingdoms. Transmitted through infected grain, airborne cauldrons, and tainted waterways, the sickness rapidly kills living mortals and raises them as mind-bound thralls of the Scourge.",h="The Plague of Undeath is a supernatural contagion concocted by the Lich King under the direction of the...",sec={{t="Origins in the Ice",b="The plague was devised by the fallen shaman Ner'zhul after he was transformed into the Lich King by Kil'jaeden. From his icy seat atop the Frozen Throne in Northrend, Ner'zhul perfected the sickness as an unholy vanguard for the Burning Legion's invasion. Testing his power on isolated human villages across Northrend, he discovered that he could snuff out life and psychically command the corpses that arose shortly after. Ner'zhul's telepathic summon reached across the Great Sea to the Kirin Tor mage Kel'Thuzad, who abandoned Dalaran to serve the entity. Kel'Thuzad organized the secretive Cult of the Damned, whose mortal infiltrators laid the groundwork to poison Lordaeron from within.",sp=0},{t="The Grain of Andorhal",b="Kel'Thuzad and the dreadlord Mal'Ganis chose Lordaeron's agricultural heartland to distribute the contagion. The cult secured distribution centers around Andorhal, packaging the plague into crates of grain destined for population centers across the north, including Brill and Stratholme. Unsuspecting villagers baked bread with the contaminated shipments, falling sick with burning joints, oozing sores, and milky eyes. Within three days, entire communities perished, only to immediately rise as flesh-eating zombies bound to the Lich King's psychic call. Prince Arthas Menethil raced to intercept the shipments, culminating in the horrific culling of Stratholme's citizens before the infected bread could transform them into Mal'Ganis's army.",sp=1},{t="A Land Corrupted",b="The blight extends far beyond humanity. While non-human fauna generally suffer sickening fevers or rot rather than rising as undead thralls, the land itself bears the brunt of demonic and necromantic decay. Across the Plaguelands, massive plague cauldrons pump alchemical and magical vapors directly into the atmosphere, corrupting the surrounding land, where carrion grubs and colossal spore-choked mushrooms thrive. The Argent Dawn actively battles this airborne corruption by sabotaging these cauldrons, reversing their catalytic mixtures to burn the Scourge rather than feed it. Meanwhile, in the Undercity, the Royal Apothecary Society experiments tirelessly to refine a New Plague designed to slay both living foes and the undead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="How was the plague originally spread across Lordaeron?",a="Kel'Thuzad's Cult of the Damned infiltrated the distribution hub of Andorhal. They loaded grain supplies with the plague and shipped crates to towns throughout the kingdom, such as Stratholme and Brill. When the local populace consumed the contaminated flour, they succumbed to the sickness and rose as undead.",al={"how did plague spread","infected grain","plagued grain distribution","grain of andorhal"},it="history"},{q="What happens to someone infected by the plague?",a="Victims rapidly experience weakness, nausea, joint aches, and fever. Skin breaks out into irritated boils and leaking yellow-green lesions, while eyes turn milky. Severe hemorrhaging from the ears and eyes quickly follows. Most mortals die within three days, only to rise soon after as reanimated servants awaiting the commands of the Lich King.",al={"plague symptoms","what does the plague do","how fast does plague kill"},it="threat"},{q="Why does the plague affect humans differently than other races?",a="The Lich King deliberately engineered the plague to target humanity in order to obliterate the Kingdom of Lordaeron. Non-human wildlife and races exposed to the plague often suffer horrific rot, delirium, and fever, but rarely reanimate automatically as Scourge thralls without additional direct necromantic spells.",al={"why humans","does plague affect elves","are orcs immune to plague"},it="target"},{q="Can the Plague of Undeath be cured?",a="No established cure is known in the current era. Natural medicines cannot reverse the plague; once a victim has died and risen, the transformation cannot be undone.",al={"is plague curable","cure for the plague","healing the plague"},it="power"},{q="What are the giant cauldrons in the Plaguelands for?",a="The Scourge erected massive cauldrons across the former farmlands to vent vaporized plague directly into the air. This airborne delivery corrupts the soil, clouds the sky, and sickens passing beasts. The Argent Dawn works tirelessly to counteract these cauldrons by turning their reagents into holy or toxic counters against the Scourge.",al={"plague cauldrons","what are cauldrons doing","why are cauldrons in fields"},it="purpose"},{q="What is the Forsaken's New Plague?",a="Developed in the bowels of the Undercity by the Royal Apothecary Society, the New Plague is a bespoke chemical and magical pathogen. Unlike the Scourge's version, the Forsaken design their formula specifically to kill both living mortals and the undead thralls of the Lich King.",al={"forsaken plague","royal apothecary society plague","new plague undercity"}},{q="What is the Plague of Undeath?",a="It is a potent magical disease conjured by Ner'zhul, the Lich King, under the direction of the demon lord Kil'jaeden. Engineered to drain life and spread undeath, the affliction was designed as a weapon to destroy mortal civilizations and reanimate their dead as mindless thralls under the Lich King's psychic dominion.",al={"what is the plague","plague of undeath explained","scourge plague"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why is it called the Plague of Undeath?",a="It earned the name because death is not its conclusion. While natural diseases merely kill their hosts, this affliction reanimates those who succumb to it. Victims perish rapidly from agonizing symptoms only to rise again as mindless, walking undead bound to the will of the Lich King.",al={"why plague of undeath","origin of plague name","why is it called undeath"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="The Plague of Undeath wiped out the Kingdom of Lordaeron and tore families apart, standing as the direct cause of humanity's greatest historical catastrophe.",["race:Undead"]="Many Forsaken carry the physical scars of the plague, having lived through its agony and died only to break free from the Lich King's psychic grip years later.",["class:Paladin"]="The Order of the Silver Hand shattered trying to contain the contagion, leaving paladins to rely solely on the Holy Light to purge its foul residue.",["class:Priest"]="Priests face the ultimate corruption of body and spirit in the plague, driving their faith to burn away necrotic sickness before it festers.",["faction:Alliance"]="The loss of northern kingdoms to the plague remains an unhealed wound for the Alliance, driving their ongoing crusade to cleanse the Plaguelands.",["faction:Horde"]="While orcs and tauren viewed the northern sickness as a human curse, the Royal Apothecary Society's research has brought the horror of plague experimentation directly into the Horde."},kw={"andorhal","blight","cauldrons","grain","kel'thuzad","mal'ganis","ner'zhul","plague","scourge","stratholme","undeath"},rel={"topic:scourge","topic:lich-king","topic:cult-of-the-damned","topic:arthas-menethil","subzone:andorhal","subzone:stratholme","subzone:cauldron","topic:royal-apothecary-society"},src={"Plague of Undeath"}}
E["topic:priest"] = {t="topic",n="Priest",z="general",s="Priests are spiritual guides and conduits of immense unseen power who shape belief, mend flesh, and guard souls. Channeling the radiant energies of faith or plumbing the terrifying depths of the Void, they serve as the vital heart of any war party. Across Azeroth, their traditions range from the Church of the Holy Light to the Moon Goddess's Sisterhood and shadowy Forsaken cults.",h="Priests are spiritual guides and conduits of immense unseen power who shape belief, mend flesh, and guard...",sec={{t="Vessels of Faith and Devotion",b="Priests guide the spiritual destiny of their communities. While physical combatants test their mettle with steel, priests draw power directly from conviction. Through divine connection, they soothe grievous wounds, raise fallen companions from death's threshold, and weave mystic barriers to repel incoming harm. Their presence on the field often marks the difference between survival and annihilation.",sp=0},{t="Light and Shadow",b="The priesthood is not solely an instrument of benevolence. Just as life holds both illumination and darkness, the priest walks a perilous line between divine restoration and mental devastation. Those who master the holy arts call down searing righteous vengeance to smite enemies, while practitioners of Shadow magic delve into the Void to dominate minds, twist willpower, and inflict agonizing pain from afar.",sp=0},{t="A Tapestry of Faiths",b="No single faith defines Azeroth's priests. In human and dwarven lands, the Church of the Holy Light codifies a philosophy of virtue, having adapted its fragile Clerics of Northshire into a hardened order after the First War. In Kalimdor, the night elven Sisterhood of Elune honors the Mother Moon with starlight and grace, while troll witch doctors beseech the primal Loa. Among the newly risen Forsaken, many have turned from former dogmas toward the Cult of the Forgotten Shadow, preaching that darkness and light must exist in delicate equilibrium.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the difference between a priest and a paladin?",a="During the First War, human clerics suffered immense slaughter on the battlefield because they lacked martial defense. In response, Archbishop Alonsus Faol and the Church formed the Knights of the Silver Hand, training armored holy warriors called paladins. Priests remain robed casters devoted purely to spiritual ministry, prayer, and deeper mastery of the mind and shadow.",al={"priest vs paladin","why play priest not paladin","paladin or priest lore"}},{q="Can priests use Shadow magic without losing their faith?",a="Yes. Spiritual balance dictates that light cannot exist without darkness. Many priests study shadow to comprehend the perils threatening their flock, while others, like the Forsaken Cult of the Forgotten Shadow, actively embrace dark energies as a necessity of existence, arguing that one must understand both forces to truly master the self.",al={"why do priests use shadow","shadow priest lore","shadow and holy balance"}},{q="Do all playable races worship the same thing as priests?",a="No. Humans and dwarves primarily follow the Church of the Holy Light, a virtue-based philosophy rather than worship of a deity. Night elves revere Elune through her Sisterhood, trolls beseech spirits and Loa, and undead Forsaken practitioners follow the Cult of the Forgotten Shadow or grapple with their fractured connection to their former human faith.",al={"priest religions","what gods do priests worship","elune vs holy light priest"}},{q="Who leads the human priesthood in Stormwind?",a="The Cathedral of Light in Stormwind serves as the primary seat of the human church following the fall of Lordaeron. The clergy there, along with surviving clerics from Northshire, oversee humanity's spiritual recovery and guide the souls of the Alliance in these unsettled times.",al={"who leads church of holy light","stormwind cathedral leader","priest leader stormwind"},it="leader"},{q="Why do the Forsaken still have priests?",a="Many undead retained memories and techniques from their living devotions in Lordaeron. After facing the abandonment of death, some embraced the Cult of the Forgotten Shadow, wielding dark energies while studying the balance between darkness and light. Others still stubbornly call upon the Holy Light, though doing so brings agonizing physical torment to their decaying flesh.",al={"undead priests lore","can undead use the light","forsaken shadow priests"}},{q="What is the Sisterhood of Elune?",a="The Sisterhood of Elune is an ancient order of night elf priestesses established long before the War of the Ancients. Led by High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind, they revere the moon goddess Elune, serving as healers, spiritual guides, and fiercely capable leaders within night elf society.",al={"night elf priests","priestesses of the moon","sisterhood of elune"},it="history"},{q="What is a Priest in the lore?",a="Priests are masters of the spiritual and guiding voices for their people. Leaving behind the safety of shrines and temples, they walk the front lines to mend grievous injuries, soothe broken spirits, and shield allies from harm. Unlike warriors or mages who rely purely on physical might or arcane formulas, priests channel their power through deep faith and an understanding of the mortal mind. They guide beliefs, bolster courage, and when necessary, turn the divine forces of creation and devastation against their foes.",al={"what is a priest","priest lore","priest class lore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does a Priest's power come from?",a="A priest's magic flows directly from faith and spiritual devotion. For humans, dwarves, and high elves, this typically means drawing upon the Holy Light—a benevolent, universal force of virtue and creation. Night elf priestesses instead channel the celestial power of their moon goddess, Elune, while troll witch doctors and shadow priests look to the loas or the darker forces of Shadow and Void. Ultimately, their ability to mend flesh or unravel minds depends entirely on their will and the strength of their convictions.",al={"where do priests get their power","priest power source","how do priests cast spells"},it="power",add=true},{q="What does it mean for a new adventurer to become a Priest?",a="Taking up the mantle of a priest means answering a call to spiritual leadership and sacrifice. While you wear simple cloth armor and lack the physical resilience of frontline soldiers, you hold absolute sway over life, death, and the minds of those around you. You are counted upon to preserve your companions through devastating battles, restore the fallen, or balance the holy energies of life against the terrifying ruin of shadow magic to break the enemy's resolve.",al={"becoming a priest","why become a priest","role of a priest adventurer"},it="for_you",add=true}},ang={["class:Priest"]="As a priest, you walk the line between pure radiance and mental devastation, commanding both the restorative mercies of prayer and the punishing horrors of the Void.",["race:Human"]="Human priests inherit the resilient tradition of the Northshire Clerics, serving as the spiritual bedrock of the Alliance through the Church of the Holy Light.",["race:NightElf"]="Night elf priestesses honor the Sisterhood of Elune, channeling starlight and lunar grace under the guidance of High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken priests redefine their shattered faith through the Cult of the Forgotten Shadow, embracing the balance of dark energies when former vows brought only ruin.",["class:Paladin"]="Paladins share holy philosophies with the priesthood, having evolved from the Church's need to protect robed clerics after the bloody lessons of the First War."},kw={"cathedral","cleric","cult","elune","healing","holy light","priest","shadow","sisterhood","void"},rel={"topic:church-of-the-holy-light","topic:light","topic:elune","topic:knights-of-the-silver-hand","npc:tyrande-whisperwind","topic:forsaken","topic:paladin","quest:5650"},src={"Priest"}}
E["topic:quilboar"] = {t="topic",n="Quilboar",z="general",s="Quilboar are aggressive, boar-like humanoids native to central Kalimdor, renowned for their sharp spinal quills and relentless territorial savagery. Claiming descent from the fallen demigod Agamaggan, they dwell within dense mazes of razor-sharp brambles across Durotar, Mulgore, and the Barrens. Their fierce struggle over water, soil, and sacred ground makes them a constant threat to travelers and Horde settlements.",h="Quilboar are aggressive, boar-like humanoids native to central Kalimdor, renowned for their sharp spinal...",sec={{t="Children of the Great Boar",b="Quilboar revere Agamaggan, the colossal boar Ancient who perished battling the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients ten thousand years ago. According to their sacred traditions, the quilboar are his mortal offspring. Where Agamaggan bled and died across central Kalimdor, enormous thorny vines erupted from the scorched earth, forming sprawling root-mazes that the quilboar treat as holy ground. His crystallized blood endures across the Barrens as crimson Blood Shards, which their geomancers dig up and revere as powerful talismans.",sp=0},{t="Bramble Harrows and Geomancy",b="Rather than building traditional towns of stone or timber, quilboar cultivate colossal briars that choke surrounding vegetation and render nearby ground barren. Tribes are typically guided by powerful geomancers—practitioners of an earthbound, crude shamanism who consult stones and crystals to direct the clan. These mystics call upon Agamaggan's spirit to summon spectral boars into combat. Because the arid central plains offer few oases, quilboar view water as the ultimate treasure; they stage lavish feasts when rare rains fall and defend water holes with vicious tenacity.",sp=0},{t="The War for the Barrens",b="For generations, quilboar clashed bitterly with the nomadic tauren and roaming centaur packs. When Thrall led the Orcish Horde into Kalimdor, orcs rapidly colonized Durotar and established trade routes through the Barrens, driving displaced tribes into open revolt. Quilboar war parties ambush supply wagons, are suspected of frightening tallstriders into wagon trains, and stage raids on outposts like Crossroads and Camp Taurajo. Two principal tribes dominate the open plains: the Razormane and the Bristleback, both engaged in simultaneous feuds against the Horde and each other.",sp=0},{t="The Shadow over Razorfen",b="The spiritual heart of quilboar civilization lies at the southern edge of the Barrens within Razorfen Kraul and the adjacent Razorfen Downs, tangled mazes formed directly from Agamaggan's gargantuan remains. An ambitious crone named Charlga Razorflank seized dominion over the Kraul, orchestrating aggressive assaults against rival clans and Horde territories. Worse still, the traditional quilboar capital of Razorfen Downs has fallen under the sway of the Scourge. In league with the lich Amnennar the Coldbringer, corrupted Death's Head quilboar have succumbed to undeath, turning the ancestral tomb into a plague-ridden redoubt.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why do quilboar hate the Horde so much?",a="Quilboar occupied the fertile oases and thorned ravines of Durotar and the Barrens long before the Horde arrived. When Thrall's people landed and claimed land to build Orgrimmar, they displaced native quilboar dens. Compounding this, the Horde allied with the tauren, whom the quilboar had fought for generations. Now, the tribes view every caravan, outpost, and water well as stolen ancestral ground.",al={"why do quilboar attack orcs","quilboar horde conflict","why are quilboar hostile"},it="hostile"},{q="What is the relationship between quilboar and Agamaggan?",a="Agamaggan is a colossal boar demigod who fought the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients ten thousand years ago. Quilboar claim to be his mortal descendants. The massive briars they inhabit sprouted where his blood soaked the earth, and the sacred tunnels of Razorfen Downs are said to be carved from his very remains.",al={"agamaggan quilboar god","who is agamaggan","quilboar demigod"},it="ties"},{q="What are the red stones quilboar carry?",a="Those crimson crystals are Blood Shards, formed from the petrified blood of Agamaggan. Quilboar dig them up throughout the Barrens, believing they contain the vitality and strength of their fallen ancestor. Geomancers use these stones as catalysts in earth rituals and combat enchantments.",al={"blood shards","red rocks quilboar","blood of agamaggan"},it="item"},{q="What kind of magic do quilboar use?",a="Quilboar practice geomancy, an elemental craft akin to crude shamanism focused on stone, earth, and thorns. They revere the reading of stones and gems above all other mystical arts. Tribal geomancers call upon earth spirits, summon brambles to entangle foes, and conjure boar spirits in Agamaggan's image.",al={"quilboar magic","quilboar geomancers","what is geomancy"},it="power"},{q="Why are quilboar so protective of water?",a="The Barrens is an arid wasteland where natural springs and oases are exceedingly scarce. In quilboar culture, water represents survival, wealth, and spiritual blessing. They celebrate even the lightest desert rains with tribal feasts and fiercely guard springs against encroaching caravans and herds.",al={"quilboar water","why quilboar hoard water","quilboar oases"},it="motive"},{q="What is the difference between Razorfen Kraul and Razorfen Downs?",a="Both are massive briar mazes sprouting from Agamaggan's carcass in southern Kalimdor. Razorfen Kraul serves as the stronghold of the crone Charlga Razorflank, who rallies living quilboar against all outsiders. Razorfen Downs was the race's ancestral capital, but it has fallen into the hands of the Scourge lich Amnennar the Coldbringer, who raises deceased quilboar as undead minions.",al={"kraul vs downs","razorfen dungeons","where do quilboar live"},sp=1,it="place"},{q="What are quilboar?",a="Quilboar are a primitive, aggressive race of boar-like humanoids native to Kalimdor. Short, hunched, and muscular, their most striking feature is the array of razor-sharp spines that protrudes from their backs and heads. Believed to be the mortal descendants of the demigod Agamaggan, they revere massive bramble thorns and live in fiercely protective tribal societies governed largely by powerful geomancer shamans.",al={"what is quilboar","who are quilboars","quilboar race"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are quilboar found in the Barrens and Durotar?",a="Quilboar flock to areas dominated by colossal, thorn-covered roots, believing them to be sacred ground grown from the blood of their fallen god, Agamaggan. Central Kalimdor—spanning the Barrens, Durotar, and Mulgore—contains the heart of these brambles, culminating in the massive colonies of Razorfen Kraul and Razorfen Downs. Their shaman intentionally cultivate and spread these choking thorns wherever they settle.",al={"why are quilboar here","quilboar locations","why do quilboar live in the barrens"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="Who leads the quilboar?",a="Quilboar are divided into distinct tribes like the Bristleback, Razormane, and Death's Head, each led by its own chiefs and shamans. However, the crone Charlga Razorflank holds dominion over Razorfen Kraul and commands her followers. Under her tyrannical rule, quilboar stage aggressive strikes against rival tribes, the Horde, and travelers throughout the Barrens.",al={"quilboar leader","who is in charge of quilboar","quilboar crone"},it="leader",add=true},{q="Who are the quilboar allied with?",a="Most quilboar tribes are fiercely independent and despise outsiders, fighting anyone who enters their territory. However, Charlga Razorflank and her Death's Head tribe forged a dark alliance with the Scourge. This pact allowed the lich Amnennar the Coldbringer into Razorfen Downs, converting the traditional capital of the quilboar into an undead stronghold bolstered by reanimated boar-folk.",al={"quilboar allies","do quilboar have allies","who do quilboar work for"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Where did the quilboar come from?",a="Quilboar believe themselves to be the mortal offspring of Agamaggan, the colossal boar demigod who fought the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients. They were already abundant before the Great Sundering. When Agamaggan fell, massive thorn-ridden vines sprouted where his blood seeped into the earth, and the quilboar came to occupy these sacred brambles.",al={"quilboar origin","quilboar history","how were quilboar created"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why are they called quilboar?",a="Their name comes from their distinct appearance: boar-like humanoids bristling with sharp, defensive quills along their spines and skulls. These spines are central to their physiology and identity, used both defensively and launched in battle, with tribal names like Bristleback and Razormane reflecting their formidable quilled backs.",al={"quilboar name meaning","why the name quilboar","why are they called quillboar"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Your ancestors fought the quilboar for watering holes and grazing land long before the orcs sailed west. Their expansionist brambles choke the natural earth that the Earth Mother sustains.",["race:Orc"]="Securing Durotar and the Barrens requires breaking the quilboar war bands, whose raids continually harass Horde trade wagons and outlying farmsteads.",["class:Shaman"]="Quilboar geomancy represents an earthy, ancient offshoot of shamanic tradition, though twisted toward territorial brutality and the conjuration of savage boar spirits.",["faction:Horde"]="Quilboar pose an immediate logistical hazard across central Kalimdor, ambushing vital caravans traveling between Orgrimmar, the Crossroads, and Thunder Bluff."},kw={"agamaggan","barrens","blood shards","brambles","bristleback","charlga razorflank","geomancer","quilboar","quillboar","razorfen","razormane"},rel={"topic:agamaggan","zone:barrens","zone:razorfenkraul","subzone:razorfen-downs-classic","npc:charlga-razorflank","topic:tauren","quest:5052","quest:899"},src={"Quilboar"}}
E["topic:ravenholdt"] = {t="topic",n="Ravenholdt",z="general",s="Ravenholdt, also known as the Assassin's League, is an elite guild of rogues, thieves, and killers based out of a secluded mountain manor above the Hillsbrad Foothills. Led by Lord Jorach Ravenholdt, the league maintains strict neutrality from faction politics while waging an unending shadow war against their bitter rivals, the Syndicate. They test and recruit only the most cunning operatives across Azeroth, valuing peerless discipline, stealth, and lethal efficiency above all else.",h="Ravenholdt, also known as the Assassin's League, is an elite guild of rogues, thieves, and killers based out...",sec={{t="The Hidden League",b="Tucked away in the craggy mountains connecting Hillsbrad to Alterac, Ravenholdt Manor serves as the nerve center for Azeroth's premier brotherhood of assassins. Led by Lord Jorach Ravenholdt, the organization operates far beyond the jurisdiction of kings and warchiefs. Within its walls, master specialists hone every lethal art: Master Kang instructs disciples in close combat and assassination, the Thunderwood brothers brew rare and deadly poisons, and gnome craftsmen engineer intricate locks to test fledglings. To non-rogues, entering the estate is an overt flirtation with death, where an unwary step easily ends with a poisoned dagger in the ribs.",sp=0},{t="Shadow War with the Syndicate",b="For years, Ravenholdt has been locked in an escalating feud with the Syndicate, an aristocratic criminal cartel infesting Alterac and Arathi. While the League regards the Syndicate as clumsy, arrogant amateurs, their sheer numbers make them impossible to eradicate through simple slaughter. Instead, Ravenholdt employs psychological and structural disruption: by tasking contracted rogues with pickpocketing the signet emblems worn by Syndicate ringleaders, the League relies on the Syndicate's own paranoia and cutthroat hierarchy to execute disgraced members internally.",sp=0},{t="An Independent Guild of Shadows",b="Though Ravenholdt cooperates on occasion with other intelligence apparatuses like Stormwind's SI:7, they answer to no crown. Master Mathias Shaw occasionally finds Ravenholdt an ally in checking the Syndicate, yet Jorach Ravenholdt fiercely guards his autonomy. When SI:7 suffered heavy losses investigating the fallen ranger Nathanos Marris in the Plaguelands, Ravenholdt flatly refused Shaw's plea to intervene. The League picks its own battles, dedicating their resources to acquiring ancient relics, perfecting deadly arts, and keeping their blade at the throat of anyone foolish enough to cross them.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Ravenholdt?",a="Ravenholdt is an ancient, clandestine guild of thieves, assassins, and shadow-dancers governed by Lord Jorach Ravenholdt. Known informally as the Assassin's League, they operate from a secret estate nestled deep in the mountains above Hillsbrad Foothills, training promising rogues and offering deadly contracts.",al={"who are ravenholdt","what is the assassin's league","ravenholdt faction"},it="identity"},{q="Why are they fighting the Syndicate?",a="The Syndicate and Ravenholdt vie for dominance across the ruins of Alterac and the surrounding highlands. The League considers the Syndicate undisciplined, dishonorable brigands whose messy criminal operations threaten the subtle balance of power in the region. Rather than wipe them out through sheer attrition, Ravenholdt methodically undermines their leadership by stealing their emblems of rank.",al={"why syndicate vs ravenholdt","ravenholdt syndicate war","why do they hate the syndicate"},it="conflict"},{q="Can non-rogues visit Ravenholdt Manor?",a="Non-rogues can physically reach the manor, but the residents do not welcome outsiders. Guards and assassins make thinly veiled threats about poisoned drinks and daggers in the back to anyone who wanders in without shadowcraft credentials or business with Lord Ravenholdt.",al={"can other classes go to ravenholdt","warrior at ravenholdt manor","is ravenholdt rogues only"},gp=true},{q="Who is Lord Jorach Ravenholdt?",a="Lord Jorach Ravenholdt is the elusive master of the Assassin's League. Operating from his mountain estate, he maintains an extensive network of rogue trainers and informants across the continents, dispatching trusted disciples to recover powerful artifacts and neutralize rival threats.",al={"who leads ravenholdt","jorach ravenholdt","ravenholdt leader"},it="leader"},{q="What is the Rite of Cunning?",a="It is an introductory test for prospective disciples seeking Ravenholdt Manor. An initiate must use their ability to detect traps while passing through the mountain tunnel leading to the grounds; the danger is discovered during the rite.",al={"rite of cunning","ravenholdt rogue trial","ravenholdt tunnel test"}},{q="How does Ravenholdt view the Alliance and the Horde?",a="Ravenholdt is entirely neutral regarding factional politics, accepting skilled cutthroats regardless of whether they hail from Orgrimmar or Ironforge. While they maintain cordial lines of communication with organizations like Stormwind's SI:7, they refuse to act as pawns for any kingdom's war effort.",al={"is ravenholdt alliance or horde","ravenholdt alignment","who does ravenholdt work for"},it="side"},{q="Why is Ravenholdt Manor hidden in the Alterac Mountains?",a="Ravenholdt operates from a secluded manor concealed high in the mountains above Hillsbrad Foothills to guard its secrets and protect its members from outside scrutiny. Tucked away behind an easily overlooked mountain path, the estate provides an ideal stronghold for an elite assassin's guild, offering security against unwanted intrusions and placing them directly adjacent to the Syndicate forces they actively undermine across the region.",al={"why are they in alterac","ravenholdt manor location why","why hide in hillsbrad mountains"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="Who are Ravenholdt allied with?",a="Ravenholdt operates independently as an autonomous league of assassins and thieves, strictly bound to its own interests and code. However, they maintain selective professional contacts with other clandestine circles. They have accepted cooperative aid from Stormwind's intelligence service, SI:7, specifically to root out the Syndicate affliction in the northern kingdoms, though they remain purely mercenary and will refuse requests that do not serve their guild's priorities.",al={"who does ravenholdt work for","ravenholdt allies","ravenholdt connections"},it="allies",add=true},{q="What is the history of Ravenholdt?",a="Formed as the Assassin's League under the leadership of Lord Jorach Ravenholdt, the guild has long gathered the world's finest practitioners of stealth, poison, and theft. Years ago, their treasured artifact, the Horns of Nez'ra, was stolen by the Blackrock clan, sparking a relentless hunt across continents. In recent times, their primary focus has been a bitter, protracted shadow war against the noble-led Syndicate across Alterac and Arathi.",al={"history of ravenholdt","where did ravenholdt come from","origins of ravenholdt"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="Ravenholdt is your global fraternity and proving ground. The trainers, poison-brewers, and shadowmasters here offer trials, rare weapon schematics, and recognition that cannot be earned anywhere else in Azeroth.",["faction:Alliance"]="While Mathias Shaw and SI:7 occasionally coordinate with Ravenholdt to suppress the Syndicate, Lord Ravenholdt owes no allegiance to Stormwind and will casually rebuff high-stakes Alliance requests.",["faction:Horde"]="Despite its noble human exterior and location in the northern highlands, Ravenholdt judges operatives solely by their prowess, welcoming Horde stalkers who prove their mettle."},kw={"alterac","assassin","fahrad","hillsbrad","jorach","lockpicking","manor","poisons","ravenholdt","syndicate"},rel={"subzone:ravenholdt-manor","npc:lord-jorach-ravenholdt","topic:syndicate","zone:hillsbrad","zone:alterac","quest:8249","npc:mathias-shaw","quest:2300"},src={"Ravenholdt"}}
E["topic:razorfen"] = {t="topic",n="Razorfen",z="general",s="Razorfen is the collective name associated with the predominant quilboar tribe of southern Kalimdor and their sprawling thorn-choked settlements. Carved into massive briars along the southern border of the Barrens and the edge of Thousand Needles, Razorfen encompasses the strongholds of Razorfen Kraul and Razorfen Downs. It serves as both the spiritual heart and the central bastion of quilboar power.",h="Razorfen is the collective name associated with the predominant quilboar tribe of southern Kalimdor and their...",sec={{t="The Thorns of Agamaggan",b="According to legend, the colossal thorned brambles that make up the Razorfen sprouted ten thousand years ago during the War of the Ancients. When the giant boar Ancient, Agamaggan, fell in battle against the Burning Legion, his blood seeped into the earth wherever he bled and died. From that divine sacrifice grew massive vines of petrified bramble and iron-hard thorns, creating a natural fortress where his descendants, the quilboar, have dwelled ever since.",sp=0},{t="The Razorfen Tribe",b="The Razorfen tribe is among the most aggressive and entrenched of all quilboar populations. Rather than scattering into open warrens, they built complex labyrinthine communities directly within the colossal briars. From these fortresses, the tribe fiercely defends their ancestral territory against neighboring tauren tribes, Horde settlements in the Barrens, and any outsiders daring to trespass into their holy grounds.",sp=0},{t="A Divided Citadel",b="The realm of Razorfen is divided into two distinct bastions. Razorfen Kraul sits as the traditional capital and military encampment of the quilboar, guarded by their shamans and huntresses. Further south, bordering Thousand Needles, lies Razorfen Downs, the sacred ancestral city and burial grounds of the quilboar elders, currently shadowed by deeper necromantic corruption that threatens all surrounding lands.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What is Razorfen?",a="Razorfen refers to both the prominent quilboar tribe of the southern Barrens and their fortified settlements woven into massive briar labyrinths: Razorfen Kraul and Razorfen Downs.",al={"what is razorfen","razorfen meaning","razorfen lore"},it="identity"},{q="Where did the giant thorns come from?",a="The thorns are the fossilized blood and remnants of the Wild God Agamaggan, the Great Boar. When he fell fighting the Burning Legion during the ancient War of the Ancients, massive thorned tendrils grew wherever his blood struck the earth.",al={"giant briars barrens","agamaggan thorns","why giant thorns"},it="origin"},{q="What is the difference between Razorfen Kraul and Razorfen Downs?",a="Razorfen Kraul serves as the living city and main stronghold of the Razorfen tribe, filled with warriors and geomancers. Razorfen Downs is the ancestral burial ground and sacred resting place of quilboar elders, located further south toward Thousand Needles.",al={"kraul vs downs","difference between razorfen dungeons"}},{q="Why are the quilboar hostile to everyone?",a="Quilboar view the land of the Barrens and its great briars as their divine birthright granted by Agamaggan. They consider any outside races—especially the expanding Horde—to be thieving interlopers invading their holy ground.",al={"why do quilboar attack","quilboar lore"},it="hostile"},{q="Where is Razorfen located?",a="Razorfen's massive thorn networks lie in the far southern reaches of the Barrens, right along the high cliffs that descend into the salt flats and river canyon of Thousand Needles.",al={"razorfen map location","where is razorfen"},it="place"},{q="What kind of magic do the Razorfen quilboar use?",a="Traditionally, Razorfen quilboar practice earth and nature magic known as geomancy, invoking the power of stone and thorn. However, recent developments within Razorfen Downs show alarming incursions of dark necromantic arts.",al={"quilboar magic","razorfen shamans"},it="power"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Razorfen quilboar have clashed violently with tauren hunting bands and settlements across the Barrens for generations, making their eradication a matter of homeland security.",["faction:Horde"]="Controlling the southern Barrens is critical for Horde logistics, and the aggressive Razorfen raiders consistently harass caravans traveling between Mulgore, Crossroads, and the southern frontier.",["class:Druid"]="The briars of Razorfen sprouted from the sacrifice of the Ancient Agamaggan; druids view the desecration and corruption of such a sacred site with profound concern.",["class:Shaman"]="The Razorfen geomancers manipulate the spirits of earth and stone through crude elemental rites, demanding attention from shamans seeking balance."},kw={"agamaggan","barrens","brambles","downs","kraul","quilboar","razorfen","thorns"},rel={"topic:quilboar","subzone:razorfen-kraul-classic","subzone:razorfen-downs-classic","zone:thousandneedles"},src={"Razorfen"}}
E["topic:refugee"] = {t="topic",n="Refugee",z="general",s="Throughout the violent history of Azeroth, constant warfare has repeatedly driven civilian populations from their homes. From the exodus of Stormwind during the First War to the tragic flight from Lordaeron in the Third War, refugees remain living testaments to the devastating costs of invasion, plague, and betrayal.",h="Throughout the violent history of Azeroth, constant warfare has repeatedly driven civilian populations from...",sec={{t="The Exodus of Stormwind",b="When the Orcish Horde swept through the kingdom of Stormwind during the First War, settlements like Goldshire, Moonbrook, and Grand Hamlet burned. Following the assassination of King Llane Wrynn, Sir Anduin Lothar took command of the kingdom's remnants. He gathered surviving soldiers and desperate civilians, escorting them to the harbor as the city burned. While some citizens hid in the catacombs beneath the Cathedral of Light, Lothar led a massive fleet across the Great Sea to the northern shores of Lordaeron. Arriving at Southshore, the exiled survivors—including the young Prince Varian Wrynn and High Clerist Lady Mara Fordragon—were granted sanctuary by King Terenas Menethil II.",sp=0},{t="Flight from the Scourge",b="A generation after welcoming Stormwind's exiles, the people of Lordaeron faced ruin of their own. During the Third War, the Scourge plague and the legions of the undead systematically dismantled the kingdom. Prince Arthas Menethil slaughtered his own subjects as he swept across the realm, leaving survivors scattered and terrified. Lady Jaina Proudmoore managed to lead an expedition of refugees across the sea to Kalimdor, eventually establishing a settlement at Theramore. However, countless others remained behind in the Eastern Kingdoms, hunted relentlessly by the Scourge, the Forsaken, and monstrous horrors that arose in the ruins.",sp=0},{t="Turned Away at the Gate",b="Those fleeing the devastation of Lordaeron who could not escape across the sea desperately sought shelter wherever walls still stood. Many traveled south to the massive Greymane Wall, begging for entry into the isolationist kingdom of Gilneas. Instead of sanctuary, they found sealed gates and armed indifference. Left destitute on the borders of Silverpine Forest, these haggard survivors huddled in makeshift camps, supported only by selfless caretakers like Dalin Forgewright while facing starvation, undead raiders, and the predatory worgen roaming near Shadowfang Keep.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What happened to the people who fled Stormwind during the First War?",a="Sir Anduin Lothar led thousands of survivors aboard a desperate fleet across the Great Sea to the northern kingdom of Lordaeron. They made landfall at the port village of Southshore and were granted refuge by King Terenas Menethil II until Stormwind was eventually reclaimed and rebuilt.",al={"stormwind refugees first war","where did stormwind flee","exodus of stormwind"},it="history"},{q="Did all of Stormwind's citizens escape the First War?",a="No. Many died in the streets, while others hid in the vast rooms and catacombs beneath the Cathedral of Light. Uncounted survivors wandered the wilderness of Azeroth aimlessly, where orcish hunters ruthlessly tracked and killed them for sport.",al={"stormwind survivors","who stayed in stormwind","cathedral of light refugees"},it="history"},{q="Where did the survivors of Lordaeron go?",a="Many boarded ships under Lady Jaina Proudmoore and sailed west to Kalimdor, eventually settling the island outpost of Theramore. Others scattered across the Eastern Kingdoms, seeking haven in places like Southshore, the Hinterlands, or futilely pleading at the sealed Greymane Wall.",al={"lordaeron refugees","where did lordaeron people go","survivors of the plague"},it="history"},{q="Why didn't Gilneas let the refugees from Lordaeron in?",a="Gilneas chose complete isolationism, closing and barring the massive Greymane Wall to cut itself off from the rest of the world and avoid external wars. When the desperate, sickly victims of the Scourge arrived at the gates, they were firmly turned away and left exposed in Silverpine Forest.",al={"greymane wall refugees","gilneas closed gates","why did greymane lock out survivors"},it="history"},{q="Who helped the refugees stranded outside the Greymane Wall?",a="Dalin Forgewright dedicated his life to caring for the haggard and sickly refugees trapped outside the gates of Gilneas, tending to their ailments and offering what solace he could in the perilous wilderness.",al={"dalin forgewright","who helped silverpine refugees"},it="people"},{q="Who took in the Stormwind exiles when they reached Lordaeron?",a="Marshal Marcus Redpath greeted the exhausted fleets when they landed at Southshore. He immediately dispatched a messenger to Capital City to notify King Terenas Menethil II, who welcomed the southern survivors with open arms.",al={"southshore landing","marcus redpath refugees","who greeted stormwind"},it="allies"},{q="What is a refugee in Azeroth?",a="Refugees are survivors forced from their homes by the unending wars, plagues, and cataclysms that ravage Azeroth. Across the world, whole populations have been displaced when their kingdoms fell—from the citizens of Stormwind driven north across the Great Sea during the First War, to the survivors of Lordaeron fleeing the Scourge in the Third War. Stripped of shelter and safety, they wander in search of sanctuary.",al={"what is a refugee","who are refugees","refugee meaning"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are there so many refugees across Azeroth?",a="You find displaced folk scattered across the world because successive conflicts have shattered entire nations in recent decades. The sacking of Stormwind by the Horde scattered survivors throughout the Eastern Kingdoms, while the Scourge's destruction of Lordaeron sent desperate remnants wandering toward places like the barricaded Greymane Wall or following Jaina Proudmoore overseas to Kalimdor.",al={"why are there refugees","where do refugees come from","refugees reason"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="Almost every human family carries the trauma of displacement, whether fleeing the burning of Stormwind to Southshore a generation ago or escaping the butcher's work of Arthas in Lordaeron.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance of Lordaeron was born directly from the plea of homeless Stormwind refugees, proving that shared survival and sanctuary are the foundations of the modern Alliance.",["class:Priest"]="The Holy Light compels clergy to aid the dispossessed, following the example of High Clerist Lady Mara Fordragon and the caretakers who sheltered the wounded in ruined sanctuaries.",["race:Undead"]="Many of the Forsaken were once the very Lordaeron refugees who failed to outrun the plague and the Scourge, now inhabiting the ruined kingdom they died trying to flee."},kw={"exodus","greymane wall","jaina proudmoore","lordaeron","lothar","refugee","silverpine","southshore","stormwind"},rel={"topic:first-war","topic:third-war","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:lordaeron","subzone:southshore","subzone:greymane-wall","npc:jaina-proudmoore","quest:1476"},src={"Refugee"}}
E["topic:riverpaw-pack"] = {t="topic",n="Riverpaw pack",z="multi",s="The Riverpaw pack is a savage clan of gnolls inhabiting the borderlands of Elwynn Forest and Westfall. Known for their brutal raids and ruthless dominance over local kobolds, they have grown increasingly organized and brazen following the Third War. With Stormwind's military stretched thin, their presence threatens vital roads and settlements throughout the heartland.",h="The Riverpaw pack is a savage clan of gnolls inhabiting the borderlands of Elwynn Forest and Westfall.",sec={{t="A Plague on the Heartland",b="For decades, the Riverpaw clan prowled the fringes of human civilization, but their boldness has surged in recent years. In Elwynn Forest, packs led by the infamous brute Hogger prowl the southern woods around Forest's Edge and roam the shores of Stone Cairn Lake. In Westfall, they roam across the Dust Plains and along the coasts, ambushing solitary travelers and raiding homesteads. Deputy Rainer at Westbrook Garrison and Captain Danuvin at Sentinel Hill rely heavily on mercenary blades to cull their numbers, issuing standing bounties for painted armbands and severed paws.",sp=0},{t="Merciless Taskmasters",b="Beyond petty ambushes, the Riverpaws exhibit an unusual drive for conquest and labor. They drove a clan of kobolds out of the Redridge Mountains and forced them into the gold mines of Elwynn Forest and Westfall, working the small creatures into relentless exhaustion to extract ore. This cruel tyranny over the mines suggests the gnolls are no longer simple scavengers living hand-to-mouth, but a faction amassing wealth and consolidating strategic ground right under the crown's nose.",sp=0},{t="Shadows and Gold",b="The Riverpaw pack's sudden surge in discipline, armaments, and magic is not organic. Recovered ledgers and documents indicate that the gnolls are engaged in financial transactions with the Defias Brotherhood. While the Stormwind Army initially dismissed the pack as an animalistic nuisance, officials such as Wiley the Black and Captain Danuvin have come to realize the Riverpaws are being bankrolled and directed as part of a coordinated effort to destabilize the kingdom.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who leads the Riverpaw gnolls?",a="The most infamous leader of the Riverpaw pack is Hogger, a massive and ferocious gnoll stalking the southwestern woods of Elwynn Forest near Forest's Edge. The Stormwind authorities consider him an urgent threat and have posted a massive bounty for his head.",al={"riverpaw leader","who leads the riverpaw","hogger riverpaw"},it="leader"},{q="Where are Riverpaw gnolls found?",a="The Riverpaw pack primarily infests southwestern Elwynn Forest around Forest's Edge and Stone Cairn Lake, as well as the open expanses of Westfall, particularly throughout the Dust Plains and along the coastline.",al={"riverpaw locations","where to find riverpaws","riverpaw gnoll spawn"},it="why_here"},{q="Why are the Riverpaw gnolls working with the Defias Brotherhood?",a="Documents recovered from Riverpaw scouts reveal that payments are exchanged between the clan and the Defias Brotherhood. The Defias fund and equip the Riverpaws to spread chaos, pillage resources, and tie down local militias across Westfall and Elwynn, weakening Stormwind's defense.",al={"riverpaw defias connection","defias paying gnolls","gnoll brotherhood connection"},sp=1,it="allies"},{q="Why are kobolds working in the gold mines near the Riverpaw?",a="The Riverpaw gnolls conquered a clan of Redridge kobolds and enslaved them, forcing them into the mines of Elwynn Forest and Westfall. The gnolls serve as merciless overseers, compelling the kobolds to dig out precious gold to fuel their war efforts.",al={"gnolls and kobolds","why do gnolls have kobold slaves","riverpaw kobolds"}},{q="Why hasn't the Stormwind Army wiped out the Riverpaw pack?",a="The kingdom's official military is severely overextended, with few reinforcements sent to rural garrisons like Sentinel Hill and Westbrook. Local marshals must rely on passing adventurers and militia bounties to keep the gnoll population from completely overwhelming the territory.",al={"why aren't guards fighting gnolls","stormwind gnoll response"},it="danger"},{q="What is the historical relationship between the Riverpaws and Stormwind?",a="The Riverpaws have clashed with Stormwind for generations. Years prior to the First War, a pack led by Matriarch Bluepelt boldly attempted to abduct Prince Llane Wrynn, cementing their reputation as a brazen, persistent threat to the royal realm.",al={"riverpaw history","prince llane gnolls","matriarch bluepelt"},it="history"},{q="What is the Riverpaw pack?",a="The Riverpaw pack is an aggressive clan of gnolls indigenous to Elwynn Forest and Westfall. Ferocious, hyena-like scavengers and brigands, they roam the countryside raiding farms, plundering travelers, and clashing with local garrisons. Recently, they have demonstrated an alarming degree of organization, enslaving kobolds to mine gold and aligning themselves with human outlaws to destabilize Stormwind's territories.",al={"riverpaw gnolls identity","who are the riverpaw","what are riverpaws"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are the Riverpaw gnolls hostile to travelers?",a="Like all gnolls, the Riverpaws are vicious, territorial scavengers driven by bloodlust and greed. They view any traveler through Elwynn or Westfall as prey, food, or plunder. Their hostility has only grown bolder and more coordinated as shadowy backers supply them with weapons, magic, and tactical direction to terrorize settlements and drive human defenders out of the countryside.",al={"why do riverpaw attack","riverpaw gnoll hostility","why are gnolls hostile"},it="hostile",add=true}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Riverpaws represent an immediate security crisis for the Alliance heartland, cutting off trade routes between Goldshire, Westfall, and Redridge while Stormwind's armies remain absent.",["race:Human"]="For the farmers and homesteaders of Westfall and Elwynn, the Riverpaws are a nightmare at their doorstep, slaughtering families, stealing livestock, and seizing local mines.",["class:Warrior"]="Town marshals pay well in coin and prestige for warriors willing to march into the brush to cull the Riverpaw brutes and duel their warlords like Hogger.",["class:Rogue"]="Intercepting Riverpaw messengers reveals that their savage violence is orchestrated by criminal syndicates, yielding valuable intelligence on the Defias network."},kw={"defias","dust plains","elwynn","forest's edge","gnolls","hogger","kobold slaves","riverpaw","westfall"},rel={"npc:hogger","zone:elwynn","zone:westfall","topic:defias-brotherhood","quest:176","quest:11","quest:102","subzone:westbrook-garrison"},src={"Riverpaw pack"}}
E["topic:rogue"] = {t="topic",n="Rogue",z="general",s="Rogues are masters of stealth, espionage, and sudden, lethal combat who dictate the terms of engagement from the shadows. Relying on light leather armor, dual-wielded blades, and brewed toxins, they strike unawares before vanishing back into the dark. Across Azeroth, they serve as scouts, assassins, pickpockets, and agents for factions and hidden syndicates alike.",h="Rogues are masters of stealth, espionage, and sudden, lethal combat who dictate the terms of engagement from...",sec={{t="Strike from the Shadows",b="Rogues eschew the heavy plate and open confrontation of traditional frontline warriors. Clad in lightweight leather to remain completely silent and mobile, they stalk their quarry unseen. When they engage, they seize the opening moments through precision and surprise, aiming for vital organs or exposed joints. Utilizing an ever-flowing pool of energy and rapid weapon combinations, a rogue softens up an enemy with carefully chained strikes before unleashing a catastrophic finishing blow.",sp=0},{t="Poisons and Street Smarts",b="A blade alone is rarely enough for a professional cutpurse or killer. Rogues are skilled alchemists of lethal concoctions, hand-crafting venomous coatings to numb limbs, slow escapes, or rapidly sap the vitality of their targets. Coupled with lockpicking tools, thrown weapons, and distracting trickery, they excel at slipping past fortified perimeters, disarming traps, and infiltrating vaults that would stump a company of soldiers.",sp=0},{t="Guilds, Crowns, and Syndicates",b="While many rogues operate as lone wolves, highwaymen, or street-level thugs, the finest operatives belong to organized intelligence networks and assassin guilds. Stormwind relies on the clandestine operatives of SI:7, Ironforge harbors the covert Hidden Circle, and the Forsaken utilize the relentless Deathstalkers. In Orgrimmar, the shadowy remnants of the Shattered Hand serve the Warchief, while the elite, neutral League of Assassins answers to Lord Jorach Ravenholdt.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What armor and weapons do rogues use?",a="Rogues wear leather armor to ensure they can move silently and swiftly without encumbrance. In combat, they dual-wield one-handed armaments, most notably daggers, swords, maces, and fist weapons. They also carry silent ranged weapons such as throwing knives, bows, or crossbows for utility and pulling marks.",al={"rogue gear","what can rogues equip","rogue armor type"},gp=true},{q="How does stealth work for a rogue?",a="Stealth is a disciplined blend of light-footed movement, exploiting shadows, and subtle mysticism. It renders a rogue practically invisible to distant onlookers, though moving too close to an alert guard or enemy can give away their presence. Entering stealth allows rogues to position behind prey to deliver devastating ambushes, garrotes, and disabling strikes.",al={"how does rogue stealth work","can rogues turn invisible","stealth lore"},it="power",gp=true},{q="What is the Ravenholdt League?",a="The League of Assassins, headquartered at Ravenholdt Manor in the Alterac Mountains, is an ancient, clandestine brotherhood led by Lord Jorach Ravenholdt. Unlike faction-bound intelligence units, Ravenholdt is a guild dedicated purely to the discipline, honor, and business of the assassin's craft, taking contracts regardless of political allegiance.",al={"who are ravenholdt","assassins league","lord jorach ravenholdt"},it="allies"},{q="What is SI:7?",a="SI:7 is the premier covert intelligence and reconnaissance wing of the Kingdom of Stormwind. Headquartered in Stormwind City, its field agents, snipers, and thieves handle espionage, high-profile assassinations, and border reconnaissance to protect Alliance interests across Azeroth.",al={"stormwind si7","si7","stormwind intelligence"},it="allies"},{q="How do rogues fight in prolonged combat?",a="When an assassination cannot be completed in an initial strike, a rogue uses agility and footwork to evade return blows. They execute sequential, rapid attacks with their dual weapons to exploit weaknesses—building tactical openings that culminate in lethal finishing blows like ruptures or eviscerations.",al={"rogue combat style","combo points lore","how do rogues fight"},it="conflict",gp=true},{q="Do rogues use magic?",a="Most rogues rely primarily on physical conditioning, martial training, alchemical concoctions, and mundane stealth. However, advanced practitioners often weave minor mysticism or shadow magic into their repertoire, helping them conceal their physical form, slip into shadows in broad daylight, and vanish instantly from sight.",al={"is rogue stealth magic","do rogues cast spells"},it="power"},{q="What is a Rogue in the lore?",a="Rogues are masters of stealth, subterfuge, and opportunism. They encompass elite assassins, sneaky pickpockets, waylaying bandits, scouts, and intelligence operatives. Operating outside the constraints of traditional military honor, rogues dictate the terms of engagement: stalking targets from the shadows, striking vital organs before an enemy can react, and vanishing into thin air. Their allegiance often belongs to whoever pays best, whether serving noble patrons, criminal rings like the Defias Brotherhood, or shadowy state espionage networks.",al={"what is a rogue","rogue lore","who are rogues"},it="identity",add=true},{q="What does it mean for a new adventurer to become a Rogue?",a="Choosing the path of a rogue means leaving behind fair fights and straightforward heroics. You rely on quick wits, swift blades, and quiet footwork rather than heavy plate or brute force. Whether you offer your daggers as a mercenary, work in the shadows for your faction's intelligence handlers, or simply take what you can carry, your survival depends on preparation, venom-tipped steel, and striking only when the advantage is entirely yours.",al={"becoming a rogue","starting as a rogue","role of a rogue"},it="for_you",add=true}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="For a rogue, every mission is an exercise in leverage, patience, and lethal precision, whether working as an operative for high command or picking pockets in city alleys.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance clandestine power runs through organizations like Stormwind's SI:7 and Ironforge's Hidden Circle, who handle internal subversion and foreign threats.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde depends on the espionage of the Shattered Hand in Orgrimmar and Sylvanas Windrunner's feared Deathstalkers in the Undercity to scout enemy territory and eliminate threats quietly."},kw={"assassin","deathstalker","eviscerate","lockpicking","poisons","ravenholdt","rogue","shattered hand","si:7","stealth"},rel={"topic:ravenholdt","npc:lord-jorach-ravenholdt","quest:2300","quest:1858","topic:defias-brotherhood","topic:forsaken","zone:orgrimmar","subzone:forlorn-cavern"},src={"Rogue"}}
E["topic:royal-apothecary-society"] = {t="topic",n="Royal Apothecary Society",z="general",s="The Royal Apothecary Society is an alchemical guild established under Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and based in the Apothecarium beneath the Undercity. Composed of Forsaken researchers, its primary mission is compounding a lethal New Plague capable of eradicating the Scourge while claiming vengeance against the living. While Horde allies believe the society seeks a cure for undeath, its researchers actively harvest venoms, slimes, and toxic reagents across Azeroth to perfect weapons of mass destruction.",h="The Royal Apothecary Society is an alchemical guild established under Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and based in...",sec={{t="Vengeance Through Alchemy",b="Founded in the wake of the Forsaken's liberation, the Royal Apothecary Society serves as the scientific and alchemical arm of Lady Sylvanas Windrunner's kingdom. From their vats and subterranean laboratories in the Apothecarium, researchers seek to weaponize the very force that damned them. Their preeminent endeavor is the creation of a 'New Plague'—a concoction intended to eradicate the Scourge and destroy the Lich King. Unlike traditional human alchemy, which prioritizes elixirs of healing or transmutation, the Society experiments ruthlessly with foul pathogens, caustic acids, and necromantic blends, raising fallen warhorses as skeletal steeds and brewing deadlier strains of toxic blight.",sp=0},{t="A Calculated Deception",b="To the outside world, the Society presents an air of misunderstood scholars. When dealing with their allies in Orgrimmar and Thunder Bluff, Forsaken representatives suggest their alchemists toil tirelessly to uncover a remedy for the curse of undeath. In truth, curing the affliction is of little interest to Master Apothecary Faranell and the dark council. The Society works in absolute secrecy to hone toxins that spare no one, secretly testing their efficacy against both living and undead test subjects to ensure utter annihilation of their enemies.",sp=1},{t="Outposts Across Azeroth",b="The reach of the Apothecary Society extends far beyond the canals of the Undercity. Field apothecaries are dispatched to remote outposts across Lordaeron and Kalimdor. In Silverpine and Hillsbrad, agents like Apothecaries Renferrel and Lydon gather local floras and distill venoms. In the arid Barrens, Apothecary Helbrim catalogs strange desert flora and creatures, while Apothecary Zamah operates beneath Thunder Bluff in the Pools of Vision. Wherever natural toxins, shifting oozes, or virulent organisms exist, the Society sends acolytes to collect samples for the Apothecarium's cauldrons.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Royal Apothecary Society?",a="The Royal Apothecary Society is the Forsaken alchemical organization headquartered in the Apothecarium within the Undercity. Created under Queen Sylvanas Windrunner, its members study virulent pathogens, necromancy, and toxic reagents to craft chemical weapons for the Forsaken.",al={"what is the ras","who are the apothecaries","forsaken alchemy guild"},it="identity"},{q="What is the New Plague they are working on?",a="The New Plague is a bespoke alchemical pathogen developed by the Society under Sylvanas's direct orders. Its ultimate purpose is to possess the power to exterminate the Scourge and the Lich King, while also remaining lethal to living beings who threaten the Forsaken.",al={"new plague explained","what is the forsaken plague","why make a new plague"},it="purpose"},{q="Does the Horde know what the Society is really brewing?",a="No. The Forsaken maintain the facade that their research focuses on curing the affliction of undeath and finding a peaceful place for their kind. The Tauren and Orcs believe the apothecaries seek a medical remedy, unaware that the Society is engineering a lethal weapon of eradication.",al={"does thrall know about the plague","horde stance on apothecaries","do orcs know about the plague"},it="ties"},{q="Who leads the Royal Apothecary Society?",a="While the Society ultimately answers to Queen Sylvanas Windrunner, its daily scientific efforts and research within the Undercity are directed by senior overseers such as Master Apothecary Faranell.",al={"leader of the apothecaries","ras leader","who runs the apothecarium"},it="leader"},{q="Why are apothecaries stationed in places like the Barrens or Thunder Bluff?",a="Field apothecaries search for rare biological agents, exotic venom, fungi, and mutagenic oozes not found in Lordaeron. Agents like Helbrim in the Crossroads and Zamah in Thunder Bluff study local ecology and collect dangerous samples to send back to the laboratories in the Undercity.",al={"why are apothecaries in kalimdor","apothecaries outside tirisfal","apothecary zamah pools of vision"},it="why_here"},{q="How does the Society create Forsaken mounts?",a="Rather than allowing fallen warhorses to rot, the Royal Apothecary Society blends dark alchemy and necromancy to reanimate deceased steeds, raising them as skeletal mounts to serve Forsaken riders in battle.",al={"where do undead horses come from","forsaken mount lore","skeletal horse origin"}},{q="Who is the Royal Apothecary Society allied with?",a="The Society serves Lady Sylvanas Windrunner directly as an organ of the Forsaken state, working in close cooperation with her dreadlord enforcer Varimathras for supplies and subjects. Through the Forsaken, they are nominally part of the Horde, though their relationship with the rest of the faction is built on deceit: while the orcs and tauren believe the Society seeks a cure for undeath, the apothecaries operate purely in service to Sylvanas's vengeance.",al={"apothecary society allies","who do the apothecaries work for","who controls royal apothecary society"},it="allies",add=true},{q="How was the Royal Apothecary Society formed?",a="The Society was created shortly after Sylvanas established the Forsaken realm in the Undercity. A passionate apothecary named Putress impressed the Banshee Queen with his vision: rather than merely suffering the plague that created them, the Forsaken should tame the Plague of Undeath and turn it into a lethal weapon against their enemies. Sylvanas endorsed the plan, appointed Putress Grand Apothecary, and ordered Varimathras to grant the new society whatever resources and prisoners it needed to construct its laboratories beneath the capital.",al={"origin of the royal apothecary society","how did the apothecary society start","history of royal apothecary society"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="The Society represents the scientific might of your people, turning the very plague that butchered Lordaeron into an instrument of vengeance against the Scourge and all who oppose you.",["faction:Horde"]="Forsaken representatives claim their alchemical work seeks an antidote to restore their cursed condition, though their secluded cauldrons in the Apothecarium reek of deadlier ambitions.",["profession:Herbalism"]="The Society places an enormous premium on deadly flora, harvesting toxic fungi, stranglekelp, and grave moss from every corner of Azeroth to fuel their virulent cauldrons.",["race:Tauren"]="Out of pity for the cursed dead, the elders permitted Apothecary Zamah into the Pools of Vision, believing the Forsaken genuinely sought a cleansing cure for their affliction."},kw={"alchemy","apothecarium","faranell","helbrim","lydon","new plague","royal apothecary society","sylvanas windrunner","undercity","zamah"},rel={"subzone:apothecarium","npc:master-apothecary-faranell","npc:sylvanas-windrunner","quest:367","zone:undercity","topic:forsaken","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:scourge"},src={"Royal Apothecary Society"}}
E["topic:ruins-of-gilneas"] = {t="topic",n="Ruins of Gilneas",z="general",s="Gilneas is a secluded human kingdom occupying a rugged, storm-lashed peninsula south of Silverpine Forest. Following the Second War, King Genn Greymane severed ties with the outside world and locked his nation behind the colossal Greymane Wall, leaving neighboring territories abandoned and the kingdom shrouded in total isolation.",h="Gilneas is a secluded human kingdom occupying a rugged, storm-lashed peninsula south of Silverpine Forest.",sec={{t="A Nation Withdrawn",b="Gilneas was once an assertive maritime powerhouse among the human realms, claiming lineage from the ancient empire of Arathor. King Genn Greymane participated reluctantly in the Second War, believing his kingdom could defend itself without foreign entanglements. Bitter over the loss of Gilnean lives and unwilling to help finance the internment camps for captured orcs, Greymane severed relations with the Alliance of Lordaeron. To permanently shield his realm from outside calamity, he ordered the construction of a massive stone barricade along the kingdom's northern frontier.",sp=0},{t="The Severed Frontier",b="Erecting the Greymane Wall demanded painful geopolitical compromises. The natural mountain passes used to anchor the ramparts cleaved straight through the holdings of Lord Darius Crowley. As a consequence, territories such as Ambermill and Pyrewood Village were cut off on the northern side, stranded outside the gate. When the Scourge swept across Lordaeron during the Third War, King Greymane refused entreaties for aid and barred the doors to human refugees, leaving the surrounding villages to face the plague and the undead alone.",sp=0},{t="A Looming Shadow",b="Darkness gathered just beyond the wall. During the Third War, the Dalaran archmage Arugal summoned feral worgen to bolster the Kirin Tor against the Scourge. The beasts slaughtered the undead, then turned on the wizards themselves, besieged Baron Silverlaine's keep and spread through the shadows of Silverpine. Today, Gilneas remains locked behind sealed gates and treacherous sea reefs, an impenetrable enigma steeped in eerie rumors and total silence.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Can players enter Gilneas?",a="No. The kingdom lies entirely behind the Greymane Wall at the southern border of Silverpine Forest. The massive iron gate remains firmly barred, and the peninsula's coastal borders are guarded by sheer sea cliffs and treacherous reefs, making it completely inaccessible.",al={"can you go past greymane wall","is gilneas open","get into gilneas"},it="arrival",gp=true},{q="Why was the Greymane Wall built?",a="King Genn Greymane believed Gilneas was self-sufficient and resented expending resources and soldiers to aid other human nations. Frustrated by debts, internment camps, and foreign conflicts following the Second War, he constructed the wall to isolate Gilneas permanently from the rest of Lordaeron.",al={"why did gilneas isolate","greymane wall purpose","why closed off"},it="purpose"},{q="What happened to Pyrewood Village and Ambermill?",a="Both settlements were part of the holdings of Lord Darius Crowley. Because the Greymane Wall was built along the most defensible mountain ridges, it stranded Pyrewood and Ambermill on the northern exterior, separating them from the kingdom when the doors were sealed.",al={"why is pyrewood outside wall","ambermill gilneas connection","crowley lands"},it="ruin"},{q="Is Gilneas part of the Alliance?",a="Not currently. Genn Greymane formally withdrew from the Alliance of Lordaeron years ago. Gilneas maintains no diplomatic relations, military treaties, or trade with Stormwind, Ironforge, or any other Alliance power.",al={"gilneas faction","are gilneans in the alliance","gilneas alliance"},it="allies"},{q="What connection does Arugal have to Gilneas?",a="Arugal's tragedy unfolded just outside the Greymane Wall. During the Third War, the Dalaran archmage summoned feral worgen to bolster the Kirin Tor against the Scourge. The creatures slaughtered the undead but then turned on the wizards, overran Shadowfang Keep and spread throughout Silverpine, driving Arugal mad.",al={"arugal gilneas","how did arugal get worgen"},it="ties"},{q="Who rules Gilneas?",a="Gilneas is ruled by King Genn Greymane, an unyielding monarch who has fiercely protected his nation's independence at the cost of abandoning the world beyond his gates.",al={"gilneas leader","king of gilneas"},it="leader"},{q="What is Gilneas?",a="Gilneas is an ancient human kingdom situated on a rugged peninsula southwest of Silverpine Forest. Once an influential member of the original Alliance of Lordaeron, its rulers chose a path of fierce isolationism after the Second War. Today, the entire kingdom remains sealed away behind the towering Greymane Wall, cut off from the rest of Azeroth by land and guarded by stormy seas and jagged reefs.",al={"what is gilneas","ruins of gilneas","gilneas kingdom"},it="identity",add=true},{q="What is the history of Gilneas?",a="Gilneas arose over two thousand years ago as an independent city-state during the expansion of the human empire of Arathor. Known for seafaring and self-reliance, the kingdom aided in founding Kul Tiras before joining the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War. Disillusioned with the costs of the conflict and unwilling to help pay for orcish internment camps, King Genn Greymane severed ties with his allies and constructed the Greymane Wall to shut out foreign troubles.",al={"gilneas history","origin of gilneas","how was gilneas founded"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="For humans of Lordaeron and Stormwind, Gilneas stands as a bitter reminder of how pride and isolationism shattered the unity of the Seven Kingdoms prior to the Third War.",["race:Undead"]="Forsaken forces patrolling southern Silverpine keep a wary eye on the colossal gate, knowing an entire living human kingdom sits untouched and hostile right at their border.",["class:Mage"]="The tragic history of Archmage Arugal's summoning beneath the Greymane Wall serves as a grim warning of what transpires when magic is weaponized without proper restraint."},kw={"ambermill","arugal","darius crowley","genn","gilneas","greymane","greymane wall","pyrewood","silverpine","worgen"},rel={"subzone:greymane-wall","zone:silverpine","npc:arugal","subzone:pyrewood-village","subzone:ambermill","topic:worgen","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","npc:darius-crowley"},src={"Ruins of Gilneas"}}
E["topic:sargeras"] = {t="topic",n="Sargeras",z="general",s="Sargeras is the fallen titan and supreme creator of the Burning Legion, bent on scouring all life from the cosmos. Once the noble champion and defender of the Pantheon, his descent into madness led to the Sundering of ancient Azeroth and nearly every demonic catastrophe since.",h="Sargeras is the fallen titan and supreme creator of the Burning Legion, bent on scouring all life from the...",sec={{t="Champion of the Pantheon",b="Before his fall, Sargeras was the greatest and noblest warrior among the titans. Entrusted by the Pantheon of Order to safeguard their grand creations across the Great Dark Beyond, he waged war against the chaotic demons spawned from the Twisting Nether. Incapable of conceiving pure evil, Sargeras trapped countless fiends away within the prison world of Mardum. Yet eons of relentless warfare against cosmic horrors gradually wore down his altruistic spirit.",sp=0},{t="Despair and the Burning Crusade",b="While traversing the cosmos, Sargeras encountered a world thoroughly corrupted by the monstrous Old Gods and learned of the dark powers lurking in the Void. Horrified by the prospect of a slumbering titan worldsoul falling into utter corruption, he concluded that creation was fundamentally flawed. Believing that a lifeless universe was preferable to one consumed by void, he shattered Mardum, bathed himself in chaotic fel fire, and marshaled the liberated demons into the unstoppable Burning Legion.",sp=0},{t="Obsession with Azeroth",b="Ten thousand years ago, the reckless magic of Queen Azshara and her Highborne reached across the stars, alerting Sargeras to Azeroth's existence. Whispering through the depths of the Well of Eternity, the Dark Titan seduced Azshara into opening a portal for his vanguard. Though that ancient invasion culminated in the catastrophic Sundering, Sargeras never ceased scheming to consume Azeroth—orchestrating the opening of the Dark Portal and guiding the demonic corruption of the orcish Horde.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Sargeras?",a="Sargeras is a fallen titan who once served as the Pantheon's greatest warrior. Driven by despair over cosmic corruption, he betrayed his kind and forged the Burning Legion to purge all life from the universe.",al={"sargeras backstory","who is the dark titan","creator of the burning legion"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Sargeras want to destroy the universe?",a="He discovered that cosmic entities known as the Void Lords had sent Old Gods to infect slumbering titan worldsouls. Fearing the birth of an unstoppable, corrupted dark titan, he chose to destroy all creation rather than let it be claimed by darkness.",al={"sargeras motive","why burning crusade","why does sargeras want to destroy everything"},it="motive"},{q="What is Sargeras's connection to Queen Azshara?",a="Ten thousand years ago, Queen Azshara's reckless use of the Well of Eternity drew Sargeras's attention. Viewing him as a god, Azshara and her Highborne attempted to summon the Dark Titan through the Well, triggering the devastating War of the Ancients and the Great Sundering.",al={"sargeras and azshara","well of eternity sargeras","ancient invasion azshara"},it="ties"},{q="Did Sargeras create the demons?",a="No. Demons originally spawned naturally within the chaotic Twisting Nether. Sargeras fought and imprisoned them for ages before choosing to shatter their prison world of Mardum, uniting the demonic races under his command to form the Burning Legion.",al={"did sargeras make demons","where did the burning legion come from"},it="origin"},{q="What happened to the other titans?",a="When the Pantheon confronted Sargeras at Nihilam to plead with him to halt his Burning Crusade, he refused to turn back. In the ensuing clash, the fallen titan overwhelmed the Pantheon with chaotic fel power and destroyed their physical forms.",al={"where are the other titans","pantheon fate","did sargeras kill the pantheon"},it="history"},{q="How was Sargeras connected to the opening of the Dark Portal?",a="Long after the Sundering, Sargeras continued his designs on Azeroth from afar, orchestrating events to corrupt the orcs of Draenor and using the guardian Medivh to tear open the Dark Portal, bringing invasion once again to mortal shores.",al={"sargeras orcs","sargeras dark portal","did sargeras bring orcs"},it="conflict"},{q="Who are Sargeras's allies in the cosmos?",a="Sargeras commands the Burning Legion, a vast and terrifying horde of demonic entities he bound to his will. Rather than fighting alongside equal allies, he rules over corrupted races such as the eredar lords Archimonde and Kil'jaeden, dreadful pit lords like Mannoroth, cunning nathrezim dreadlords, and endless ranks of lesser demons. All in the Legion exist solely to serve his apocalyptic crusade.",al={"sargeras allies","who serves sargeras","sargeras burning legion"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="For the kaldorei, Sargeras is the ultimate architect of the Great Sundering, whose seductive whispers corrupted Queen Azshara and shattered ancient Kalimdor ten millennia ago.",["class:Warlock"]="Warlocks draw upon the very fel powers and demon servitors bound together by the Dark Titan, wielding the ruinous sorceries that formed the Legion.",["race:Orc"]="The tragic history of the orcish clans—blood-curse, madness, and the invasion of Azeroth—traces directly back to the manipulations of Sargeras and his demonic lieutenants.",["class:Mage"]="Mages remember Sargeras as a cautionary terror, proving that unchecked arcane ambition will inevitably attract the gaze of the Burning Legion across the stars."},kw={"aggramar","azshara","burning legion","dark titan","fel","mardum","pantheon","sargeras","sundering"},rel={"topic:burning-legion","topic:titan","topic:old-god","topic:highborne","zone:azshara","subzone:dark-portal","topic:horde"},src={"Sargeras"}}
E["topic:satyr"] = {t="topic",n="Satyr",z="general",s="Satyrs are twisted, caprine demons born from the curse of the Highborne sorcerer Xavius during the ancient War of the Ancients. Once night elves, they were warped by Sargeras into horned, hooved beasts dedicated to spreading fel corruption, tormenting their former kin, and furthering the Burning Legion's will. Across the wilds of Kalimdor, scattered satyr sects corrupt pristine woodlands, desecrate moonwells, and subvert vulnerable mortal cults.",h="Satyrs are twisted, caprine demons born from the curse of the Highborne sorcerer Xavius during the ancient...",sec={{t="Origins of the Curse",b="Ten thousand years ago during the War of the Ancients, the Highborne councilor Xavius failed to keep the Burning Legion's initial gateway stable against Malfurion Stormrage. Enraged by this blunder, Sargeras dismantled Xavius's spirit and rebuilt him as a punishment and tool: his torso retained elven features, but he gained wicked horns, taloned claws, a leonine tail, and the cloven lower body of a goat. Sent back into Zin-Azshari, Xavius gleefully passed his fel affliction to other power-hungry Highborne. Though Xavius was struck down, his demonic progeny survived, continually seeking to reproduce their kind by corrupting others.",sp=0},{t="The War of the Satyr",b="Following the Great Sundering, the surviving demons and satyrs regrouped to wage the War of the Satyr against the shattered night elf civilization. Desperate to counter the satyrs' vicious demonic magic, night elven druids unleashed the unruly Pack Form, inadvertently giving birth to the first feral worgen. The worgen tore through the satyr hosts, shattering their unified army. Robbed of central leadership, the remnants fractured into scattered sects, lingering in dark forests and ancient ruins while harboring deep hatred for their kaldorei cousins.",sp=0},{t="Corrupters of Kalimdor",b="Today, satyr clans inhabit deep woodland enclaves across Kalimdor, from Darkshore and Ashenvale to Felwood, Azshara, and Feralas. They feed directly on the land's pure magic, leeching and spoiling nature until their hooves swell with drained arcane energy. Satyrs frequently turn peaceful furbolg tribes violent, taint ancient moonwells, and serve as covert liaisons for the Shadow Council and the Burning Blade. While some work alongside Twilight's Hammer cultists or hide within ancient ruins like Dire Maul, all remain bound to the shadow of the Burning Legion.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What exactly is a satyr?",a="A satyr is a demon transformed from a night elf through an ancient fel curse. They possess cloven hooves, horns, sharp claws, and a tail, retaining an elven torso while wielding dark fel sorcery and physical savagery.",al={"what are satyrs","satyr race","satyr lore"},it="identity"},{q="Who was the first satyr?",a="Lord Xavius, the Highborne sorcerer and chief advisor to Queen Azshara, was the first satyr. After Xavius failed to defend the Burning Legion's portal during the War of the Ancients, Sargeras remade his body into a demonic, horned form as both punishment and a dark gift.",al={"first satyr","who created satyrs","xavius satyr"},it="origin"},{q="Can satyrs make new satyrs?",a="Yes. Existing satyrs can inflict their transformative curse on other beings, primarily night elves, though the magic has weakened over the millennia and successful transformations are far rarer today. Scattered cultists occasionally revere Xavius hoping to earn the transformation.",al={"how are satyrs made","satyr curse spread","becoming a satyr"},it="power"},{q="Are there female satyrs?",a="Yes, female satyrs exist, though they are rarely seen or distinguished by outside scholars. A known example is the Fallen Priestess Driana, an elven priestess who succumbed to the demonic affliction.",al={"female satyrs","are all satyrs male","girl satyr"},it="people"},{q="Why do satyrs target moonwells and forests?",a="Satyrs feed on purity and natural arcane magic, leeching it directly from pristine lands and night elf moonwells. They store this stolen energy in their hooves, which become swollen and uncloven as they absorb immense magical power.",al={"why do satyrs corrupt moonwells","satyr hooves lore","satyr magic"},it="motive"},{q="Where are satyrs found right now?",a="Satyrs are scattered in ruined and corrupted reaches across Kalimdor, including Satyrnaar and Xavian in Ashenvale, Bashal'Aran in Darkshore, Jadefire Run in Felwood, Sargeron in Desolace, the Haldarr and Legash encampments in Azshara, and within Dire Maul in Feralas.",al={"satyr locations","where do satyrs live","satyr zones"},it="why_here"},{q="Why are satyrs hostile to travelers?",a="Satyrs are thoroughly demonic beings consumed by cruelty and spite. They delight in inflicting physical and emotional pain upon mortals, harboring a deep, ancient hatred particularly for night elves. As servants of demonic corruption, they see any uncorrupted travelers as trespassers and prey, actively striving to taint the land and butcher anyone who stands in the way of spreading their fel corruption.",al={"why do satyrs attack","are satyrs hostile","why satyrs fight"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who leads the satyrs?",a="They have no single overall ruler. Following the War of the Satyr, their centralized hierarchy shattered completely, forcing them into fractured sects and cults. Each local band answers to its own warlord or sorcerer—such as Rethazar the Fallen at Satyrnaar or Prince Xavalis in Felwood. Many continue to revere the memory of Xavius, the first satyr, hoping he will return to command them.",al={"satyr leader","who is the satyr king","satyr leadership"},it="leader",add=true},{q="Who are satyrs allied with?",a="Most satyrs remain bound to the will of the Burning Legion, operating alongside demonic remnants and demonic cults like the Shadow Council and the Burning Blade. While some isolated sects act on their own or ally with other dark forces such as the Twilight's Hammer, they ultimately remain demonic thralls who will answer the call whenever the Legion commands them.",al={"satyr allies","who do satyrs serve","satyr factions"},it="allies",add=true},{q="What is the history of the satyrs?",a="Satyrs arose during the War of the Ancients when Sargeras punished the Highborne sorcerer Xavius by reshaping his soul and body into the first horned, cloven-hoofed demon. Xavius passed this demonic curse to other ambitious Highborne. After the Legion fell, the satyrs waged a desperate war against the night elves until they were broken and scattered, lingering in ruined forests as bitter, fragmented remnants.",al={"satyr history","how were satyrs created","where did satyrs come from"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Satyrs represent the ultimate betrayal of kaldorei heritage, serving as a bitter reminder of the Highborne's arrogance during the War of the Ancients.",["class:Druid"]="Satyrs actively poison the wilds, corrupt furbolgs, and defile sacred groves, making their destruction an urgent imperative for the Cenarion Circle.",["class:Warlock"]="As beings of pure fel energy and demonic malice, satyrs demonstrate the corruptive mastery of the Burning Legion's ancient curses.",["faction:Alliance"]="Sentinels and Alliance adventurers constantly fight to purge satyr encampments encroaching on night elven settlements throughout Ashenvale and Darkshore.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde clashes violently with satyr sects in Ashenvale and Desolace, rooting out their clandestine alliances with the Burning Blade."},kw={"corrupter","demon","fel","highborne","jadefire","sargeron","satyr","satyrnaar","xavian","xavius"},rel={"topic:war-of-the-ancients","topic:highborne","topic:night-elf","topic:burning-legion","zone:ashenvale","zone:felwood","subzone:satyrnaar","quest:1842"},src={"Satyr"}}
E["topic:scarlet-crusade"] = {t="topic",n="Scarlet Crusade",z="general",s="Born from the shattered remnants of the Knights of the Silver Hand, the Scarlet Crusade is a fanatical holy order obsessed with purging the undead from Lordaeron. Blighted by paranoia and radical zealotry, they view anyone outside their ranks as carriers of the plague and shoot outsiders on sight. From strongholds across Tirisfal Glades and the Plaguelands, their crimson banners wage a ruthless war against Scourge and living alike.",h="Born from the shattered remnants of the Knights of the Silver Hand, the Scarlet Crusade is a fanatical holy...",sec={{t="Rise from the Ashes",b="When Prince Arthas betrayed Lordaeron and murdered King Terenas Menethil II, the Order of the Silver Hand was broken. Surviving paladins and priests rallied under leaders like Alexandros Mograine, Saidan Dathrohan, and General Abbendis to reclaim their ruined homeland. Reorganizing their forces at Hearthglen and Tyr's Hand, they swore to cleanse the undead scourge with holy fire. Yet as the war dragged on, tragedy and suspicion festered within their ranks. A faction led by Abbendis grew bitterly intolerant of non-human races, insisting that the fate of Lordaeron was the burden of mankind alone. What began as a desperate resistance soon devolved into an unyielding, xenophobic crusade.",sp=0},{t="The Great Schism",b="As the Crusade's dogma hardened into religious madness, moderate commanders recoiled. Lord Maxwell Tyrosus and his followers argued that retaking the north required alliances across Azeroth, seeking aid from the dwarves, gnomes, and night elves. Rejected by the zealots, Tyrosus and other disillusioned knights abandoned the order to form the Argent Dawn, remaining faithful to the pure, inclusive tenets of the Holy Light. Meanwhile, the Scarlet Crusade seized the Scarlet Monastery in Tirisfal Glades as their heart of power, slaughtering travelers and refugees under the hysterical belief that all who walk the Plaguelands carry the Scourge's contagion.",sp=0},{t="A Puppet in Crimson",b="The Crusade's extreme descent into butchery was not entirely of their own making. During an assault on Stratholme, Grand Crusader Saidan Dathrohan was separated from his men and quietly murdered by the dreadlord Balnazzar. Possessing Dathrohan's corpse, the demon assumed direct control over the Scarlet ranks. Balnazzar subtly fueled their paranoia, orchestrating the murder of Highlord Alexandros Mograine through the jealousy of his son Renault. Behind grand speeches of purity and righteous fury, the crusaders serve as unknowing puppets to the very demonic darkness they believe the Light calls them to destroy.",sp=2}},faq={{q="Why is the Scarlet Crusade hostile to everyone?",a="The Crusade has succumbed to extreme paranoia after the fall of Lordaeron. They believe that anyone who has spent time in northern Lordaeron without being cleansed by their order is an active or latent carrier of the plague of undeath. To preserve their vision of pure humanity, they execute all outsiders on sight.",al={"why are scarlets hostile","are scarlets bad guys","why does scarlet crusade attack me"},it="hostile"},{q="Where did the Scarlet Crusade come from?",a="They originated from surviving knights and priests of the Knights of the Silver Hand after Lordaeron fell during the Third War. Regrouping in Hearthglen and the Scarlet Monastery under figures like Saidan Dathrohan and Alexandros Mograine, they pledged to eradicate the Scourge by any means necessary.",al={"scarlet crusade origin","who founded scarlet crusade","scarlet crusade history"},it="history"},{q="How is the Scarlet Crusade different from the Argent Dawn?",a="Both orders splintered from the original Silver Hand, but while the Argent Dawn accepts all races and remains true to the compassionate virtues of the Light, the Scarlet Crusade became a fanatical, human-supremacist cult that massacres refugees and travelers under the guise of holy cleansing.",al={"argent dawn vs scarlet crusade","difference between scarlet and argent dawn"},it="conflict"},{q="Where are the Scarlet Crusade's main bases located?",a="Their primary bastion is the Scarlet Monastery in northeastern Tirisfal Glades. They also control the fortified town of Hearthglen in the Western Plaguelands, Tyr's Hand in the east, and the Scarlet Bastion inside the ruins of Stratholme, alongside numerous scout towers across the region.",al={"scarlet crusade bases","scarlet locations","where are scarlet crusaders"},it="why_here"},{q="Who actually leads the Scarlet Crusade?",a="Outwardly, Grand Crusader Saidan Dathrohan commands the Crusade from Stratholme, supported by High Inquisitor Sally Whitemane and Scarlet Commander Mograine at the Monastery.",al={"who is leader of scarlet crusade","scarlet crusade grand crusader"},sp=2,it="leader"},{q="Can the Scarlet Crusaders still use the Holy Light if they are evil?",a="Yes. In the cosmology of the Light, wielding its power does not require moral goodness, but absolute, unwavering conviction and faith. The crusaders genuinely believe their brutal crusade is pure and just, allowing them to channel holy wrath despite their cruelty.",al={"why can scarlets use the light","scarlet crusade paladins light","do scarlets have the light"},it="power"},{q="What is the Scarlet Crusade?",a="The Scarlet Crusade is a fanatical religious order of human zealots dedicated to wiping out all undead from the fallen kingdom of Lordaeron. Originally formed from surviving paladins and priests of the Knights of the Silver Hand after the Third War, their righteous cause quickly devolved into paranoid extremism. Convinced that anyone outside their ranks could carry the plague, they violently purge any outsider they encounter.",al={"who are scarlet crusade","scarlet crusaders who are they","what are the scarlets"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Who is the Scarlet Crusade allied with?",a="They have no true allies. While born from the ruins of the human Alliance of Lordaeron, the Scarlet Crusade views all outsiders as potential carriers of the Scourge plague or traitors to humanity. They reject the Alliance of Stormwind, murder night elves, dwarves, and gnomes on sight, and wage relentless war against both the Scourge and the Forsaken.",al={"scarlet crusade allies","who supports scarlet crusade","are scarlets part of the alliance"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["class:Paladin"]="The Scarlet Crusade represents the darkest perversion of the Silver Hand's teachings, demonstrating how righteous conviction can twist into tyrannical fanaticism without humility and compassion.",["race:Undead"]="The Scarlet Crusade is the Forsaken's most immediate living nemesis, contesting the borders of Tirisfal Glades and actively hunting any undead who break free from the Lich King's grasp.",["faction:Alliance"]="Though they wear the armor and speak the language of fallen Lordaeron, the Scarlet Crusade considers the modern Alliance compromised and executes Alliance emissaries without hesitation.",["class:Priest"]="Studying the Crusade reveals the terrifying potency of faith stripped of moral restraint, where holy scripture is used to justify ruthless inquisitions and village purges."},kw={"argent dawn","ashbringer","hearthglen","saidan dathrohan","sally whitemane","scarlet crusade","scarlet monastery","silver hand","tyr's hand"},rel={"zone:scarlet","zone:tirisfal","zone:westernplaguelands","topic:argent-dawn","npc:sally-whitemane","subzone:stratholme","subzone:hearthglen","subzone:tyr-s-hand"},src={"Scarlet Crusade"}}
E["topic:scourge"] = {t="topic",n="Scourge",z="general",s="The Scourge is an apocalyptic army of undead monsters, necromancers, and enslaved spirits bound to the will of the Lich King. Originally created by the Burning Legion to destabilize Azeroth ahead of the Third War, the legion of walking corpses broke free from demonic control and shattered the kingdoms of Lordaeron and Quel'Thalas. Today, they wage unending war from their arctic stronghold in Northrend and their corrupt dominion over the Plaguelands.",h="The Scourge is an apocalyptic army of undead monsters, necromancers, and enslaved spirits bound to the will...",sec={{t="A Plague of Demonic Design",b="The Scourge was born as an instrument of destruction forged by the Burning Legion. The demon lords bound the spirit of the fallen orc shaman Ner'zhul to the Frozen Throne in distant Northrend, fashioning him into the Lich King. With terrifying telepathic power, he was commanded to prepare Azeroth for conquest. The Lich King unleashed the Plague of Undeath southward into the unsuspecting human realm of Lordaeron, relying on the mortal traitors of the Cult of the Damned to poison grain supplies. Those who perished arose not as restful dead, but as twisted thralls entirely subservient to the Lich King's iron mind.",sp=0},{t="The Fall of the North",b="The Scourge achieved total devastation when Prince Arthas Menethil fell to the Lich King's whispers. Seeking vengeance in Northrend, Arthas claimed the cursed blade Frostmourne and forfeited his soul, becoming the Scourge's paramount death knight. Returning home, Arthas slew his father King Terenas, butchered Capital City, and unleashed the undead horde across Lordaeron. The Scourge swept through towns like Darrowshire and Andorhal, butchered the Silver Hand paladins, destroyed Dalaran to summon Archimonde, and carved a dead scar through Quel'Thalas to resurrect Kel'Thuzad as a lich.",sp=0},{t="Betrayal and the Schism",b="The Lich King never intended to serve the Burning Legion permanently. Through Arthas, Ner'zhul secretly engineered the Legion's defeat by goading Illidan Stormrage into slaying the dreadlord Tichondrius in Felwood. Enraged, the demon lord Kil'jaeden later ordered Illidan to obliterate the Frozen Throne. Illidan's assault fractured the icy prison, causing the Lich King's mental grip over his minions to waver. Seizing on this weakened control, a faction of free-willed undead led by the resurrected banshee Sylvanas Windrunner broke away, christened themselves the Forsaken, and seized the ruins of Lordaeron.",sp=0},{t="The Present Threat",b="Though weakened by the fracturing of the Frozen Throne and the rebellion of the Forsaken, the Scourge remains the deadliest force in the northern world. Led in the field by the lich Kel'Thuzad, its abominations, crypt fiends, and necromancers dominate the corrupted Eastweald and Westweald, now known as the Plaguelands. From grim bastions and blighted fields, they continuously wage war against the Forsaken of the Undercity, the fanatic zealots of the Scarlet Crusade, and the remaining enclaves of the Alliance.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Scourge?",a="The Scourge is a vast empire of undead thralls, necromancers, and warped beasts bound mentally to the Lich King. They exist solely to destroy all living things and convert the corpses of the fallen into obedient servants.",al={"what is the scourge","undead scourge","who are the scourge"},it="identity"},{q="Who leads the Scourge?",a="The Scourge is commanded by the Lich King from his Frozen Throne in the distant, icy continent of Northrend. In the ruined lands of Lordaeron, his armies are directed locally by powerful lieutenants such as the lich Kel'Thuzad.",al={"who is the scourge leader","scourge master","who controls the scourge"},it="leader"},{q="How does the Scourge differ from the Forsaken?",a="The Scourge are mindless or dominated undead enslaved to the psychic will of the Lich King. The Forsaken are former Scourge victims who regained their free will when the Lich King's power weakened. Led by Lady Sylvanas Windrunner, the Forsaken despise the Scourge and fight fiercely to annihilate them.",al={"scourge vs forsaken","are forsaken scourge","why are undead fighting each other"},it="conflict"},{q="Why did the Scourge attack Lordaeron?",a="The Burning Legion ordered the Lich King to spread the Plague of Undeath across Lordaeron to slaughter its people, weaken the Alliance, and clear away any resistance before the demons began their invasion of Azeroth during the Third War.",al={"why destroy lordaeron","scourging of lordaeron","why did scourge invade"},it="history"},{q="Who is the Cult of the Damned?",a="The Cult of the Damned is a mortal organization founded by the archmage Kel'Thuzad. Its members are living humans who willingly serve the Lich King, lured by promises of dark magical power and eternal life through undeath. They were responsible for spreading the tainted grain that initiated the plague.",al={"cult of the damned","scourge cultists","why do humans join scourge"},it="allies"},{q="How was Prince Arthas corrupted by the Scourge?",a="Driven to obsession while trying to save his kingdom from the plague, Prince Arthas pursued the dreadlord Mal'Ganis to Northrend. In desperation, Arthas took up the cursed runeblade Frostmourne, which stole his soul. Broken and twisted by the Lich King's whispers, he returned as the Scourge's premier champion and butchered his own homeland.",al={"how did arthas turn","arthas scourge","why did arthas betray his people"},it="history"},{q="Why is the undead army called the Scourge?",a="The necromancer Kel'Thuzad bestowed the name upon the army as he watched its ranks swell with freshly infected victims in northern Lordaeron. Beholding the monstrous host of mindless undead and devoted cultists rising under the Lich King's dark will, he proclaimed that the army would act as a scourge to sweep down upon the living and scour humanity from the face of the world.",al={"why called scourge","scourge name origin","how did scourge get its name"},it="name",add=true},{q="Why are the Scourge hostile to everyone?",a="The Scourge exists for a single horrific purpose: the eradication of all mortal life. Bound telepathically to the absolute will of the Lich King, its legions of rotting corpses, banshees, and monstrosities feel no mercy, exhaustion, or doubt. They view any living traveler as both an enemy to slaughter and fresh material to raise into their ever-expanding army of the dead.",al={"why does the scourge attack","why is scourge hostile","scourge aggressive why"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Where did the Scourge come from?",a="The Scourge was originally created by the demonic Burning Legion to soften Azeroth's defenses ahead of their invasion. The Legion took the spirit of the orc shaman Ner'zhul, bound him as the Lich King within the Frozen Throne in distant Northrend, and granted him dominion over necromancy. From his icy seat, he unleashed the Plague of Undeath southward into Lordaeron, subverting mortals into an unstoppable legion of undead servants.",al={"where did scourge come from","scourge origin","how was scourge created"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Why are the Scourge found throughout Lordaeron?",a="Lordaeron was ground zero for the Scourge's devastation of humanity. The Cult of the Damned contaminated the northern grain supplies with the Plague of Undeath, killing vast populations and raising them as mindless thralls. After Prince Arthas returned from Northrend as the Lich King's champion and murdered King Terenas, the Scourge swept across the kingdom, claiming its towns and transforming the Eastweald into the blighted Plaguelands you see today.",al={"why is scourge in plaguelands","scourge lordaeron why","why are undead in lordaeron"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Undead"]="Every Forsaken was once an enslaved instrument of the Scourge, forced to butcher friends and kin until the Lich King's grip slipped. Eradicating the Scourge is not just a war of survival; it is a campaign of absolute personal vengeance.",["race:Human"]="The Scourge destroyed humanity's greatest kingdom, slaughtered the line of Menethil, and transformed the heartland of Lordaeron into a blighted wasteland.",["class:Paladin"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand were shattered by the Scourge when Prince Arthas turned on his own mentors and murdered Uther the Lightbringer. For any paladin, cleansing the Scourge with the Holy Light is a sacred duty.",["faction:Alliance"]="The memory of Lordaeron's fall remains an open wound for the Alliance, driving their ongoing military efforts to reclaim the Plaguelands and avenge the fallen.",["faction:Horde"]="With the Forsaken now accepted into the Horde, the entire coalition finds itself drawn into the brutal, ongoing war against the remnants of the Lich King's armies in the north."},kw={"arthas","cult of the damned","frostmourne","frozen throne","kel'thuzad","lich king","lordaeron","plague","plaguelands","scourge","undead"},rel={"topic:lich-king","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:cult-of-the-damned","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:forsaken","topic:lordaeron","topic:third-war","topic:burning-legion"},src={"Scourge"}}
E["topic:second-war"] = {t="topic",n="Second War",z="general",s="The Second War was the continent-spanning clash across the Eastern Kingdoms between the Orcish Horde and the newly forged Alliance of Lordaeron. Sparked after the sack of Stormwind, the war devastated Khaz Modan and northern human realms before turning at the gates of Capital City. It ended in the destruction of the Dark Portal and the dispersal of the old Horde.",h="The Second War was the continent-spanning clash across the Eastern Kingdoms between the Orcish Horde and the...",sec={{t="A Continent at War",b="Following the fall of Stormwind during the First War, Regent Anduin Lothar led human refugees north across the Great Sea to Lordaeron. King Terenas Menethil II convened the Council of Seven Nations, forging the Alliance of Lordaeron alongside the high elves of Quel'Thalas, the Bronzebeard dwarves of Ironforge, and the gnomes of Gnomeregan. Meanwhile, Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer consolidated the Horde, forging alliances with forest troll tribes and the Steamwheedle Cartel while subjugating red dragons through the Dragonmaw clan to invade the northern continents.",sp=0},{t="The Invasion of the North",b="The Horde launched devastating land and naval offensives, quickly overrunning Khaz Modan and driving the dwarves and gnomes behind the sealed gates of Ironforge and Gnomeregan. Advancing north across the Thandol Span and through the seas, orcish forces burned their way through Hillsbrad and the Hinterlands before laying siege to Capital City itself. The kingdom of Alterac betrayed the Alliance, guiding orc divisions through mountain passes. Despite devastating dragon attacks, the Alliance rallied around the newly created Knights of the Silver Hand and paladins who wielded the Holy Light against orcish warlocks and death knights.",sp=0},{t="The Turning Tide and the Dark Portal",b="The war turned when the warlock Gul'dan deserted the Horde with his followers to pursue personal power, forcing Doomhammer to divert critical forces from the front lines. The Alliance counterattacked, breaking the siege of Lordaeron and systematically driving the Horde back south across the Thandol Span, liberating Khaz Modan along the way. In the final mountain passes of the Burning Steppes, Anduin Lothar fell in single combat against Doomhammer, but his lieutenant Turalyon rallied the troops and routed the orcs. The Alliance pursued the survivors all the way to the Black Morass, shattering the remnants and demolishing the Dark Portal.",sp=0}},faq={{q="How did the Second War begin?",a="After the Horde razed Stormwind during the First War, Orgrim Doomhammer recognized that the northern human realms would eventually retaliate. He decided to strike first before humanity could organize. At the same time, Stormwind refugees led by Anduin Lothar warned King Terenas Menethil II of Lordaeron, leading to the creation of the Alliance of Lordaeron.",al={"why did the second war start","how did it start","second war cause"},it="origin"},{q="Who won the Second War?",a="The Alliance of Lordaeron won a decisive victory. After repelling the Horde from Capital City and driving them south through Khaz Modan, Alliance forces defeated Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer at the foot of Blackrock Mountain and dismantled the Dark Portal in the Black Morass.",al={"who won","alliance or horde win","second war victor"},it="conflict"},{q="What happened to Khaz Modan during the fighting?",a="The Horde overran the surface lands of Khaz Modan to seize its rich mineral deposits and forges. The Bronzebeard dwarves and their gnomish allies blew up mountain passes and tunnels to slow the march, eventually retreating entirely behind the sealed gates of Ironforge and Gnomeregan. The Horde laid siege to Ironforge but could never breach its gates before the Alliance pushed south and liberated the realm.",al={"khaz modan second war","ironforge siege second war","what happened to the dwarves"},it="ruin"},{q="Why did the Horde lose when they reached Capital City?",a="At the height of the siege of Lordaeron's capital, the warlock Gul'dan abandoned the front line, taking the Stormreaver and Twilight's Hammer clans with him to hunt for ancient demonic power. Doomhammer was forced to split his army to punish the deserters, fatally weakening his siege lines just as the Alliance counterattack struck.",al={"why did the horde lose","gul'dan betrayal second war","why did doomhammer fail"},it="motive"},{q="Who was Anduin Lothar?",a="Sir Anduin Lothar was the Lion of Azeroth and the Regent of Stormwind after King Llane's assassination. He led the survivors north and was named Supreme Commander of the Alliance armies. Lothar was killed in single combat against Orgrim Doomhammer in the Burning Steppes near the end of the war, inspiring Turalyon to lead the Alliance to ultimate victory.",al={"who is lothar","anduin lothar death","supreme commander lothar"},it="leader"},{q="Where did the final battle take place?",a="The decisive army-level battle took place around Blackrock Mountain in the Burning Steppes, where Doomhammer was defeated. The surviving orcs fled to the Black Morass, where the Alliance fought a final engagement at the Dark Portal itself, destroying the rift and imprisoning or scattering the remaining Horde clans.",al={"last battle second war","dark portal battle","burning steppes second war"},it="place"},{q="What was the Second War?",a="The Second War was a massive, continent-spanning conflict fought across the Eastern Kingdoms between the Orcish Horde and the original Alliance of Lordaeron. Beginning shortly after the fall of Stormwind, it pitted humanity and their newfound allies—the dwarves of Khaz Modan, the gnomes of Gnomeregan, and the high elves of Quel'Thalas—against Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde, bolstered by forest trolls, goblins, and enslaved red dragons.",al={"what is second war","second great war","who fought second war"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Who were the factions and allies in the Second War?",a="The war united races on both sides into vast coalitions. The Alliance of Lordaeron brought together the seven human kingdoms alongside Bronzebeard and Wildhammer dwarves, Gnomeregan's gnomes, and high elves. Opposing them was the Orcish Horde, joined by the Amani forest trolls, the goblins of the Steamwheedle Cartel, and red dragons forced into servitude by the Dragonmaw clan using the Demon Soul.",al={"second war alliances","who fought with who second war","second war factions"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Why is it called the Second War?",a="It is named the Second War because it immediately followed the First War, the initial conflict triggered when orcs first surged through the Dark Portal and razed the Kingdom of Stormwind. When the survivors fled north and Orgrim Doomhammer pushed across the continent to eradicate the remaining human kingdoms, the renewed clash became the second Great War between the two mortal factions.",al={"why called second war","second great war name","name second war"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Second War was the defining crucible that forged the modern Alliance, uniting humans, dwarves, gnomes, and elves against an existential threat.",["faction:Horde"]="The conflict marked the collapse of the old blood-cursed Horde, leading directly to the internment camps and the eventual rebirth of the new Horde under Thrall.",["class:Paladin"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand were founded specifically during this conflict by Archbishop Alonsus Faol and Uther the Lightbringer to counter the fel magic and death knights of the Horde.",["race:Dwarf"]="Khaz Modan suffered widespread devastation during the war, enduring a protracted Horde siege behind the sealed mountain gates of Ironforge until Alliance armies lifted it."},kw={"alliance of lordaeron","blackrock mountain","dark portal","doomhammer","guldan","lothar","second war","silver hand","thandol span"},rel={"topic:first-war","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","npc:orgrim-doomhammer","topic:anduin-lothar","subzone:dark-portal","topic:dragonmaw-clan","zone:ironforge","zone:burningsteppes"},src={"Second War"}}
E["topic:sentinels"] = {t="topic",n="Sentinels",z="general",s="The Sentinels are the elite, predominantly female military force of the night elves, formed ten thousand years ago after the War of the Ancients. Guided by the Sisterhood of Elune and commanded by General Shandris Feathermoon, they guard kaldorei settlements and defend sacred lands from Ashenvale to Feralas. Today, they wage a bitter war against Horde deforestation while watching over their new home on Teldrassil.",h="The Sentinels are the elite, predominantly female military force of the night elves, formed ten thousand...",sec={{t="Born from the Sundering",b="Before the Great Sundering, the military of the ancient Kaldorei Empire had devolved into a decadent hierarchy where rank was decided by noble lineage rather than martial skill. The devastation of the War of the Ancients proved that this aristocratic structure was utterly unfit to defend Azeroth. In the aftermath, High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind spearheaded radical reforms across night elf society. She turned to the Sisterhood of Elune, an order that had survived intact and possessed extensive experience in tactics, combat, and healing. From its devout ranks, Tyrande forged the Sentinels: a standing martial army dedicated to selflessly protecting their emerging society without regard to noble heritage.",sp=0},{t="Structure and Warfare",b="While Tyrande Whisperwind remains the spiritual supreme commander, active military leadership belongs to her lieutenant, General Shandris Feathermoon, operating out of Feathermoon Stronghold in Feralas. The Sentinels are renowned for disciplined marksmanship, deadly moonglaives, and specialized divisions. Their forces consist of elite archers, nightsaber-riding huntresses, and scouting hippogryph riders who dominate the skies. Centuries before the Third War, they fought under Archdruid Fandral Staghelm during the brutal War of the Shifting Sands in Silithus, halting the qiraji swarm. Every night elf settlement—from Astranaar to the sprawling branches of Darnassus—relies upon Sentinel garrisons for security.",sp=0},{t="Defending Sacred Soil",b="During the Third War, the Sentinels mounted desperate defenses against both demonic invaders and early incursions by the Horde, suffering immense losses when Cenarius fell in Ashenvale. After surviving the Battle of Mount Hyjal and pursuing Illidan across Lordaeron, they returned to find Kalimdor irrevocably changed. Today, the Sentinels face peril on multiple fronts. On Teldrassil, garrisons contend with corrupted wildlife and enraged furbolgs. Farther south, the Silverwing Sentinels clash relentlessly with the Warsong Outriders in Warsong Gulch, resolved to drive the Horde out of Ashenvale and defend the ancient forests from further exploitation.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who leads the Sentinels?",a="High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind oversees the organization politically and spiritually, having founded it from the Sisterhood of Elune. The practical day-to-day command of the army falls upon General Shandris Feathermoon, who directs operations across Kalimdor from Feathermoon Stronghold.",al={"sentinel leader","who is in charge of sentinels","shandris or tyrande"},it="leader"},{q="Why are most Sentinels female?",a="Following the War of the Ancients, traditional night elf society divided responsibilities along spiritual and martial lines: women dominated the priesthood of Elune and formed the military Sentinels, while men overwhelmingly entered the Cenarion Circle as druids. This strict division shaped the army for millennia, though society has slowly begun shifting in the wake of the Third War.",al={"why only female sentinels","women army night elf","can men be sentinels"},it="people"},{q="Where is the Sentinels' main base of operations?",a="Outside of the night elf capital of Darnassus, their primary fortress is Feathermoon Stronghold, situated on an island off the coast of Feralas. Prominent garrisons are also stationed throughout Ashenvale, such as Astranaar and Silverwing Outpost.",al={"sentinel base","sentinel headquarters","feathermoon stronghold"},it="place"},{q="What weapons do the Sentinels specialize in?",a="Sentinels are celebrated marksmen utilizing recurve bows, but their cultural hallmark is the three-bladed moonglaive. Huntresses wield these glaives from atop ferocious nightsaber panthers, while airborne riders strike from the backs of armored hippogryphs.",al={"sentinel weapons","moonglaive","what do sentinels use"}},{q="Why are the Sentinels fighting in Warsong Gulch?",a="The Silverwing Sentinels are locked in a territorial war against the Horde's Warsong Outriders. The orcs have heavily deforested Ashenvale to feed their lumber supply, desecrating forests considered sacred by the night elves, who have sworn to banish every last invader.",al={"warsong gulch sentinels","silverwing sentinels","why ashenvale war"},it="conflict"},{q="Did the Sentinels fight in the Third War?",a="Yes. They were the primary front-line defense when the Burning Legion and the Scourge marched on Kalimdor. Led by Tyrande, they held the line until an alliance was forged with the human and orc refugees, culminating in the decisive victory at the Battle of Mount Hyjal.",al={"sentinels third war","sentinels hyjal","sentinels wc3"}},{q="What are the Sentinels?",a="The Sentinels, or the Sentinel Army, are the standing military force and primary guardians of the night elf race. Deeply entwined with the Sisterhood of Elune, their ranks consist of devout, elite warriors—chiefly deadly archers, saber-mounted huntresses, and aerial hippogryph riders. They patrol ancient woodlands, guard night elven settlements, and defend kaldorei territories against all outside threats.",al={"who are sentinels","sentinel army","night elf sentinels"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where are the Sentinels found across Kalimdor?",a="You will encounter Sentinels stationed in nearly every night elf settlement and wilderness outpost across northern and central Kalimdor. They maintain a heavy garrison guarding their new home in Teldrassil and their capital of Darnassus. Across Ashenvale, Darkshore, and Stonetalon, they actively repel invading Horde lumber expeditions, corrupted furbolgs, and demonic remnants, while maintaining their southern fortress of Feathermoon Stronghold in Feralas.",al={"sentinel locations","where do sentinels patrol","why sentinels here"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="Why are the Sentinels hostile to the Horde?",a="If you march under the Horde banner, the Sentinels view you as an invading trespasser. Bitter hostility began during the Third War when the Warsong clan slew their revered demigod Cenarius. Today, the Horde continues to harvest timber from the sacred groves of Ashenvale, driving the Sentinels to violently defend their ancestral borders from all encroaching orcs and their allies.",al={"why sentinels attack me","sentinels kos horde","are sentinels hostile"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who are the Sentinels allied with?",a="The Sentinels serve Darnassus and answer directly to High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind and the Sisterhood of Elune. As the night elves joined the Grand Alliance, the Sentinels fight alongside humans, dwarves, and gnomes, deploying troops to assist allied campaigns and standing guard at joint outposts across the world.",al={"sentinel allies","who do sentinels fight for","are sentinels alliance"},it="allies",add=true},{q="How was the Sentinel Army formed?",a="The Sentinels were forged in the aftermath of the War of the Ancients ten thousand years ago. Following the collapse of the old empire—where military leadership had decayed into idle noble status—High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind reformed their martial forces. Drawing upon the battle-tested, disciplined ranks of the Sisterhood of Elune, she created a vigilant, standing army to ensure the night elves would never again fall unprepared to demonic invasion.",al={"sentinel origin","origin of sentinels","history of sentinels"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="Every kaldorei hamlet owes its survival to the Sentinels' watchful eyes, and many young women aspire to earn their stripes in the army of Shandris and Tyrande.",["faction:Horde"]="Sentinels are lethal woodland skirmishers who strike without warning from the shadows of Ashenvale, fiercely contesting Horde logging efforts.",["class:Hunter"]="Sentinel archers and beast-riding huntresses represent the pinnacle of nocturnal marksmanship and animal companionship on Kalimdor.",["class:Priest"]="Because the Sentinels were born directly from the Sisterhood of Elune, military duty and sacred devotion to the Moon Goddess remain inextricably linked."},kw={"ashenvale","feathermoon","huntress","kaldorei","moonglaive","nightsaber","sentinels","shandris","silverwing","tyrande"},rel={"npc:shandris-feathermoon","npc:tyrande-whisperwind","topic:night-elf","topic:elune","subzone:feathermoon-stronghold","zone:ashenvale","topic:war-of-the-ancients","npc:warsong-outrider"},src={"Sentinels"}}
E["topic:shadow-council"] = {t="topic",n="Shadow Council",z="general",s="The Shadow Council was the secret cabal of warlocks and necrolytes that orchestrated the corruption of the Old Horde on Draenor and guided its invasion of Azeroth. Founded by Gul'dan under the direction of the Burning Legion, the cult manipulated clan chieftains from behind the scenes until Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer purged their stronghold in Blackrock Mountain.",h="The Shadow Council was the secret cabal of warlocks and necrolytes that orchestrated the corruption of the...",sec={{t="Architects of the Blood Curse",b="Before the crossing into Azeroth, Gul'dan brought together a clandestine assembly of warlocks, necrolytes, and opportunistic chieftains to manipulate the orcish clans. By elevating Blackhand the Destroyer as a malleable figurehead, the Shadow Council ruled the Old Horde from the dark. They disseminated fel magic across Draenor, coordinated the conquest of Shattrath and the Temple of Karabor, and fed the blood of the demon Mannoroth to the clans, binding the orcs to the will of the Burning Legion.",sp=0},{t="The Crossing and Blackrock Mountain",b="When Kil'jaeden vanished, the Shadow Council entered a pact with Medivh—the Guardian possessed by Sargeras—to breach the barrier between worlds. Using the life essences of thousands of captives to empower the Dark Portal, the Council guided the Horde into the Kingdom of Stormwind. After securing Blackrock Mountain through an uneasy arrangement brokered by Cho'gall with the Dark Iron dwarves and the fire elementals of Ragnaros, the warlocks oversaw the war effort from the upper spire.",sp=0},{t="Doomhammer's Purge and Death Knights",b="Following Medivh's death, Gul'dan fell into a comatose trance. Orgrim Doomhammer seized the moment: he slew Blackhand, interrogated Garona to expose the Council's inner sanctum, and launched an assault that obliterated most of the warlocks. Awaking to find his cult shattered, Gul'dan bargained for his life by offering Doomhammer a terrifying counter to human magic. Imbuing the spirits of slaughtered Shadow Council warlocks into the corpses of fallen human knights, Gul'dan created the first death knights.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who founded the Shadow Council?",a="The Shadow Council was founded by the orc warlock Gul'dan on Draenor. Serving the demon lord Kil'jaeden, Gul'dan gathered like-minded practitioners of fel magic to pull the strings of the clans while using Warchief Blackhand as an unwitting puppet.",al={"who made shadow council","shadow council founder","guldan council"},it="origin"},{q="What happened to the Shadow Council during the First War?",a="When Gul'dan was incapacitated after Medivh's death, Orgrim Doomhammer killed Blackhand and took command of the Horde. Doomhammer tortured the assassin Garona to uncover the Council's hidden sanctuary in Blackrock Spire, leading a purge that wiped out nearly all of its warlocks.",al={"how did shadow council fall","doomhammer shadow council","purge of the shadow council"},it="history"},{q="How did the first death knights come from the Shadow Council?",a="After Doomhammer eradicated the Council, Gul'dan bargained for clemency by promising a dark weapon to match human sorcerers. He took the lingering souls of his slain warlocks—most notably Teron Gorefiend—and infused them into the preserved bodies of fallen Stormwind knights, creating the first generation of death knights.",al={"shadow council death knights","who became death knights","teron gorefiend council"}},{q="Did all orc warlocks belong to the Shadow Council?",a="No. While the Shadow Council controlled the widespread adoption of fel magic across Draenor, several practitioners refused membership. Notable figures such as Drek'Thar of the Frostwolves and Nekros Skullcrusher learned the dark arts but remained strictly loyal to their respective clans instead of Gul'dan's cabal.",al={"were all warlocks shadow council","drekthar shadow council","nekros shadow council"}},{q="What was Cho'gall's role in the Council?",a="Cho'gall was Gul'dan's foremost apprentice and chief lieutenant. He led the Twilight's Hammer clan within the Council's orbit and utilized his hidden ties to the Old Gods to broker a truce with the Dark Iron dwarves and fire elementals in Blackrock Mountain.",al={"chogall shadow council","chogall role","twilight hammer shadow council"}},{q="Does the Shadow Council still exist?",a="The original Council was shattered by Doomhammer, and its remnants further fractured when Gul'dan died at the Tomb of Sargeras. However, splintered cultists, surviving warlocks, and demonic agents continuing the Legion's work across Azeroth and the ruins of Draenor still trace their lineage to Gul'dan's organization.",al={"is shadow council around","shadow council today","shadow council present"},it="today"},{q="What is the Shadow Council?",a="The Shadow Council is a clandestine cult of mortal warlocks dedicated to serving the demonic Burning Legion. Founded on Draenor by the orc warlock Gul'dan, the Council worked from the shadows to manipulate the Orcish Horde, corrupt its clans with fel magic, and coordinate the genocide of the draenei before steering the orcs through the Dark Portal into Azeroth.",al={"what is shadow council","who are the shadow council","shadow council meaning"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Who do the Shadow Council serve?",a="The Shadow Council serves the Burning Legion and its demon lords, notably Kil'jaeden. While mortal leaders like Gul'dan have directed its day-to-day machinations, the cult's ultimate purpose has always been carrying out the Legion's dark designs across both Draenor and Azeroth.",al={"who do shadow council serve","shadow council allies","who leads shadow council","shadow council demon masters"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Why are the Shadow Council hostile to travelers?",a="The Shadow Council operates entirely in secrecy to further the Burning Legion's schemes of destruction and demonic corruption. Anyone outside their demonic fold—whether Alliance or the new Horde—is an obstacle, an enemy, or fuel for their dark soul-draining rituals.",al={"why does shadow council attack","shadow council hostile why","why is shadow council evil"},it="hostile",add=true}},ang={["class:Warlock"]="The Shadow Council formalized the study of fel sorcery and demonic pacts among mortals; modern warlocks draw directly upon the summoning rites and soul-harvesting techniques they pioneered.",["race:Orc"]="The Shadow Council engineered the ruin of Draenor and the blood pact that cursed the orcish race, leaving deep generational shame that Thrall's new Horde actively strives to purge.",["faction:Horde"]="Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer's brutal slaughter of the Shadow Council marked the turning point where the Horde first attempted to sever itself from demonic puppet masters.",["faction:Alliance"]="The kingdom of Stormwind fell largely to the covert operations of the Shadow Council, from the opening of the Dark Portal to the assassination of King Llane Wrynn."},kw={"blackhand","blackrock mountain","burning legion","cho'gall","dark portal","death knight","fel magic","gul'dan","mannoroth","shadow council","teron gorefiend"},rel={"npc:orgrim-doomhammer","topic:old-horde","subzone:blackrock-mountain","topic:burning-legion","subzone:dark-portal","topic:twilight-s-hammer","topic:medivh","topic:first-war"},src={"Shadow Council"}}
E["topic:shaman"] = {t="topic",n="Shaman",z="general",s="Shaman are the spiritual mediators and elemental guides of the Horde, communing with the primordial forces of earth, air, fire, and water. Balancing raw nature through totems, lightning, and ancestral healing, they serve as the spiritual core of orc, tauren, and troll culture under the leadership of Warchief Thrall.",h="Shaman are the spiritual mediators and elemental guides of the Horde, communing with the primordial forces of...",sec={{t="Mediators of the Elements",b="Unlike priests who revere the divine or mages who bend the arcane, shaman interact directly with the primal forces of nature. The elements of earth, air, fire, and water are inherently chaotic and locked in perpetual conflict. A shaman does not enslave these forces, but petitions and negotiates with them, serving as a balance keeper between primal fury and the mortal world. Through wooden and stone totems planted into the soil, shaman channel these elemental bargains to protect allies, hinder foes, or mend the wounded with cleansing water.",sp=0},{t="Loss and Rebirth Among Orcs",b="Shamanism was once the ancient foundation of orcish clan life on Draenor. However, Kil'jaeden and the Shadow Council deceived the shamans into war against the draenei, causing the elemental spirits to withdraw their favor. Stripped of their gifts, many former shaman succumbed to demonic corruption and became warlocks, nearly extinguishing the tradition entirely. Only after the Second War did Thrall, guided by Drek'Thar of the Frostwolf clan, rediscover the ancient rites. Thrall's ascension as Warchief revitalized shamanism, elevating it into the moral compass of the New Horde.",sp=0},{t="Traditions of the Horde",b="Across Kalimdor, shamanic traditions vary widely among the Horde's allied peoples. Tauren mystics, who founded the ancient Earthen Ring over two centuries ago, treat communion with the earth and the spirits as a sacred cultural anchor. Darkspear trolls approach the discipline through reverence for the loa and ancestral wild spirits, weaving voodoo and nature rites together. Across all these tribal borders, shaman stand as essential community elders, advisers, and fearsome front-line defenders.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Which races can become shaman?",a="Orcs, tauren, and trolls are the Horde's prominent shamanic peoples. Their traditions draw on elemental spirits, ancestors, and, among trolls, the loa.",al={"who can be shaman","shaman races","can alliance be shaman"},it="people"},{q="How do shaman get their magic?",a="Shaman derive their power through communion with the elemental spirits of earth, air, fire, and water, as well as the spirits of their ancestors. Rather than commanding these energies by force, a shaman asks for their aid and balances their chaotic nature, often anchoring these connections through ritual totems.",al={"how does shaman magic work","shaman source of power","where do totems come from"},it="power"},{q="Why did the orcs lose their shaman powers before the First War?",a="The demon lord Kil'jaeden impersonated ancestral spirits to trick the orc shamans into attacking the peaceful draenei. Offended by the senseless slaughter, the elemental spirits of Draenor severed ties and refused to answer calls. Left defenseless, many desperate shaman turned to fel magic and became warlocks.",al={"why did orc shaman become warlocks","draenor shaman fall","kiljaeden trick shaman"},it="history"},{q="Who brought shamanism back to the orcs?",a="Thrall, the son of Durotan, sought out the Frostwolf clan in the Alterac Mountains. Under the tutelage of the elder shaman Drek'Thar, Thrall learned the ancient spiritual bonds, breaking the demonic lethargy that plagued his people and reinstating shamanism as the heart of the New Horde.",al={"who revived shamanism","thrall and drek'thar shaman","shaman return"},it="history"},{q="What is the Earthen Ring?",a="The Earthen Ring is an ancient organization of shaman founded centuries ago by the tauren. Its practitioners dedicate themselves to maintaining harmony among the volatile elemental spirits and healing regions afflicted by spiritual unrest.",al={"earthen ring lore","who are earthen ring","shaman faction"},it="allies"},{q="How does a shaman differ from a druid?",a="Druids commune with the Emerald Dream, plant life, and animal spirits to safeguard the living cycle of nature. Shaman instead moderate the inanimate, primordial elements of the physical world—earth, fire, water, and wind—alongside the ancestral spirits of the dead.",al={"druid vs shaman","difference between druid and shaman","shaman or druid"},it="identity"},{q="What is a shaman in lore?",a="Shaman are spiritual guides and mortal mediators who commune directly with the natural elements: earth, air, fire, and water. Rather than serving as masters of the divine, they act as moderators between primal, chaotic elemental forces that are in unending conflict. In tribal societies like those of the orcs, tauren, and trolls, shaman serve as revered advisors, seers, and protectors. In battle, they call upon these elemental energies to unleash lightning, mend wounds with cleansing water, or empower allies through carved totems.",al={"what is a shaman","shaman lore","who are shamans"},it="identity",add=true},{q="What does it mean to become a shaman?",a="Walking the path of the shaman means learning to listen to the living world and earning the respect of its spirits. You do not command elemental forces through arcane mastery or sheer will; you commune with them, bringing balance to their volatile fury. As an initiate, you craft and carry totems that focus the elements of earth, fire, water, and air. Whether fighting on the front lines with weapon-imbued elemental force, healing allies through ancestral guidance, or calling down storms, your strength relies entirely upon keeping harmony with the primal spirits.",al={"becoming a shaman","why become a shaman","path of the shaman"},it="for_you",add=true}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Your powers are granted through mutual respect with fickle elemental spirits and ancestors; maintaining balance is both your martial duty and your spiritual burden.",["faction:Horde"]="Shamanism represents the spiritual renaissance of the Horde, providing a path of honor and nature worship that replaced demonic corruption.",["race:Tauren"]="Tauren have practiced shamanism for generations through the Earthen Ring, serving as patient spiritual anchors for the younger cultures of the Horde.",["race:Orc"]="Reclaiming the path of the shaman allowed the orcs to overcome the lethargy of the internment camps and reclaim their ancestral heritage.",["race:Troll"]="Darkspear shaman channel the primal elements through their tribal traditions, bridging the reverence of nature spirits and ancient loa.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance forces view shaman with wariness, encountering them on the battlefield as fierce warriors whose totems command lightning, tremors, and sudden healing."},kw={"air","ancestors","drek'thar","earth","earthen ring","elements","fire","primal","shaman","thrall","totem","water"},rel={"npc:thrall","topic:tauren","topic:darkspear-tribe","topic:new-horde","topic:horde","topic:druid","topic:burning-legion","zone:durotar"},src={"Shaman"}}
E["topic:shan-do"] = {t="topic",n="Shan'do",z="general",s="Shan'do is a revered Darnassian honorific meaning \"honored teacher\" or \"honored instructor.\" Traditionally used within night elven society and druidic circles, it denotes deep reverence toward a mentor who guides another along the path of wisdom and natural magic. Its direct counterpart is thero'shan, meaning \"honored student.\"",h="Shan'do is a revered Darnassian honorific meaning \"honored teacher\" or \"honored instructor.\" Traditionally...",sec={{t="A Title of Deep Reverence",b="In the Darnassian language, shan'do translates directly to \"honored teacher\" or \"honored instructor.\" The prefix \"shan\" carries the meaning of honor, while the suffix \"do\" denotes authority, seniority, or mastery—a linguistic root shared with words like minn'do (\"mother\"). It is far more than an occupational label: addressing an elder or mentor as shan'do expresses solemn humility, filial devotion, and enduring respect for their guidance.",sp=0},{t="The Lineage of the Druids",b="The title is most famously intertwined with the history of the Cenarion Circle. The demigod Cenarius was the original shan'do to Malfurion Stormrage, instructing him in the ways of nature before the War of the Ancients. In turn, Malfurion became shan'do to generations of mortal druids, to the point where \"Shan'do Stormrage\" is his standard styling among night elves, forest keepers, and reverent allies across Kalimdor.",sp=0},{t="Mentors Beyond the Circle",b="While most common among Cenarion druids—such as disciples addressing Archdruid Naralex or the slumbering Malfurion—the honorific is fundamentally Darnassian, not exclusively religious. During the War of the Ancients, Illidan Stormrage briefly addressed the human sorcerer Rhonin as shan'do while learning from his peculiar arcane techniques. The title represents the bond of transmission between master and pupil, irrespective of the school of thought.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What does the word shan'do actually mean?",a="In Darnassian, shan'do translates to \"honored teacher\" or \"honored instructor.\" It is used to show deep deference and gratitude toward a mentor.",al={"shando meaning","what does shando translate to","shando translation","darnassian shando"},it="name"},{q="What is the opposite or student version of shan'do?",a="The complementary honorific is thero'shan, which translates to \"honored student.\" Mentors address their apprentices as thero'shan to acknowledge their devotion and discipline.",al={"opposite of shando","student word in darnassian","theroshan meaning","what is thero'shan"}},{q="Who is most commonly called Shan'do?",a="Archdruid Malfurion Stormrage is the figure most commonly hailed as Shan'do by the night elves and the Cenarion Circle. Cenarius was also addressed as shan'do by his original pupils, and other prominent leaders such as Naralex carry the honorific from their followers.",al={"who is shando","shando stormrage","who has the title shando"},it="fame"},{q="Is the title shan'do only used by druids?",a="No. While it is deeply rooted in Cenarion druidic tradition, shan'do is a Darnassian linguistic courtesy. Any master of craft, magic, or wisdom can theoretically be addressed as shan'do by their student; for instance, Illidan Stormrage once called the human wizard Rhonin shan'do while studying his spellcraft.",al={"can non druids be shando","is shando only for druids","shando outside druidism"}},{q="What is the meaning and role of a Shan'do?",a="Shan'do is an esteemed Darnassian courtesy title translating to \"honored teacher\" or \"honored instructor.\" It is bestowed upon revered mentors within night elven culture, most notably among druids who guide apprentices in the ways of nature. The title conveys deep reverence, placing the teacher in a position of sacred guidance and elder authority over their thero'shan, or honored student.",al={"what is shando","who is shando","shan'do meaning"},it="identity",add=true},{q="What is the history of the title Shan'do?",a="The honorific dates back millennia to the earliest teachings of nature and arcane magic. During the ancient era before the Great Sundering, the demigod Cenarius served as shan'do to Malfurion Stormrage, instructing him as the first mortal druid. Even Illidan Stormrage once addressed the temporal-displaced mage Rhonin as shan'do when learning the subtleties of sorcery. Over the ages, it has endured as the quintessential Darnassian honorific for masters of profound spiritual and magical disciplines.",al={"history of shando","where did shando come from","origin of shando"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="As part of your native tongue, shan'do is among the most solemn courtesies you can offer an elder or teacher, carrying millennia of cultural weight.",["class:Druid"]="Every druid traces their teachings through a chain of mentors back to Shan'do Stormrage and Cenarius himself; uttering the title honors that unbroken chain.",["race:Tauren"]="Tauren druids studying alongside the night elves in Moonglade recognize the title, using it respectfully when addressing mentors like Malfurion Stormrage."},kw={"cenarius","darnassian","druid","honored teacher","malfurion","shan'do","shando","thero'shan"},rel={"npc:malfurion-stormrage","topic:cenarius","topic:druid","npc:naralex","topic:war-of-the-ancients","npc:remulos","quest:952","quest:963"},src={"Shan'do"}}
E["topic:skyborne"] = {t="topic",n="Skyborne",z="general",s="The Skyborne, or shen'dorei, are an offshoot of high elves descended from rebels who fled Eldre'Thalas after opposing Prince Tortheldrin's demonic pacts. Striking a desperate bargain with wind spirits, their ancestors escaped Azeroth to dwell atop the floating Zephras Isle within Skywall. Today, with their elemental benefactors abruptly gone, their floating sanctuary faces internal fractures and physical collapse.",h="The Skyborne, or shen'dorei, are an offshoot of high elves descended from rebels who fled Eldre'Thalas after...",sec={{t="Flight from Eldre'Thalas",b="Following the Sundering, the Shen'dralar of Eldre'Thalas languished without the Well of Eternity. Prince Tortheldrin devised a dark salvation: summon the demon Immol'thar and siphon its power. Horrified nobles and citizens resisted, fearing another Legion invasion. In response, Tortheldrin executed a bloody purge of dozens of houses. From the surviving shadows rose a resistance spearheaded by a masked figure known only as the Shal'nan ('the Veiled One'). When a desperate rebel raid on Tortheldrin's ritual sanctum descended into catastrophic chaotic magic, the Shal'nan rallied the survivors and fled west to the port of Eldre'narr, taking to the seas pursued by the Prince's fleet.",sp=0},{t="Sanctuary in the Sky",b="Cornered on the open ocean, the exiles forged a pact with primordial wind spirits. These beings lifted Zephras Isle straight into the skies, anchoring it safely within Skywall—the Elemental Plane of Air. Relieved of absolute rule, the Shal'nan stepped down during the consecration of the Shrine of the Four Winds, placing governance in the hands of an elected council. Over millennia in this alien realm, the night elf exiles evolved into fair, slender high elves. In recent generations, an elemental influence known as the Sky-Touched Blessing has emerged, causing a growing number of children to be born with pale blue skin.",sp=0},{t="A Shattering Calm",b="For centuries, life in Skywall flourished in isolation. The wind spirits strictly forbade mention of the Windlord, Al'Akir, brushing off questions with eerie unease. A few centuries ago, catastrophe struck: the elemental benefactors vanished without explanation, and the Shal'nan disappeared along with them. Deprived of their caretakers, the arcane pylons anchoring Zephras Isle began failing. Outlying landmasses broke away, refugees flooded what remained, and desperation fueled the rise of bandits and the Al'Aketh—a fanatical cult dedicated to Al'Akir. Today, society fractures between the High Order, who seek salvation in reclaiming ancestral arcane magic, and the Windshapers, who cling to elemental rites.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who are the Skyborne?",a="The Skyborne, or shen'dorei ('the hidden people'), are high elves descended from ancient night elves who rebelled against Prince Tortheldrin in Eldre'Thalas. To escape his vengeful fleet, they struck a pact with wind spirits, who relocated their island, Zephras Isle, into the Elemental Plane of Air.",al={"what are skyborne","who are shen'dorei","shendorei","skyborne lore"},it="identity"},{q="Why do some Skyborne have blue skin?",a="Millennia in the Elemental Plane of Air have subtly reshaped their biology. A recent phenomenon known as the Sky-Touched Blessing causes an increasing proportion of children—now roughly three out of ten—to be born with blue skin. Scholars predict their ancestral night elf traits will soon be entirely supplanted.",al={"sky-touched blessing","why are they blue","blue elves","sky touched"},it="origin"},{q="Who was the Shal'nan?",a="The Shal'nan, or 'the Veiled One', was the masked leader who spearheaded the resistance against Prince Tortheldrin's demon summoning in Eldre'Thalas. After leading the survivors to safety in Skywall, he abdicated to establish a democratic council, serving his people until mysteriously vanishing centuries ago alongside their elemental benefactors.",al={"who is the veiled one","shalnan","the masked rebel","leader of skyborne"},it="leader"},{q="What is happening to Zephras Isle?",a="Zephras Isle is held aloft within Skywall by magical pylons attuned to elemental power. When the wind spirits who maintained them abruptly disappeared centuries ago, the anchoring pylons began to fail. Portions of the landmass have broken off into the void, stranding survivors and driving displaced elves to banditry.",al={"why is zephras isle crumbling","zephras falling","what happened to skywall island"},it="ruin"},{q="What are the High Order and the Windshapers?",a="They are the two primary cultural factions of the Skyborne. The High Order descends from Highborne magisters and seeks to rebuild their crumbling society through disciplined arcane mastery. The Windshapers trace their roots to ordinary night elf citizens and revere the elemental and spiritual customs cultivated during their time in Skywall.",al={"skyborne factions","high order vs windshapers","windshaper lore"},it="people"},{q="Who are the Al'Aketh?",a="The Al'Aketh are a desperate cult of Skyborne who worship Al'Akir the Windlord. While their ancestors' elemental benefactors feared and avoided speaking of Al'Akir, the collapse of Zephras Isle led homeless and disenfranchised refugees to turn to the elemental lord for salvation and power.",al={"alaketh cult","cult of al'akir","skyborne cult"},it="threat"},{q="Where do the Skyborne stand in the world today?",a="Their civilization faces slow collapse. Centuries ago, the wind spirits and the Shal'nan vanished, destabilizing the ancient pylons holding Zephras Isle together. With parts of their realm crumbling into ruin, communication cut off from other settled islands, and refugees flooding the isle, surviving Skyborne have fractured. The High Order and Windshapers vie over whether arcane heritage or elemental traditions will save them, while desperate exiles turn to banditry or join the Al'Akir-worshipping Al'Aketh cult.",al={"skyborne current situation","state of the skyborne","where are the skyborne now"},it="today",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The Skyborne share direct bloodlines with the ancient Shen'dralar and northern night elves, though millennia in Skywall have physically altered them into high elves with an increasing prevalence of elemental blue skin.",["class:Mage"]="The High Order among the Skyborne preserves ancient Highborne spellcraft brought out of Eldre'Thalas, seeking to salvage their crumbling island through rigorous arcane mastery.",["class:Shaman"]="The Windshapers represent an elven lineage deeply intertwined with the wind spirits of Skywall, relying on elemental communion rather than the arcane traditions of their Highborne forebears."},kw={"al'aketh","eldre'thalas","high order","shal'nan","shen'dorei","sky-touched","skyborne","skywall","windshapers","zephras isle"},rel={"zone:zephras","subzone:dire-maul","topic:highborne","topic:skywall","topic:elemental-plane","npc:ventaari-brightwish","quest:93552","zone:feralas"},src={"Skyborne"}}
E["topic:skywall"] = {t="topic",n="Skywall",z="general",s="Skywall is the vast, storm-swept domain of the air elementals within the Elemental Plane. Banished here by the titans in ancient days, the creatures of wind, cloud, and lightning dwell under the reign of Al'Akir the Windlord.",h="Skywall is the vast, storm-swept domain of the air elementals within the Elemental Plane.",sec={{t="A Prison in the Heavens",b="When the titans overthrew the Old Gods eons ago, they crafted the Elemental Plane as a secure dimensional prison to contain Azeroth's unruly elemental spirits. The lords of the elements fought fiercely amongst themselves, ultimately carving out distinct dominions. The realm of wind and storm became known as Skywall, ruled by Al'Akir the Windlord. While sealed away from the surface world, its tempests continue to swirl in eternal isolation.",sp=0},{t="The War of Elements",b="Banishment did not end the ancient animosity between the elemental rulers. Skywall has long waged border disputes against neighboring realms like the Firelands. One of the most infamous clashes was the Elemental Sundering, wherein Prince Thunderaan the Windseeker was ambushed and betrayed by Garr and Baron Geddon, the chief lieutenants of Ragnaros the Firelord. Ragnaros devoured the prince's essence and bound the fragments into a shattered talisman.",sp=0},{t="Isles of the Mist",b="Skywall is not composed solely of raging gales and djinn citadels. Among the cloudbanks drifts Zephras Isle, a sanctuary inhabited by the Shen'dorei. Mortals on Azeroth rarely pierce the veil into the domain of air, though reckless spellcasters and weapon-crafters across history have courted disaster attempting to siphon the living storms of the Windlord.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Skywall?",a="Skywall is the dominion of air elementals located within the dimensional Elemental Plane. Created by the titans to imprison the servants of the Old Gods, it is a realm formed entirely of endless skies, storms, clouds, and lightning.",al={"what is skywall","skywall lore","domain of air"},it="identity"},{q="Who rules Skywall?",a="Skywall is ruled by Al'Akir the Windlord, the supreme Elemental Lord of air. From within his tempestuous kingdom, he holds dominion over the djinn, cyclones, and living storms.",al={"who leads skywall","skywall boss","ruler of air"},it="leader"},{q="How was Skywall created?",a="After defeating the Old Gods in Azeroth's primordial past, the titan-forged fashioned the Elemental Plane to seal away the chaotic elementals. Within this dimensional prison, the air elementals seized their own vast territory, which became Skywall.",al={"origin of skywall","how was skywall made"},it="history"},{q="What happened to Prince Thunderaan?",a="During the Elemental Sundering, Prince Thunderaan was ambushed by Ragnaros the Firelord's lieutenants, Garr and Baron Geddon. Ragnaros consumed the air prince's essence and imprisoned what remained in a shattered talisman of elemental binding.",al={"thunderaan lore","what happened to thunderaan","windseeker fate"}},{q="Who lives in Skywall?",a="Skywall is populated by air elementals, powerful djinn lords, lightning revenants, and elemental storm drakes. It is also home to Zephras Isle, a sanctuary where the Shen'dorei dwell.",al={"skywall inhabitants","skywall denizens","who is in skywall"},it="residents"},{q="Can mortals reach Skywall?",a="Mortals cannot easily reach Skywall, as it exists in a separate dimensional prison. Historically, only powerful sorcerers manipulating elemental rifts or those seeking the isolated Shen'dorei on Zephras Isle have touched its skies.",al={"how to get to skywall","travel to skywall","skywall portal"},it="arrival"}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Skywall is the primordial source of the raw, violent winds and lightning that shamans revere and beseech when communing with the spirits of air.",["class:Mage"]="Conjurors and arcanists have historically sought to tap the boundless atmospheric energies of Skywall, though such attempts to siphon Al'Akir's tempests often end in ruin."},kw={"air elemental","al'akir","elemental plane","shen'dorei","skywall","stormwall","thunderaan","windlord","zephras isle"},rel={"topic:elemental-plane","npc:thunderaan","zone:zephras","npc:highlord-demitrian","topic:titan","topic:old-god","subzone:uldum"},src={"Skywall"}}
E["topic:steamwheedle-cartel"] = {t="topic",n="Steamwheedle Cartel",z="general",s="The Steamwheedle Cartel is the largest and most influential goblin enterprise operating across modern Azeroth. Fiercely neutral, the cartel governs bustling commercial hubs like Booty Bay, Ratchet, and Gadgetzan, serving Alliance and Horde patrons alike so long as their coin is genuine. Their sprawling operations encompass cross-continental shipping, alchemy, salvaging, and cutthroat mercenary contracting.",h="The Steamwheedle Cartel is the largest and most influential goblin enterprise operating across modern Azeroth.",sec={{t="A Profitable Neutrality",b="Originating from the subterranean goblin capital of Undermine, the Steamwheedle Cartel initially allied with the Old Horde during the Second War, supplying war machines, sappers, and naval engineering. After the dust settled, the cartel's leaders realized that picking sides cost more than it earned. Embracing absolute neutrality, they expanded across both continents, opening trade networks that welcome Alliance and Horde alike. Today, as open tensions simmer, Steamwheedle ports provide vital common ground where anyone with gold can purchase passage, bank resources, and hire local specialists.",sp=0},{t="Ports and Outposts",b="The cartel's influence rests on strategic ports and frontier towns. In Stranglethorn Vale, Baron Revilgaz presides over Booty Bay, a fortified harbor town carved into the cliffs alongside the Blackwater Raiders. Across the Great Sea, Chief Engineer Gazlowe directs Ratchet on the coast of the Barrens, running a deep-water ferry line directly back to Booty Bay. Deep within the deserts of Tanaris lies the boomtown of Gadgetzan, while smaller operations like Steamwheedle Port and remote outposts in Desolace, Azshara, and Stonetalon Mountains secure regional resources.",sp=0},{t="Rivals and Reavers",b="Steamwheedle trade is constantly imperiled by opportunistic cutthroats. In the Southern Seas, the Bloodsail Buccaneers continually menace Booty Bay's naval lanes, while Southsea Freebooters raid the coastlines near Ratchet. On land, the cartel frequently clashes with their corporate arch-rivals, the Venture Company, competing violently for timber rights, oil fields, and shredder technology across Stonetalon and the Barrens. To maintain order and enforce trade contracts, cartel barons routinely hire passing mercenaries to settle grievances with extreme prejudice.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Steamwheedle Cartel?",a="The Steamwheedle Cartel is a massive, neutral goblin trading conglomerate based out of Undermine. They operate several major independent towns across Azeroth—including Booty Bay, Ratchet, and Gadgetzan—offering banking, engineering, transport, and auction houses to all factions.",al={"who are steamwheedle","steamwheedle goblins","what is steamwheedle"},it="identity"},{q="Are the Steamwheedle goblins part of the Horde or Alliance?",a="Neither. The cartel maintains strict political neutrality. While they previously worked with the Old Horde during the Second War, they severed formal faction allegiances to maximize profit. Today, they happily trade with both the Alliance and the Horde, enforcing peace in their towns with armed bruisers.",al={"is steamwheedle horde","is steamwheedle cartel neutral","steamwheedle faction"},it="side"},{q="Which major cities belong to the Steamwheedle Cartel?",a="Their three primary urban centers are Booty Bay in southern Stranglethorn Vale, Ratchet on the eastern shore of the Barrens, and Gadgetzan in the desert of Tanaris. They also maintain smaller hubs like Steamwheedle Port and isolated trading outposts.",al={"steamwheedle towns","cartel cities","goblin towns list"},it="place"},{q="Who leads the Steamwheedle Cartel?",a="Trade Prince Steamwheedle led the cartel during the Second War. Current regional operations are overseen by bosses such as Baron Revilgaz in Booty Bay and Chief Engineer Gazlowe in Ratchet.",al={"steamwheedle leader","trade prince steamwheedle","who runs steamwheedle"},it="leader"},{q="Why are the Steamwheedle goblins enemies with the Venture Company?",a="The Venture Company is a rival goblin enterprise known for cutthroat strip-mining and deforestation. The two factions constantly compete for the same territorial concessions, industrial blueprints, and natural resources across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms.",al={"venture co vs steamwheedle","why hate venture co","venture company rivalry"},it="conflict"},{q="What happens if you fight inside a Steamwheedle port?",a="Steamwheedle towns employ heavily armed goblin bruisers who will swiftly attack anyone who breaks the peace, regardless of faction. Fighting other players or citizens within city limits results in swift retribution from the guards and a severe hit to your reputation with the cartel.",al={"guards in booty bay","pvp in ratchet","ratchet bruisers"},it="danger",gp=true},{q="Where did the Steamwheedle Cartel come from?",a="Originating from the subterranean metropolis of Undermine on Kezan, the Steamwheedle Cartel is the oldest and largest merchant syndicate in goblin society. Before the First War, they seized control of Blackwater Cove in Stranglethorn Vale, transforming it into the bustling haven of Booty Bay. During the Second War, Trade Prince Steamwheedle allied the cartel with Orgrim Doomhammer's Horde, providing zeppelins, alchemists, and engineers for profit. When that conflict ended, the cartel recognized that strict neutrality across Azeroth offered vastly superior trade opportunities, prompting them to open neutral ports and trading outposts spanning both continents.",al={"steamwheedle history","origin of steamwheedle cartel","steamwheedle background"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why are the Steamwheedle Cartel found in so many distant lands?",a="The Steamwheedle Cartel pursues profit wherever trade routes, rare resources, or regional turmoil emerge. Establishing settlements like Booty Bay in Stranglethorn Vale, Ratchet along the Barrens coast, and Gadgetzan in Tanaris grants them dominion over maritime passage and frontier commerce between the continents. Their independent outposts and ventures allow them to trade openly with both the Alliance and the Horde while harvesting minerals, brokering salvage, and bankrolling lucrative contracts regardless of local peril.",al={"why is steamwheedle everywhere","why are goblins in ratchet and booty bay","steamwheedle locations why"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["profession:Engineering"]="Cartel hubs like Ratchet and Gadgetzan are premier gathering spots for Goblin Engineering schematics, shredder parts, and experimental mechanical components.",["faction:Alliance"]="For Alliance travelers, Steamwheedle ferry routes and desert outposts provide indispensable waystations and flight paths across remote, Horde-heavy wilderness.",["faction:Horde"]="While Gazlowe and his crew maintain historic working relationships with the orcs of Durotar, the cartel still demands cold hard coin for their services and neutrality.",["class:Rogue"]="The lawless fringes of Booty Bay and Ratchet offer bountiful employment for thieves, lockpicks, and covert contractors willing to fleece pirates and rivals alike."},kw={"booty bay","bruisers","cartel","gadgetzan","gazlowe","goblins","ratchet","revilgaz","steamwheedle","undermine"},rel={"subzone:ratchet","subzone:booty-bay","subzone:gadgetzan","subzone:steamwheedle-port","quest:1040","quest:887","quest:764","zone:barrens"},src={"Steamwheedle Cartel"}}
E["topic:stonefield-family"] = {t="topic",n="Stonefield family",z="general",s="The Stonefield family is a lineage of human farmers residing at the Stonefield Farm in western Elwynn Forest. Known for their long-standing rural roots, they tend their homestead while caught up in an ongoing rivalry with their neighboring peers, the Maclures. Despite the hostility between the two households, younger generations find themselves entangled across the family divide.",h="The Stonefield family is a lineage of human farmers residing at the Stonefield Farm in western Elwynn Forest.",sec={{t="Life on the Homestead",b="The Stonefields are quintessential Elwynn agrarian stock, working the soil south of the road leading to Westfall. The homestead revolves around Gramma Mildred Stonefield, Ma Stonefield, and young Tommy Joe Stonefield. Daily life on the farm involves tending crops and managing livestock while fending off local pests and dealing with nearby kobold incursions that plague the region's mines and borders.",sp=0},{t="The Maclure Feud",b="Separated only by a thin strip of woodland and pasture, the Stonefields and their neighbors, the Maclures, maintain a bitter and protracted grudge. Little love is lost between the older generations of both houses, with petty squabbles and territorial animosity coloring every interaction. Despite this fierce neighborhood dispute, a secret romance has blossomed between young Tommy Joe Stonefield and Maybell Maclure, forcing sympathetic travelers to act as discreet go-betweens.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where does the Stonefield family live?",a="The Stonefields reside at the Stonefield Farm, located in southwestern Elwynn Forest, just south of the main road heading toward the Westfall border.",al={"stonefield farm location","where are the stonefields","stonefield family location"},it="place"},{q="Who are the known members of the Stonefield family?",a="Prominent household members include matriarch Gramma Mildred Stonefield, Ma Stonefield, Homer Stonefield, and the young Tommy Joe Stonefield. Other kin mentioned in family history include Bernice Stonefield and the late Uncle Stonefield.",al={"who is in the stonefield family","stonefield members","stonefield family tree"},it="people"},{q="Why do the Stonefields hate the Maclures?",a="The Stonefields and the Maclures are entangled in a classic rural family feud over old territorial grudges and neighborhood stubbornness, with both households stubbornly refusing to reconcile their grievances.",al={"stonefield maclure feud","why are stonefields fighting maclures","maclure rivalry"},it="conflict"},{q="How does the feud affect Tommy Joe Stonefield?",a="Tommy Joe Stonefield is secretly in love with Maybell Maclure from the rival farmstead. Because of the open hostility between their kin, the pair must rely on discreet intermediaries to exchange letters and tokens of affection.",al={"tommy joe maclure","tommy joe romance","who is tommy joe in love with"},it="ties"},{q="Is the family aligned with Stormwind?",a="Yes. As human residents of Elwynn Forest, they fall under the protection of the Kingdom of Stormwind and the Alliance, although they primarily concern themselves with the daily survival of their farm and regional threats.",al={"are stonefields alliance","stonefield loyalty"},it="side"},{q="What threats does the Stonefield family face?",a="Aside from their friction with the Maclure household, the Stonefields must deal with encroaching kobolds vermin, roving wildlife, and the general lack of Stormwind guards patrolling rural farmlands.",al={"stonefield dangers","problems at stonefield farm"},it="threat"},{q="Who are the Stonefields?",a="The Stonefields are an established human farming family residing in Elwynn Forest. Operating out of the Stonefield Farm southwest of Goldshire, they work the land while contending with surrounding wilderness threats and an ongoing, bitter feud with their neighbors, the Maclures.",al={"stonefield family lore","who are stonefield","stonefields"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="For humans of Stormwind, the Stonefields reflect the resilient working-class backbone providing grain and produce to feed the kingdom.",["faction:Alliance"]="Alliance adventurers passing toward Westfall often handle the mundane chores and personal squabbles of the Stonefields to keep Elwynn's breadbasket stable."},kw={"elwynn forest","gramma stonefield","maclure","mildred stonefield","stonefield","stonefield family","stonefield farm","stonefields","tommy joe"},rel={"subzone:stonefield-farm","npc:tommy-joe-stonefield","npc:gramma-stonefield","npc:ma-stonefield","npc:maybell-maclure","zone:elwynn","topic:human","topic:alliance"},src={"Stonefield family"}}
E["topic:stonemasons-guild"] = {t="topic",n="Stonemasons Guild",z="general",s="The Stonemasons Guild was the collective of master builders and artisans responsible for reconstructing Stormwind City and Nethergarde Keep after the Second War. Following a bitter dispute over payment with the House of Nobles that ended in deadly riots, the guild was forcibly dissolved, driving its members into exile and giving rise to the Defias Brotherhood.",h="The Stonemasons Guild was the collective of master builders and artisans responsible for reconstructing...",sec={{t="Architects of the Realm",b="Following the devastation of the First and Second Wars, the Alliance of Lordaeron commissioned the Stonemasons Guild to erect Nethergarde Keep in the Blasted Lands to monitor the shattered Dark Portal. Led by elected Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef, the artisans demonstrated a mastery of masonry that rivaled the Royal Stonecutters Union of the dwarves. Impressed by their labor, the House of Nobles hired the guild to embark upon the monumental task of rebuilding Stormwind City from ruins, trusting them to restore the capital to its ancient grandeur.",sp=0},{t="Toil and Betrayal",b="For years, VanCleef and an army of craftsmen toiled through Elwynn, raising the soaring spires of Stormwind Keep and the massive arches of the Cathedral of Light. However, royal finances collapsed under heavy military debts incurred in southern campaigns. Influenced by aristocratic politics, the House of Nobles refused to pay the guild their agreed salaries and fees. When VanCleef petitioned for restitution, the nobles answered by unilaterally disbanding the guild, sparking immense fury among the laborers who had bled to rebuild the city.",sp=0},{t="Blood in the Streets",b="The wage dispute erupted into violent unrest throughout Stormwind. During a desperate effort to broker peace, Queen Tiffin Wrynn was struck and killed by a thrown projectile, sending King Varian into profound grief and vengeance. The Crown cracked down ruthlessly, capturing key conspirators like VanCleef's lieutenant, Bazil Thredd, while VanCleef and hundreds of ruined stonemasons fled into the countryside of Westfall. Exiled and branded as traitors, the desperate artisans transformed their trade union into the militant Defias Brotherhood.",sp=1},{t="The Hidden Hand",b="The standoff between the nobility and the builders was not merely a matter of bankrupt coffers. Behind the scenes, the royal court advisor Lady Katrana Prestor actively fanned the embers of discord. She convinced the aristocracy that the Stonemasons' bills were extortionate and their work shoddy, while simultaneously provoking the craftsmen into outright rebellion. By paralyzing Stormwind with internal strife and cutting off supplies to Westfall, the black dragon Onyxia successfully fractured the kingdom from within.",sp=2}},faq={{q="What was the Stonemasons Guild?",a="The Stonemasons Guild was an association of human artisans, architects, and stonecutters led by Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef. They were renowned for rebuilding Stormwind City and Nethergarde Keep after the Second War.",al={"who were the stonemasons","stonemasons guild lore","what did the stonemasons do"},it="identity"},{q="Why did the Stonemasons Guild become the Defias Brotherhood?",a="When the rebuilding of Stormwind was complete, the House of Nobles refused to pay the guild's wages due to crippling debt and court intrigue. The resulting protests turned into deadly riots, and the exiled workers organized the Defias Brotherhood to claim their stolen wealth by force.",al={"why defias mad","why did stonemasons turn evil","origin of defias brotherhood"},it="conflict"},{q="Who was the leader of the Stonemasons Guild?",a="Edwin VanCleef served as the elected Guildmaster. Under his direction, the guild earned fame for its incredible stonework before he was exiled and founded the Defias Brotherhood.",al={"stonemasons guildmaster","vancleef guild","who led the stonemasons"},it="leader"},{q="What notable structures did the Stonemasons build?",a="The guild built Nethergarde Keep at the edge of the Blasted Lands to oversee the Dark Portal, and entirely rebuilt Stormwind City after the First War, including the Cathedral of Light and Stormwind Keep.",al={"what did stonemasons build","did stonemasons build stormwind","nethergarde keep builders"},it="fame"},{q="Did all the Stonemasons join the Defias Brotherhood?",a="Not all joined the rebellion. Notably, master craftsman Baros Alexston broke ties with Edwin VanCleef and stayed in Stormwind to accept the post of City Architect, earning the enduring hatred of his former guildmates.",al={"did all stonemasons become bandits","baros alexston stonemason","did any stonemasons stay in stormwind"},it="people"},{q="How did Queen Tiffin Wrynn die?",a="Queen Tiffin was one of the few nobles sympathetic to the workers and attempted to calm the gathering crowds during the wage riots. She was struck and killed instantly by a projectile thrown from the mob, forever ending any chance of peaceful reconciliation.",al={"tiffin wrynn death","how did varian's wife die","queen tiffin stone riot"}},{q="Who was Bazil Thredd in the Stonemasons Guild?",a="Bazil Thredd was Edwin VanCleef's trusted assistant and chief lieutenant during their time in the guild. Unlike VanCleef, Thredd was apprehended during the aftermath of the riots and remains locked inside the Stormwind Stockade.",al={"bazil thredd backstory","thredd stonemason","vancleef lieutenant"},it="people"},{q="What is the history of the Stonemasons Guild?",a="Following the Second War, the Stonemasons Guild first constructed Nethergarde Keep in the Blasted Lands before being commissioned by the House of Nobles to rebuild Stormwind City from the ruins left by the Orcish Horde. Composed of skilled engineers and artisans, they spent years restoring the kingdom to glory, shaping landmarks like the Cathedral of Light and Stormwind Keep. However, rising royal debts and political maneuvering led the nobility to refuse payment, culminating in riots, Queen Tiffin's death, and the guild's exile into Westfall.",al={"stonemasons history","stonemasons background","origin of stonemasons"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why are the Stonemasons called that?",a="They were named for their craft as professional stonecarvers, builders, and architects. Functioning as a chartered trade union akin to Ironforge's Royal Stonecutters Union, the guild brought together masons and artisans whose masterwork masonry was capable of constructing massive stone fortresses, cathedrals, and city ramparts.",al={"why called stonemasons","stonemasons guild name meaning"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="The beautiful white stone of Stormwind stands as a bitter testament to the kingdom's greatest internal tragedy, reminding human travelers of the cost of aristocratic hubris.",["race:Dwarf"]="Dwarven masons deeply respected the Stonemasons Guild, noting that their precision and towering ramparts rivaled Ironforge's own Royal Stonecutters Union.",["class:Rogue"]="The transformation of master tradesmen into deadly highwaymen, lockpicks, and assassins proves just how far VanCleef's tactical cunning reached.",["faction:Alliance"]="The collapse of the guild left the breadbasket of Westfall defenseless and overrun by outlaws, straining the Alliance's supply lines right outside the capital."},kw={"alexston","defias","nethergarde","nobles","stonemasons","stormwind","thredd","tiffin","vancleef"},rel={"topic:defias-brotherhood","npc:edwin-vancleef","topic:house-of-nobles","zone:stormwind","npc:baros-alexston","subzone:nethergarde-keep","npc:onyxia","quest:389"},src={"Stonemasons Guild"}}
E["topic:stormwind-army"] = {t="topic",n="Stormwind Army",z="general",s="The Stormwind Army is the standing military force charged with defending the Kingdom of Stormwind and preserving the security of the Grand Alliance in the south. Though legendary for its resilient knights and marshals, the army is currently crippled by royal absence, political infighting, and crippling troop shortages across the provinces.",h="The Stormwind Army is the standing military force charged with defending the Kingdom of Stormwind and...",sec={{t="A Legacy of Iron and Blood",b="The army traces its martial lineage through centuries of brutal conflict, from early frontier clashes against gnolls to the desperate defense against the Gurubashi trolls. In the First War, under King Llane Wrynn I and Sir Anduin Lothar, the Knights of Stormwind mounted legendary ambushes against the Orcish Horde, yet fell when the capital burned. Surviving remnants evacuated to Lordaeron, spearheading the formation of the Alliance of Lordaeron and fighting through the Second War. After the Dark Portal's fall, General Turalyon helped reform and train a renewed Stormwind military alongside a restored kingdom.",sp=0},{t="Structure and Ranks",b="Headquartered within Stormwind Keep, the military relies on dedicated regional garrisons overseen by the Stormwind Marshals. Within the stone walls of the capital, the Stormwind City Guard maintains civil order, while the elite Royal Guard attends the boy-king Anduin Wrynn and Highlord Bolvar Fordragon. Beyond the gates, the army deploys outposts to patrol Elwynn Forest, keep order along trade roads, and man defensive ramparts as far as the Redridge Mountains, assisted on the water by the Stormwind Fleet.",sp=0},{t="A Thin Blue Line",b="Despite its proud traditions, the modern army is stretched beyond its breaking point. Debts incurred by the House of Nobles to expand military reach into Stranglethorn fueled the bitter rift with the Stonemasons Guild, culminating in civil riots, Queen Tiffin's death, and the birth of the Defias Brotherhood. With King Varian missing, the House of Nobles has routinely withheld troops, funding, and supplies from outlying settlements like Westfall, Lakeshire, and Duskwood, forcing local marshals to deputize wandering adventurers to carry the realm's burdens.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why are troops missing from towns like Lakeshire and Sentinel Hill?",a="With King Varian Wrynn missing, the House of Nobles holds political sway in the capital and refuses to dispatch regular regiments or funding to the provinces. Towns like Sentinel Hill and Lakeshire have been left to assemble ragtag militias or hire mercenaries to defend themselves against bandits, gnolls, and orcs.",al={"where is the stormwind army","why no soldiers in westfall","why won't stormwind help"},it="motive"},{q="What is the difference between the Stormwind Army and the Stormwind Guard?",a="The Stormwind Army encompasses the broader national military, including frontier marshals, garrisons, and expeditionary knights across the kingdom. The Stormwind Guard is the specific branch dedicated to enforcing law, patrolling the streets, and defending the gates within Stormwind City and surrounding rural hubs.",al={"guard vs army","who are stormwind guards","stormwind military branches"}},{q="Who commands the Stormwind Army right now?",a="In the absence of the missing King Varian Wrynn, military authority officially rests with the boy-king Anduin Wrynn, guided directly by Regent Highlord Bolvar Fordragon. However, their strategic decisions are constantly contested and undercut by political machinations in the House of Nobles.",al={"who leads stormwind army","stormwind army commander","supreme commander stormwind"},it="leader"},{q="Why do marshals give out deputy badges to adventurers?",a="Because the Stormwind Army is critically short-staffed and denied reinforcements from the capital, regional marshals like McBride in Northshire and Dughan in Goldshire grant acting deputy status to competent outsiders so they can legally eliminate outlaws and defend borders.",al={"deputy status","why deputize players","deputy stormwind"},it="purpose"},{q="What happened to the army during the First War?",a="Led by Sir Anduin Lothar, the army held the Orcish Horde at bay with disciplined cavalry strikes, but was overwhelmed when King Llane was assassinated. Lothar gathered the surviving soldiers and civilians, fleeing across the Great Sea to Lordaeron, where those veterans formed the core of the original Alliance forces.",al={"first war stormwind army","did stormwind army lose","lothar stormwind army"},it="history"},{q="Where does the army currently maintain active garrisons?",a="The army retains formal garrisons throughout Elwynn Forest, including Northshire, Goldshire, and Westbrook Garrison. Westfall and Duskwood rely on local militias, while soldiers remain in Lakeshire after losing Stonewatch Keep and await reinforcements.",al={"stormwind garrisons","where is army stationed","stormwind troop locations"},it="place"},{q="What is the Stormwind Army?",a="The Stormwind Army is the primary military force of the human Kingdom of Stormwind and a core pillar of the Grand Alliance. Based in Stormwind City, the army includes foot soldiers, heavily armored knights, and officers known as marshals. In addition to defending the realm's core territories across southern Azeroth, its commanders oversee provincial garrisons and commission local militias and adventuring deputies to maintain order.",al={"what is stormwind army","who are stormwind army","stormwind military"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Who is the Stormwind Army allied with?",a="The army serves the Crown of Stormwind, upholding the authority of the House of Wrynn and the ruling council. Beyond the kingdom's borders, it is formally bound to the Grand Alliance, standing in common defense alongside Ironforge, the gnomes of Gnomeregan, and the night elves of Kalimdor. Domestically, it collaborates with the Stormwind Fleet and the capital's City Guard.",al={"stormwind army allies","who does the army serve","who do they work for"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="For human warriors, the Knights of Stormwind and the infantry of Lothar represent the gold standard of disciplined martial service, though many struggle with the nobility's abandonment of the frontier.",["class:Paladin"]="The army's heavy cavalry and leadership work closely with the Order of the Silver Hand and Highlord Bolvar Fordragon to bring the Light's justice to lawless lands.",["faction:Alliance"]="With Lordaeron shattered by the Scourge, Stormwind's army serves as the vital defensive backbone of the Grand Alliance in the Eastern Kingdoms.",["race:Human"]="To the citizenry of Stormwind, the royal army is both an enduring symbol of survival against impossible odds and a frustrating reminder of bureaucratic neglect in distant hamlets."},kw={"anduin lothar","bolvar fordragon","defias","deputy","garrison","knights of stormwind","marshals","stormwind army","stormwind guard"},rel={"topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:stormwind-guard","topic:house-of-nobles","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:varian-wrynn","zone:elwynn","npc:marshal-mcbride","quest:18"},src={"Stormwind Army"}}
E["topic:stormwind-guard"] = {t="topic",n="Stormwind Guard",z="general",s="The Stormwind Guard comprises the regional garrisons and sentries tasked with enforcing the kingdom's laws and defending its surrounding territories. Stretched perilously thin by political gridlock and distant military campaigns, its scattered marshals struggle to hold back gnolls, orcs, and bandits across Elwynn and Redridge.",h="The Stormwind Guard comprises the regional garrisons and sentries tasked with enforcing the kingdom's laws...",sec={{t="Shield of the Crown",b="Operating as a vital branch of the Stormwind Army, the Stormwind Guard patrols the highways, crossroads, and stone watchtowers that bind the kingdom together. In outlying settlements, governance and defense fall to appointed marshals—such as Marshal McBride in Northshire Valley, Marshal Dughan in Goldshire, and Marshal Marris in Lakeshire. These officers muster recruits and maintain order, relying on trusty brown horses known for stamina to cover ground between fortified outposts like Westbrook Garrison.",sp=0},{t="A Border Stretched Thin",b="Following the Third War, the Guard successfully kept Elwynn Forest safe and quiet, but recent years have brought crisis. With King Varian Wrynn vanished and the kingdom's standing army tied down abroad, regional garrisons have received little aid. At Westbrook Garrison, Deputy Rainer can barely hold the western border against Riverpaw gnolls and Defias thieves, forcing local commanders to outsource crucial security tasks and bounties to visiting adventurers.",sp=0},{t="The Abandoned Territories",b="The Guard's true crisis stems from decisions made within the capital's royal court. Under the influence of Lady Katrana Prestor, the kingdom deemed Westfall too costly to garrison, pulling its troops out entirely and allowing Edwin VanCleef's Defias Brotherhood to seize the region. Duskwood suffered the same fate; its soldiers were withdrawn to Darkshire's despair, forcing the abandoned locals to establish the ragtag Night Watch for self-defense.",sp=1},{t="The Siege in the East",b="While Duskwood and Westfall were abandoned, the crown kept a tenuous military presence in the Redridge Mountains. Even so, the guard's position is desperate. Overwhelmed by Shadowhide gnolls and Blackrock orcs descending from the Burning Steppes, soldiers were forced to cede Stonewatch Keep. In Lakeshire, blacksmith Verner Osgood burns through iron merely keeping guards outfitted, while exhausted sentries scan the road waiting for a 2nd Battalion that never arrives.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the difference between the Stormwind Guard and the City Guard?",a="While the Stormwind City Guard polices the capital inside its fortified walls, the Stormwind Guard—often organized as regional garrisons—is responsible for defending the crown's outer territories, patrolling the roads, and holding watchtowers across Elwynn Forest and Redridge.",al={"stormwind guard vs city guard","are they the same guards","city guard"}},{q="Why aren't there any Stormwind Guards in Westfall?",a="Influenced by Lady Katrana Prestor's counsel regarding state expenses, the royal court decided the upkeep was too burdensome and withdrew all official garrisons. This complete absence of military protection allowed Edwin VanCleef's Defias Brotherhood to rapidly overrun the territory's farms and roads.",al={"why did guards leave westfall","westfall army","where are westfall guards"}},{q="Why did the guard leave Duskwood?",a="Much like Westfall, Duskwood saw its royal garrisons recalled back toward the capital as resources dried up. Stripped of official crown protection amid rising dark magic and horrors, the remaining townsfolk of Darkshire had to form their own militia, the Night Watch, to survive.",al={"duskwood guards","why did stormwind abandon darkshire","darkshire garrison"}},{q="Who leads the local garrisons?",a="Local defense is governed by appointed regional marshals. Marshal McBride oversees Northshire Valley, Marshal Dughan commands the forces centered in Goldshire, and Marshal Marris directs the desperate defense of Lakeshire in the Redridge Mountains.",al={"who is in charge","guard leaders","marshals"},it="leader"},{q="Why are the guards in Lakeshire in such bad shape?",a="The Redridge garrison was routed from Stonewatch Keep by invading Blackrock orcs and local gnolls. Trapped in Lakeshire, they suffer from acute equipment shortages—blacksmith Verner Osgood consumes nearly all available iron just rearming sentries—and are waiting for promised reinforcements that royal leaders have delayed.",al={"redridge guards losing","what happened at stonewatch","lakeshire garrison trouble"}},{q="Who is Marshal Haggard?",a="Marshal Haggard is a venerable retired commander who once led the Stormwind Guard during calmer days, when off-duty soldiers could still travel south to drink together at the Scarlet Raven Tavern in Darkshire.",al={"marshal haggard lore","retired marshal","old guard marshal"}},{q="What is the Stormwind Guard?",a="The Stormwind Guard, often known as the Stormwind Garrisons, is the territorial military branch of the Stormwind Army. Unlike the Stormwind City Guard who protect the capital's streets, these guards patrol the kingdom's outer territories—manning watchtowers, keeping roads secure, and enforcing royal law across Elwynn Forest and the Redridge Mountains.",al={"who are stormwind guards","stormwind garrison","what are stormwind guards"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why are the Stormwind Guard stationed where they are?",a="They are stationed at outposts and scout towers every few miles along main roads to protect settlements and travelers from threats like gnolls, kobolds, and bandits. While they once maintained order across Westfall and Duskwood, court politics and resource shortages forced them to pull back, leaving active garrisons mainly in Elwynn Forest and Redridge.",al={"where are stormwind guards","guard locations","why guard towers"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="Who do the Stormwind Guard fight for?",a="They serve the Kingdom of Stormwind and the Alliance under the authority of the crown and Highlord Bolvar Fordragon. Locally, they answer to appointed marshals and cooperate closely with town magistrates, local militia, and Alliance adventurers willing to reinforce their hard-pressed outposts.",al={"stormwind guard allegiances","who do guards serve","stormwind army faction"},it="allies",add=true},{q="What is the history of the Stormwind Guard?",a="Following the Third War, the Guard maintained widespread stability, keeping watchtowers manned to protect Elwynn, Duskwood, and Westfall. However, recent political intrigue and royal court maneuvering led by Lady Katrana Prestor caused funding and troops to be withdrawn from outlying regions. With much of the army abroad or held back, the remaining garrisons now struggle to hold basic border posts like Westbrook and Lakeshire.",al={"stormwind guard history","origins of the guard","history of stormwind garrison"},sp=1,it="history",add=true},{q="Why are they called the Stormwind Guard?",a="They bear the name of the Kingdom of Stormwind, representing royal authority and military law beyond the capital's walls. They are also known as the Stormwind Garrisons because their ranks are split into regional outposts stationed in fortified towers and towns across the realm.",al={"why stormwind guard","stormwind guard name meaning","why the garrison name"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The Stormwind Guard represents the kingdom's front line, but its abandonment of sister settlements exposes the deep political cracks threatening the heart of the Alliance.",["race:Human"]="For humans of the southern farmlands, the guard's blue tabards once signified absolute safety, making their sudden absence in Westfall and Duskwood feel like a bitter betrayal.",["class:Warrior"]="From Northshire recruits to hardened veterans at Westbrook Garrison, human warriors cut their teeth marching alongside regional marshals against gnolls and bandits.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="A guard garrison is only as tough as its armor; in places like Lakeshire, local smiths exhaust entire iron reserves merely keeping the garrison's blades and shields serviceable."},kw={"garrison","goldshire","lakeshire","marshal dughan","marshal marris","marshal mcbride","stonewatch","stormwind guard","westbrook garrison"},rel={"topic:stormwind-army","topic:stormwind-kingdom","npc:marshal-dughan","npc:marshal-marris","zone:elwynn","zone:redridge","topic:night-watch","topic:defias-brotherhood"},src={"Stormwind Guard"}}
E["topic:stormwind-kingdom"] = {t="topic",n="Stormwind (kingdom)",z="general",s="The Kingdom of Stormwind is the southernmost and currently foremost human realm on Azeroth, holding dominion over Elwynn Forest, Westfall, Duskwood, and the Redridge Mountains. Sacked and shattered during the First War, it was rebuilt through blood and labor after the Second, rising to become the military and cultural center of the Alliance following the tragic collapse of Lordaeron in the north. Today, the kingdom stands beset from within and without, ruled by the ten-year-old King Anduin Wrynn under the watchful guidance of Highlord Bolvar Fordragon while its crowned monarch remains missing.",h="The Kingdom of Stormwind is the southernmost and currently foremost human realm on Azeroth, holding dominion...",sec={{t="A Legacy of Defiance",b="Stormwind was established over a thousand years ago by descendants of the ancient Arathi empire who ventured south beyond the crags of Khaz Modan into fertile, forested valleys. For centuries, the northern kingdoms dismissed Stormwind as an isolated, rural hinterland, even ignoring desperate pleas for aid during the Gnoll War. Led by King Barathen Wrynn, the realm defeated the gnolls and later the Gurubashi trolls alone, forging a culture fiercely proud of its self-reliance and the Light.",sp=0},{t="Ashes and Restoration",b="That independence was nearly extinguished when the Orcish Horde poured through the Dark Portal. Stormwind City burned to ash in the First War, its King Llane slain. The surviving populace fled north across the Great Sea led by Sir Anduin Lothar, rallying the northern realms into the Alliance of Lordaeron. Once the Orcish threat was crushed in the Second War, refugees returned home, hiring the Stonemasons Guild to rebuild their magnificent capital of white stone and blue banners. When the Scourge wiped out Lordaeron during the Third War, Stormwind became humanity's greatest remaining bastion.",sp=0},{t="A Fractured Realm",b="Beneath its soaring towers, Stormwind struggles to govern its vast southern holdings. While Elwynn Forest remains relatively secure, neighboring regions like Westfall are swarming with outlaws, Redridge faces constant orc and gnoll incursions, and the darkened woods of Duskwood have fallen out of the realm's control. A bitter dispute between the House of Nobles and the Stonemasons sparked the violent Defias riot that took the life of Queen Tiffin, scattering displaced workers into open rebellion across the provinces.",sp=1},{t="The Hollow Throne",b="The kingdom's instability deepened with the sudden, quiet disappearance of King Varian Wrynn while sailing to a diplomatic summit with the Horde at Theramore. To preserve fragile public order, the crown was placed on his ten-year-old son, Anduin Wrynn, with Highlord Bolvar Fordragon named Lord Regent. Yet royal authority is divided and stalled: the House of Nobles, guided closely by the enigmatic Lady Katrana Prestor, repeatedly vetoes military deployments, leaving local towns to fend for themselves while dangerous secrets fester within Stormwind Keep.",sp=2}},faq={{q="Who is currently ruling Stormwind?",a="Formally, the crown belongs to King Anduin Wrynn, who took the throne as a ten-year-old child. Because he is a minor, real governmental authority is wielded by Highlord Bolvar Fordragon as Lord Regent, with heavy legislative counsel and oversight from Lady Katrana Prestor and the House of Nobles.",al={"who is king of stormwind","current ruler","who runs stormwind","king of stormwind"},it="leader"},{q="What happened to King Varian Wrynn?",a="King Varian departed Stormwind on a confidential diplomatic voyage to Theramore to negotiate peaceful relations with the Horde. He vanished under mysterious circumstances along the way. His fate remains a guarded state secret to keep the kingdom's populace and external rivals from descending into chaos.",al={"where is varian","varian missing","what happened to the king","varian wrynn backstory"}},{q="Why was Stormwind destroyed in the past?",a="Stormwind was the first human nation encountered when the Orcish Horde invaded through the Dark Portal in the First War. Despite a stubborn defense led by King Llane and Sir Anduin Lothar, the city was breached, sacked, and razed, forcing the surviving population to evacuate north by sea to Lordaeron.",al={"first war stormwind","fall of stormwind","when did stormwind burn","stormwind destroyed"},it="ruin"},{q="Why are Westfall, Redridge, and Duskwood lacking royal soldiers?",a="The royal military is stretched thin and paralyzed by court politics. Lady Katrana Prestor and the House of Nobles frequently block requests to send garrisons outside Elwynn Forest, arguing that capital funds and defense forces cannot be wasted policing peripheral lands, leaving militias like the People's Militia and the Night Watch to fight alone.",al={"why no soldiers","why no army help","abandoned towns","outlying towns undefended"},it="conflict"},{q="What territory does the Kingdom of Stormwind claim?",a="Historically, Stormwind rules the southern continent of Azeroth, encompassing Elwynn Forest, the Redridge Mountains, Westfall, and Duskwood (formerly Brightwood). It also previously maintained frontier posts reaching south into northern Stranglethorn Vale.",al={"stormwind borders","stormwind lands","what lands are stormwind"},it="place"},{q="Why did the Stonemasons turn against Stormwind?",a="After rebuilding Stormwind City into a masterpiece of white stone, the Stonemasons Guild presented their invoice. Influenced by noble politics, the House of Nobles refused full payment, while the stonemasons refused to compromise. The ensuing city riots turned deadly, killing Queen Tiffin Wrynn and driving the guild out to become the outlaw Defias Brotherhood.",al={"why defias mad","stonemasons riot","defias backstory","why did builders rebel"},it="motive"},{q="What is the Kingdom of Stormwind?",a="The Kingdom of Stormwind, also called the Kingdom of Azeroth, is the southernmost of humanity's Seven Kingdoms and the heart of the Grand Alliance. Settled by descendants of the ancient Arathor empire, it encompasses fertile territories stretching across Elwynn Forest, Westfall, the Redridge Mountains, and Duskwood. Following the fall of Lordaeron to the Scourge in the north, Stormwind now stands as the strongest remaining bastion of human power and civilization in the world.",al={"what is stormwind","who is stormwind","stormwind kingdom"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Who are the Kingdom of Stormwind allied with?",a="Stormwind is the pillar of the Grand Alliance, maintaining close ties with the Ironforge dwarves and Gnomeregan gnomes of Khaz Modan, as well as the night elves across the sea in Kalimdor. Its people also share profound spiritual and historical bonds with the Church of the Holy Light. While it once stood isolated from the northern human kingdoms, Stormwind now leads the unified factions striving to defend civilization across Azeroth.",al={"stormwind allies","who is stormwind allied with","alliance of stormwind"},it="allies",add=true},{q="What is the history of Stormwind?",a="Founded roughly twelve hundred years ago by Arathi migrants who journeyed south past Khaz Modan, Stormwind prospered as an independent, agrarian realm. It survived severe conflicts like the Gnoll War and Gurubashi War before being utterly sacked by the Orcish Horde in the First War. Led by Lord Anduin Lothar, the survivors fled north to Lordaeron, returning after the Second War to rebuild their gleaming capital. With Lordaeron's recent fall to the Scourge, Stormwind has become humanity's paramount stronghold.",al={"stormwind history","history of stormwind","where did stormwind come from"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="Stormwind is humanity's beating heart and final sovereign stronghold after the ruin of Lordaeron, Alterac, and Dalaran in the north.",["faction:Alliance"]="As the primary military and economic anchor of the modern Alliance, Stormwind provides shelter, leadership, and resources to dwarven, gnomish, and night elven allies.",["class:Paladin"]="The kingdom honors the ancient holy traditions of the Church of the Holy Light and the legacy of the Silver Hand, housing consecrated orders within the Cathedral of Light.",["class:Rogue"]="Stormwind's bitter domestic strife and the shadow of the Defias uprising have filled its back alleys, sewers, and countryside with opportunistic cutpurses and political spies."},kw={"anduin","azeroth","bolvar","elwynn","first war","house of nobles","prestor","stonemasons","stormwind","varian","wrynn"},rel={"zone:stormwind","zone:elwynn","zone:westfall","zone:redridge","zone:duskwood","topic:varian-wrynn","topic:house-of-nobles"},src={"Stormwind (kingdom)"}}
E["topic:syndicate"] = {t="topic",n="Syndicate",z="general",s="The Syndicate is a widespread human criminal organization founded and led by dispossessed nobles of the fallen kingdom of Alterac. Operating out of fortified strongholds across the Alterac Mountains, Hillsbrad Foothills, and the ruins of Stromgarde Keep, they wage an uncompromising war against both the Alliance and the Horde to restore their lost aristocracy.",h="The Syndicate is a widespread human criminal organization founded and led by dispossessed nobles of the...",sec={{t="Fallen Nobility",b="When the kingdom of Alterac collapsed in dishonor following King Aiden Perenolde's treachery during the Second War, its aristocracy was stripped of land and title. Refusing to accept their exile, the remaining Alteraci nobility banded together under the late king's son, Prince Aliden Perenolde. Adopting distinctive orange bandanas and standardizing their ranks with hired thugs, highwaymen, and hedge wizards, they forged the Syndicate. To the outside world, they behave like common brigands, raiding caravans, ambushing travelers, and running extortion rings. In truth, their criminal enterprises exist solely to fund an organized effort to reconquer their ancestral domains and subjugate surrounding lands.",sp=0},{t="Strongholds of the North",b="The Syndicate maintains entrenched footholds across northern Lordaeron. In the snowy Alterac Mountains, they fortified the ruined town of Strahnbrad and established encampments throughout the Uplands and Dandred's Fold. To the south, they hold the crumbling remnants of Durnholde Keep in the Hillsbrad Foothills, using it as a springboard to harass Southshore and raid supply routes. Further east across the Thoradin's Wall, Lord Falconcrest marshals an occupying Syndicate army inside the historic ruins of Stromgarde Keep in Arathi, actively battling the battered Stromgarde Militia for mastery over the Arathi Highlands.",sp=0},{t="Enemies on All Fronts",b="Unlike ordinary outlaws, the Syndicate harbors grand ideological ambitions that earn them the hatred of almost every regional faction. They view the Alliance as mortal enemies who stole their rightful heritage, frequently targeting Southshore officials such as Magistrate Henry Maleb. Towards the Horde, their stance is openly supremacist: following the philosophies of Aedelas Blackmoore, they view orcs merely as beasts fit for slave labor and seek to rebuild the internment camp system. Sylvanas Windrunner's Forsaken execute captured Syndicate members on sight, while the clandestine thieves' guild of Ravenholdt wages a constant shadow war against their sloppy, arrogant operatives.",sp=0},{t="Puppets of the Shadow Council",b="While the Syndicate's noble leaders believe they are fighting for human supremacy and their birthright, they are being steered from within by darker masters. Cultists belonging to the Argus Wake have thoroughly infiltrated their upper ranks, manipulating men like Lord Falconcrest into seizing Stromgarde Keep. Far from serving human interests, the Argus Wake ultimately reports to the Shadow Council based in Felwood's Jaedenar, using the Syndicate's armies and stolen territories as a mortal vanguard to clear the way for the Burning Legion.",sp=2}},faq={{q="Who are the Syndicate?",a="The Syndicate is a large criminal organization made up of dispossessed nobles, brigands, and mercenaries originating from the fallen kingdom of Alterac. Led by exiled aristocrats like Prince Aliden Perenolde and Lord Falconcrest, they use highway robbery, assassination, and military occupation in an attempt to rebuild Alterac.",al={"what is the syndicate","syndicate background","who are these guys in orange"},it="identity"},{q="Why does the Syndicate hate the Alliance?",a="Alterac betrayed the Alliance of Lordaeron during the Second War. In punishment, the Alliance dismantled the kingdom and stripped its nobles of their lands. The Syndicate's leaders consider the Alliance their mortal enemies and actively sabotage Alliance towns, trade caravans, and military garrisons like Stromgarde Keep.",al={"why does syndicate attack alliance","syndicate alliance relationship","alterac vs alliance"},it="conflict"},{q="Why does the Syndicate capture and enslave orcs?",a="The Syndicate subscribes to the harsh philosophies of former Durnholde commander Aedelas Blackmoore, viewing orcs as savage beasts fit only for unpaid servitude. Prince Aliden Perenolde sought to re-establish the Alliance internment camp system, using enslaved orcs captured in the Hillsbrad Foothills and Alterac as forced labor.",al={"syndicate orc slaves","why are orcs in cages in strahnbrad","syndicate slavery"},it="motive"},{q="What is the Syndicate's relationship with Ravenholdt?",a="Ravenholdt is a prestigious guild of professional assassins and thieves based in the Alterac Mountains who view the Syndicate as amateurish, undisciplined brutes. To undermine the Syndicate, Ravenholdt contracts rogues to pickpocket their emblems, as disgraced Syndicate operatives are ruthlessly culled by their own leadership upon losing them.",al={"ravenholdt vs syndicate","why does ravenholdt hate syndicate","syndicate emblems"},it="ties"},{q="Who is really pulling the strings behind the Syndicate?",a="While the nobles think they are fighting for their lost titles, the organization is secretly manipulated by a warlock sect called the Argus Wake. Led by figures like Nagaz and Marez Cowl, the Argus Wake serves the Shadow Council in Jaedenar, using the Syndicate's territorial gains to prepare the region for the Burning Legion.",al={"who leads the syndicate","argus wake syndicate","shadow council syndicate"},sp=2,it="power"},{q="Where are the Syndicate's primary strongholds?",a="The Syndicate operates extensively across three zones: the Alterac Mountains (Strahnbrad and Dandred's Fold), the Hillsbrad Foothills (Durnholde Keep), and the Arathi Highlands (Northfold Manor), where they also occupy a massive quarter of Stromgarde Keep under Lord Falconcrest.",al={"where are syndicate bases","syndicate locations","where to find syndicate"},it="place"},{q="Why is the Syndicate hostile to travelers?",a="The Syndicate views all outsiders as threats to their criminal plots and territorial ambitions. They despise the Alliance for dismantling Alterac after the Second War, regard the Horde merely as brutes fit only for forced labor, and brook no interference in their banditry, assassinations, and raids.",al={"why do syndicate attack","syndicate hostile","why syndicate attack players"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who leads the Syndicate?",a="The organization was founded and led in the Alterac Mountains by Prince Aliden Perenolde, the son of Alterac's traitorous late king. In the Arathi Highlands, operations are commanded by Lord Falconcrest from the ruins of Stromgarde Keep, supported by regional overseers like Baron Vardus and Master Ryson.",al={"syndicate leader","who is in charge of syndicate","syndicate boss"},it="leader",add=true},{q="Who is the Syndicate allied with?",a="Openly, they rely on rogue mercenaries, bandits, and corrupt human nobility while fighting nearly every established faction. In secret, however, the Syndicate is manipulated by the Argus Wake, a sinister warlock coven serving the Shadow Council at Jaedenar, which promised to restore Alterac's rule in exchange for footholds across northern Lordaeron.",al={"syndicate allies","who works with syndicate","syndicate partners"},sp=1,it="allies",add=true},{q="Where did the Syndicate come from?",a="The Syndicate was born from the ruins of the Kingdom of Alterac. After King Aiden Perenolde betrayed the Alliance during the Second War, Alterac was stripped of sovereignty and fell into ruin. Deposed nobles and disgruntled aristocrats banded together under Prince Aliden Perenolde, turning to smuggling, banditry, and terrorism to reclaim their lost lands.",al={"syndicate origin","syndicate history","how was syndicate formed"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["class:Rogue"]="The Ravenholdt guild pays well for thieves willing to slip behind enemy lines and pickpocket emblems directly from Syndicate brigands, capitalizing on their internal paranoia.",["race:Orc"]="The Syndicate views your people as beasts destined for chains and internment, making the liberation of captured brethren in Strahnbrad and Durnholde an urgent matter of honor.",["faction:Alliance"]="Syndicate operatives murdered Southshore's former magistrate and continually push the beleaguered Stromgarde Militia toward total extinction in the Arathi Highlands.",["faction:Horde"]="Warchief Thrall and Lady Sylvanas both maintain active bounties against the Syndicate, dispatching scouts to eliminate their encampments and recover stolen relics."},kw={"aliden perenolde","alterac","bandanas","durnholde keep","falconcrest","ravenholdt","strahnbrad","stromgarde","syndicate","uplands"},rel={"topic:alterac-kingdom","topic:ravenholdt","subzone:stromgarde-keep","subzone:durnholde-keep","subzone:strahnbrad","subzone:dandred-s-fold","zone:alterac","quest:5726"},src={"Syndicate"}}
E["topic:tauren"] = {t="topic",n="Tauren",z="general",s="The tauren, or shu'halo in their native tongue, are a noble race of nomadic bovine humanoids native to central Kalimdor. Deeply devoted to the Earth Mother, shamanism, and the natural world, they were driven near extinction by ruthless centaur clans before forging a bond with Thrall's New Horde. Today, united under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, they have established a permanent home across the verdant plains of Mulgore and the high rises of Thunder Bluff.",h="The tauren, or shu'halo in their native tongue, are a noble race of nomadic bovine humanoids native to...",sec={{t="Nomads of the Barrens",b="For countless generations, the tauren lived as seasonal nomads wandering the arid plains of the Barrens, hunting the great kodo and listening for the voice of the Earth Mother. However, their peaceful wanderings were plagued by centuries of brutal bloodshed. The ferocious centaur tribes, born from the tragic union of Zaetar and Princess Theradras, waged an unrelenting war of extermination against them. Lacking a fortified homeland and reluctant to wage endless war, the tauren tribes were pushed to the brink of annihilation, their very survival hanging by a thread.",sp=0},{t="The New Horde and Thunder Bluff",b="Survival came from an unexpected alliance across the sea. During the Third War, Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof encountered Warchief Thrall and his newly arrived orcs. Recognizing a shared spirit of honor and a longing for shamanic heritage, the two leaders joined forces. With the Horde's assistance, the tauren crushed the marauding centaur and claimed the lush, sheltered grasslands of Mulgore. Upon its windswept mesas, Cairne raised the city of Thunder Bluff. Indebted by this salvation, the tauren marched alongside the Horde to Mount Hyjal to turn back the Burning Legion.",sp=0},{t="Spiritual Guardians and Internal Tensions",b="Within the Horde, the tauren act as a steadying, peaceful conscience. Their mastery of nature and the elements helped revitalize orcish shamanism, while Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem fosters rare diplomatic and spiritual ties with night elf druids to investigate the growing corruption within the Emerald Dream. Yet unity remains fragile. While most tribes revere Cairne's wisdom, the xenophobic and ruthless Grimtotem clan, headed by the scheming elder crone Magatha, resides within Thunder Bluff while secretly plotting to usurp leadership and steer the shu'halo toward supremacy.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why did the tauren join the Horde?",a="The tauren were on the verge of being wiped out by the ruthless centaur of the Barrens. Warchief Thrall and his orcs arrived and fought alongside Cairne Bloodhoof, driving the centaur back and helping the tauren secure Mulgore. Bound by deep honor and shared spiritual values, Cairne pledged his people's loyalty to the New Horde in gratitude.",al={"why join horde","tauren horde alliance","why are tauren in horde"},it="allies"},{q="Who leads the tauren?",a="The tauren are led by High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, the wise, venerable leader of the Bloodhoof tribe. Respected for his strength and measured judgment, he unites the disparate tribes from his seat atop Thunder Bluff, assisted by Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem and Elder Crone Magatha Grimtotem.",al={"tauren leader","tauren chieftain","who rules thunder bluff"},it="leader"},{q="What is the Earth Mother?",a="The Earth Mother is the central deity of tauren belief. The shu'halo believe she created the world, the sun, and the moon, and they revere her through a lifestyle deeply attuned to the natural balance, seasonal migration, hunting, and elemental respect.",al={"earthmother","who is the earth mother","tauren religion"},it="power"},{q="Why do the tauren hate the centaur?",a="Centaur hunted the tauren for sport and warred with them for centuries across central Kalimdor. Viewing the tauren's peaceful spirituality as weakness, the centaur chased them from place to place and nearly brought about the total extinction of the shu'halo before the orcs intervened.",al={"tauren centaur war","centaur feud"},it="conflict"},{q="Can tauren be druids?",a="Yes. Though druidism faded from their culture during the long nomadic wars with the centaur, the tauren originally learned nature magic from Cenarius in ancient times. Recently, Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem has rekindled these teachings with the help of the night elves, making tauren druids a cornerstone of the Cenarion Circle.",al={"tauren druid lore","how did tauren become druids"}},{q="Are all tauren loyal to Cairne Bloodhoof?",a="Most tribes revere Cairne, but the Grimtotem tribe remains a bitter exception. Led by Magatha Grimtotem, they believe the tauren are superior to all other races and despise Cairne's alliance with the Horde, quietly working to undermine his peaceful reign.",al={"grimtotem loyalty","tauren civil war"},it="ties"},{q="What is the story of the Tauren?",a="The tauren, or shu'halo in their native tongue, are an ancient, nomadic race of large bovine humanoids native to Kalimdor's central plains. For countless centuries, they lived in harmony with nature and revered the Earth Mother, but were driven nearly to extinction by ceaseless wars against the savage centaur. Facing destruction, High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof allied with Thrall and the newly arrived Horde during the Third War. Together, they drove back the centaur, secured the lush grasslands of Mulgore, and constructed the great mesa-city of Thunder Bluff, finally giving their people a permanent, united homeland.",al={"who are the tauren","tauren history","what are the tauren","tell me about tauren"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do the Tauren stand in the world today?",a="The tauren now thrive from their capital on the high bluffs of Mulgore, united under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof and firmly allied with the Horde. Yet peace remains fragile. Beyond securing their borders from lingering centaur raids and hostile Bristleback quilboar, the tauren face internal tension from the scheming Grimtotem tribe. Meanwhile, tauren druids study a spreading corruption deep within the Emerald Dream, working alongside night elven Cenarion allies while lending their spiritual wisdom and shamanic heritage to guide their allies in Orgrimmar.",al={"tauren current situation","where are the tauren now","what is happening with the tauren"},it="today",add=true},{q="What does it mean to be a Tauren adventurer?",a="To walk the plains as a tauren is to carry the wisdom of the Earth Mother and honor the sacred bond forged between your people and the Horde. You represent a deeply spiritual, reverent culture known for both its gentle restraint and unmatched physical might in battle. Whether you commune with spirits as a shaman, walk the path of the druid under Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem, or bear arms as a stalwart hunter or warrior, you defend Mulgore's hard-won peace and venture forth to repay your blood-debt to Thrall's Horde across Kalimdor.",al={"playing a tauren","what does it mean to be tauren","tauren role","tauren adventurer"},it="for_you",rc="Tauren",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As shu'halo, your people's ancient nomadic wandering has given way to a prosperous homeland in Mulgore, secured through the unbreakable brotherhood between Cairne and Thrall.",["faction:Horde"]="The tauren provide the Horde with its moral compass and deep spiritual roots, tempering the aggressive instincts of the orcs and trolls with ancient wisdom and honor.",["class:Druid"]="Through Archdruid Hamuul Runetotem, tauren druids work side by side with the night elves of the Cenarion Circle, putting factional rivalries aside to heal the land and protect the Emerald Dream.",["class:Shaman"]="Tauren shamanic traditions are among the oldest in Azeroth, and their teachings were essential in helping the orcs rediscover their sacred elemental bonds after the demonic taint of the past wars."},kw={"barrens","cairne bloodhoof","centaur","earth mother","hamuul runetotem","kodo","magatha grimtotem","mulgore","shu'halo","tauren","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:cairne-bloodhoof","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore","zone:barrens","topic:earth-mother","topic:centaur","npc:magatha-grimtotem","quest:1489"},src={"Tauren"}}
E["topic:third-war"] = {t="topic",n="Third War",z="general",s="The Third War was the apocalyptic conflict that shattered the old world order just a few years ago. Orchestrated by the Burning Legion and unleashed through the Lich King's Plague of Undeath, the war saw the fall of the Kingdom of Lordaeron and the near-total destruction of Quel'Thalas before culminating in a desperate, unified stand at Mount Hyjal.",h="The Third War was the apocalyptic conflict that shattered the old world order just a few years ago.",sec={{t="The Plague and the Prince",b="The war began subtly in northern Lordaeron, disguised as a virulent sickness carried in infected grain distributed from Andorhal. The tainted harvest did not merely slaughter its victims; it raised them as ravenous ghouls bound to the Lich King's will. Prince Arthas Menethil raced to stem the contagion alongside the Kirin Tor sorceress Jaina Proudmoore. Blinded by grief, fury, and dreadlord manipulations, Arthas purged the infected population of Stratholme before pursuing his foe to the frozen waste of Northrend. There, he took up the cursed runeblade Frostmourne, casting aside his soul. He returned south only to murder his father, King Terenas, delivering Lordaeron into the hands of the Scourge.",sp=0},{t="The Invasion of the Legion",b="Empowered by death, Arthas crushed the high elven realm of Quel'Thalas to resurrect the necromancer Kel'Thuzad at the Sunwell. The undead army then laid siege to Dalaran, seizing the spellbooks necessary to summon the demon lord Archimonde into Azeroth. With the commander of the Burning Legion walking mortal soil, Dalaran was leveled in an arcane display of devastation. The Scourge had served its purpose as an advance guard, and the Burning Legion turned its sights toward the eternal power resting across the Great Sea.",sp=0},{t="The Flight West and Mount Hyjal",b="Guided by cryptic warnings from the mysterious Prophet—later revealed as the redeemed Guardian Medivh—Warchief Thrall led the New Horde across the Great Sea, while Lady Jaina Proudmoore evacuated surviving Alliance refugees. In ancient Kalimdor, the newcomers clashed with the reclusive night elves before all three factions recognized the supreme threat looming over them. Beneath the World Tree Nordrassil atop Mount Hyjal, mortals stood together against impossible odds. Archimonde was destroyed by ancestral wisps when he reached the tree, shattering the Legion's vanguard and ending the war.",sp=0},{t="A Scarred World",b="Although Archimonde fell, the peace of 25 ADP is fragile and stained with loss. The Alliance of Lordaeron is gone, leaving the kingdom of Stormwind and Ironforge to anchor the southern Alliance while Lordaeron sits rotting as the Plaguelands. In Kalimdor, Thrall's Horde carved out Durotar, yet tensions with the newly allied night elves remain volatile. The Scourge endures in the northern ruins, and the freed undead—the Forsaken—now hold the catacombs of Capital City, waging a war of survival against both former masters and vengeful crusaders.",sp=0}},faq={{q="When did the Third War happen?",a="The Third War began in 20 ADP with the outbreak of the Plague of Undeath in Lordaeron and ended in 21 ADP at the Battle of Mount Hyjal. In the current year of 25 ADP, its devastation is only four years past, meaning the ruins are still fresh and nearly every adult living today fought in it or lost family to it.",al={"when was the third war","how long ago was the war","third war dates"},it="history"},{q="How was the Third War different from the First and Second Wars?",a="The First and Second Wars were primarily struggles between the Old Horde and the kingdoms of the Alliance. The Third War was a global demonic invasion that shattered entire nations. Mortals fought not just one another, but the cosmic threat of the Burning Legion and its undead Scourge across three continents, forcing mortal enemies into a desperate truce.",al={"difference between third war and second","why was third war different","horde vs alliance third war"},it="conflict"},{q="What happened to the Kingdom of Lordaeron?",a="Lordaeron was destroyed from within. The Cult of the Damned contaminated the regional grain supply with the Plague of Undeath, slaughtering the populace and raising them as monsters. Its own crown prince, Arthas, succumbed to corruption, assassinated his father King Terenas II, and handed the broken kingdom to the Scourge.",al={"fate of lordaeron","why is lordaeron destroyed","what happened to terenas"},it="ruin"},{q="Who was the Prophet that warned everyone to go to Kalimdor?",a="The Prophet was Medivh, the Last Guardian of Tirisfal. Brought back to atone for his past role in opening the Dark Portal, Medivh traveled in the guise of a raven-cloaked wanderer, warning leaders that Lordaeron was doomed and urging them to unite on the forgotten continent of Kalimdor.",al={"who was the prophet","raven prophet","medivh third war"},it="origin"},{q="Why are the night elves part of the Alliance now?",a="For ten millennia, the night elves lived in strict isolation. The Third War brought the Legion back to their sacred forests and forced them to sacrifice their immortality to slay Archimonde at the World Tree. Facing a radically changed world, growing resource conflicts with Thrall's Horde, and surviving Scourge corruption, they abandoned their solitude and forged an alliance with the races of the Eastern Kingdoms.",al={"why did night elves join alliance","night elves third war","kaldorei joining alliance"},it="allies"},{q="How did the Horde escape the war in Lordaeron?",a="Warchief Thrall trusted Medivh's prophetic dreams. He rallied the liberated clans from the internment camps, raided the human ports to commandeer sailing ships, and fled across the Great Sea before the Scourge or the Legion fully consumed Lordaeron.",al={"how did thrall leave lordaeron","orcs sailing west","horde kalimdor flight"},it="motive"},{q="What was the Third War?",a="The Third War was a catastrophic global conflict that saw the demonic Burning Legion launch its second invasion of Azeroth, aided by the undead Scourge. Beginning with the Plague of Undeath in northern Lordaeron and the betrayal of Prince Arthas Menethil, the war quickly spread across the Eastern Kingdoms and into Kalimdor. Unlike earlier conflicts between just orcs and humans, it engulfed nearly every major race on Azeroth before culminating in a desperate, united stand atop Mount Hyjal.",al={"what is the third war","third war summary","explain third war"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why is it called the Third War?",a="It is named the Third War because it followed the First and Second Wars fought between the original orcish Horde and the Grand Alliance of Lordaeron. Because this invasion brought unprecedented global devastation and demon armies, it is also known as the Third Great War, the Chaos War, and the Reign of Chaos.",al={"third war name origin","why called third war","reign of chaos name"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["faction:Alliance"]="The fall of Lordaeron shifted the center of Alliance leadership south to Stormwind and Ironforge, while survivors look northward with burning desires to reclaim their homeland.",["faction:Horde"]="For Thrall's Horde, the war was a crucible of redemption: Grommash Hellscream slew Mannoroth to break the demonic blood curse forever, allowing the orcs to build a sovereign nation in Kalimdor.",["race:Undead"]="The Forsaken are living monuments to the war's horror—citizens of Lordaeron slaughtered by the plague and forced to serve the Scourge until breaking free to claim the Undercity.",["race:NightElf"]="The defense of Nordrassil cost the kaldorei their immortality and exposed their ancient homelands to the wider world for the first time in ten thousand years.",["class:Paladin"]="The Knights of the Silver Hand were torn apart during the war when Arthas disbanded them, murdered Uther the Lightbringer, and turned their holy halls into charnel houses."},kw={"archimonde","arthas","hyjal","mannoroth","medivh","nordrassil","scourge","stratholme","terenas","third war"},rel={"topic:scourge","topic:burning-legion","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:nordrassil","topic:lordaeron","topic:plague-of-undeath","topic:medivh","topic:new-horde"},src={"Third War"}}
E["topic:titan"] = {t="topic",n="Titan",z="general",s="The titans are a primordial race of colossal, godlike beings who wander the cosmos bringing order and shaping worlds. In ancient antiquity, they came to Azeroth to vanquish the chaotic Old Gods, establish massive terrestrial facilities, and foster life before departing into the Great Dark Beyond.",h="The titans are a primordial race of colossal, godlike beings who wander the cosmos bringing order and shaping...",sec={{t="Architects of the Cosmos",b="The titans are cosmic giants forged from primordial matter and arcane magic, embodying the forces of order and creation. In their journeys across the Great Dark Beyond, they sought out slumbering worlds to cultivate, raising up continents, calming chaotic elemental forces, and designing life. To enforce balance, they established powerful constructs made of stone, iron, and sea to watch over their works.",sp=0},{t="The Ordering of Azeroth",b="Long ago, the Pantheon of titans discovered Azeroth in the grip of the chaotic elemental lords and the horrific Old Gods. The titans waged war against these parasitic horrors, banishing the elementals to pocket planes and sealing the monstrous Old Gods deep beneath the crust of the earth. Upon securing the world, they carved great biomes, established vault complexes like Uldaman, and entrusted five great dragons—the Dragon Aspects—with stewardship of the land, magic, time, and life itself before journeying back among the stars.",sp=0},{t="The Fallen Champion",b="Not all titans remained aligned with the Pantheon's vision of perfect order. Sargeras, once the greatest warrior and defender of the titans, grew despondent and terrified by the deep cosmic darkness and demonic malevolence he was charged with combating. Rejecting the work of his kin, Sargeras turned to nihilism, founding the demonic Burning Legion to extinguish all creation rather than allow it to be corrupted.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Are the titans still alive and on Azeroth?",a="The titans themselves departed Azeroth eons ago into the Great Dark Beyond after defeating the Old Gods and ordering the world. They left behind titanic watchers, stone and iron constructs, and vast buried vault complexes to oversee their design.",al={"where are the titans","are titans on azeroth","titan locations"},it="place"},{q="Why do the dwarves care so much about the titans?",a="Dwarves descend directly from the earthen, a race of subterranean stone beings fashioned by the titans to carve the foundations of the world. Through archaeology and the excavation of ruins like Bael Modan and Uldaman, dwarves seek to uncover their own forgotten lineage.",al={"dwarf titan connection","why dwarves dig","dwarves and the creators"},it="ties"},{q="What is the Pantheon?",a="The Pantheon is the governing council of the titans, led by the Highfather Aman'Thul. Other members include Khaz'goroth the shaper of worlds, Eonar the life-binder, Norgannon the keeper of celestial magics, Golganneth the lord of skies, and Aggramar, the champion who replaced Sargeras.",al={"titan pantheon","who leads the titans","titan leaders"}},{q="Did the titans create the Dragon Aspects?",a="Yes. Before leaving Azeroth, the Pantheon channeled their powers through the keepers to bless five proto-dragons. Alexstrasza, Ysera, Malygos, Nozdormu, and Neltharion were granted immense power to safeguard life, nature, magic, time, and the earth.",al={"titan dragons","aspect origins","who made the aspects"}},{q="Is Sargeras a titan?",a="Sargeras was once the champion of the Pantheon, defending the cosmos against chaos and demons. Driven to despair and madness by the darkness he witnessed, he broke away from his brethren to form the Burning Legion, dedicating himself to burning down the ordered universe.",al={"who is sargeras","is sargeras a god","sargeras origin"}},{q="Did a titan fall in Silithus?",a="Prophecies recorded by the Qiraji Prophet Skeram claim that a titan battled an ancient evil in the southern deserts of Silithus and fell. However, scholars and explorers debate whether this entity was a true titan or merely one of their titanic constructs.",al={"silithus titan","prophecy of c'thun titan","did c'thun kill a titan"},it="history"},{q="What are the titans?",a="The titans are colossal, planet-sized cosmic entities made of arcane magic and the primordial matter of the universe. Roaming the Great Dark Beyond as living worlds wrapped in stardust and mountain peaks, they are the supreme champions of Order and creation. Wherever they travel across the cosmos, they bring structure to formless worlds, establish life, and seek out slumbering spirits of their own kind.",al={"who are the titans","what is a titan","titan lore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where did the titans come from?",a="Titans are born from worldsouls, nascent cosmic spirits that take shape within the fiery, primordial cores of a rare few planets across the universe. A worldsoul sleeps for uncounted ages, infusing its world with potent Spirit energy until it matures into full consciousness. Upon awakening, it rises as a living world of immense arcane power to join its kindred in ordering the cosmos.",al={"titan origin","origin of titans","how were titans created"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who leads the titans?",a="The titans are led by Highfather Aman'Thul, the eldest among them and the head of their governing council, the Pantheon. Wielding immense temporal and cosmic power, Aman'Thul guides their mission across the Great Dark Beyond to order worlds and awaken dormant worldsouls.",al={"who is the titan leader","leader of the titans","leader of the pantheon"},it="leader",add=true}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="The titans are revered as the divine Makers. Discovering their origins through titanic relics is the central mission of the Explorers' League and King Magni Bronzebeard.",["race:Gnome"]="Gnomes share a mechanical legacy originating from titanic creations, driving their natural affinity for clockwork design and excavation.",["class:Mage"]="Arcane magic is the fundamental lifeblood of the titans, and its ordered principles trace directly to the structural enchantments woven across the world by the Pantheon.",["profession:Mining"]="Miners dig deep into the bones of the earth, often unearthing pristine titan-forged metal nodes and architectural foundations that predate recorded history."},kw={"aman'thul","earthen","eonar","khaz'goroth","makers","norgannon","order","pantheon","titan"},rel={"npc:magni-bronzebeard","topic:old-god","topic:sargeras","topic:burning-legion","topic:emerald-dream","npc:deathwing","zone:silithus","npc:greatfather-winter"},src={"Titan"}}
E["topic:trogg"] = {t="topic",n="Trogg",z="general",s="Troggs are brutish, subterranean humanoids descended from the earliest, failed titan-forged experiments sealed deep within the earth. Having recently clawed their way to the surface after dwarven excavations disturbed their ancient vaults, these relentless diggers have overrun dwarven quarries and driven the gnomes out of their beloved capital of Gnomeregan.",h="Troggs are brutish, subterranean humanoids descended from the earliest, failed titan-forged experiments...",sec={{t="The Flawed Predecessors",b="Long before mortal races recorded history, the titan-forged keepers sought to fashion servants from stone using the Forge of Wills. Their initial design went awry, yielding stone-skinned brutes of low intelligence and unbridled savagery. Distressed by their botched creations, the keepers refined the matrix into the earthen race, who would eventually become the ancestors of the dwarves. Unwilling to annihilate the imperfect troggs, the giantess Ironaya sealed them deep underground within the vault of Uldaman, intending for them to remain trapped in eternal stasis.",sp=0},{t="Awakened by the Pickaxe",b="For countless millennia, the troggs lay dormant until the Explorers' League of Ironforge breached the ancient subterranean complexes of Khaz Modan. Waking in an unfamiliar era, swarms of furious troggs poured from the depths, pursuing their distant dwarven relatives with ancestral fury. Finding the surface bright and unwelcoming, they carved sprawling tunnel networks beneath Dun Morogh, Loch Modan, and the Badlands, quickly ambushing quarries, archaeological dig sites, and trade outposts.",sp=0},{t="The Catastrophe at Gnomeregan",b="Guided by subterranean vibration and noise, trogg swarms tunneled directly up into Gnomeregan. Desperately outmatched by sheer numbers and savage strength, the gnomes fought a bitter war in the subterranean corridors. In a desperate gambit engineered by Sicco Thermaplugg, High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque unleashed radioactive venting to exterminate the infestation. Tragically, the irradiated gas killed roughly eighty percent of the gnomish populace while merely enraging the troggs, leaving the grand techno-city an irradiated trogg stronghold.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What exactly are troggs?",a="Troggs are subterranean humanoids created as the first, flawed generation of stone servants by the titan-forged keepers. Distressed by their raw brutality, the keepers locked them in stasis vaults like Uldaman rather than destroying them, until dwarven miners inadvertently unearthed them thousands of years later.",al={"trogg origins","what is a trogg","trogg lore"},it="identity"},{q="Are troggs related to dwarves?",a="Yes. Both races trace their origins to the Forge of Wills. Troggs represent the keepers' unrefined first draft, while the earthen—who later succumbed to the Curse of Flesh and became dwarves—were the perfected second iteration.",al={"are dwarves and troggs related","trogg dwarf connection"},it="ties"},{q="How did troggs capture Gnomeregan?",a="Drawn by the vibrations of heavy machinery, the troggs tunneled into Gnomeregan from below. Despite gnomish defensive inventions, the invaders overwhelmed the defenders through sheer numbers. A failed attempt to purge them with radioactive gas killed most of the gnomes instead, cementing trogg control over the city.",al={"why are troggs in gnomeregan","gnomeregan trogg attack"},it="conflict"},{q="Are troggs capable of speech or advanced culture?",a="Troggs possess very limited intellect, communicating mostly through guttural grunts and roaring. They value pure brute strength above all else, choosing tribal chieftains through violence. While some manifest rudimentary shamanistic spellcasting, their craftsmanship is crude, rarely advancing beyond crude clubs, stone arrows, and stolen relics.",al={"can troggs talk","trogg culture","trogg intelligence"},it="power"},{q="Why do troggs collect ancient relics?",a="Explorers believe their compulsion to hoard ancient titan relics is an instinctual remnant of their titan-forged origins. However, troggs rarely understand what they carry, using priceless artifacts as blunt cudgels or primitive good-luck charms.",al={"why do troggs hoard artifacts","trogg treasure"},it="purpose"},{q="Are there troggs outside of Khaz Modan?",a="Yes. Subterranean trogg clans have established nests across Azeroth, including the Ragefire troggs deep within Ragefire Chasm beneath Orgrimmar, the Gravelflint troggs in Darkshore, and groups throughout the Badlands and Alterac.",al={"trogg locations","troggs in durotar","troggs in kalimdor"},it="place"},{q="Why are troggs so hostile to other races?",a="Troggs are driven almost entirely by raw instinct, cunning, and sheer aggression. Upon being disturbed from their ancient slumber deep underground, their immediate impulse was to vent their fury on the surface dwellers, particularly their dwarven cousins who broke into their vaults. Simpleminded and territorial, they view other living beings either as threats to be crushed or as meals to be eaten, readily resorting to violence and even cannibalism without provocation.",al={"why troggs attack","are troggs aggressive","why do troggs hate dwarves"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who leads the troggs?",a="Troggs have no unified ruler or central civilization. Instead, they organize into scattered tribes and clans, such as the Rockjaw, Stonesplinter, and Stonevault troggs. Within each tribe, leadership belongs solely to the strongest, largest, and most vicious brute who can enforce rule through intimidation and bloodshed, such as Boss Bruggor in Dun Morogh or Grimlok within Uldaman.",al={"trogg leader","trogg chieftain","trogg king"},it="leader",add=true},{q="Do troggs have any allies?",a="Almost universally, troggs fight alone. Their savagery, cannibalistic tendencies, and hostility toward other races make diplomacy impossible, and they despise outsiders. A rare exception exists among the Stonesplinter clan, who have established loose ties with the kobolds of the Tunnel Rats, but genuine allegiances beyond their own kind are practically nonexistent.",al={"trogg allies","who works with troggs","trogg factions"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Where did troggs come from?",a="Troggs were the first generation of titan-forged crafted by the titanic keepers using the Forge of Wills. The design proved flawed and overly complex, producing stone-skinned, brutish creatures lacking higher intellect. Distressed by the result, the keepers refined their subsequent efforts into the earthen and sealed the defective troggs in stasis vaults like Uldaman. Over millennia, afflicted by the Curse of Flesh, they slept beneath Azeroth until dwarven miners unwittingly excavated their tombs.",al={"origin of troggs","trogg history","how were troggs created"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:Gnome"]="Troggs are the bitter bane of your people, responsible for shattering your underground home and scattering the surviving gnomes to Tinker Town in Ironforge.",["race:Dwarf"]="These savage cousins were unearthed by your own pickaxes in your quest to uncover titan ancestry, making their incursions across Khaz Modan a direct consequence of dwarven archaeology.",["profession:Mining"]="Trogg nests frequently occupy rich mineral veins and deep mines like Gol'Bolar, forcing miners to defend their claims with pickaxes and hammers.",["faction:Alliance"]="Clearing trogg infestations across Dun Morogh, Loch Modan, and Gnomeregan remains a vital defensive effort to keep the mountain kingdom and its refugees safe."},kw={"caverndeep","earthen","forge of wills","gnomeregan","grubbis","oggleflint","rockjaw","stonesplinter","trogg","uldaman"},rel={"zone:gnomeregan","subzone:uldaman","zone:dunmorogh","zone:lochmodan","zone:ragefire","npc:gelbin-mekkatorque","npc:sicco-thermaplugg","topic:explorers-league"},src={"Trogg"}}
E["topic:troll"] = {t="topic",n="Troll",z="general",s="Trolls are among the oldest sentient races native to Azeroth, having arisen around the Well of Eternity long before the Sundering. Renowned for their incredible physical regeneration, worship of primal spirits known as loa, and mastery of voodoo, their fractured empires once ruled vast swaths of the ancient world. Today, while ancient kingdoms like the Gurubashi and Amani remain deeply hostile to outsiders, the Darkspear tribe has found a lasting haven within Thrall's Horde.",h="Trolls are among the oldest sentient races native to Azeroth, having arisen around the Well of Eternity long...",sec={{t="Children of the Wilds",b="Long before the Great Sundering shattered ancient Kalimdor, the primal energies of the Well of Eternity nurtured the rise of trollkind. Emerging as savage, highly agile hunter-gatherers, the early trolls possessed a unique physiology that granted them astonishing regenerative powers, allowing them to recover rapidly from grievous wounds and even regrow severed limbs over time. Settling around the sacred mountains of Zandalar, they directed their reverence toward Kalimdor's elusive Wild Gods, whom they called loa. Under the spiritual guidance of shadow hunters and witch doctors, these rituals coalesced into the practice of voodoo and the construction of holy temple peaks like Zuldazar.",sp=0},{t="The War of the Swarm",b="Roughly sixteen thousand years ago, curious trolls unwittingly broke an ancient taboo, awakening the C'Thraxxi horror Kith'ix. The beast rallied the aqiri swarms from the underground warrens, threatening to consume all mortal life. Faced with total annihilation, the disparate tribes united under the Zandalari to forge the Empire of Zul. Through relentless guerrilla ambushes and the summoned might of the loa, the trolls drove back the swarms. Driven to eradicate the insectoid threat, mighty tribes branched outward across the continent: the Drakkari seized the frozen north, the Gurubashi battered the aqir in the southwest, and the fierce Amani slew Kith'ix itself, founding their city of Zul'Aman upon the monster's bones.",sp=0},{t="A Fractured Legacy",b="With the insect swarms broken and contained, the unity of the Empire of Zul dissolved. Each great faction withdrew into its own sovereign empire, developing distinct cultures, physical traits, and tribal hierarchies adapted to forests, jungles, and frozen wastes. Over millennia of separation, rivalry, and territorial wars against younger races, troll dominion slowly decayed into ruin and insularity. Most remaining troll kingdoms view all other races with fierce, unyielding hostility, clinging bitterly to the ruins of their former greatness. Only the exile jungle trolls of the Darkspear tribe have chosen a new path, joining the Horde alongside orcs and tauren.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Can trolls really regenerate lost limbs?",a="Yes. Trolls possess an extraordinary regenerative physiology that allows them to recover from physical trauma at a speed unmatched by most other mortal races. Over time, a troll can even regrow lost fingers and entire limbs, making them exceptionally resilient in battle.",al={"troll healing","troll limb regrowth","how do trolls heal"},it="power"},{q="What are loa?",a="The loa are the primal deities worshipped by trollkind across virtually every tribe. In reality, they are Azeroth's elusive Wild Gods—powerful animal spirits that trolls honor through offerings, meditation, and ritual magic. Priests and shadow hunters call upon the loa to grant blessings, guidance, and devastating power in battle.",al={"who are loa","troll gods","what do trolls worship"}},{q="What was the Empire of Zul?",a="The Empire of Zul was the grand alliance of all troll tribes, formed roughly sixteen thousand years ago under the guidance of the Zandalari. It united the race to combat the rampaging aqiri insect swarms. After driving the aqir into the continental fringes, the alliance naturally disbanded as tribes founded their own independent kingdoms.",al={"first troll empire","ancient troll kingdom","history of zul"},it="history"},{q="Why are most trolls hostile to players?",a="Most troll tribes, such as the forest trolls of the Amani or the jungle trolls of the Gurubashi, possess ancient, insular cultures that harbor deep hatred for outsiders. Centuries of conflict with encroaching races have made them fiercely protective of their ancestral territories, attacking trespassers on sight.",al={"why are trolls bad","hostile trolls","evil trolls"},it="hostile"},{q="How did the Darkspear join the Horde?",a="The Darkspear jungle trolls were driven from their ancestral lands and trapped on an island under siege by murlocs and sea witches. Warchief Thrall and his orc fleet intervened, saving the Darkspear from annihilation. In gratitude and shared necessity, their chieftain pledged the tribe's loyalty to the newly formed Horde.",al={"why are trolls horde","darkspear horde origin","thrall and trolls"},it="allies"},{q="What is voodoo?",a="Voodoo is an ancient school of spiritual and dark magic practiced by troll witch doctors, shadow hunters, and priests. It weaves together ancestral communion, loa worship, curses, hexes, and potent herbal concoctions to harm foes and empower allies.",al={"troll voodoo","how does voodoo work","witch doctor magic"}},{q="What is the story of the trolls?",a="Trolls are among the oldest native peoples of Azeroth, having risen from the wilds near the Well of Eternity in primordial Kalimdor. Possessing natural agility, ferocious strength, and astonishing regenerative healing, they gathered around the sacred mountains of Zandalar to worship the primal Wild Gods known as loa. Under the Zandalari, ancient troll tribes united into the great Empire of Zul to defeat the insectoid aqiri swarms. In time, that mighty empire splintered into distinct civilizations—including the Gurubashi, Amani, and Drakkari—each establishing sprawling temple cities across the known world.",al={"who are trolls","troll origins","troll history"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do the trolls stand in the world today?",a="The ancient troll empires have largely fractured or fallen into isolation and decline, separated by continents and ancestral rivalries. Most tribes hold fierce loyalty only to their own kind, viewing outsiders with deep hostility. However, not all walk alone. The Darkspear tribe of jungle trolls, having fled the destruction of their island home, now stands as a core member of Thrall's Horde, carving out a new destiny alongside the orcs and tauren in Kalimdor while other tribes cling to their dwindling strongholds.",al={"trolls today","where are the trolls now","current state of trolls"},it="today",add=true},{q="What does it mean for a new adventurer to be a troll?",a="If you venture forth as a troll, you walk in the footsteps of one of the world's most resilient and enduring ancestries. As part of the Darkspear tribe, you carry the cunning, agility, and fierce survival instincts of the jungle, guided by the ancient spirits of the loa and the subtle art of voodoo. Unlike the insular and hostile empires of the past, your people have chosen a new path, offering your strength and mystical heritage to champion the Horde and secure an honorable future for your tribe.",al={"playing a troll","being a darkspear","troll player character"},it="for_you",rc="Troll",add=true}},ang={["race:Troll"]="Your heritage stretches back further than almost any civilization on Azeroth. While your cousins in Zul'Gurub and Zul'Aman cling to bitter, isolationist pride, the Darkspear forge a surviving legacy through the strength of the Horde.",["faction:Horde"]="The cunning, agility, and spiritual strength of the Darkspear trolls provide essential scouts, shadow hunters, and shamans to the warchief's banner.",["race:NightElf"]="Elven history and ancient myths trace bitter rivalries back to troll kingdoms, viewing troll religious voodoo and sacrificial rites with severe wariness.",["class:Priest"]="Troll priesthood is inextricably tied to the loa and shadow hunting, communing directly with animal spirits and primal dark forces rather than the Holy Light."},kw={"amani","aqir","darkspear","drakkari","gurubashi","loa","regeneration","troll","trolls","voodoo","zandalari"},rel={"topic:darkspear-tribe","npc:vol-jin","topic:horde","topic:old-god","topic:alliance","topic:titan"},src={"Troll"}}
E["topic:twilight-s-hammer"] = {t="topic",n="Twilight's Hammer",z="general",s="The Twilight's Hammer is a nihilistic, apocalyptic cult dedicated to awakening the Old Gods and bringing about the end of Azeroth. Originally an orcish clan led by the ogre-mage Cho'gall, it has evolved into a secretive, multiracial order actively conducting dark rituals across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms.",h="The Twilight's Hammer is a nihilistic, apocalyptic cult dedicated to awakening the Old Gods and bringing...",sec={{t="Origins in Shadow",b="The Twilight's Hammer began on Draenor as an exiled clan of Pale orcs residing in the cavernous Underpale beneath Oshu'gun. Communion with Void forces drove them to embrace a grim prophecy: the Hour of Twilight, an inevitable cosmic doom when shadow engulfs all existence. Seeking to bend their terrifying shadow magic to the Horde's war effort, Gul'dan sent the two-headed ogre-mage Cho'gall to lead them. Cho'gall convinced the Pale that the Horde was merely an engine of destruction meant to usher in their beloved apocalypse. Bound by this prophecy, the clan codified their mad teachings into the Twilight Canticle—pages crafted from the flayed, inscribed skin of the Pale themselves.",sp=0},{t="Arrival on Azeroth",b="When the Twilight's Hammer crossed the Dark Portal during the First War, their apocalyptic fanaticism only deepened. In the soil of Azeroth, the cultists sensed the genuine whispers of the Old Gods—ancient horrors chained beneath the earth. Blackhand tolerated their erratic, ritualistic behavior solely because Cho'gall managed their bloodlust, deploying them alongside Kilrogg Deadeye's Bleeding Hollow clan in early assaults on Stormwind Keep. The clan later operated deep within Grim Batol during the Second War, overseeing critical refineries until Gul'dan's betrayal fractured the Horde. Though Orgrim Doomhammer ordered the traitorous clan butchered near the Tomb of Sargeras, Cho'gall and a core cadre of zealots survived and fled west across the Great Sea.",sp=0},{t="A Spreading Cancer",b="Upon reaching the forgotten shores of Kalimdor, the cult transformed from a shattered orc clan into a clandestine, multiracial conspiracy. Seducing disillusioned members of both the Alliance and the Horde, the Twilight's Hammer recruits any soul broken enough to welcome oblivion. In northern Kalimdor, led by Twilight Lord Kelris, cultists have entrenched themselves within the submerged halls of Blackfathom Deeps, feeding sacrifices to Aku'mai, a monstrous pet of the Old Gods. Further north in Darkshore, their excavators disturb the colossal fossilized remains at the Master's Glaive. Across the southern deserts of Silithus, camps of robed fanatics tend abyssal windstones, listening for the stirring of ancient horrors entombed beneath the sands.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Twilight's Hammer trying to achieve?",a="They seek the Hour of Twilight: the total annihilation of all life and order on Azeroth. Rather than seeking conquest or wealth, the cult views oblivion as a sacred blessing and actively works to release the ancient Old Gods chained beneath the world.",al={"what do cultists want","twilights hammer goal","twilight hammer purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Are they an orc clan or a cult?",a="They began as an orc clan on Draenor composed of deranged Pale outcasts led by Cho'gall. After fleeing the Second War, they abandoned clan boundaries and transformed into a secret, multiracial cult, enlisting humans, night elves, dwarves, and anyone willing to worship the Void.",al={"are they orcs","is twilights hammer a clan","twilights hammer races"},it="identity"},{q="Who leads the Twilight's Hammer?",a="The cult was founded and overseen by Cho'gall, a cunning two-headed ogre-mage and former apprentice of Gul'dan. Local chapters operate under regional commanders, such as Twilight Lord Kelris in Blackfathom Deeps.",al={"who is twilight hammer boss","twilights hammer leader","cho'gall"},it="leader"},{q="Why are they in Blackfathom Deeps?",a="Led by Twilight Lord Kelris, the cultists occupied the sunken night elven temple along the Zoram Strand to worship and feed sacrificial victims to Aku'mai, a primordial three-headed hydra favored by the Old Gods.",al={"blackfathom cultists","kelris bfd","what are they doing in ashenvale"},it="place"},{q="What are they doing at the Master's Glaive in Darkshore?",a="The Master's Glaive marks the petrified carcass of Soggoth the Slitherer, a colossal ancient servant of the Old Gods slain during the mythic wars of Kalimdor. Cultists congregate there to commune with its lingering dark energies and seek portents of their masters' awakening.",al={"masters glaive cultists","darkshore twilight hammer","soggoth"},it="place"},{q="Why did Orgrim Doomhammer try to wipe them out?",a="During the height of the Second War, Cho'gall and Gul'dan abandoned the Horde's march on Lordaeron to seek godhood at the Tomb of Sargeras. Furious at the treachery that cost the Horde its victory, Warchief Doomhammer dispatched loyal troops to slaughter the defecting clans.",al={"doomhammer twilights hammer","second war betrayal"},it="history"},{q="Why are the Twilight's Hammer hostile to outsiders?",a="The Twilight's Hammer serves the Old Gods, seeking the utter annihilation of Azeroth and the coming of the Hour of Twilight. They view all outside civilizations—Alliance, Horde, and neutral alike—as obstacles to their apocalyptic grand design. Anyone not pledged to their nihilistic cause is an enemy to be eliminated or sacrificed in their dark rituals.",al={"why is twilight's hammer hostile","twilight hammer attack on sight","why do cultists attack me"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who are the Twilight's Hammer working for?",a="Their true masters are the Old Gods, ancient beings of void and shadow imprisoned deep beneath Azeroth. The cult works to shatter those prisons, awakening entities like C'Thun in Silithus. They also align themselves with the servants of these dark masters, including the elemental lords like Ragnaros the Firelord and ancient horrors like Aku'mai in Blackfathom Deeps.",al={"who do twilight hammer serve","twilight hammer masters","who are twilight hammer allied with"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Where did the Twilight's Hammer clan originate?",a="They began on Draenor as the Pale, outcasts who dwelled beneath Nagrand and worshipped apocalyptic visions of the Void. Gul'dan sent the ogre-mage Cho'gall to bring them into the Old Horde. Convinced that the Horde was the vessel meant to herald the Hour of Twilight, the Pale organized into the Twilight's Hammer clan and crossed through the Dark Portal into Azeroth.",al={"twilight hammer origin","where did twilight hammer come from","history of twilight's hammer"},it="history",add=true},{q="Why is the Twilight's Hammer called that?",a="The name comes from the apocalyptic prophecy cherished by the Pale on Draenor: the Hour of Twilight, a time when darkness will extinguish all life in the universe. Cho'gall convinced the Pale that the Horde would serve as the hammer that smashes existence to bring about that twilight, adopting the title for their clan.",al={"why are they called twilight's hammer","twilight hammer name meaning","origin of twilight hammer name"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["class:Priest"]="The Twilight's Hammer manipulates the same Void and shadow magic that shadow priests draw upon, serving as a dark warning of what happens when communion with the whispers overwhelms mortal sanity.",["class:Warlock"]="Though originally affiliated with Gul'dan's Shadow Council, the Twilight's Hammer abandoned demonic fel mastery in favor of the primordial madness of the Old Gods, making them unpredictable rivals rather than allies.",["faction:Horde"]="The cult is a shameful relic of the Old Horde's darkest days. Thrall's new Horde has rejected their madness entirely and actively executes any zealots attempting to seduce young orcs back into nihilism.",["race:NightElf"]="The defilement of holy sites like Blackfathom Deeps and the Master's Glaive by alien cultists represents a direct spiritual and physical assault on the sacred lands of northern Kalimdor."},kw={"aku'mai","blackfathom deeps","cho'gall","hour of twilight","kelris","master's glaive","old gods","silithus","twilight's hammer","void"},rel={"npc:twilight-lord-kelris","npc:aku-mai","subzone:master-s-glaive","zone:blackfathom","topic:old-god","zone:silithus","topic:shadow-council","npc:orgrim-doomhammer"},src={"Twilight's Hammer"}}
E["topic:varian-wrynn"] = {t="topic",n="Varian Wrynn",z="multi",s="King Varian Wrynn is the rightful ruler of the Kingdom of Stormwind, currently missing after vanishing en route to a diplomatic summit in Theramore. In his absence, his ten-year-old son Anduin wears the crown, guided by Highlord Bolvar Fordragon and Lady Katrana Prestor.",h="King Varian Wrynn is the rightful ruler of the Kingdom of Stormwind, currently missing after vanishing en...",sec={{t="A Boyhood in Ash",b="Varian was just a boy when the First War shattered his life. He watched his father, King Llane Wrynn, die by the hand of the assassin Garona Halforcen as the Old Horde stormed the capital. Rescued by Anduin Lothar, the orphaned prince fled north with the kingdom's survivors to Southshore. In Capital City, King Terenas Menethil II took Varian in like a second son. The young prince grew up in exile alongside Prince Arthas Menethil, bearing the memory of a burned home until the Alliance of Lordaeron broke the Horde and reclaimed Stormwind.",sp=0},{t="A Troubled Restoration",b="Crowned at eighteen, Varian took on the immense task of resurrecting his ruined kingdom. To restore the capital, he commissioned the Stonemasons Guild under Edwin VanCleef. But when the grand work was finished, the corrupt House of Nobles refused to pay the promised wages, and the stonemasons refused to back down. Varian found himself paralyzed between the entrenched nobility and the furious artisans. The dispute erupted into violent riots in the streets of Stormwind, during which a tossed paving stone struck and killed Varian's beloved wife, Queen Tiffin.",sp=0},{t="The Vanished King",b="Devastated by Tiffin's death, Varian spent years in deep melancholy, though he eventually rallied to care for his son, Anduin. Seeking to defuse simmering tensions across the sea, Varian recently departed Stormwind on a private voyage to Theramore Isle for peace talks arranged by Lady Jaina Proudmoore. He never reached the summit. The king disappeared without a trace along the crossing, leaving Stormwind vulnerable, whispers of conspiracy spreading through the court, and the regency in the hands of Highlord Bolvar.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where is King Varian right now?",a="Officially, no one in Stormwind knows. Varian vanished while sailing across the Great Sea to Theramore for secret diplomatic talks. The House of Nobles and the palace guard have concealed the full truth from the public to avoid panic, claiming simply that the king is indisposed.",al={"where is varian","where did the king go","is varian alive","varian location"},it="place"},{q="Who is currently ruling Stormwind in his place?",a="His young son, Anduin Wrynn, holds the throne as the child-king. Because Anduin is only ten years old, real administrative and military authority rests with Highlord Bolvar Fordragon, who acts as Prince Regent, heavily influenced by the royal counselor Lady Katrana Prestor.",al={"who rules stormwind","who is king now","stormwind leader"},it="in_charge"},{q="What happened to Varian's wife?",a="Queen Tiffin was killed during the Stonemason Riots in Stormwind. When negotiations collapsed over unpaid wages for the city's reconstruction, rioting stonemasons hurled stones into the crowd, and a stray rock struck the queen in the head, killing her.",al={"what happened to tiffin","queen of stormwind","how did varian's wife die"}},{q="Why was Varian going to Theramore?",a="Varian was traveling to Theramore Isle at the invitation of Lady Jaina Proudmoore to attend a high-level diplomatic summit aimed at easing hostilities between the Alliance and Thrall's Horde.",al={"why did varian leave","theramore summit","varian peace talks"},it="purpose"},{q="How did Varian escape the First War?",a="After King Llane was assassinated and the Horde burned Stormwind, High Commander Anduin Lothar evacuated Prince Varian and the remaining civilian survivors by sea. They sailed north to Lordaeron, where King Terenas Menethil II took the young prince into his court.",al={"varian first war","young varian","fall of stormwind varian"},it="history"},{q="What was Varian's relationship with Arthas Menethil?",a="They grew up as close companions and rivals in Lordaeron while Varian lived in exile. The two princes often sparred together. However, Arthas's descent into madness, the murder of his father Terenas, and his betrayal of Lordaeron turned that past friendship into bitter hatred.",al={"did varian know arthas","varian and arthas","were arthas and varian friends"},it="ties"},{q="Who is Varian Wrynn?",a="King Varian Wrynn is the rightful ruler of the Kingdom of Stormwind and the heir of the House of Wrynn. As a boy, he witnessed the fall of his kingdom and the assassination of his father, King Llane, during the First War. Exiled to Lordaeron, he returned years later after the Alliance reclaimed his homeland, taking the throne at eighteen to rebuild Stormwind. Recently, however, the king vanished under mysterious circumstances while traveling on a diplomatic voyage, leaving the kingdom in turmoil.",al={"who is varian","king varian","king of stormwind","who is king wrynn"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Who are Varian Wrynn's allies?",a="Varian stands at the heart of the Grand Alliance. His closest counselors and protectors include Highlord Bolvar Fordragon, who rules as regent during his absence, and the royal advisor Lady Katrana Prestor. His greatest historical bonds lie with the kingdoms of the Alliance of Lordaeron, fostered by King Terenas Menethil II, who fostered young Varian after Stormwind's fall. As Stormwind's king, he commands its armies and knights.",al={"varian allies","who does varian work with","varian wrynn faction"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["race:Human"]="For humans of Stormwind, Varian is the beloved son of King Llane who returned from exile to rebuild their home; his sudden disappearance casts a shadow of dread over the entire realm.",["class:Warrior"]="Trained by veteran knights and hardened by the horrors of the First War, King Varian is admired across the Alliance as a formidable warrior and a fierce martial leader.",["faction:Alliance"]="Varian's absence leaves the Alliance without its strongest southern monarch, creating a political vacuum that emboldens dissidents and threatens border security from Westfall to Redridge."},kw={"anduin","king","missing","stonemasons","stormwind","theramore","tiffin","varian","wrynn"},rel={"zone:stormwind","topic:stormwind-kingdom","topic:house-of-nobles","topic:anduin-lothar","topic:arthas-menethil","topic:terenas-menethil-ii"},src={"Varian Wrynn"}}
E["topic:venture-company"] = {t="topic",n="Venture Company",z="general",s="The Venture Company is a notoriously ruthless goblin cartel focused on aggressive resource extraction, heavy industrial machinery, and logging. Unaligned with the major goblin ports like Booty Bay or Ratchet, the cartel strip-mines and clear-cuts territories across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms without regard for local populations or the natural world.",h="The Venture Company is a notoriously ruthless goblin cartel focused on aggressive resource extraction, heavy...",sec={{t="Industrial Plunder",b="Led overall by Mogul Razdunk from afar, the Venture Company operates by a simple corporate philosophy: if it can be chopped down, drilled into, or blasted out of a mountain, they will exploit it. The cartel deploys some of the most advanced heavy machinery on Azeroth, including massive lumber-harvesting shredders, drilling rigs, and industrial refineries. Unlike traditional merchant houses that focus on shipping or retail, the Venture Company specializes in seizing raw resources by force, ignoring treaties, borders, and local sovereign rights.",sp=0},{t="Encroaching on Kalimdor",b="The cartel's rapid expansion into Kalimdor has earned them widespread enmity. In Mulgore, Supervisor Fizsprocket pushed drilling and mining rigs into the eastern bluffs despite being formally denied mineral rights by the tauren, polluting pristine water wells across the plains. Farther north, the company turned the Barrens' Sludge Fen into a chemical sewer to power oil pumps, while claiming the Horde's former Boulder Lode Mine and running foul experiments with samophlanges and toxic brews. In Stonetalon's Windshear Crag, logging operations under the mad leper gnome Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle have decimated whole groves with colossal Super Reaper 6000 machines.",sp=1},{t="Corporate Rivalries and Shady Contracts",b="The Venture Company is widely despised even by fellow goblins. Baron Revilgaz of Booty Bay frequently contracts out strikes against their base camp and oil rig at Lake Nazferiti, seeking both to thwart their territorial encroachment and steal their cutting-edge shredder fuel regulators. The Tinkers' Union in Ratchet openly runs corporate espionage against them to pilfer engineering blueprints. Worse still, SI:7 and the Shattered Hand have discovered the company trafficking dangerous alchemical compounds: selling Zanzil's deadly mind-altering poisons to Defias necromancers in Westfall and utilizing toxic fumes to breed mutated drone laborers.",sp=2}},faq={{q="Who leads the Venture Company?",a="The president of the cartel is Mogul Razdunk, who governs the organization's overarching corporate ventures from afar. Day-to-day work sites across Azeroth are overseen by regional supervisors, foremen, and hired overseers such as Supervisor Fizsprocket in Mulgore, Supervisor Lugwizzle at the Sludge Fen, and Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle in Stonetalon.",al={"venture co leader","venture company boss","who runs venture co","mogul razdunk"},it="leader"},{q="Why does the Horde hate the Venture Company?",a="The cartel operates illegal, aggressive resource sites on ancestral Horde lands. In Mulgore, they invaded the eastern mountains without permission and fouled the sacred water wells of the tauren. In the Barrens, they seized the Horde's Boulder Lode Mine and polluted the Sludge Fen into a noxious oil slick.",al={"tauren venture co","why horde hate venture co","mulgore venture company"},it="conflict"},{q="Are they affiliated with the Steamwheedle Cartel or Booty Bay?",a="No. The Venture Company is a separate, predatory organization. They are fierce corporate enemies of the Steamwheedle Cartel; Baron Revilgaz of Booty Bay frequently hires mercenaries to disrupt their logging in Stranglethorn Vale, and Sputtervalve of Ratchet regularly sends operatives to sabotage their shredders and steal their blueprints.",al={"are they steamwheedle","venture co booty bay","ratchet venture co"},it="ties"},{q="Why is a leper gnome running operations in Windshear Crag?",a="Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle was hired by the Venture Company for his ruthless mechanical genius, designing the massive Super Reaper 6000 logging machine. His hiring caused an uproar among the cartel's goblin engineers, such as Ziz Fizziks, and even angered the Trade Princes in Undermine, who disapproved of Razdunk consorting with the outcast gnome.",al={"gerenzo wrenchwhistle","gnome in stonetalon","why leper gnome venture"}},{q="What is the Venture Company doing in Westfall?",a="The Venture Company had an illicit trading arrangement with the Defias Brotherhood. They acquired Zanzilli poisons and sold the mixture to Defias operative Klaven Mortwake in Westfall, who used the toxic concoctions to manufacture mindless Defias Drones similar to the laborers found in Venture Company operations.",al={"venture co westfall","klaven mortwake venture co","venture defias"},sp=1,it="why_here"},{q="What is being produced at the Sludge Fen?",a="The cartel erected an industrial drilling rig directly atop what used to be a clear natural spring, pumping and refining oil while dumping toxic sludge across the northern Barrens. In addition to oil, researchers there conduct tests on complex mechanical samophlanges and synthesize toxic mixtures.",al={"sludge fen oil","barrens venture co oil rig","samophlange sludge fen"},it="purpose"},{q="What is the Venture Company?",a="The Venture Company is a ruthless, goblin-run cartel dedicated to plundering Azeroth's natural resources for maximum profit. Specializing in heavy machinery, mining, and clear-cutting, their philosophy is simple: if you can dig it up, chop it down, or blow it out of a mountain, they will. They operate entirely outside local laws and traditional ethics, using shredders, drills, and armed enforcers to extract wealth from wilderness frontiers.",al={"who are venture co","what is venture company","venture co lore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why is the Venture Company hostile to travelers?",a="The Venture Company operates illegal, aggressive resource grabs across contested and sovereign lands, from the sacred hills of Mulgore to the forests of Stonetalon. Because they routinely trespass, steal claims, and cut out competitors, their operations are heavily fortified. Their foremen view any outsider as an industrial spy, a rival claimant, or a vengeful local come to sabotage their machinery, and they instruct their enforcers to shoot first.",al={"why does venture co attack","venture company aggro","why is venture company hostile"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who are the Venture Company allied with?",a="The Venture Company primarily works for its own profit, but it frequently cuts shady deals with outside factions when coin is on the table. They have established under-the-table trade with the Defias Brotherhood, selling them Zanzil-derived alchemical mixtures and toxic concoctions. Aside from such mercenary transactions, they maintain few friends, routinely stepping on the toes of neutral cartels like the Steamwheedle Cartel and drawing outrage from the trade princes in Undermine.",al={"venture co allies","who does venture company work with","venture co partnerships"},it="allies",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Venture Company invaded the sacred hills of Mulgore without permission, poisoning ancestral water wells and clear-cutting timber in defiance of the Earth Mother.",["class:Druid"]="Their heavy clear-cutting in Stonetalon Mountains and industrial pollution of natural springs in Mulgore and the Barrens represent blatant, unchecked desecration of the land.",["class:Rogue"]="Both the Shattered Hand and SI:7 keep close watch on the cartel's poison trafficking, infiltrating their towers and sabotaging their high-level foremen to acquire their deadly chemical mixtures.",["profession:Engineering"]="The cartel manufactures some of the finest, most advanced shredders and mechanical regulators on Azeroth, making their blueprints prized targets for corporate theft."},kw={"fizsprocket","gerenzo","puzik gallywix","razdunk","samophlange","shredder","sludge fen","super reaper","venture co","venture company","windshear crag"},rel={"zone:stonetalon","zone:barrens","zone:mulgore","zone:stranglethorn","topic:defias-brotherhood","topic:goblin","quest:764","quest:1093"},src={"Venture Company"}}
E["topic:war-of-the-ancients"] = {t="topic",n="War of the Ancients",z="general",s="Ten thousand years ago, the reckless sorcery of Queen Azshara and her Highborne tore a portal through the Well of Eternity, unleashing the demonic Burning Legion onto Azeroth. Led by the young druid Malfurion Stormrage, Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind, and commander Jarod Shadowsong, a desperate coalition of mortals and ancient forest demigods repelled the invaders. The resulting destruction of the Well triggered the Great Sundering, violently shattering the ancient continent of Kalimdor and permanently altering the face of the world.",h="Ten thousand years ago, the reckless sorcery of Queen Azshara and her Highborne tore a portal through the...",sec={{t="The Highborne's Folly",b="At the height of the ancient Kaldorei Empire, Queen Azshara and her elite caste of sorcerers, the Highborne, relentlessly drew upon the vast arcane depths of the Well of Eternity. Their reckless manipulation sent massive ripples into the Great Dark Beyond, attracting Sargeras, the master of the Burning Legion. Entranced by promises of ultimate power and perfection, Azshara and her advisor Xavius opened a trans-dimensional rift within the Well. Legions of felhounds, felguard, doomguard, and monstrous commanders poured into the capital city of Zin-Azshari, slaughtering all non-Highborne and beginning their crusade to purge the world of life.",sp=0},{t="The Kaldorei Resistance",b="Horrified by the slaughter, night elves outside the Highborne caste rallied under the seasoned general Lord Kur'talos Ravencrest. The resistance was bolstered by remarkable heroes: the first mortal druid Malfurion Stormrage, the devout priestess of Elune Tyrande Whisperwind, and Malfurion's gifted brother Illidan. When imperial forces assassinated Ravencrest and the demon armies pressed hard, command fell to the brilliant tactician Jarod Shadowsong. Realizing the elves could not survive alone, Jarod reached across old racial boundaries, recruiting the earthen, the furbolgs, and the mountain tauren into a unified frontline against the Legion's onslaught.",sp=0},{t="The Price of Victory",b="The war escalated to world-shattering proportions when the demigod Cenarius rallied the ancient Wild Gods and dragonflights into the fray. Determined to stop Sargeras himself from stepping foot onto Azeroth, Malfurion and the resistance made a desperate push to collapse the portal over the Well of Eternity. Their desperate gambit succeeded in casting the demonic host back into the Twisting Nether, but the strain proved too great. The Well violently imploded, triggering the Great Sundering that shattered Kalimdor into separate continents and flooded the center of the world, birthing the Great Sea and forever marking Kaldorei culture.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What caused the War of the Ancients?",a="Queen Azshara and her Highborne sorcerers relentlessly experimented with the arcane energies of the Well of Eternity. Their spells sent waves across the cosmos that attracted the dark titan Sargeras, who convinced the Highborne to open a gateway within the Well so the Burning Legion could enter Azeroth.",al={"why did the war start","who caused the war of the ancients","well of eternity portal"},it="origin"},{q="What was the Great Sundering?",a="The Great Sundering was the cataclysm that ended the war. To stop Sargeras from entering the world, the resistance collapsed the portal inside the Well of Eternity. The backlash caused the Well to violently implode, tearing the single supercontinent of Kalimdor apart into separate landmasses and forming the Great Sea.",al={"what is the sundering","how did kalimdor break","well of eternity explosion"},it="conflict"},{q="Who led the mortal forces against the Legion?",a="Lord Kur'talos Ravencrest initially rallied the resistance. Following his death on the battlefield, commander Jarod Shadowsong took over supreme leadership, directing the combined ground forces alongside crucial battlefield heroes including Malfurion Stormrage and Tyrande Whisperwind.",al={"who led the resistance","kaldorei resistance leaders"},it="leader"},{q="Did other races fight alongside the night elves?",a="Yes. Breaking long-held elven prejudices, commander Jarod Shadowsong accepted the aid of several mortal races, uniting earthen, furbolg tribes, and mountain tauren against the demonic invasion alongside the woodland demigod Cenarius and his wild brethren.",al={"tauren in war of the ancients","who helped the night elves"},it="allies"},{q="What happened to Queen Azshara during the war?",a="Azshara remained inside her palace at Zin-Azshari until the bitter end, obsessed with bringing Sargeras into Azeroth. When the Well of Eternity imploded and the oceans swallowed the capital, the queen and her surviving Highborne loyalists sank into the churning depths.",al={"where is azshara","did queen azshara die"}},{q="Why is arcane magic mistrusted by night elves today?",a="The Kaldorei witnessed firsthand how the Highborne's unchecked thirst for arcane magic summoned the Burning Legion and obliterated ancient Kalimdor. In the aftermath, the night elves strictly outlawed arcane sorcery, turning instead to druidism and faith in Elune to protect their lands.",al={"why do night elves hate mages","kaldorei magic ban"},it="today"},{q="What was the War of the Ancients?",a="The War of the Ancients was Azeroth's first major war against the demonic Burning Legion, fought roughly ten thousand years ago. Corrupted by promises of godlike power, Queen Azshara and her Highborne cast spells through the Well of Eternity to bring Sargeras and his demonic legions into the world. The night elf resistance, joined by early mortal races, the dragonflights, and the Wild Gods, fought desperately to halt the invasion, culminating in the destruction of the Well and the Great Sundering.",al={"what is war of the ancients","war of the ancients explained","first legion invasion"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why is it called the War of the Ancients?",a="The conflict earned its name both from the ancient era in which it took place—ten millennia before the modern age—and the pivotal role played by the Ancients themselves. Demigods and Wild Gods like Cenarius, alongside the ancient dragonflights, marched to war alongside mortal defenders to save Azeroth from demonic annihilation.",al={"why called war of the ancients","name war of the ancients meaning","war of the ancients name"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:NightElf"]="The war shaped every aspect of modern Kaldorei society, explaining their deep mistrust of arcane magic, their reverence for Cenarius, and the enduring leadership of Malfurion and Tyrande.",["class:Druid"]="The conflict marks the birth of mortal druidism, when Malfurion Stormrage learned the ways of the wild from Cenarius and proved that the power of nature could defeat cosmic ruin.",["class:Mage"]="Highborne reckless arrogance during the war serves as the ultimate historical warning across Azeroth about the catastrophic dangers of unchecked arcane draw.",["race:Tauren"]="Tauren warriors preserve the proud memory of standing shoulder-to-shoulder with forest gods and ancient mortals to drive the Legion back ten millennia ago.",["class:Priest"]="Tyrande Whisperwind's mastery of Elune's grace during the war elevated the Priesthood of the Moon into the heart of night elven military and civic leadership."},kw={"azshara","burning legion","cenarius","highborne","jarod shadowsong","malfurion","ravencrest","sargeras","sundering","tyrande","war of the ancients","well of eternity"},rel={"topic:burning-legion","npc:malfurion-stormrage","npc:tyrande-whisperwind","topic:cenarius","topic:highborne","topic:night-elf","topic:sargeras","zone:azshara"},src={"War of the Ancients"}}
E["topic:warlock"] = {t="topic",n="Warlock",z="general",s="Warlocks are spellcasters who bargain with dark powers, commanding demons, debilitating curses, and devastating fire and shadow magics. While deeply feared and distrusted by nearly every civilized society on Azeroth, some wield these forbidden arts in defense of their factions, turning the weapons of the Burning Legion against its own agents.",h="Warlocks are spellcasters who bargain with dark powers, commanding demons, debilitating curses, and...",sec={{t="Forbidden Arts",b="Where ordinary spellcasters see corruption and ruin, warlocks see opportunity. They achieve victory through calculated cruelty, striking from a distance while cloaked in shadow and fire. By siphoning the vital essences and souls of their enemies, warlocks fuel their most catastrophic rituals, raining brimstone from the sky and breaking minds with agonizing curses. Because they wear only simple cloth and possess little physical resilience, they rely on summoned horrors to intercept danger, willingly sacrificing demonic thralls to ensure their own survival.",sp=0},{t="Demonic Servitude",b="A warlock's authority over the Twisting Nether begins with petty bargains and grows into iron domination. Novices learn to command mischievous imps to hurl flame, but as a practitioner's dark understanding deepens, they bind far deadlier entities: voidwalkers torn from shadowy depths to absorb physical punishment, seductive succubi capable of beguiling enemy minds, and ravenous felhunters that devour magical energies. Each summon demands dangerous ritual focus, for the Nether's denizens constantly test their masters for any flicker of weakness.",sp=0},{t="A Legacy of Ruin",b="The roots of orcish demonology trace back to Draenor, where the demon lord Kil'jaeden seduced the orc shaman Gul'dan with the promise of supreme power. Gul'dan founded the secretive Shadow Council and transformed many shaman—starting with warriors of the Blackrock clan—into masters of fel magic. The rampant use of demonic magic withered Draenor's fertile plains into barren red wasteland, heralding the Old Horde's invasion through the Dark Portal. In human lands, the Kirin Tor long executed those who dabbled in forbidden demon-worship, yet ambitious mages regularly succumbed to the lure of untamed sorcery.",sp=0},{t="Tolerated Pariahs",b="In the modern era, warlocks walk a razor's edge between utility and execution. In Stormwind, practitioners meet hidden away in gloomy tavern basements, far from the watchful eyes of the Cathedral of Light. In Orgrimmar, they cluster in the shadowed chasms of the Cleft of Shadows, kept under scrutiny by Thrall's shamanistic regime. While their methods invite revulsion, neither Alliance nor Horde can completely dismiss the devastating edge warlock magic offers on a hostile battlefield.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What makes a warlock different from a mage?",a="Mages draw upon the orderly structure of arcane magic to manipulate fire, frost, and energy. Warlocks disregard safe limits, diving directly into chaotic fel magic, shadow, and demonic summoning. Many warlocks began their careers as mages who grew impatient with academic restrictions and sought forbidden shortcuts to overwhelming power.",al={"warlock vs mage","difference between warlock and mage","mage or warlock"}},{q="Are all warlocks evil?",a="Not all warlocks are inherently wicked, but their magic certainly is. While many fall into madness or join demonic cults, player warlocks often pledge their loyalty to their faction, turning dark sorcery, stolen souls, and bound demons against external threats like the Scourge or the Burning Legion.",al={"are warlocks evil","can warlocks be good","why does the alliance allow warlocks"}},{q="How do warlocks summon and control demons?",a="Warlocks use ritual circles, dark incantations, and soul energy to pull creatures out of the Twisting Nether. Once summoned, the demon must be broken and bound to the caster's will through sheer mental dominance. Should a warlock's concentration slip, the demon will happily slaughter its master.",al={"how do warlock pets work","demonic summoning lore","binding demons"}},{q="Where did orc warlocks come from?",a="Orc warlocks were created on Draenor when Kil'jaeden manipulated Gul'dan into abandoning shamanism for demonic sorcery. Gul'dan formed the Shadow Council and trained other orcs, primarily from the Blackrock clan, spreading fel corruption across their homeland until Draenor's soil withered and died.",al={"how did orcs become warlocks","gul'dan warlocks","orc fel magic origin"},it="origin"},{q="Why do the Alliance and Horde tolerate warlocks in their cities?",a="Neither faction truly trusts them. In Stormwind and Ironforge, warlocks practice in secret basements and forgotten tunnels to avoid public outrage. In Orgrimmar, they are sequestered in the Cleft of Shadows. Leaders tolerate them purely because their terrifying curses and summonings are undeniably effective weapons in wartime.",al={"why are warlocks allowed","are warlocks legal","warlock legality"},it="place"},{q="What are soul shards used for?",a="Warlocks drain the living essence of defeated enemies to forge crystallized soul shards. These soul fragments serve as potent batteries, providing the raw energy needed to bypass natural magical laws, summon monstrous demons, and cast their most destructive spells.",al={"why do warlocks take souls","soul shard lore","harvesting souls"},it="item"},{q="What is a Warlock in the lore?",a="Warlocks are insatiable spellcasters who pursue forbidden, destructive sorcery where others see only ruin. Unafraid to delve into dark arts and peer into the Twisting Nether and the Void, they dominate demonic minions, afflict enemies with crippling curses and diseases, and incinerate foes with hellfire. Though many succumb to corruption or serve the Burning Legion, some choose to turn these profane powers against Azeroth's enemies, maintaining uneasy alliances while hiding in the shadows.",al={"what is a warlock","warlock lore","warlock class lore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does a Warlock's power come from?",a="A warlock draws upon destructive fel energy, shadow magic, and chaotic fire, often commanding denizens pulled straight from the Twisting Nether. Their greatest sorceries are fueled directly by the stolen vitality of their victims, tearing away and consuming mortal souls to replenish the dreadful energy expended in casting. Through dark pacts and sheer force of will, they enslave otherworldly beings and channel raw entropy to devastate their opposition.",al={"where do warlocks get their magic","warlock power source","how do warlocks get power"},it="power",add=true},{q="What does it mean for a new adventurer to become a Warlock?",a="Choosing the path of a warlock means trading universal trust for absolute dominance and forbidden power. You begin by mastering dark curses and binding minor imps, gradually bending more terrible entities—voidwalkers, succubi, and felhunters—to your command. Because most societies fear and despise demonic sorcery, you will practice your craft in secluded cellars, covens, or subterranean caverns, relying on cunning and summoned servants to endure battle while weakening foes from afar.",al={"becoming a warlock","why become a warlock","choosing warlock"},it="for_you",add=true}},ang={["class:Mage"]="Mages view warlocks with acute contempt and fear, seeing them as undisciplined sorcerers whose reckless dealings with the Nether endanger all mortal life.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman despise warlock magic above all else, remembering how Gul'dan's fel sorcery severed their ancestors' ties to the spirits and killed Draenor's land.",["race:Orc"]="For orcs, practicing demonology carries the bitter stain of the Old Horde's blood curse, though some retain the arts to survive in a hostile world.",["class:Paladin"]="Devotees of the Holy Light consider demonic summoning an abominable heresy and monitor practicing warlocks with open hostility."},kw={"affliction","demonology","destruction","fel","felhunter","gul'dan","imp","shadow council","succubus","twisting nether","voidwalker","warlock"},rel={"topic:burning-legion","topic:shadow-council","topic:first-war","topic:blackrock-clan","topic:mage","topic:shaman","topic:light"},src={"Warlock"}}
E["topic:warrior"] = {t="topic",n="Warrior",z="general",s="Warriors are the quintessential frontline combatants of Azeroth, mastering heavy plate armor, shields, and nearly every form of martial weaponry. Fueling their most devastating strikes with pure combat rage, they charge into the thick of battle to defend weaker allies or carve through enemy ranks. They are found across every race and civilization, serving as everything from stalwart city guards and royal champions to wandering mercenaries.",h="Warriors are the quintessential frontline combatants of Azeroth, mastering heavy plate armor, shields, and...",sec={{t="Masters of Steel and Shield",b="Warriors rely neither on divine prayers nor arcane incantations. Their power stems entirely from physical endurance, battlefield discipline, and an exhaustive mastery of weaponry. Clad in the heaviest plate mail, they dictate the frontline. Some fight behind towering shields to withstand punishing assaults and draw peril away from spellcasters and archers. Others step forward with two-handed polearms, axes, and greatswords, or wade into melee wielding a blade in each hand to cleave through whole formations.",sp=0},{t="The Power of Battle Rage",b="In the furnace of battle, pain and blood ignite a warrior's inner fire. Known simply as rage, this resource swells as warriors inflict and absorb punishment. Rather than yielding to exhaustion or bleeding wounds, seasoned fighters channel this combat fury directly into bone-shattering strikes, sweeping whirlwinds of steel, and deafening battle cries that bolster nearby comrades while shattering enemy morale.",sp=0},{t="A Universal Tradition",b="Unlike classes tied to specific creeds or elemental patrons, the warrior tradition is universal to every race on Azeroth. Stormwind relies on the disciplined knight-champions of the Brotherhood of the Horse, Ironforge marshals its stout Forgeguard, and the Horde rallies around orcish blademasters, tauren braves, and Forsaken Deathguards. Whether driven by honor, territorial defense, or cold coin, those who answer the call of arms form the unbroken backbone of every standing army.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What powers a warrior if they do not use magic?",a="Warriors rely on martial skill, tactical training, and rage. Rage builds directly from the clash of combat—both by striking enemies and absorbing incoming blows. This surging combat adrenaline fuels their most devastating maneuvers, such as sweeping weapon strikes, concussive shield bashes, and intimidating shouts.",al={"how do warriors fight","warrior resource","what is rage"},it="power"},{q="What races can become warriors?",a="Every playable race in Azeroth can be a warrior. The need for heavily armored frontline soldiers exists across all mortal cultures, from the stoic dwarven defenders of Khaz Modan to the savage orcish shock troops of Durotar.",al={"who can be warrior","warrior races","can every race be warrior"},it="people"},{q="What role do warriors serve in a combat party?",a="Warriors typically lead from the front. Stalwart protection warriors use shields and plate armor to absorb punishment and keep dangerous foes focused on them. Offensive arms and fury warriors instead bring crushing two-handed weapons or paired blades to overwhelm enemies through brutal physical damage.",al={"warrior role","do warriors tank","arms vs fury vs prot"},it="purpose"},{q="What weapons are warriors able to wield?",a="Warriors are masters of almost every weapon known to mortal warfare. They can wield swords, maces, axes, polearms, daggers, and staves, in both one-handed and two-handed grips, as well as heavy shields and ranged weapons like bows, guns, and crossbows.",al={"warrior weapons","can warriors use bows","what can warriors equip"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Who are some famous warriors in Azeroth's history?",a="Renowned warriors include Lord Anduin Lothar, the Champion of Stormwind and Supreme Commander of the Alliance of Lordaeron; Grommash Hellscream, chieftain of the Warsong clan; and Cairne Bloodhoof, High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes.",al={"famous warriors","legendary warriors","who are warrior heroes"},it="fame"},{q="Where do new warriors receive their formal training?",a="Warriors train anywhere soldiers gather. Human recruits drill in Northshire Abbey, dwarves and gnomes learn at Anvilmar in Coldridge Valley, night elves train in Shadowglen, orcs and trolls practice in the Valley of Trials, and Forsaken fighters report to the Deathknell barracks.",al={"warrior trainers","where do warriors learn","warrior trainer locations"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is a warrior?",a="Warriors are the quintessential frontline combatants of Azeroth, relying on sheer physical prowess, mastery of arms, and heavy armor rather than mystical arts. They come from every culture and background, serving as stalwart city guards, fearsome berserkers, mercenary soldiers, and seasoned commanders. Equipped with shields and a vast array of weaponry, warriors charge directly into the fray to draw the enemy's wrath and shield their more fragile companions from harm.",al={"what is a warrior in lore","warrior lore identity","who are warriors"},it="identity",add=true,nx={8}},{q="What does it mean to walk the path of a warrior?",a="Choosing the warrior's life means dedicating yourself to constant training, discipline, and uncompromising martial perfection. You step onto the battlefield without spells to shield you, putting your faith entirely in heavy plate, your chosen steel, and your instincts. Whether seeking glory, fortune as a mercenary, or defending your people from looming perils, you are the living wall that meets danger head-on, turning raw combat fury into decisive strikes.",al={"becoming a warrior","what does it mean to be a warrior","life of a warrior"},it="for_you",add=true}},ang={["class:Warrior"]="Every battle you fight tests your stamina and nerve; by turning the pain of incoming blows into pure rage, you control the front line.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Warriors rely entirely on blacksmiths to hammer out the heavy plate mail, sturdy bucklers, and honed blades necessary to survive mortal combat.",["faction:Alliance"]="The footmen and knights of the Alliance form the defensive vanguard protecting human kingdoms, dwarven mountain holds, and elven forests.",["faction:Horde"]="For the Horde, martial prowess is a way of life, with grunt shock troops and tauren warriors leading every territorial advance."},kw={"arms","berserker","fury","melee","plate","protection","rage","shield","vanguard","warrior"},rel={"npc:thrall","topic:alliance","topic:horde","topic:tauren","topic:first-war","topic:second-war","topic:third-war","subzone:anvilmar"},src={"Warrior"}}
E["topic:warsong-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Warsong clan",z="general",s="The Warsong clan is one of the most ferocious and legendary fighting forces among the orcs, formerly led by the revered chieftain Grommash Hellscream. Having survived exile, the internment camps, and demonic corruption, the clan now operates primarily in Ashenvale as the Warsong Outriders, fiercely securing lumber for the fledgling Horde while clashing ceaselessly with the night elves.",h="The Warsong clan is one of the most ferocious and legendary fighting forces among the orcs, formerly led by...",sec={{t="A Legacy of Defiance",b="Long before crossing into Azeroth, the Warsong forged their reputation across the open plains of Draenor through mobile wolf-rider tactics, breaking ogre empires through sheer tenacity. Their violent temperament was so feared that Warchief Blackhand kept them behind during the First War to prevent erratic rampages. They finally surged through the Dark Portal under Grommash Hellscream after the Second War, only to be stranded on Azeroth when Draenor collapsed. While other orcs languished in Alliance internment camps, the Warsong evaded capture, wandering the wilderness and clinging to their warlike spirit until Thrall united with Hellscream to forge the New Horde.",sp=0},{t="Blood and Redemption",b="Upon arriving in Kalimdor during the Third War, Thrall ordered Hellscream and his clan into Ashenvale to harvest timber and establish an outpost. The intrusion enraged the night elves and the forest demigod Cenarius. Pushed to the brink of slaughter, the Warsong succumbed once more to the pit lord Mannoroth's demonic blood to prevail, slaying Cenarius in a frenzy of fel corruption. Thrall and his allies managed to purge Hellscream of the curse, culminating in a fateful showdown where Grom gave his life to destroy Mannoroth. His heroic sacrifice broke the demon blood curse forever, cementing the Warsong clan's place in Horde history.",sp=0},{t="The War for Ashenvale",b="In the present day, the clan continues Grommash's mission under the banner of the Warsong Outriders. From strongholds like Splintertree Post and active logging encampments, they harvest timber crucial for building the young Horde nation in the arid wastes of Durotar. Their presence has sparked a bitter territorial war with the night elven Silverwing Sentinels. Driven by their ancestral zeal, the Warsong show no sign of relinquishing Warsong Gulch, cutting deep into the ancient forest despite endless ambushes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who leads the Warsong clan right now?",a="Following the death of Grommash Hellscream at the end of the Third War, the Warsong clan is largely integrated into Thrall's New Horde. Their operational forces in the field, particularly the Warsong Outriders in Ashenvale, answer to local commanders and the overall authority of Warchief Thrall.",al={"warsong leader","who is warsong chief","current warsong chieftain"},it="leader"},{q="Why are the Warsong logging in Ashenvale?",a="The New Horde is based in the arid plains of Durotar and the Barrens, lands barren of substantial timber. The Warsong were sent into the rich forests of Ashenvale to gather lumber essential for constructing cities like Orgrimmar, maintaining military fortifications, and sustaining the expanding Horde.",al={"why harvest ashenvale","warsong lumber","orcs cutting trees ashenvale"},it="why_here"},{q="How did the Warsong kill Cenarius?",a="During the Third War, the demigod Cenarius led the night elves in a brutal defense of Ashenvale, driving the Warsong into desperation. The clan drank from a pool tainted with the blood of the pit lord Mannoroth, transforming into fel orcs whose empowered fury was potent enough to overwhelm and slay Cenarius.",al={"cenarius death","who killed cenarius","warsong vs cenarius"}},{q="How did the Warsong escape the internment camps after the Second War?",a="Unlike many orc clans that were captured when the Second War ended, Grommash Hellscream and the Warsong evaded Alliance capture by hiding in the remote wilderness of Lordaeron. They waged guerrilla warfare and maintained their fighting spirit until Thrall found them.",al={"warsong internment camps","grom evasion","warsong escape"}},{q="What is Warsong Gulch?",a="Warsong Gulch is a canyon valley in southern Ashenvale where the Warsong Outriders clash directly with the night elven Silverwing Sentinels. The Outriders fight to secure their lumber operations, while the Sentinels battle to drive the orcs completely out of their sacred woodland.",al={"wsg lore","what is warsong gulch","warsong battlefield"}},{q="Who was Grommash Hellscream?",a="Grommash 'Grom' Hellscream was the legendary chieftain of the Warsong clan and a mentor to Thrall. Though he was the first orc to drink the demon blood on Draenor, he ultimately redeemed himself and freed all orcs from demonic bondage by slaying the pit lord Mannoroth, sacrificing his life in the deed.",al={"who is grom","grom hellscream","grommash"}},{q="What is the Warsong clan?",a="The Warsong clan is a fiercely martial orcish clan renowned for its aggressive nature and mobile wolf riders. Long led by the legendary chieftain Grom Hellscream, the clan now serves as an integral pillar of Thrall's Horde, spearheading logging and military campaigns in the dense forests of Ashenvale.",al={"who are the warsong clan","who are the warsong","warsong clan info"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where did the Warsong clan come from?",a="The Warsong originally hailed from the fertile plains of Nagrand on Draenor, where they fought constant skirmishes against the Gorian Empire's ogres. Kept behind during the First War due to their wild bloodlust, they eventually crossed through the reopened Dark Portal under Grom Hellscream. Evading human internment camps after the Second War, they later followed Thrall across the Great Sea to Kalimdor.",al={"warsong clan history","warsong origin","where are the warsong from"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who is the Warsong clan allied with?",a="The Warsong clan is fully pledged to Thrall's New Horde. They fight shoulder-to-shoulder with their fellow orcs, the Darkspear trolls, the tauren of Mulgore, and the newly admitted Forsaken, harvesting vital lumber to supply the Horde's growing settlements across Durotar and the Barrens.",al={"warsong allies","who do the warsong fight for","warsong faction"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Why is the Warsong clan hostile to Alliance travelers?",a="As a central military force of the Horde, the Warsong clan views members of the Alliance as mortal enemies. Their deep-seated hatred is further inflamed in Ashenvale, where night elven Silverwing Sentinels and their allies fiercely contest the clan's aggressive clear-cutting of the sacred ancient woodlands.",al={"why do warsong attack","why are warsong hostile","are warsong enemy"},it="hostile",add=true}},ang={["race:Orc"]="The Warsong represent both the greatest shame and highest redemption of the orcish people: the first to drink the blood of Mannoroth, but also the clan whose chieftain died to shatter the demon's hold over all orcs.",["race:NightElf"]="The Warsong are viewed with intense hatred by the night elves, not only for deforesting sacred woodlands in Ashenvale, but for the unforgivable slaughter of the demigod Cenarius.",["faction:Horde"]="Without the lumber harvested by the Warsong Outriders in Ashenvale, the New Horde would lack the raw materials necessary to fortify its settlements and expand its holdings in Kalimdor.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Warsong embody the unrelenting aggression of the orcs, proving to many in the Alliance that peace between the two factions remains impossible so long as their axes swing in Ashenvale."},kw={"ashenvale","cenarius","grommash","hellscream","mannoroth","outriders","splintertree","warsong"},rel={"topic:grommash-hellscream","zone:ashenvale","npc:thrall","topic:cenarius","topic:new-horde","npc:warsong-outrider","subzone:splintertree-post","topic:sentinels"},src={"Warsong clan"}}
E["topic:wildhammer-clan"] = {t="topic",n="Wildhammer clan",z="general",s="The Wildhammer clan is a rugged, nature-dwelling branch of the dwarven race famed for taming wild gryphons and wielding storm-charged hammers. Driven from their ancestral fortress of Grim Batol centuries ago, they carved a fiercely independent home across the wooded peaks of the Hinterlands. Though reclusive by nature, they remain stalwart friends of the Alliance, defending the skies whenever called upon.",h="The Wildhammer clan is a rugged, nature-dwelling branch of the dwarven race famed for taming wild gryphons...",sec={{t="Exile from Grim Batol",b="Before the War of the Three Hammers, the Wildhammers lived in the foothills around Ironforge. When High King Modimus Anvilmar died, civil war fractured the dwarven kingdom, and the victorious Bronzebeards claimed Ironforge for themselves. Thane Khardros led his clan north to establish a magnificent hold inside the peak of Grim Batol. Yet peace was short-lived: the defeated Dark Irons soon laid siege to the fortress. While Khardros personally slew the Dark Iron sorceress Modgud, her dying curse blighted the mountain so deeply that the halls became uninhabitable. Refusing an invitation to move back under Bronzebeard rule, the Wildhammers chose self-reliance, marching north into the forests and rocky crags of the Hinterlands.",sp=0},{t="Riders of the Storm",b="In the lush highlands of their new realm, the Wildhammers built Aerie Peak. Unlike their subterranean cousins, these dwarves embraced the open sky and living rock, developing a deep, spiritual kinship with the native gryphons. Forging enchanted stormhammers in secluded aviaries, the Wildhammers became the finest aerial cavalry in Azeroth. During the Second War, when Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer pushed his forces toward Quel'Thalas and assaulted Aerie Peak, Supreme Commander Anduin Lothar and the Alliance came to their aid. In gratitude, High Thane Kurdran Wildhammer pledged his gryphon riders to the coalition, dogfighting the Horde's enslaved red dragons across the skies of Lordaeron and the Great Sea.",sp=0},{t="Loyal to the End",b="When the Dark Portal reopened, Kurdran Wildhammer led his riders into Draenor with the Alliance Expedition, sacrificing his return to seal the rift—an act commemorated by a colossal statue in Stormwind's Valley of Heroes. Back in Aerie Peak, High Thane Falstad stepped up to lead the clan. Even as the human kingdoms squabbled and fractured in later years, the Wildhammers remained steadfast partners of King Terenas Menethil II. When the Scourge swept across Lordaeron in the Third War, Wildhammer flyers took to the air once more, fighting desperately to protect allied lands and innocent refugees from the walking dead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Wildhammer clan based right now?",a="Their primary bastion is Aerie Peak, located in the mountainous forest of the Hinterlands, north of the Wetlands and the Thandol Span.",al={"wildhammer home","where do wildhammers live","wildhammer location"},it="place"},{q="Why don't the Wildhammers live in Ironforge?",a="Following the death of High King Modimus Anvilmar, the Wildhammer clan lost the civil war for control of Ironforge to the Bronzebeards. Thane Khardros led his clan north to build a new life in Grim Batol rather than bow to Bronzebeard rule.",al={"why did wildhammer leave ironforge","war of the three hammers wildhammer"},it="why_here"},{q="What happened to their fortress at Grim Batol?",a="During the War of the Three Hammers, the Dark Iron sorceress Modgud laid siege to Grim Batol. Thane Khardros slew her, but her dark sorcery infected the halls with foul corruption, making the mountain completely uninhabitable and forcing the clan into the Hinterlands.",al={"why is grim batol abandoned","grim batol curse","modgud grim batol"},it="ruin"},{q="Who leads the Wildhammer clan today?",a="High Thane Falstad Wildhammer leads the clan from Aerie Peak. He took command after his predecessor, Kurdran Wildhammer, joined the Alliance Expedition through the Dark Portal.",al={"wildhammer leader","high thane wildhammer","who rules aerie peak"},it="leader"},{q="How did the Wildhammers join the Alliance?",a="During the Second War, Orgrim Doomhammer attacked the Hinterlands while marching toward Quel'Thalas. Supreme Commander Anduin Lothar led the Alliance to defend Aerie Peak, driving off the Horde and forest trolls. Moved by their bravery, the Wildhammers joined the Alliance of Lordaeron.",al={"why are wildhammers alliance","wildhammer alliance history"},it="allies"},{q="Are the Wildhammer dwarves closely allied with Ironforge?",a="Yes. While the two clans fought during the War of the Three Hammers, they united to defeat the Dark Irons. To honor their friendship, they constructed the Valley of Kings in Loch Modan, and they have maintained a firm alliance ever since.",al={"bronzebeard wildhammer relationship","do ironforge and wildhammer get along"},it="ties"},{q="What is the Wildhammer clan?",a="The Wildhammer clan is one of the three great dwarven peoples of Azeroth, renowned for their close bond with nature, their independence, and their fearless aerial mastery. Unlike their Bronzebeard cousins who prefer subterranean halls, the Wildhammers dwell openly among high peaks and lush forests. Renowned across the world as peerless gryphon riders, they take to the skies wielding mystic, lightning-infused stormhammers to defend their homelands and aid their long-standing allies.",al={"who are the wildhammers","wildhammer clan overview","wildhammer dwarves"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where did the Wildhammer clan come from?",a="Long ago, the Wildhammers lived in the crags and foothills around Ironforge under High King Modimus Anvilmar. Following his death, the civil strife of the War of the Three Hammers erupted. Banished from Ironforge by the victorious Bronzebeards, Thane Khardros led his clan north to carve out the grand fortress of Grim Batol. After surviving a harrowing siege by the Dark Irons that left Grim Batol cursed and uninhabitable, the Wildhammers migrated further north into Lordaeron, eventually settling throughout the forested mountains of the Hinterlands.",al={"wildhammer history","wildhammer clan origins","origin of the wildhammer clan"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["race:Dwarf"]="For a dwarf of Ironforge, the Wildhammers are kin bound by old oaths. Though they prefer windy crags and open skies over deep mines, their bravery with the stormhammer commands the deepest respect.",["faction:Alliance"]="The Alliance owes much of its aerial supremacy to the Wildhammers, whose lightning-infused gryphon riders turned the tide against the Horde's red dragons during the Second War.",["class:Hunter"]="The bond Wildhammer mountaineers share with their wild gryphons represents the pinnacle of beast mastery, built on lifelong partnership rather than simple dominion.",["class:Shaman"]="Wildhammer tradition channels raw storm energy through mystic hammers, maintaining a bond with elemental lightning and nature far older than the modern church traditions of Ironforge."},kw={"aerie peak","falstad","grim batol","gryphon","hinterlands","khardros","kurdran","stormhammer","wildhammer"},rel={"topic:dwarf","zone:hinterlands","topic:bronzebeard-clan","subzone:grim-batol","topic:dark-iron-clan","topic:alliance-of-lordaeron","topic:second-war","subzone:valley-of-kings"},src={"Wildhammer clan"}}
E["topic:worgen"] = {t="topic",n="Worgen",z="general",s="Worgen are fierce, lupine humanoids cursed with uncontrollable primal rage, moving upright yet hunting with bestial swiftness. Once thought to be mere nightmares conjured from other planes, these savage pack-hunters now terrorize Silverpine Forest, Ashenvale, and Duskwood.",h="Worgen are fierce, lupine humanoids cursed with uncontrollable primal rage, moving upright yet hunting with...",sec={{t="Children of the Pack",b="Long before their recent emergence in Lordaeron and Kalimdor, wolf-men were whispered of in folktales and cautionary fables. In reality, their origins date back over nine millennia to the War of the Satyr. Seeking to harness the untamed fury of the wolf Ancient Goldrinn, radical night elf druids led by Ralaar Fangfire embraced the forbidden wolf pack form. When combined with the sacred Scythe of Elune, the magic violently backfired, transforming them into ferocious lupine monsters driven by bloodlust. Incapable of distinguishing friend from foe, their virulent bite spread the affliction to fellow night elves. To halt the nightmare, Malfurion Stormrage and the Cenarion Circle banished the cursed shapeshifters beneath the sacred boughs of Daral'nir into an eternal slumber within the Emerald Dream.",sp=0},{t="Arugal's Folly",b="During the Third War, as the Scourge hordes overran Lordaeron and descended toward the Greymane Wall, Archmage Arugal sought a weapon to save Gilneas. Drawing from the forbidden research of the Dalaran wizard Ur, Arugal pierced the veil and summoned the slumbering beasts into Silverpine Forest. The summoned worgen devastated the undead invaders with savage abandon, but the archmage could not leash their fury. The beasts turned on Gilnean soldiers, spreading their curse through fang and claw. Driven mad by guilt and fascination, Arugal adopted the monsters as his children, seizing Baron Silverlaine's ancestral seat—now rechristened Shadowfang Keep—and cursing nearby villagers into monstrous nocturnal servitude.",sp=0},{t="The Scythe Awoken",b="Across the sea in Kalimdor, another summoning unleashed the worgen anew. Desperate to purge the lingering demonic remnants of the Burning Legion from Felwood, the night elf Sentinel Velinde Starsong unearthed the Scythe of Elune. Channelling the ancient relic, she called forth wolf-men to tear apart the demonic satyrs. Yet as battles raged, Velinde realized that the scythe had slipped beyond her command—the beasts began to manifest without her consent, slipping into the dark forests of Ashenvale and beyond. Now roaming in wild, feral packs, the worgen spread terror wherever the shadow of their moonlit hunts falls.",sp=1}},faq={{q="What are worgen?",a="Worgen are ferocious, upright lupine humanoids possessed of terrifying strength, predatory senses, and dark shadow magic. They primarily dwell in secluded woodlands and ruins, hunting in packs under the thrall of uncontrollable bestial fury.",al={"what is a worgen","worgen lore","wolf men"},it="identity"},{q="Where did the worgen originally come from?",a="They were originally night elf druids known as the Druids of the Pack. Seeking to master the untamed spirit of the wolf Ancient Goldrinn during the War of the Satyr, their ritual using the Scythe of Elune corrupted them into ravenous beasts, prompting Malfurion Stormrage to banish them into the Emerald Dream.",al={"worgen origin","who made worgen","how were worgen created"},it="origin"},{q="How did worgen end up in Silverpine Forest?",a="During the Third War, Archmage Arugal summoned them from their slumber using the esoteric research of the late Dalaran wizard Ur. Arugal hoped they would repel the Scourge battering the Greymane Wall, but the unleashed beasts turned on everyone.",al={"silverpine worgen","why are worgen in silverpine","who summoned worgen"},it="why_here"},{q="How does someone become a worgen?",a="The condition spreads as a virulent supernatural affliction. A bite or scratch from an active worgen infects humanoids, eventually causing victims to succumb to feral urges and transform into wolf-beasts unless restrained by potent discipline or specialized sorcery.",al={"worgen curse","can you turn into a worgen","worgen bite"}},{q="What is the Scythe of Elune?",a="The Scythe of Elune is a powerful relic created during the War of the Satyr by binding a fang of the wolf Ancient Goldrinn to a staff of Elune. It holds deep, volatile dominion over the worgen curse and possesses the power to summon and agitate them.",al={"scythe of elune","scythe elune lore"}},{q="Who is Archmage Arugal?",a="Arugal was a Dalaran archmage who served Gilneas. After summoning the worgen to fight the Scourge, he succumbed to madness, abandoning Gilneas to rule over the haunted ruins of Shadowfang Keep alongside his monstrous 'children.'",al={"arugal","who is arugal"}},{q="Why are worgen in Ashenvale and Kalimdor?",a="The night elf Sentinel Velinde Starsong used the recovered Scythe of Elune to summon worgen against demonic invaders in Felwood. The relic soon began pulling worgen into the world autonomously, allowing packs to break loose into Ashenvale.",al={"ashenvale worgen","kalimdor worgen","terrowulf"}},{q="Why are worgen hostile to travelers?",a="Most worgen you encounter are consumed by feral, primal fury. The curse strips away reason and self-control, driving those afflicted into a frenzy where they view outsiders as prey or threats to be torn apart. Even those who embrace the transformation voluntarily, like the cultists following Alpha Prime or Archmage Arugal, view mortals as enemies to be slaughtered or cursed into joining their packs.",al={"why do worgen attack","are worgen hostile","why are worgen aggressive"},it="hostile",add=true},{q="Who leads the worgen?",a="The worgen do not answer to a single master, but gather in packs under powerful figures. In Silverpine Forest, many serve Archmage Arugal at Shadowfang Keep, who summoned them from their slumber, while others follow Alpha Prime—the first worgen—and his Wolf Cult. Independent packs run wild across Lordaeron, Duskwood, and Kalimdor, answering only to their alpha or primal instincts.",al={"worgen leader","who controls the worgen","worgen master"},it="leader",add=true},{q="Who are the worgen allied with?",a="The worgen have no broad alliances. In Silverpine, certain packs answer to the renegade Archmage Arugal and his Wolf Cult out of Shadowfang Keep. Otherwise, these feral beasts run rampant across the wilderness, attacking Scourge, Alliance, and Forsaken without distinction. They serve only their primal hunger and the leaders of their individual packs.",al={"worgen allies","who do worgen serve","worgen factions"},it="allies",add=true},{q="Where did the worgen come from?",a="The worgen originated thousands of years ago during the War of the Satyr. Night elven druids seeking to harness the untamed fury of the wolf Ancient Goldrinn formed the Druids of the Pack. Combining Elune's magic with the wolf form through the Scythe of Elune, they transformed into savage beast-men. When their bloodlust proved uncontrollable and contagious, Malfurion Stormrage banished them to deep slumber within the Emerald Dream, where they remained until recent summonings dragged them back into Azeroth.",al={"history of worgen","worgen backstory","how were worgen created"},it="history",add=true}},ang={["class:Druid"]="The worgen are an enduring cautionary tale of the wolf pack form, demonstrating the fatal danger of invoking Goldrinn's untamed wrath without the balance of the Cenarion Circle.",["race:NightElf"]="The existence of the worgen remains a heavily veiled chapter of ancient kaldorei history, tied directly to the War of the Satyr and the sorrow of Priestess Belysra and Malfurion Stormrage.",["race:Undead"]="In Silverpine Forest, the Forsaken battle feral worgen packs and Arugal's twisted cultists for mastery over the region south of the Ruins of Lordaeron.",["class:Mage"]="Dalaran's Kirin Tor long debated the research in Ur's mystical treatises; Arugal's catastrophic conjuring proved Ur's warnings that these nightmare creatures should never be summoned."},kw={"arugal","druids of the pack","goldrinn","pyrewood","scythe of elune","shadowfang keep","silverpine","ur","velinde starsong","worgen"},rel={"npc:arugal","zone:shadowfang","zone:silverpine","quest:1043","quest:1013","topic:druid","topic:emerald-dream","npc:baron-silverlaine"},src={"Worgen"}}
